Version 5.0 (unreleased)
========================

1. Constraint Diff is removed. Its zero-length rectangles followed neither
   the strict nor the non-strict semantics of diffn. Use
   new Diffn(rectangles, true) (strict, minizinc diffn) or
   new Diff2(rectangles) (non-strict, minizinc diffn_nonstrict) instead;
   Diff.builder() becomes Diffn.builder()...strict(true).build() or
   Diff2.diff2Builder(). With positive lengths all of them accept the
   same solutions, but they may search differently. Diff2, Disjoint and
   DisjointConditional, which extended Diff, now extend the package-private
   AbstractDiff and keep their behaviour. The Scala diff2 wrappers now post
   Diff2, as documented, instead of Diff.

2. The MiniZinc solver configuration org.jacop.msc declares -debug, so
   minizinc --solver org.jacop -debug model.mzn prints the constraints
   as imposed in JaCoP; before, minizinc rejected -debug and it had to
   be passed with --fzn-flags -debug. The MiniZinc IDE shows it as a
   solver option.

3. New constraint CircuitCost(x, costs, cost): x is a circuit and cost is
   the sum of its arc costs. It bounds the cost and removes arcs with the
   assignment relaxation (incremental Hungarian method, reduced costs) and,
   for symmetric costs, the Held-Karp 1-tree bound. The FlatZinc front end
   posts it as a redundant constraint on circuit(x) with
   cost = sum(i)(d[i, x[i]]); -Dorg.jacop.fz.circuitCost=false turns this off.

4. CumulativeUnary (and CumulativeUnaryOptional, MiniZinc disjunctive) is
   about 2x faster on job shop models with the same pruning. The overload
   check and detectable precedences run in linear time after sorting on
   the union-find time line of Fahimi, Ouellet and Quimper (Constraints,
   2018) instead of the Theta tree; not-last reads ect(Theta \ {i}) from
   the tree without changing it; edge finding builds its tree bottom up;
   and the bounds of the tasks are read once per pass and again only after
   a rule changed them. Search trees are unchanged.

5. CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) runs its unary rules (detectable
   precedences, not-first/not-last, edge finding) for any number of tasks.
   Before, with more than 100 tasks (max_edge_find_size) it ran only the
   time-table profile, contrary to properties.txt; a single machine with
   400 tasks then timed out where it is now solved in 808 nodes, and job
   shops with 150 and 200 jobs reach better makespans. max_edge_find_size
   now applies to Cumulative only.

6. New constraint IncreasingSum(x, s, strict): x is increasing (strictly
   or not) and s is its sum, bounds consistent in linear time per call
   (Petit, Regin and Beldiceanu, CP 2011). The FlatZinc front end posts it
   as a redundant constraint on an int_lin_eq or int_lin_le with unit
   coefficients whose variables are ordered by increasing/decreasing or by
   chains of x[i] <= x[i+1] from symmetry breaking;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.increasingSum=false turns this off. On number
   partitioning it cuts failures up to 13x.

7. Constraints have a degree weight, Constraint.setDegreeWeight(w): the
   number of constraints each counts as in the static degree of its
   variables, Var.sizeConstraintsOriginal(), which MostConstrainedStatic
   reads (FlatZinc most_constrained, occurrence, impact and
   smallest_most_constrained). It is 1 by default; Var.adjustDegree(n)
   credits a variable with constraints a reformulation removed. The
   redundant constraints that the FlatZinc front end adds (disjunctive
   cliques, CircuitCost, IncreasingSum) have weight 0, so they no longer
   change the variable order of these searches, and a store derived by
   presolve (org.jacop.presolve.Derivation) keeps the static degrees of
   its parent.

8. A FlatZinc model without search annotation is solved with free search,
   as with -f (dom/wdeg, restarts, nogoods), instead of labelling its
   variables in input order with domain splitting. On 78 such models
   (MiniZinc benchmarks and the regression set, 60 s) it answers 65
   instead of 54: queens n = 50 to 400 within 8 s instead of no solution,
   and jobshop la09, orb02 and orb10 proved. --default-search input
   restores the input order search. FreeSearch now reads the clock at
   every node, so a time limit holds when nodes are slow.

9. The set order constraints AleB and AltB (MiniZinc set_le and set_lt,
   sets compared as sorted lists of elements) are bounds consistent: an
   element stays undecided in a set only when the constraint has a
   solution with it and one without it, and the cardinalities are narrowed
   to those of the solutions. Before, they only removed the elements of b
   below min(lub(a)) and checked fixed sets. On steiner-triples from the
   MiniZinc benchmarks the search tree shrinks up to 4x.

10. The FlatZinc front end posts the jacop_count constraints (MiniZinc
    count with a fixed value) over the same list as one CountValues
    instead of one Count per value. As Count constraints each of them was
    queued on every change of a list variable and scanned the list, so
    magicseq with n = 300 found no solution in 60 s; it is now solved in
    0.2 s, and n = 500 in 0.7 s. CountValues also uses that the counts and
    the variables taking no counted value add up to the list length, so
    search trees can be smaller.

11. WeightedSpanningTree (MiniZinc weighted_spanning_tree, d_weighted_
    spanning_tree, dwst) has a cost bound for directed graphs too: the new
    ArborescenceBound computes Edmonds' minimum arborescence containing the
    selected edges, with a virtual root for an unknown root, and removes
    edges and root values whose reduced cost (Houndji, Schaus, Hounkonnou
    and Wolsey, CPAIOR 2017) exceeds the cost's upper bound. On degree-
    constrained minimum arborescences with 12 to 60 nodes (60 s) JaCoP
    proved 2 of 16 optima before and proves all 16 now, each within 0.7 s
    of search and 2,600 nodes; Gecode 6.4.0 proves 1 and OR-Tools CP-SAT
    9.15 proves 9.

12. New constraint IncreasingNValue(x, count): x is non-decreasing and
    takes count distinct values, domain consistent in time linear in the
    total size of the domains of x (Beldiceanu, Hermenier, Lorca and
    Petit, CPAIOR 2010). The FlatZinc front end posts it instead of Values
    for a jacop_nvalue (MiniZinc nvalue) whose variables are ordered, by
    the same order detection as IncreasingSum (now in IncreasingDetector);
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.increasingNValue=false turns this off. On generated
    slot-assignment models (symmetric tasks, minimise the slots used),
    four of fifteen instances that took 12 s, 40 s or more than 60 s are
    solved in under a second.

13. New balancing constraints Deviation(x, s, d) and Spread(x, s, d):
    sum(x) = s and d = sum(i)(abs(n * x[i] - s)), or the sum of
    (n * x[i] - s)^2, with general forms over abs(a * x[i] - b) and
    (a * x[i] - b)^2. They are bounds consistent on x for d >= cost and
    raise d.min to the least cost of an assignment with sum s (Schaus,
    Deville, Dupont and Regin, CP 2007; Schaus and Regin, EJCO 2014).
    The FlatZinc front end posts them as redundant constraints on a sum of
    int_abs or squared terms over variables whose sum an int_lin_eq fixes,
    as sum(p)(abs(n * load[p] - total)) with sum(load) = total compiles;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.balance=false turns this off. JaCoP's MiniZinc library
    adds the predicates deviation and spread. On 28 balanced academic
    curriculum instances with a deviation or spread objective JaCoP proves
    52 of the 54 optima in under 3 s each; without the new constraints it
    proves none in 20 s.

14. New constraint StockingCost(x, d, h, H, c) for lot sizing (Houndji,
    Schaus, Wolsey and Deville, CP 2014 and Constraints 2019): order i is
    produced in period x[i] <= d[i], at most c orders per period, and
    sum h[i] * (d[i] - x[i]) <= H. It bounds H by the greedy optimum of
    the relaxation without the lower bounds of x and raises the minima of
    x that the maximum of H allows (bounds consistent on the relaxation
    for equal costs). MiniZinc predicate stocking_cost in the JaCoP
    library; the FlatZinc front end also posts it as a redundant
    constraint on an alldifferent (or gcc) with a cost sum over the same
    variables; -Dorg.jacop.fz.stockingCost=false turns this off. On the
    lot sizing part of the 46 CSPLib prob058 instances it proves 39 optima
    in 60 s instead of 19.

15. New constraint UnaryTransition(s, d, family, transitions[, optional]):
    a unary resource with sequence-dependent transition (setup) times
    between families of tasks, also with optional tasks (Dejemeppe, Van
    Cauwelaert and Schaus, CP 2015; Van Cauwelaert, Dejemeppe and Schaus,
    J. Scheduling 2020). Overload, detectable precedences, not-last and
    edge finding run on Theta-Lambda trees whose completion times count a
    lower bound of the transitions among the families of their tasks, plus
    a pairwise rule. MiniZinc predicate disjunctive_setup in the JaCoP
    library (also over var opt int starts); the FlatZinc front end posts it
    instead of CumulativeUnary on a disjunctive clique whose disjunctions
    carry transition times. It explains its own propagation for lazy
    clause generation (--lcg): bounds, absent optional tasks and failures
    by pairs, cuts of tasks that must precede, not-first/not-last and
    edge-finding sets, found again from the bounds before the atom. On 38
    job shops and single machines with setups (60 s, --lcg) they raise
    the optima proved from 0-5 to 8-12, depending on the model and the
    search; before, the generic reason explained 83 to 86 % of the reasons.

16. The FlatZinc front end posts a redundant Knapsack on multi-dimensional
    knapsacks: a profit (a sum of 0/1 variables with positive, not all
    equal coefficients that is bounded from below, or that defines a
    variable) and capacity rows over the same variables (sums with
    positive coefficients bounded from above). The Knapsack is posted on
    one surrogate row, the capacity rows weighted by the dual values of
    their linear relaxation (a small bounded simplex), so it prunes with
    the bound of the relaxation of all rows together; each linear
    constraint alone sees only its own row. It is posted only when that
    bound fixes a variable at the root by its reduced cost.
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.knapsack=false turns this off. On the 7 multi-knapsack
    instances of the MiniZinc benchmarks JaCoP now finds the solution in
    under 0.3 s in every mode (default, -f, --lcg); before, it found none
    in 30 s on 5 of them.

17. New constraints InterDistance(x, p), |x[i] - x[j]| >= p for all pairs,
    bounds consistent in O(n^2) (Quimper, Lopez-Ortiz and Pesant, AAAI
    2006), and MultiInterDistance(x, m, p), at most m values in every
    window of p, bounds consistent (Ouellet and Quimper, IJCAI 2011). The
    mznlib has inter_distance and multi_inter_distance for them. The
    FlatZinc front end posts a cumulative with fixed equal durations p >= 2
    and fixed equal resource use as MultiInterDistance with m = limit div
    use (InterDistance for m = 1), and adds InterDistance to a disjunctive
    clique of equal fixed lengths; -Dorg.jacop.fz.interDistance=add keeps
    the cumulative too and =false turns this off. On 90 runway instances
    with time windows (one to three runways, 30 to 90 aircraft, 60 s) it
    answers 79 instead of 63; Gecode answers 59 and Chuffed 60.
18. New constraint Reservoir(times, changes, min, max) and a MiniZinc
    predicate reservoir in the JaCoP library: the level, the sum of the
    changes of the events at or before each time, stays within min..max.
    It propagates with the balance constraint of Laborie (AIJ 2003) over
    the precedences x + c <= y of the model, which the FlatZinc front end
    gives it. On 30 project scheduling instances with storages (60 s) it
    finds solutions on 29 instead of 25 and proves the optima both prove
    1.8 times faster than the decomposition.

19. New constraint EnergyPrecedence (Laborie, AIJ 2003): the tasks that
    must end before a time point (such as a makespan) need their energy
    between their earliest start and that point, and symmetrically for
    the tasks that must start after it. The FlatZinc front end posts it on
    every cumulative whose tasks are ordered by the precedences of the
    model; -Dorg.jacop.fz.energyPrecedence=false turns this off. On the
    560 cumulative benchmark instances (30 s) it proves 215 optima instead
    of 211 and loses none.

18. New constraint OverlappingAlldiff(s, t): alldifferent(s) and
    alldifferent(t) on lists that share variables, propagated together on
    the bounds (Bessiere, Katsirelos, Narodytska, Quimper and Walsh,
    AAAI 2010). It bounds how many shared and how many own variables of
    each list can take values in each interval, closes the bounds under
    additivity of the counts, and couples them through the two
    alldifferents, which detects every failure of the bound relaxation and
    applies the paper's rules 2a-2d. The FlatZinc front end posts it next
    to the two all_different constraints when they share at least half of
    the unfixed variables of each; -Dorg.jacop.fz.overlappingAlldiff=false
    turns this off. On the paper's random instances (three alldifferents
    sharing 10 to 12 variables, 90 instances, 60 s) JaCoP solves 80
    instead of 65; on the paper's pathological family it proves
    unsatisfiability at the root where it timed out from n = 4.

19. New set constraint AllDisjoint(s): the sets s are pairwise disjoint.
    Besides the pairwise rule of AdisjointB it uses the cardinalities of
    the sets (Bessiere, Hebrard, Hnich and Walsh, CP 2004): elements are
    assigned to sets, or to no set, by a flow that meets the minimal
    cardinalities, and the strongly connected components of its residual
    graph give bounds consistency on the set bounds. MiniZinc all_disjoint
    now posts it instead of n(n-1)/2 jacop_disjoint. Social golfers search
    the same trees as before; on project allocation models (sets of
    students with cardinality bounds, 8 to 32 projects, 60 s) it finds
    a first solution on 3 of 18 instances where the pairwise version finds
    none and a better objective on 8 more.

19. New constraint ChannelOrder(x, b, t, atMost): b[i] <=> x <= t[i] (or
    x > t[i]), the order counterpart of ChannelReif, with one propagator
    for all thresholds of x and explanations for lazy clause generation.
    Presolve (--presolve on|check) replaces the reified comparisons of one
    variable with constants (int_le_reif, int_lt_reif, int_ge_reif,
    int_gt_reif) by one ChannelOrder, a presolve rule;
    -Djacop.presolve.orderLiterals=false turns it off. On the 42 presolve
    dev-tier instances with such reifications (60 s) search is the same and
    solve time is 14 % lower (9 % with --lcg).

20. Presolve breaks variable symmetries (--presolve on|check, unless
    --symmetry off). The FlatZinc model becomes a coloured graph (Puget,
    CP 2005): variables coloured by domain, constraints by name and
    constants, argument positions by place, with the arguments whose order
    does not matter (linear terms, all_different, bool_clause, cumulative
    tasks, bin packing items, the two sides of int_eq, ...) given one
    colour. New org.jacop.presolve.GraphAutomorphisms finds generators of
    its automorphism group by individualization and refinement (as nauty,
    saucy and bliss), with the variables as the base in lex-leader order,
    within at most 1 s (-Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetryTime, in ms) and a
    twentieth of the time left. Each generator g posts X <=lex g(X).
    The objective variable is fixed by every symmetry, so optima are kept;
    solutions are lost, so it is off when a satisfaction problem asks for
    more than one solution (-a, -n). Pigeonhole with 10 pigeons and 9
    holes needs 2,392 failures instead of 764,943.

21. CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) is faster again, with the same
    search trees: 4.6x on job shops (geometric mean over 23 instances from
    6x6 to 200x20; 1.2x to 40x, more tasks per machine gain more) and 2.2x
    on open shops. A fixed task that ends before every other task can
    start is left out of the unary rules and the time-table for the rest
    of the subtree; in a search that schedules from left to right these
    are most tasks. A rule that pruned nothing is not run again until a
    bound changes. With constant durations and resource uses a unary
    time-table replaces the sweep of the cumulative profile and removes
    the same values, holes included. Edge finding updates only the Lambda
    values on the path of a removed task and stops where they no longer
    change.

22. Steiner (MiniZinc steiner and dsteiner) has a cost bound: the new
    SteinerBound runs Wong's dual ascent on the directed cut relaxation
    (no LP solver) and, with the reduced costs, removes nodes and edges
    that no tree within max(cost) can use. Selected edges cost 0 and add
    their weight; a dsteiner root that is not fixed is replaced by a
    virtual root. It is posted only when no weight is negative. On
    geometric Steiner trees with 20 to 120 nodes and Steiner arborescences
    with 20 to 80 nodes (60 s) JaCoP proves all 28 optima in under 2 s,
    against 3 before.

23. Binpacking reasons on the number of items of each bin (bin packing
    with cardinality, Schaus et al. CP 2012; Derval, Regin and Schaus,
    Constraints 2018). A new optional argument card (constructor and
    builder) gives the number of items of each bin, items of weight 0
    included. The constraint bounds it from the loads (the fewest heaviest
    items that reach the minimum load, the most lightest items within the
    maximum) and from the total number of items, bounds the loads from it,
    and removes an item from a bin when the bin would then need too much
    or reach too little ("too big", "too small"). The FlatZinc front end
    passes the counts of a global_cardinality (or global_cardinality_low_up)
    over the same bin variables, as in curriculum models;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingCardinality=false turns it off. On 40
    generated balanced academic curriculum instances JaCoP proves 37 optima
    in 60 s against 30 before. Without card the rules are off by default;
    cardinalityReasoning(true) turns them on.

24. Subcircuit and Circuit explain their own propagation for lazy clause
    generation (--lcg), by cuts of the successor graph (Francis and
    Stuckey, Constraints 2014): an arc on no cycle, a node that cannot
    join the required ones, a node every path between two required nodes
    passes through (the dominator pruning), the fixed arcs of a chain for
    the arc that would close it early (Circuit), and Hall sets for the
    rest. Before, both used the generic reason: on mario (--lcg -f) every
    Subcircuit reason, 266 atoms on average; now none, with 9 atoms on
    average. On cvrp P-n16-k8 the Circuit reasons fall from 122 atoms
    (all generic) to 19. tpp is proved about 1.7x faster; mario, cvrp,
    is, p1f and stochastic-vrp end as before within 60 s.

25. --lcg -f finds solutions on cutstock and largescheduling, where it
    found none in 60 s. Under lazy clause generation the time table of
    Cumulative keeps to the bounds of the starts: a hole of thousands of
    start values was thousands of atoms, each with its own literals. Its
    explanations look for overloads only where the compulsory-part load
    changes, weigh energy windows in one sweep per window start, and explain
    a task left with no start instead of giving the generic reason. Without
    a search annotation, the runs of LCG free search alternate with dom/wdeg
    on the default search variables, as they alternate with an annotation.
    On the 66 MiniZinc benchmark instances with cumulative or without an
    annotation (60 s), 3 go from no solution to a solution, 3 more are
    proved optimal, and none lose a solution or a proof.

26. --lcg works on models with set variables; before, it printed "--lcg
    ignored: the model has float or set variables" and searched without
    learning. Each set variable is mirrored by a 0/1 variable for the
    membership of each element of its least upper bound that is not in its
    greatest lower bound and by a variable for its cardinality (new
    constraint org.jacop.set.constraints.SetMirror). Every change of the
    set's domain is passed to the mirror inside the constraint that made
    it, so the mirror's atoms have that constraint as reason and stand for
    the set in the generic explanations of the set constraints (new
    interface org.jacop.lcg.Mirrored). The explanation checker
    (-Djacop.lcg.check=true) checks the set constraints too. Under --lcg
    the decisions of a set_search annotation on an element are taken as
    the literal that the element is in the set. Set constraints that hold
    element by element (AintersectBeqC, AunionBeqC, AdiffBeqC, AinB, AeqB,
    AdisjointB, EinA) explain a membership by the memberships of the same
    element in their other sets, and SetMirror explains what the
    cardinality gives, instead of the generic reason over every element.
    AleB and AltB (set_le, set_lt) explain a membership by the memberships
    and cardinalities that leave their order automaton no solution with
    the other membership, found by relaxing them one at a time
    (steiner-triples 09: 1.1 s instead of 10.9 s). Float variables still
    turn --lcg off.

27. Lazy clause generation explains XmulYeqZ removals by bounds first: the
    product range of x and y (squares when x = y), the quotient range of a
    factor, and the square roots of z for x * x = z, citing only the
    bounds that matter. Before, a removal of more than 64 values, or with a
    factor ranging over 1024 values or more, got the generic reason. On gbac
    (squares over 0..1566) generic reasons drop from about half of the
    XmulYeqZ reasons to almost none and the reasons from 17 to 2 atoms;
    over the 46 MiniZinc benchmark instances with a variable product
    (--lcg -f, 60 s) the solved ones take 2 % less time (geometric mean)
    and gbac reaches a better objective on 5 of 6.

27. New constraint GlobalCardinality(x, values, counters) and
    GlobalCardinality(x, values, low, up): domain consistent global
    cardinality (Regin, AAAI 1996) on x, with the counters as intervals;
    values outside values are not restricted. The FlatZinc front end posts
    it for global_cardinality (jacop_count_values, _bounds and the closed
    forms) annotated :: domain, which used to be ignored. It explores the
    search tree of Gecode's domain consistent gcc. On rows and columns
    with cardinality bounds and forbidden values it solves 8 of 12 random
    instances in 60 s where counting solves 6, with 3x to 900x fewer
    nodes; on Challenge models that do not ask for :: domain counting is
    as good and cheaper, so it stays the default.

28. FlatZinc objective presolve (the objective equation shifted by the
    exactly-one sets of the model) runs without --presolve too, with
    degree weight 0. It reads n = count(x, v) (jacop_count) as
    n = sum(x[i] = v) when every x[i] has a literal for v, and the shifted
    cost terms of each variable encoded by value literals become one cost
    variable y = c[x] (ElementInteger). The reformulated equation replaces
    the constraint defining the objective when it has no more variables,
    and is used only when it gives cost variables or has at most half the
    variables of the definition; a stronger root bound alone did not pay
    on vrp under --lcg. On jp-encoding (60 s) JaCoP proves data300 in
    4.4 s instead of no proof and data200 in 17 s with --lcg -f, and
    data1400 under --lcg -f ends at 62,978 instead of 68,422;
    bus_scheduling r1a is proved in 3.1 s instead of not proved.
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.objectivePresolve=false turns it off.

28. With lazy clause generation (--lcg) an equality (XeqY, and int_eq_reif
    and int_eq_imp in FlatZinc) gives a variable of more than 10,000
    values only the bounds of the other one, not its holes: every value
    removed between the bounds was a != atom with its own literals, so
    copying a sparse domain into a large one made millions of them. On
    liner-sf-repositioning fm3_5, --lcg -f created 8.9 million literals in
    10 nodes and ran out of memory; it now finds 60 solutions in 60 s with
    119 thousand literals (tp7_10 too goes from an error to a solution).
    Of the 258 vs-ortools instances (60 s, --lcg -f) the limit changes 6;
    groupsplitter u15g3pref2 also improves (232 against 181-189) and the
    others keep their result. -Djacop.lcg.holeLimit changes the limit.

29. New constraint AlldiffSum(x, rel, s): alldifferent(x) and
    sum(x) rel s, rel ==, <= or >=, bounds consistent for <= and >=
    (Beldiceanu, Carlsson, Petit and Regin, ECAI 2012, O(n log n)). The
    FlatZinc front end posts it next to each int_lin_eq / int_lin_le with
    unit coefficients over variables that all_different constraints make
    pairwise different (killer sudoku cages, kakuro runs, magic square
    rows); -Dorg.jacop.fz.alldiffSum=false turns this off. Enumerating all
    solutions of 9x9 killer sudokus with cages of up to 5 or 7 cells needs
    58 % fewer nodes; with free search 22x22 kakuro are solved in 10 s
    instead of 115 s with one left unsolved. gardner_sum_square finds
    another optimal solution.

30. The FlatZinc front end also posts the redundant SubcircuitCost when a
    successor is fixed and written as a constant, as MiniZinc writes
    succ[LuigiHouse] = MarioHouse in mario; before, the detection gave up on
    such a subcircuit. All 6 mario instances of the OR-Tools comparison
    are now proved in 60 s: in 0.6 to 13 s under --lcg -f (2 before) and
    0.6 to 38 s by default (2 before).

31. Free search (-f, with and without --lcg) tries the cost variables of
    FlatZinc objective presolve first, cheapest value first (largest when
    maximising), before the annotation it alternates with, and with
    --lcg gives core-guided search 0.1 of the time instead of 0.5 when
    there are such variables. On jp-encoding (60 s, --lcg -f) the four
    large instances end 0.3% to 2.4% above the optimum instead of 13% to
    57% (data1400: 44,693 against 68,403, optimum 43,626), and data200
    and data300 are proved in about 7 s. Models without cost variables
    search as before.

32. The FlatZinc front end adds the linear relaxation of the model's linear
    constraints (org.jacop.constraints.lp.LinearRelaxation) as a
    propagator; --lp off turns it off. A bounded dual simplex, warm-started
    from the previous node, solves it during search. A Farkas certificate
    of an infeasible relaxation, or the duals of the objective, are
    rounded to integer multipliers and the combination of the rows is
    computed exactly, so each deduction is a valid linear inequality,
    explained by LinearForm under --lcg. Its cost is bounded: solves spend
    a work credit earned by each call (a solve that only tightens bounds
    a sixteenth of its work, a solve that fails the node refills it), a
    simplex stalled at its pivot limit 32 times in a row is given up, and models whose tableau would exceed 4M entries are left
    out (-Dorg.jacop.lp.maxTableau). On the 414 vs-ortools instances
    (20 s) JaCoP proves 116 instead of 106 (153 instead of 147 with
    --lcg) and loses no proof, including wwtpp-real UNSAT instances in
    about a second and rel2onto optima. LinearExplanation now merges a
    variable that occurs with weights of both signs and refuses reasons
    whose sums overflow a long.

33. --lcg -f branches on defined variables last. Activity branching picked
    the variables of defines_var annotations (sums, shortest-path values,
    the objective) as often as the model's decision variables, though they
    follow from those; it now branches on them only once every other
    variable is fixed. On the 42 instances of road-cons, city-position,
    gbac, celar, vrp, spot5 and trucking (60 s) the objective is better on
    32 and worse on 4: celar 1.4x to 3.5x, gbac about 2x, city-position
    1.5x to 2x. Trucking solved as a satisfaction problem finds a solution
    in 0.02 s, where it found none in 10 s. -Djacop.lcg.late=none turns it
    off.

34. Shorter linear explanations for lazy clause generation (--lcg). A
    lifted linear reason spends its slack on the terms that cost least
    first, so it leaves out as many terms as the slack allows, and the
    entailment reasons of reified linear constraints are lifted too. The
    FlatZinc front end splits a linear constraint of more than 40 terms
    into sums of blocks of about sqrt(n) terms, each with a new variable
    (--free-config lcg-split=<n>, 0 = off): its reasons cite about sqrt(n)
    bounds instead of n. On cutstock type10_TEST9 the demand sums' reasons
    go from 841 to 17 atoms and learnt clauses from 574 to 19; on vrp,
    spot5, gbac and cutstock (60 s, --lcg -f) more instances end better
    than worse.

35. Binpacking with cardinality also reasons on groups of consecutive
    bins: the items whose bins all lie in the group are packed in it, its
    count and load bounds are the sums over its bins, and the rules of one
    bin (counts, "too small", "too big", load bounds) are applied to the
    group. Each bin of a group can be fillable on its own while the group
    is not, as in curriculum models where precedences confine courses to
    ranges of periods. When the group rules keep pruning nothing they are
    run at fewer and fewer calls until they prune again. On 40 generated
    balanced academic curriculum instances (60 s) JaCoP proves all 40
    optima, against 37 before, and bacp_p10_n71_s0_1_6 needs 42 failures
    instead of 251,141; the minizinc-benchmarks bacp, curriculum and gbac
    instances end as before.

36. Free search (-f) for optimisation spends about one failure in twenty on
    objective probes, after OR-Tools CP-SAT's objective_lb_search and
    objective_shaving workers: runs with the objective bounded by its lower
    bound plus an offset (0 at first, doubled after each refuted probe,
    halved after a probe that reached its fail limit). A probe that
    explores its whole tree raises the lower bound at the root for good;
    probes do not teach dom/wdeg, give no restart nogoods and do not count
    as restarts. FreeSearch.Builder.probeObjective(share, failLimit) in the
    Java API (off by default), --free-config probe=<share> (0 = off),
    probe-fails=<n> in FlatZinc, and the statistics objectiveProbes,
    objectiveProbesRefuted and objectiveProbeFailures with -s. On 375
    optimisation instances of the free-search benchmark sets (60 s) it
    proves 6 more optima and loses 2, is 5 to 11 % faster on the
    instances both prove in three of the four sets (linear-to-program
    60 s -> 11 s, road-cons 60 s -> 1 s), and ends with a better objective
    on 24 of the others and a worse one on 21.

37. New constraint SubcircuitCostPair(x, c1, s1, c2, s2): two sums of arc
    costs on one subcircuit, such as a prize collected within a length
    budget, bounded together by Lagrangian relaxation: for lambda >= 0,
    s1 >= min assignment of (c1 + lambda * c2) - lambda * max(s2), and the
    same for the other sides. It searches lambda on the concave piecewise
    linear bound, prunes arcs by reduced costs, and backs off a direction
    that prunes nothing. The FlatZinc front end weighs the sum of a
    redundant SubcircuitCost against each other sum on the subcircuit
    (also a prize made of tests only), except under --lcg;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.circuitCostPair=true or false decides. On mario
    (MiniZinc challenge) the default search proves the optimum 3x faster
    (geometric mean of 5 instances, medium_4 32 s to 3 s) and -f stays
    within 0.5 s; on the 6 hardest orienteering instances proved in 30 s
    it is 1.4x faster (1.3x with -f), with an extra proof on
    orienteering_40_1.

38. A LinearInt that drops its fixed variables restores that state on
    backtracking only at the levels where it changed, instead of being
    called at every removed level. Many short sums, such as the blocks of
    a long sum split under --lcg, no longer slow down backtracking:
    skill_allocation_mzn_1m_2 (-f --lcg) solves in 11.9 s instead of
    17.2 s with the same search. Store.recordChange(Stateful) is the new
    hook for such state.

39. Not-last in CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) now tests every
    task, as Vilim's algorithm does. A task whose latest completion lets
    no new task into Theta was skipped, and a task bounded by its own
    latest start now gets the largest latest start of the other tasks.
    Pruning is stronger, so search trees can change: the job shop and
    open shop instances solved to optimality keep their node counts, and
    a 150x15 job shop reaches makespan 8992 in 30 s instead of 9003.

40. Faster FlatZinc front end and input order tie-breaking. SimpleSelect
    breaks ties in input order from an int array kept in step with its
    search array instead of two map lookups per comparison; removing
    aliased variables from the default search is linear instead of
    quadratic; table rows are sorted and deduplicated as int[] without
    boxing; and each FlatZinc constraint name is resolved to its
    generator method once. oocsp_racks_100_r1 runs in 18 s instead of
    29 s (search 5.5 s instead of 15 s), with the same search tree.

41. --lcg -f takes turns at restarts between three kinds of runs: the
    search annotation (dom/wdeg without one), the SAT solver's own decisions
    on literals with phase false and phase saving, as in CP-SAT's default
    search, and activity branching. Before, it alternated the annotation
    with activity branching only. On the 258 vs-ortools instances (20 s) it
    proves 105 instead of 100 and answers 212 instead of 207, with the
    objective better on 28 and worse on 29. Among the instances where CP-SAT
    was far ahead (60 s): gbac UD8 4,793 -> 102, UD4 5,664 -> 1,557,
    reduced_UD3 10,790 -> 2,489; city-position 5-06 772,299 -> 36,376, 5-08
    420,538 -> 6,147, 8-06 605,806 -> 117,741; celar is 10 to 45 % worse.
    --free-config lcg-branching= selects literals (decisions on literals
    only), auto (literals, with activity branching from 1000 conflicts to a
    first solution: city-position 5-05 2,850 and road_17 12 million instead
    of 99 million, but it loses first solutions on cargo, linear-to-program
    and p1f) or activity (the previous search). With them, the sums of
    lcg-split are branched on last, as defined variables, and defined 0/1
    variables (reifications) are no longer branched on last: on
    skill-allocation 5d_1, where all but 126 of 12,851 variables are defined
    reifications, activity branching proves the optimum in 5 s instead of
    no proof in 30 s. Under --lcg, abs(x) = y with y >= k prunes the bounds
    of x instead of each value of -k+1..k-1, which made one atom per value,
    and the semantic minimisation of learnt clauses takes linear time on a
    long group of literals on one variable (city-position 7-04 learnt
    clauses of 7,527 literals).

42. Parallel portfolio for FlatZinc: fzn-jacop -p <n> (minizinc -p) solves
    the model in n threads, each with a store of its own: the options
    given, then free search and lazy clause generation with other settings
    (up to 9 threads). The threads share the objective value of the best
    solution, which FreeSearch reads at every node and LcgSolver adds as a
    clause, and the first thread that completes decides the answer. With
    -f -p 4 on 4 cores, 60 s: 52 of the 63 lost-free instances solved
    instead of 39 and 156 of 160 regression instances instead of 144. All
    solutions of satisfaction problems (-a, -n), -o and float models stay
    on one thread. New: FreeSearch.Builder.sharedBound, LcgSolver
    .setSharedBound and stop, CdclSolver.cancel, Store.interrupt.

43. FlatZinc objective presolve expands the objective through more of the
    model: equalities (bool2int, bool_eq, a 0/1 variable and its literal
    of 1) lead a term to an equal variable that has a definition; a
    disjunction r <-> or(b) (array_bool_or, bool_clause_reif) is
    r = sum(b) when at most one b can be 1 (literals of one variable, or
    of one value for variables of one jacop_alldiff/jacop_alldistinct);
    an element y = c[x] of constants is y = sum(c_v * (x = v)) when every
    value of x has a literal. On rel2onto each matched attribute now pays
    for its leaf edge at the root (5_5: bound 153,163 instead of 33,163,
    optimum 161,783). All six instances are proved in every mode (60 s):
    by default in 0.4 to 5.7 s instead of 1.9 to 26 s, with --lcg in 0.5
    to 1.4 s (2 of 6 proved before), with --lcg -f in 0.1 to 5.3 s (1 of
    6) and with -f in 0.1 to 6.9 s (3 of 6). On the 552 instances of the
    free-search kit only rel2onto and one prize-collecting instance
    change, with the same result.

44. Under --lcg a failure of element with a constant list is explained by
    the value's atoms when that takes fewer atoms than the index's: one
    atom of the value covers every position of its constant. On
    crosswords grid-19.05 dict-80 a failure took about 6,000 atoms and
    now takes 273 on average, and --lcg -f finds solutions there (550 in
    60 s, none before). Linear reasons of a bound that skipped holes now
    cite only the holes next to the bound, not every hole of the
    variable.

45. IntVar keeps the bounds of its domain in two int fields, as Gecode's
    IntVarImp does, so min(), max(), singleton() and value() no longer
    test the domain class and load from the domain. The fields are updated
    on every domain event other than ANY and on backtracking; search is
    unchanged (same node counts). Paired runs, 10 s: on the 100-instance
    dev tier (8 reps) the default search is 8.7% faster (geometric mean),
    -f 17.6% and --lcg 3.3%; on mznbench (249 instances, 3 reps) 8.5%,
    13.6% and 1.5%. Code that changes a variable's domain in place other
    than through the domain's in methods, addDom or setDomain (for example
    x.domain.subtractAdapt(v) before search) must call x.refreshBounds().

46. LCG free search (--lcg -f) can take turns between deciding the 0/1
    variables first and one order of activity for all variables: with
    --free-config lcg-booleans-first=turns its runs change between the two
    orders at each restart (on, the default, and off fix one order, as
    does -Djacop.lcg.booleansFirst). Each order wins clearly on some
    models (without: smelt, java-routing, jobshop swv07, on-call-rostering;
    with: community-detection, mario), and no static feature of the model
    predicted which. On the 249 mznbench instances of the free-search kit
    (60 s, lcg-split=0, two runs each), turns solved 161 and 159 against
    156 and 159 with Booleans first, and was better on 23 instances and
    worse on 10 in both runs. With lcg-split=40 it loses celar graph05,
    whose core-guided phase stalls without Booleans first, so it is not
    the default yet. New: LcgSolver.setBooleansFirst,
    setBooleansFirstTurns and the statistics lcgBooleanVariables,
    lcgWideVariables and lcgBooleansFirst.

47. --lcg -f no longer spends half of the time on core-guided search when
    branch and bound would prove the optimum at once. The core-guided
    phase now runs for 2% of the time, then branch and bound for 5%, then
    the rest of the core-guided phase, picking up where it stopped. The
    branch and bound in between bounds the objective by an assumption, not
    a clause, so the core-guided strata stay easy. nfc 18_3_10 takes 1.2 s
    instead of 25 s, fast-food ff36 1.5 s instead of 16 s, amaze 1 to 3 s
    instead of 15 to 31 s. On 97 instances (60 s, the OR-Tools set, a
    Challenge sample and the slow easy ones) the score is 53.0 against
    40.0, with 60 proofs against 58 and none lost (--free-config
    lcg-core-probe=<share>, lcg-core-delay=<share>, 0 = core-guided search
    first).

48. Optional ahead-of-time cache for fzn-jacop (Java 25, JEP 483/514/515).
    The new script fzn-jacop-aot (or mvn -Paot install) trains the JVM on
    the FlatZinc models in jacop-all/target/aot-training/ through the new
    org.jacop.fz.AotTraining and writes jacop.aot; fzn-jacop uses it when
    it is newer than the jar. It saves about 0.2 s of JVM start-up and
    class loading per run: on 54 small mznbench instances the median run
    with --lcg -f is 30 % shorter with the same search. Without the cache
    nothing changes. fzn-jacop now sends JVM warnings to stderr, so they
    cannot mix with the FlatZinc output.

49. --lcg -f on a model without a search annotation that has cumulative or
    disjunctive resources (also those found from disjunctions, as in job
    shop) branches on the start times of their tasks by set times in the
    runs that used dom/wdeg: the task that can start first starts as early
    as it can. dom/wdeg found a first makespan of 3803 on jobshop la17
    (optimum 784) and branch and bound needed 273 solutions. On 36 job shop
    instances (60 s) 29 are proved instead of 17. The statistic
    lcgSetTimesStarts gives the number of start variables.

50. Under --lcg, a lazy explanation that is not kept as a learnt clause
    (more than 3 literals) goes on a reason stack beside the clause
    arena, freed when its literal is unassigned, instead of a temporary
    arena clause that only a full arena compaction (learnt clauses and
    watch lists too) could reclaim. Conflicts from the CP side no longer
    allocate a boolean[] of the whole trail nor box literals to remove
    repeats. Learnt clauses are the same. Paired runs at 20 s: --lcg on
    carpet-cutting 17% faster per failure and the dev tier unchanged
    overall; --lcg -f 3% faster on the mznbench kit (also with
    --free-config lcg-split=0).

51. Under --lcg, CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive, and unary
    resources found from disjunctions) explains the bounds its rules give
    a start time by the tasks that must run before (or after) the task:
    detectable precedences, edge finding and not-first, with each bound
    lifted to the weakest atom on the trail the rule needs. Before, the
    time-table and energy explanations of CumulativeBasic could not derive
    detectable precedences at all, so many reasons were generic (48% on
    openshop j7per10-1, 290 atoms each) and learnt clauses ran to
    thousands of literals. --lcg -f, 30 s, 36 job shop instances: 27
    proved instead of 23, 21 better and 4 worse; openshop j7per10-1 and
    gp10-4 proved in 4 runs of 4 (2 of 4 before).
    -Djacop.lcg.unaryExplain=false turns it off.

52. Steadier --lcg -f measurements. The seed of -r now reaches LCG search
    (LcgSolver.setSeed): it changes the initial activity order of the
    variables and of the literals, so a configuration can be measured with
    several seeds. Constraint scopes are LinkedHashSets, so the order in
    which constraints are attached to their variables no longer follows
    identity hash codes: --lcg -f gave different searches with and without
    -Xshare:off (gfd-schedule n50f7d40m10k4: 53,045 and 24,313 nodes) and
    now gives one. --free-config lcg-keep=1 (LcgSolver.setKeepImprovingRun)
    lets a run that improved the best solution keep its way of branching at
    the next restart, as on city-position, where the large improvements
    come from one run descending from a solution and the restart rotated
    away from it. On 92 mznbench instances (60 s, seeds 0 to 2) it halved
    the spread of the final objectives between seeds, on develop-5.0 and
    with the Booleans-first turns, but Borda moved +2.2 and -3.0, so it
    is off by default. Scripts and runs: osolpro benchmarks/lcg-variance.

53. The dom/wdeg variable selection of free search (-f) is incremental.
    The unfixed search variables and the unfixed variables of the
    constraints are kept in reversible sparse sets, each constraint
    counts its unfixed variables, and for the default weighting the
    weighted degree of each variable is kept up to date, so a node no
    longer rescans every variable and the scopes of its constraints. The
    search is the same: same scores and the same ties (the variable
    given first). On the 652 instances of the dev tier and the
    free-search kit under -f (10 s, paired runs) the nodes per second
    rise by 15% in geometric mean over the 358 instances with at least
    1 s of search; on the dev tier with 8 repetitions by 7%, with no
    instance slower by more than 5%.

54. The parallel portfolio (-p) shares the best solution, not only its
    objective value: a free search thread whose best solution is worse
    replays it in a short run (100 failures) that fixes the model's
    variables to its values, so that its large neighbourhoods and value
    phases start from the portfolio's best solution. The thread of the
    options given without -f (depth-first search) now takes the shared
    objective bound too. With -f -p 4 on 4 cores, 60 s, against the
    portfolio without sharing: 23 instances better and 6 worse of the 160
    regression instances (time of proofs x0.77), 14 better and 6 worse of
    the 63 lost-free ones. New: FreeSearch.Builder.sharedSolution,
    SharedSolution, DepthFirstSearch.setSharedBound; -s prints
    portfolioImports.
    
55. Store.watchedConstraints (a public map from variables to the constraints
    that watch them as watched literals) is removed; the store keeps these
    constraints in the variable. Use registerWatchedLiteralConstraint and
    deregisterWatchedLiteralConstraint as before, and the new
    watchedLiteralConstraints(v) and watchedLiteralConstraints() to read them.
    Waking them, and finding the position of a woken variable in Nooverlap,
    LinearBool, LexOrder, LexChain, ExtensionalSupportStr and the restart
    nogoods of free search, no longer goes through a HashMap (new
    org.jacop.util.VarIntMap). Search is unchanged; free search (-f) is about
    2.5% faster on mznbench.

56. The linear relaxation (--lp) adds cutting planes at its first solve,
    in the root propagation: up to 5 rounds of mixed-integer rounding
    (MIR) cuts, from the rows of the basis inverse of the basic columns
    with fractional values (Gomory mixed-integer cuts) and from the rows
    themselves. Each cut is the MIR of an integer combination of the
    rows, computed exactly in long arithmetic with the bounds of that
    moment, so it holds for the whole search and is explained under
    --lcg like the other rows; the floating-point simplex only proposes
    it. DualSimplex adds the rows and keeps its basis. A round that does
    not move the bound is dropped again; -Dorg.jacop.lp.cuts=false turns
    cuts off. The first solve may take 20,000 pivots
    (-Dorg.jacop.lp.rootPivots) instead of 500, within the root work
    budget. Fixes an overflow in the propagation of a combination of rows
    whose slack exceeded a long, which pruned values of solutions. On 46
    of the 422 vs-ortools instances that the change touches (20 s), proofs
    go from 24 to 29 (default), 23 to 28 (--lcg) and 25 to 30 (-f), with
    no proof lost: trucking 06, 07, 09 and 11 are proved in 1-5 s (06,
    07 and 11 also under --lcg), and the cuts lift the root bound of
    trucking 09 from 596 to the optimum 871.
    New statistics lpRootBound, lpCuts, lpCutBound.

57. Under --lcg, Cumulative explains two more kinds of pruning itself
    instead of with the generic reason. Time-table disjunctive reasoning
    (on by default in the FlatZinc front end) pushes a task j past a task
    i that every placement of j would overlap where the compulsory parts
    of the other tasks leave less than r_i + r_j; when no energy window
    explains a start, the reason is the bounds that keep i on two points
    (or an interval), j's bounds and the compulsory parts there. Upper
    bounds of variable durations and resource uses are explained by the
    time table, looking past values already out of the domain (a resource
    use in {0, 9} capped at 2 is recorded as <= 0). Edge finding and
    time-table edge finding were already explained by the energy windows.
    On the 76 instances with a cumulative in the free-search sets, generic
    Cumulative reasons fall from 0.79% (--lcg) and 5.05% (--lcg -f) to 0,
    and those of CumulativeUnary from 4.53% to 0.78% and 0.82% to 0.03%.
    Paired runs: --lcg 60 s 8 better and 3 worse, --lcg -f 30 s with
    seeds 1 to 3 16 better and 10 worse. -Djacop.lcg.cumulativePairs=false
    turns the pair reasons off. Scripts and runs: osolpro
    benchmarks/cumulative-explanations.

58. Under --lcg, JaSAT stays in stable mode (Luby restarts) once its
    first focused phase of 1,000 conflicts is over, instead of
    alternating the two modes. Restarts become far rarer, and each one
    costs the CP propagation back down from level 0. Paired runs, 60 s,
    --lcg -f on the 123 mznbench instances with at least 2,000 conflicts:
    23 better and 13 worse, 59 complete instead of 54; --lcg: 4 better,
    1 worse. The other 126 instances barely reach stable mode.
    -Djacop.lcg.stableForGood=false restores the alternation.

59. JaSAT misses no unit propagation when CP drives it. Under --lcg and
    -sat, a clause added during search, a learnt clause asserted by the
    external search, or a propagator's reason kept as a clause could have
    its true watch above its false watch's level; after a backtrack between
    the two levels it was unit and never visited (under --lcg even at level
    0). Under -sat, units asserted again by a backtrack lost their
    implications once the next level was opened. CdclSolver.setChecking
    (-Djacop.jasat.check=true) now checks at every fixpoint that no clause
    is false or unit and that every reason is valid. On 125 mznbench
    instances (60 s) --lcg solves 68 instead of 67 and --lcg -f 77 as
    before, with no measurable cost.

60. LCG explanations of Regular (and the other layered graph constraints)
    cite only the edges that lead to the removed values: the nearest cut
    or the cut nearest the ends of the graph, whichever is shorter
    (Gange, Stuckey and Szymanek, Constraints 2011), instead of every
    edge leaving the reachable nodes. Reasons on nonogram fall from 15.4
    to 3.8 atoms. On the 92 regular/mdd instances of the free-search
    kit and jacop-benchmarks (60 s, paired), --lcg solves 66 instead of
    60 and --lcg -f 72 instead of 66. -Djacop.lcg.minimality=N measures
    every N-th explanation: with -s, the %% lcg-min lines give per
    constraint class how many atoms propagation needs and how many could
    be weaker.

61. Lifted LCG explanations (linear, cumulative, subcircuit) may cite a
    bound weaker than any trail atom when the solver already has a true
    literal for it, set before the explained one. On mznbench (249,
    60 s, paired) --lcg is better on 15 instances and worse on 6
    (evilshop js-brs2-7 proved optimal, mrcpsp j30_1_10 proved UNSAT,
    rcpsp-max j30_169 26 s to 4 s); --lcg -f is level (32 to 32).
    -Djacop.lcg.liftLiterals=false turns it off; %%%mzn-stat
    lcgLiftedLiterals counts the literals cited.

62. IncreasingSum, LexOrder and CountVar (also under Reified and Implies)
    explain their own pruning under --lcg instead of the generic or
    replayed reason. IncreasingSum cites the bounds of the order and the
    sum, LexOrder the bounds of the prefix that is forced equal or
    greater and of the position that decides, CountVar the variables
    counted and those whose domains miss the value. Generic reasons fall
    from 876,758 to 0 on primes_param and from 209,374 to 0 on
    amaze_mod; LexOrder reasons on mqueens from 129 to 21 atoms. Paired
    60 s runs: --lcg better on 10 instances and worse on 1 (mqueens n11
    6 s to 4 s, mqueens n20 solved); --lcg -f is within noise on the
    order and sum instances (a rerun of the 12 that differed split 4
    to 4) and better on 4 of 8 amaze instances (amaze 2012-03-15 39 s
    to 1.4 s).

63. Shorter Cumulative explanations under --lcg. Explaining a start bound
    walks over the starts it removed; where those starts had been removed
    already, it cited one removal per value, so on carpet-cutting some
    reasons had 300 to 600 atoms. A compulsory part that rules out the
    same starts is now cited instead when it takes fewer atoms. Energy
    window reasons keep only the tasks the window's excess needs, each
    with its start bounds lifted to the overlap it must have. carpet-
    cutting test.20: 59 to 10 atoms per Cumulative reason (--lcg), 75 to
    10 (--lcg -f). Paired runs against item 56 alone: --lcg 60 s 8 better
    and 1 worse, --lcg -f 30 s with seeds 1 to 3 34 better and 11 worse,
    with new proofs on rcpsp_08, smelt_3 and rcpsp-wet j60_36_8.
    -Djacop.lcg.windowLift=false keeps the full window reasons.

64. New decomposed constraint VectorBinpacking(bin, load, w, minBin) for
    vector (multidimensional) bin packing: one Binpacking per dimension,
    a Binpacking with cardinality rules on the surrogate dimension (the
    sum of the dimensions scaled to a common capacity, Caprara and Toth
    2001), and an Alldistinct on each clique of the conflict graph (items
    that exceed the capacity of some dimension together, Gualandi and
    Lombardi, CP 2013). The FlatZinc front end posts the surrogate
    dimension for bin_packing constraints over the same bin variables, one
    per dimension (-Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingSurrogate=false turns it
    off); the cliques are off by default
    (-Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingCliques=vector, or all, turns them on). On
    75 random vector bin packing instances (30 s) the surrogate dimension
    proved 62 optima instead of 49 with the model's search, 65 instead of
    54 with free search and 59 instead of 44 with free search and --lcg;
    the cliques proved no more.

65. Presolve breaks value symmetries and orbitopes too (--presolve on|check,
    unless --symmetry off), in org.jacop.fz.SymmetryBreaking:
    - Value precedence. Integer variables whose values are only compared
      (int_eq/int_ne and x - y = 0 or != 0, all_different, all_equal,
      count, at least, at most, member, nvalue, element, gcc and bin packing
      with their value-indexed counts and loads) form a value space; groups
      with the same values form one space, as the machines of jobs that no
      constraint joins. A second graph joins the space's variables to
      vertices of their values and constants in those arguments become the
      value vertices; the generators that fix the variables permute the
      values, and a set of values they permute in every way (connected
      transpositions, or a Schreier-Sims check up to 40 values) is posted
      as one SeqPrecedeChain over the space's variables, which then come
      first in the lex-leader order of the variable symmetries. A
      minimised objective that only bounds the space from above (x <= z,
      z = max(x)), as the number of colours, is a dominance that keeps
      the precedence and the optimum.
    - Orbitopes. Generators that swap two columns of variables, row by
      row, and chain into connected swaps of columns are replaced by one
      LexChain over the columns when the columns fit the lex-leader order.
      GraphAutomorphisms now prefers transpositions, which finds them.
    On 140 generated instances (graph colouring, fewest colours, bin
    packing with integer and 0/1 models, P||Cmax, jobs on identical
    machines) at 30 s, solved instances go from 74 to 93 with the
    annotation search, 84 to 93 with -f and 89 to 93 with --lcg -f; bin
    packing from 0 to 10 with the annotation search. --lcg -f loses 2
    P||Cmax instances to a different search. Detection takes a median of
    0.05 s on one model of each directory of jacop-benchmarks; value
    precedence fires on 38 of 1,926 and orbitopes on 101.

66. Fewer SAT variables under --lcg. A value a constraint removes from
    inside a domain is now a transient [x = v] literal, released on
    backtracking unless a clause keeps it, as bound literals already were;
    before, each was linked to two new bound literals by four clauses.
    CumulativeUnary's time table no longer cuts holes into start domains
    under --lcg (the sweep of CumulativeBasic already did not): jobshop
    swv07 made 357k SAT variables in 20 s, now 48k. Annotation decisions
    x = min and x = max are taken as [x <= min] and [x >= max], without
    the [x = v] literal, and the annotation's variable selection starts
    from the position it reached at the decision level instead of the
    first variable. New statistics lcgBoundLiterals, lcgEqualityLiterals,
    lcgTransientLiterals, lcgAuxiliaryVariables, lcgSatVariables,
    lcgSatConflicts and lcgSatPropagations. Paired runs on mznbench, 30 s:
    --lcg 19 better / 3 worse, --lcg -f 50 / 52 (two runs; noise level),
    with 15% fewer SAT variables at the median.

67. Dead code removed (about 3,300 lines, no change in behaviour). Classes
    nothing used: IntervalBasedBacktrackableManager and
    SimpleBacktrackableManager (the store always uses
    TrailBacktrackableManager), SwitchesPruningLogging (no-op tracing
    stubs), LengauerTarjan and SophisticatedLengauerTarjan, TableMill,
    geost ShiftOrder and BoxDisplay (an AWT debug view behind a constant
    false switch), AbstractArithmeticConstraint and AbstractXplusYeq.
    Unused methods of the core interfaces: Domain.isNumeric(),
    noConstraints(), noSearchConstraints() and searchConstraints(),
    IntDomain.elementsSmallerThan(int), Var.afcValue(),
    Var.sizeSearchConstraints(), the Function overloads of
    Var.positionMapping and addPositionMapping, MutableVar.previous() and
    setCurrent(), MutableVarValue.previous(),
    BacktrackableManager.isRecognizedAsChanged(int), and in Store
    throwFailException(Var), clearWatchedConstraint(),
    countConstraint(int) and the field auxilaryVariables.
    The constraint replacement feature is removed too: nothing registered
    a replacement, yet Store.impose looked one up for every constraint.
    Store.registerReplacement and deregisterReplacement, the interface
    org.jacop.api.Replaceable and org.jacop.constraints.replace.ReifiedIfThen
    are gone.

66. SeqPrecedeChain (seq_precede_chain and value_precede_chain, the value
    precedence that presolve posts) and LexChain (the orbitope chains)
    explain their own pruning under --lcg instead of the generic or
    replayed reason. SeqPrecedeChain cites, for a value of rank above s,
    the removals of the value of rank s before its position (no earlier
    position can reach rank s), and for a forced position the domain that
    forces a rank and the removals of each rank's value between its latest
    position and its deadline. LexChain cites the vectors of one side of
    the chain: the smallest tuples from the first vector, or the largest
    from the last, bound the vector they reach. Failures are explained the
    same way. On a P||Cmax instance value precedence reasons fall from 22
    to 8 atoms, and none is generic or replayed (before: 38% generic, the
    rest replayed). On 25 jacop-benchmarks
    instances with value precedence or orbitopes, --lcg -f at 30 s solves
    22 instead of 20, and the 130 generated colouring, bin packing and
    identical-machine instances are level (83 to 82 solved).

63. NetworkFlow explains its pruning under --lcg instead of the generic
    reason (networks with constant arc costs). The cost bound and the
    arc bounds come from the duals of a minimum cost flow over the
    bounds before the explained atom, which certify that every flow
    costs at least sum_a min(rc l, rc u) - sum_v p b; a reason cites
    the arc bounds this needs, weakened within the slack. Feasibility
    pruning is explained by the cut that the flow search stops at, and
    the cost's upper bound by the fixed flows. On mapping, reasons fall
    from 23 to 5 atoms and generic ones to 0. Paired 60 s runs on the
    30 instances with jacop_networkflow: --lcg better on 7 and worse
    on none (mesh2x2_mp3 3.1 s to 1.6 s), --lcg -f better on 8 and
    worse on 1 (mesh2x2_mp3 4.4 s to 1.6 s).

68. Several objectives, lexicographic or Pareto. New class
    org.jacop.search.MultiObjective optimises integer objectives (each
    minimised or maximised) as a sequence of single-objective
    subproblems: lexicographic() optimises them in order, each with the
    earlier ones fixed at their optimum and required to be strictly
    better than the best solution so far; pareto() returns the Pareto
    front, each point optimised that way among the solutions no point
    found before dominates, with the later objectives bounded by the best
    solution so far, so every point found is Pareto optimal and the front
    is complete when no solution is left. MultiObjective.DepthFirst
    solves the subproblems by branch and bound in a store. The FlatZinc
    front end reads MiniZinc's goal_hierarchy annotation
    (std/experimental.mzn: min_goal, max_goal, int_min_goal,
    int_max_goal, sat_goal) for the lexicographic optimum and JaCoP's
    pareto annotation (jacop.mzn) for the Pareto front, on a solve item
    with satisfy. Each subproblem is solved as a FlatZinc model with the
    options given (-f, --lcg, --presolve, -p apply to it) and -t covers
    them all. Pareto points are printed as they are proven, followed by
    ========== when the front is complete. Float goals are not supported.

68. New constraints CostAlldifferent(x, costs, cost, offset) and
    CostGCC(x, costs, counters, cost, offset): all-different and global
    cardinality with a cost matrix (SICStus all_different/2 and
    global_cardinality/3 with cost(C, M)), cost = sum(i)(costs[i][x[i]]).
    Both use the incremental Hungarian method of CircuitCost, now for
    more values than variables and, for CostGCC, one column per allowed
    copy of a value (counter upper bounds; lower bounds are left out of
    the relaxation). The minimum and maximum assignments bound the cost
    and remove values by reduced costs. The FlatZinc front end posts
    CostAlldifferent as a redundant constraint on all_different and
    inverse with cost = sum(i)(d[i, x[i]]), also when the sum has other
    terms (a new variable for the cost part and a linear constraint);
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.costAlldifferent=false turns it off. CostGCC is posted
    only with -Dorg.jacop.fz.costGcc=true: on the Challenge models with
    such a GCC (chessboard, blocks-world, evm-super-compilation) it cost
    more time than it saved. On kidney-exchange (60 s) it proves one more
    instance and halves the time of another; rel2onto and seat-moving
    are unchanged.

68. Free search (-f) no longer depends on identity hash codes, so runs
    of the same model under other JVM flags (-Xshare:off, CDS or AOT
    caches, -XX:hashCode) or after unrelated class changes search the
    same tree. Besides the constraint scopes (item 52), the variables of
    the Boolean vector constraints (and_bool, or_bool, ...), the
    channeling constraints the FlatZinc front end collects from
    int_eq_reif and its alias equalities were hash sets or maps keyed by
    variables, so constraints were attached and posted in a different
    order: zephyrus 12_6_8_3 took 107777, 106436 or 113314 nodes to prove
    its optimum. Adaptive LNS under -f still shares its time by the wall
    clock (see item 69).

69. FlatZinc runs repeat their search from run to run of one build.
    Without -r the seed is 0 (it was drawn by the JVM), so the
    neighbourhoods of adaptive LNS, indomain_random and on_restart
    random values repeat. Under -f, runs in neighbourhoods take their
    share (lns-share, now 0.8 by default) of the propagations of the
    other runs, not of their time; by propagations a share of 1 gave
    them more time than before and lost 3 to 4 proofs per seed on 92
    mznbench instances, 0.8 is level with the old split (Borda 46.3 :
    45.7 over 3 seeds, 30 s). Under --lcg, the
    replays of a constraint that explain its pruning take at most
    jacop.lcg.replayShare (0.25) of the consistency calls the store made
    for it (Constraint.consistencyCalls()), not 25% of the time, which
    made rel2onto 5_17 take 2759 to 2770 nodes. Before, 2DBinPacking
    Class7_40_6 under -f took 3,494, 29,933 or 12,964 nodes in 20 s and
    now 22,488 every time. With a time limit, the phases of --lcg -f
    (branch and bound, core-guided search, neighbourhood runs) still
    share the time by the clock: by conflicts, the neighbourhood runs
    took 1.3 to 7.7 times the time of branch and bound instead of 3.

70. Under --lcg, XplusYeqZ keeps to bounds when x or y is fixed instead of
    copying the holes of z into the other term: each copied hole was a
    value-removal atom, a SAT variable of its own. On CELAR6-SUB4
    (frequency differences) that made 280k SAT variables in 30 s and cut
    the conflict rate threefold; now 29k, 3.3x the conflicts, and
    celar_scen06 under --lcg -f reaches 4,502 instead of 9,531 in 30 s.
    Paired mznbench runs, 30 s: --lcg -f 24 better / 21 worse (noise
    level apart from celar), --lcg 8 / 4. Without --lcg nothing changes.

71. Under --lcg, Binpacking explains its cardinality rules (card
    variables, as the FlatZinc front end adds for gbac and bacp, or
    cardinalityReasoning) instead of leaving them to the generic
    reason: the bounds of the card variables, items kept out of a bin
    or a group of consecutive bins that is full or that they would
    overload or underload, items a bin or group needs, and loads from
    the lightest or heaviest items its count allows. On gbac UD5 the
    generic share of Binpacking's reasons drops from 43% to 0.01% and
    the average reason from 33 to 15 atoms. --lcg -f, 30 s, 2 runs on
    15 gbac instances: 9 better, 3 worse, mean objective 14% lower;
    bacp unchanged. -Djacop.lcg.binpackingCounts=false turns it off.

72. Under --lcg -f, the core-guided phase gives its time to branch and
    bound when it has no solution of its own after a third of its time
    (jacop.lcg.coreNoSolution), as intended. Before, a solution of the
    branch and bound run before the phase also counted, and there nearly
    always is one, so the phase ran its whole share: city-position 7-04
    spent half of its time in cores that raised the bound to about 450
    and ended at 69,360 in 30 s, and now ends at 3,286 (CP-SAT: 2,930).
    On 96 optimisation instances of the OR-Tools comparison set, 30 s, two
    paired runs: 29 better, 11 worse. -s prints lcgCoreNoSolutionStops;
    -Djacop.lcg.coreAnySolution=true restores the old test.

73. The linear relaxation (--lp) also reads constraints that are not
    linear relations (org.jacop.constraints.Linearizations): big-M rows
    of reified and half-reified linear constraints, and/or of Booleans,
    McCormick rows of products, the lower side of max/min, abs,
    alldifferent sum bounds, value encodings of a variable by
    Booleans (b_v <-> x = v gives sum b_v = 1 and x = sum v b_v) and
    element over an encoded index. These rows are added at the first
    solve and kept only when they raise the root bound, make the
    relaxation infeasible or are at most 100 (-Dorg.jacop.lp.smallExtra);
    a relaxation whose solves in search hit the pivot limit three times
    (and in one of twenty solves) goes back to the rows without them.
    -Dorg.jacop.lp.linearize=false turns them off. On the 195 of the 422
    vs-ortools instances whose relaxation they change (20 s, paired),
    proofs go from 82 to 87 (default), 114 to 117 (--lcg) and 108 to 114
    (-f): rcpsp-wet j30_27_5, j30_43_10 and j60_19_6, maximum-dag 25_01
    and 25_04, smelt_4. New statistics lpBaseBound, lpExtraRows,
    lpExtraDropped.

74. The FlatZinc front end merges the copies MiniZinc makes of the result
   of a partial function used in a reified context (to_enum, an array
   access whose index may be out of range): each call site gets a fresh
   z with b -> z = x, c -> z = k and not b -> c. Copies with the same x,
   b and k become one variable and their three constraints are dropped;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.partialCopies=false turns this off. A clause whose
   literals are all b <-> (x = v) or b -> (x = v) on one variable x now
   also restricts x to those values when the model is loaded (leaf \/
   unused written as (x = 10) \/ (x = 11) gives x in 10..11). On the
   MiniZinc Challenge 2020 minimal-decision-sets instances the search
   tree halves and a node is about 15% cheaper; backache_train4 and
   colic_train4 are now proved within 20 minutes.

75. New constraint MultiCumulative(starts, durations, heights, limits):
    several cumulative resources used by the same tasks with fixed
    durations, heights[r][i] being the use of resource r by task i (the
    cumulative resources of SICStus multi_cumulative). One time-table sweep
    keeps a profile per resource, so the task events are sorted once and
    the constraint wakes once per change instead of once per resource; it
    prunes what one time-table cumulative per resource prunes. The FlatZinc
    front end merges the cumulative constraints with fixed durations and
    resource uses that share tasks (a start variable that is not fixed,
    with the same duration) into one MultiCumulative; each of them keeps
    its energy and edge-finding rules (Cumulative.doTimeTable(false)) and
    runs them in the next queue. Not under --lcg, where each cumulative
    explains its own time table, nor with --presolve, whose cumulative
    reductions read each resource as one constraint;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.multiCumulative=false turns it off. On the 47 MiniZinc Challenge instances it applies to (rcpsp,
    racp, rcpsp-wet, cyclic-rcpsp, mspsp, smelt, aircraft-disassembly,
    ihtc), 60 s: +7.4% nodes per second with the model search (3 better
    objectives, 1 faster proof, none worse) and +6.4% with -f (results
    within run-to-run noise).

76. fzn-jacop (and minizinc --solver org.jacop) solves with lazy clause
    generation by default, with the model's search and with -f. --lcg now
    takes on|off ("--lcg off" gives the previous default; --lcg alone still
    turns it on), and -Djacop.lcg=off changes the default. Default LCG is
    left out where LCG is not used anyway: models with float variables,
    satisfaction problems asking for more than one solution (-a, -n), -sat,
    and models loaded through FlatzincLoader for the API. Presolve stays off
    by default. On 249 MiniZinc Challenge instances (60 s) LCG proves 32
    more instances with the model's search (143 against 111, Borda 128
    against 90) and 19 more with -f, where Borda is even with plain free
    search; with -f it also proves 8 more of the 63 instances free search
    used to lose. Presolve on, also without probing and symmetry breaking,
    lost Borda in every configuration measured.

77. --lcg no longer runs out of memory on large alldiffs. Each value a
    constraint removes from inside a domain was a SAT variable, and an
    alldiff over 4096 variables of 4096 values removes 8.4 million
    values on one branch (debruijn_binary_04_06 filled a 3 GB heap in
    40 s while plain search solves it in 10 s). Past a literal budget,
    one SAT variable per KB of maximum heap (-Djacop.lcg.literalBudget),
    a removal with no literal of its own stays out of the SAT solver,
    and a clause that would cite it cites its explanation instead. The
    SAT solver also makes a literal's watch lists when a clause first
    watches it rather than up front. debruijn_binary_04_06 now solves
    in 46 s with -Xmx3G, with --lcg and with --lcg -f; below the budget
    nothing changes.

78. New constraint OrBoolWatched(list, result, negated): result is the
    disjunction of 0/1 variables (or its negation), propagated with value
    watches as the two watched literals of a clause, so a variable fixed
    to 0 that is not watched does not wake it. The FlatZinc front end
    posts int_lin_le_reif(sum(x) <= 0, b) over 8 or more Booleans with it
    instead of Not(OrBoolVector) (not under --lcg;
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.orBoolWatchMin sets the length). It visits the same
    search tree; on pattern-set-mining the default search runs 1.6-3.9x
    more nodes per second and proves ionosphere in 35 s. A constraint
    that watches values can now count as a constraint of the watched
    variable (Constraint.countsValueWatch, Var.constraintsWithWatches),
    which dom/wdeg, the failure-count selections and LNS read.

79. The FlatZinc completion search, which labels the variables a search
    on output variables leaves out, labels them in input order instead of
    smallest domain first. It only checks that one assignment exists, so
    the solutions are the same, but choosing by domain size scanned every
    unfixed variable at every node: 70% of the run on freepizza, which
    now runs up to 14x more nodes (pizza39 reaches 942033 in 20 s
    instead of 972454).

80. The linear relaxation (--lp) costs less in search. The dense dual
    simplex reads its dual values from the reduced costs of the slack
    columns instead of a product over the whole basis, and updates the
    basic values four rows at a time in the same order. The search stays
    the same on the instances checked, and on java-routing trip_8_1 a
    solve takes 0.49 ms instead of 0.90 ms. A relaxation that deduces nothing now backs off to one
    solve in up to 4096 calls instead of 64 (-Dorg.jacop.lp.maxPeriod).
    Options given as --name=value, such as --lp=off, are read as
    --name value; before, they were ignored as unknown options.

81. Under --lcg, CumulativeUnary (MiniZinc disjunctive) explains a
    failure that only its unary rules detect: a task that detectable
    precedences, edge finding or not-first push past its latest start.
    These failures got the generic reason, every start bound of the
    resource; on flexible-jobshop med04 that was 5,154 of 5,162 failures
    of the resource, and its learnt clauses fall from 458 to 24 atoms on
    average. On 40 scheduling instances with a disjunctive, 60 s, --lcg
    gets 5 better results and 1 worse (med04 2,960 to 2,643, evilshop
    js-setf3-10 now proved optimal), --lcg -f 7 better and 1 worse.

82. Under --lcg, nvalue (Values), at_most and at_least explain their
    pruning and failures instead of giving the generic reason. Values
    cites variables with pairwise disjoint intervals, or the different
    values of the fixed variables and variables whose domains miss them,
    as its propagator counts them; at_most and at_least cite the
    variables fixed to the value or without it, as count does. Count
    also explains a bound that skips holes next to the counted value.
    gfd-schedule n50f7d40m10k4 had 1.1 million generic reasons of 64
    atoms from these constraints and has none; at 30 s it ends at 611
    instead of 709 with --lcg and proves its optimum in 1.4 s instead of
    9.8 s with --lcg -f. On the 51 benchmark instances with nvalue,
    at_most, at_least or count, two paired 30 s runs: --lcg 5 better
    and 4 worse, --lcg -f 8 better and 3 worse.

83. Under --lcg, a linear bound that moves past a hole of its variable's
    initial domain wider than 4,096 values keeps its own reason. The
    explanation checked every integer between the bound the reason
    implies and the recorded one, and gave the generic reason past
    4,096; it now walks only the values of the initial domain. Variables
    over sparse values (the cubes of routing-flexible, liner-sf
    repositioning) hit this: routing_GCM_0006 had 110,000 generic linear
    reasons at 20 s and liner-sf tp7_0 37,000 of 187 atoms on average,
    and both have none. On 20 liner-sf, routing-flexible and java-routing
    instances, 30 s, two paired runs: --lcg 3 better and none worse
    (upTo1hour tp7_0 462,388 to 196,237), --lcg -f 9 better and 2 worse.

84. New constraint SymmetricAlldifferent(x, offset): x[i] = j + offset
    if and only if x[j] = i + offset, MiniZinc symmetric_all_different
    and inverse(x, x). A solution is a perfect matching of the graph
    whose edges are the pairs of variables that may take each other's
    index, found with Edmonds' blossom algorithm. It is domain
    consistent (one matching search per variable at each call);
    SymmetricAlldifferent(x, offset, false) only channels x[i] = j with
    x[j] = i and fails when no perfect matching exists, as with an odd
    number of variables and no fixed point. Under --lcg it explains a
    failure, and a removed value, with a Tutte barrier read from the
    failed search. The FlatZinc front end posts the second one for
    jacop_assignment(x, x, i, i), which MiniZinc's inverse(x, x) and
    the new fzn_symmetric_all_different give; before, it was Assignment
    (and Alldistinct under :: domain), which needs search to find an
    odd cycle. -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetricAlldifferent=domain selects the
    domain consistent one and =false Assignment.
    MiniZinc Challenge, default options, 60 s: p1f 2015 13, 15 and 17
    (UNSAT) fail at the root in under 1 s instead of 1.1-5.0 s of
    search; the 20 elitserien instances take 328 s instead of 323 s;
    the domain consistent version takes 412 s for the same objectives.

85. Local search (--free-config ls=<share>, off by default) makes
    feasibility jump moves (Luteberget and Sartor, 2023): each variable
    of a violated constraint has a jump value, the value that most
    lowers the weighted violation of all its constraints, tried at the
    bounds, next to its value and where each linear row starts or stops
    being violated; a step makes the best jump of a sample of variables,
    and at a minimum every violated constraint weighs more. Jumps are
    cached until one of the variable's constraints changes.
    LocalSearch.Builder.jump(true) selects them; ls-moves=walk restores
    the moves of one violated constraint per step. On 105 instances with
    a slow or no first solution, 30 s, local search itself finds a
    solution on 23 instances instead of 17 with -f --lcg off and on 17
    instead of 15 with --lcg -f (cutstock, cyclic-rcpsp, largescheduling
    and magicseq only with jumps).

86. FlatZinc if_then_else on integers is posed as the new constraint
    ConditionalSelect(b, x, y): y = x[k] for the first k with b[k] = 1.
    It keeps y within the bounds of the branches that can still be
    selected, rules out a branch that cannot give y its value and selects
    the only branch left, with its own LCG explanations. IfThenElse and
    Conditional over equalities, posted before, gave y no bound until the
    selecting condition was fixed. median-string (MiniZinc Challenge 2019)
    p2_10_8-0 is proved in 6 s instead of 38 s with -f, and with the
    model's search p2_2_20-2 and p2_4_20-0 are proved in 34 s and 38 s
    (not in 120 s, and in 113 s). On 62 Challenge instances with
    if_then_else, 60 s: with the model's search 12 objectives better and 3
    worse; with -f 8 proofs faster, and on two seeds 6 objectives better
    and 9 worse (ptv pax_period_20_1c, cgt and stripboard the largest).
    -Dorg.jacop.fz.conditionalSelect=false restores the old posting.

87. Under --lcg, an experimental cut-based conflict analysis, off by
    default: -Djacop.lcg.linear=learn. When a linear constraint fails,
    it is combined with the linear constraints behind its latest bounds,
    eliminating one variable at a time, until the sum is asserting; the
    sum is then posted as a learnt linear constraint (up to
    -Djacop.lcg.linearTerms=200 terms) next to the learnt clause.
    -Djacop.lcg.linear=stats only counts the outcomes (%% lcg-linear with
    -s). On the 249 mznbench instances only about 2.5% of failures end in
    such a sum. With 30 terms at most, 30 s: golomb_09 and ghoulomb
    4-9-10 prove 1.7x faster with 2.5x fewer conflicts, bus_scheduling r4
    takes 9.8 s instead of 17 s under --lcg and is proved under --lcg -f;
    over the whole set, --lcg 15 better and 6 worse, --lcg -f 13 better
    and 33 worse, mostly on instances where it never learns (noise).

88. A FlatZinc all_different over more than 100 variables with sparse
    domains (successor arrays) is posted as Alldistinct (domain
    consistency) instead of Alldiff (bounds consistency), which pruned
    nothing inside such domains. MiniZinc Challenge 2023
    multi-agent-graph-coverage rnd_n40_p1000_c20_s1 now has its first
    solution (4040, as SICStus) in 4 s and rnd_n75_p1000_c50_s1 (40474,
    as SICStus, the only fd entry with a solution) in 51 s; JaCoP found
    none in 20 minutes. Under --lcg, the all different explanation also
    walks only the values of each variable's initial domain instead of
    its whole span, and explains a value taken by a fixed variable by
    that variable's bounds without building the value graph: rnd_n75
    makes 50 times more conflicts per second.

89. New constraint MinOfSums(x, y, m): m = min(x[i] + y[i]), a term
    being x[i] alone where y[i] is null, with the bounds pruning of the
    decomposition z[i] = x[i] + y[i], m = min(z) and its own explanations
    under --lcg. The FlatZinc front end merges a sum z = x + y (int_lin_eq
    [1, 1, -1]) into the minimum (jacop_minimum, int_min) that is its only
    use, when z is not output, not in the solve item and its domain holds
    every value of x + y; -Dorg.jacop.fz.minOfSums=false turns this off.
    The shortest-path model of road-cons has one such sum for every pair
    of edges (130,000 of 138,000 variables on road_26), and every change
    of a sum woke both constraints. With --lcg off the search tree is the
    same and road_26 explores 1,350 instead of 207 nodes/s (Gecode 6.4.0
    372 on the same machine). Under --lcg, LcgSolver.setUnusedVariables
    keeps the merged sums out of branching. On the 10 road-cons
    instances, 60 s, default options: road_11 (above1hour) proves its
    optimum 5,020 in 17 s, road_33 and road_9 prove theirs 3.5 and 7
    times faster, and the other 7 end better (road_25 16,675 instead of
    22,133, road_26 2,027,861 instead of 22,018,497); with -f road_25
    reaches its optimum 15,453 in 20 s. Min, Max and XplusYeqZ (bounds
    case) read the bounds kept on IntVar and only call the domain when a
    bound changes, +7 to +11% on road-cons before the merge.

90. A FlatZinc variable declared with a bound beyond JaCoP's integer range
    (-536,870,910..536,870,909) is narrowed to it, with one warning on
    stderr; MiniZinc writes such computed bounds, for example
    var 0..4722438400 for a product in 2022 arithmetic-target. Before, a
    bound beyond 32 bits was a parser error and a bound between the range
    and 32 bits (arithmetic-target 0..1687566400, connect, code-generator,
    unit-commitment) gave a variable outside the range JaCoP supports.
    After narrowing, a completed search is reported as optimal only when
    the objective alone lost values, on its worse side, and is never
    reported as unsatisfiable. A domain wholly outside the range is
    rejected. All 3 failing arithmetic-target instances now run (60 s:
    4108 reaches the optimum 7; 6872 and 8657 reach 8 and 9 with -f,
    optimum 7); the two code-generator instances still prove their optimum.

91. Free search without lazy clause generation (--lcg off -f) chooses, for
    optimisation, the value of a variable with up to 64 values by trying
    each one and taking the one that leaves the best bound on the
    objective, as local search chooses its moves; ties keep the value of
    the last solution. The probes take at most a quarter of the time, so
    proofs of optimality are slowed by at most that much. --free-config
    value-impact=<n> (0 = off) and value-impact-time=<share>. Against the
    adaptive LNS of #322 on 119 instances (60 s): primal integral 21%
    lower, 21 better and 16 worse final objectives (gbac UD5 280 against
    6585; Yuck reaches 977, CP-SAT 372). Measured against Yuck and
    Atlantis in osolpro benchmarks/lns-ls.

92. Presolve rules have timing classes, as in SCIP: fast (the
    propagation-like rules), medium (at-most-one, redundant constraints,
    cumulative reductions) and exhaustive (probing, tabling, implied
    constraints). A round runs the costlier class only after a round of
    the cheaper ones changed fewer than --presolve-abort (default 0.001)
    times the variables, and the rounds stop after a small round at the
    top class or when the budget is spent (--presolve-time, default 1/20
    of the time left). --presolve-level fast|medium|exhaustive caps the
    class. When the rules simplified nothing, the store is searched in
    place instead of being copied: the copy alone changed the search
    (cars_60_04 went unsolved). Under --lcg, rule 8 no longer substitutes
    doubletons, which lengthened explanations (diameterc-mst 5 to 24
    atoms); -Djacop.presolve.doubletons=on brings it back. The time of
    every phase is printed as presolveTime<Phase> and the rounds per
    class as presolveRounds<Class>. --presolve-restart <f> (off by
    default) marks the constraints satisfied at the root entailed at a
    restart once the root fixed f times the variables more. On the 249
    mznbench instances, --lcg, 20 s: fast presolve with --symmetry off
    solves the same 134 as presolve off with 18 better objectives and 3
    worse (Borda 223.6 against 214.6; full presolve 206.8), at a median
    0.06 s more set-up; with -f it still loses (157 against 159 solved,
    Borda 335.0 against 355.0), and --presolve-restart 0.025 is about
    neutral there (158 solved, Borda 363.4). Presolve stays off by
    default.

93. New constraint Cumulatives (org.jacop.constraints.cumulative), the
    cumulatives of Beldiceanu and Carlsson: tasks with variable starts,
    durations, heights and machines, a bound per machine that is an
    upper or a lower limit on the load wherever a task runs, heights
    that may be negative, and optional tasks. A sweep over each
    machine's least-load profile prunes starts, machines, durations,
    heights, presence and bounds; it also forces a negative task to run
    where the others alone break the bound, a deduction the forbidden
    regions miss. Under --lcg it explains its pruning and failures.
    MiniZinc cumulatives with lower bounds or negative heights, and the
    optional form, use it instead of the quadratic standard library
    decomposition (upper bounds with non-negative heights still go to
    one cumulative per machine). No Challenge model uses cumulatives; on
    29 generated coverage, paired-task and power instances, 60 s:
    --lcg off 12 better, none worse (cover_10_3_0 optimum in 4.8 s
    instead of 42 s, three power instances find a solution); --lcg
    7 better and 7 worse (pairs_24_5_2 UNSAT not proved, power proofs
    up to 2x slower, cover_24_5_1 a first solution).

94. The experimental cut-based conflict analysis of --lcg
    (-Djacop.lcg.linear=learn, still off by default) also combines
    linear rows of non-linear constraints, made once on the root bounds:
    big-M rows of reified and implied linear relations (and of FlatZinc
    x = y and x = c implied by a Boolean), and/or, max/min, |x| and the
    McCormick rows of x*y. It also starts from an LP failure and uses the
    LP's bound prunings as reasons, through the new LinearReasons
    interface of LinearRelaxation. A failing constraint that is itself
    asserting is no longer learnt again as a copy. On the 249 mznbench
    instances, 10 s, failures that end in a learnt sum after at least one
    step went from 2.5% to 8.0% (--lcg -f) and from 2.1% to 7.0%
    (--lcg). 30 s with 30 terms at most: on the 102 instance/mode pairs
    that learn, 2 reps, --lcg -f 18 better and 9 worse, --lcg 15 and 2
    (still_life_8x8 proved in 10 s, hrc_exp2-1-4795 and maximum-dag
    faster, bus_scheduling r4 and t2 faster); over the whole set, --lcg
    13 and 11, --lcg -f 21 and 17, with 11 and 2 (11 and 3 under -f)
    where the analysis learns.

95. Geost checks volumes: in every dimension of a non-overlapping group
    that selects all spatial dimensions (and time, if selected), the
    objects that must lie inside a window must fit there by volume,
    within the hull of those objects and the InArea box. It is on by
    default; Geost.setVolumeReasoning(false) turns it off. New external
    constraint LexChain(objects) orders the origins of interchangeable
    objects lexicographically inside the sweep (symmetry breaking, like
    SICStus geost lex); it is opt-in. Shape.area() returned 0 for
    shapes made of one box; it is fixed. On the Geost benchmarks (60 s,
    input order search) volume takes squares13 from 23,031 to 4,083
    nodes and proves jobs12 and pallet2x5-12x9-11 infeasible at the
    root (101,583 nodes and the time limit before); LexChain proves
    pallet3x5-13x13-11 infeasible in 4.6 s, which times out without it.
    No MiniZinc Challenge instance 2008-2026 reaches Geost.

96. With --lcg and an objective, free search (-f) gives way to free search
    without learning when LCG is too slow: if, at a tenth of the time
    limit or later, LCG has made fewer than 50 conflicts per second and
    has a solution, it stops, the model's clauses are posted to the store
    and free search continues from the LCG solution (phase hints) with
    its cost as the bound. --free-config lcg-slow-rate=<conflicts per
    second> (0 turns it off) and lcg-slow-at=<share of the time limit>
    set the rule; the statistic lcgSlowConflicts reports a switch. On the
    59 Challenge instances that -f has not finished at 6 s (60 s), 12
    switch: 7 end better than both runs without the rule (tdtsp 20_26
    19,830 to 12,995, cvrp B-n45-k5 2,084 to 1,587), 3 worse (cargo 08
    and 16, jobshop swv07) and 2 equal. ptv 1c goes from 2,941 to 1,666,
    ptv 5a from 270 to 60, cgt_12 from 1 to -129,352 and cgt_3 from
    19,722 to -147,892.
    
97. Portfolio (-p) settings are chosen from data. Each setting was run
    alone on 240 hard instances (osolpro benchmarks/portfolio-configs) and
    the threads after the first now follow the greedy best order for both
    -f (--lcg on -f) and no options (--lcg on): LCG with literal branching,
    free search with CHS and Luby restarts, the annotation without LCG,
    the annotation with LCG, then the rest. A setting that would run the
    same search as an earlier thread is skipped: objective-only options
    (lcg-core, lcg-lb, lns, probe) on a satisfaction model and share-*
    options do not count, so lcg-bb no longer repeats lcg on satisfaction
    models. The LCG core-guided phase stops when its lower bound meets the
    bound shared by the portfolio, and hardens with it; before, it used
    only its own best solution. -f -p 4, 60 s, 169 hard instances, two
    rounds that run both builds on each instance in turn: 99 solved
    instead of 95 and 101 instead of 92, 61 and 59 instances better
    against 25 and 26 worse, proofs about 26% faster. Routing and large
    scheduling models reach somewhat worse objectives, since plain -f
    without LCG is no longer among the first four threads.

98. fzn-jacop -s prints only the standard FlatZinc statistics (nodes,
    failures, restarts, peakDepth, variables, propagators, propagations,
    nogoods, initTime, solveTime, objective, solutions) and now peakMem,
    the peak memory of the JVM in Mbytes. The statistics of JaCoP's own
    components (lcg*, presolve*, lp*, lns*, ls*, symmetry*, probe*,
    portfolio*, objectiveProbes*, goalSubproblems, nodesPerSecond and the
    per-class "%% lcg" lines) need the new option --search-statistics
    (which implies -s), or -s together with -debug. The MiniZinc solver
    configuration declares --search-statistics. A malformed
    "%%%%mzn-stat: restarts" line of -v output is fixed.

99. Under --lcg, the FlatZinc statistics report peakDepth, the deepest
    decision level of the SAT search (assumption levels included), and
    restarts, the SAT solver's restarts (as lcgRestarts). Before, both
    were always 0.

100. The FlatZinc front end solves the independent parts of a model that
    the objective does not depend on (for a satisfaction model, every
    part but the largest) once at the root, and fixes them. Each part
    gets a depth-first search in annotation order with a 2 s budget
    (-Djacop.components.budget, in ms); a part without a solution makes
    the model infeasible, and a part that runs out of time is left to
    the main search. It is skipped with -a, with -n > 1 for satisfaction
    and with on_restart. --components off turns it off. Constraints
    that watch only some of their variables (long Boolean sums, clauses)
    link all their variables. 92 of the 1,781 MiniZinc Challenge
    instances split into several parts; on the 109 checked, 60 s: same
    statuses, ghoulomb 3-7-20 and 3-8-20 prove their optimum in 2 s
    instead of 15 s, 3-10-20 in 15 s instead of 38 s, 2013 4-9-20 in
    4 s instead of 17 s, and cable_tree_wiring R046 ends at its optimum
    143,534 instead of 284,142; other objectives move by noise.

101. Presolve dual reductions also find dominated columns: if raising x
    by one and lowering y by one keeps every constraint and the
    objective, the clause x = max(x) or y = min(y) is added (x >= y for
    0-1 variables). It is left out with -a, since it can remove some of
    the improving solutions printed on the way to the optimum (the
    optimum stays). -Djacop.presolve.domcol=false turns it off. It fires
    on 37 Challenge instances (train, table-layout, connect,
    unit-commitment, wmsmc-int) and is neutral on them at 60 s.

102. fzn-jacop --proof <prefix> writes a VeriPB 3 certificate of an LCG
    search: <prefix>.opb is the model as pseudo-Boolean constraints over
    the order encoding of the domains the FlatZinc translation gives the
    variables, before the constraints propagate, and <prefix>.pbp the proof (rup steps for learnt clauses and lazy
    reasons, pol steps for the flow reasons of cardinality constraints,
    soli for improving solutions, deletions, and a conclusion UNSAT, SAT
    or BOUNDS lb ub) for veripb <prefix>.opb <prefix>.pbp.
    It is off by default, implies --lcg, one thread, bounds consistency
    and no LP, and learns no linear sums; symmetry breaking and the
    components solved before search are off under it. The core-guided phase runs
    without its at-most-one groups: the proof sums the model's lines of
    the objective's definitions into a lower-bound line, defines each
    totalizer output by two red steps, derives the totalizer clauses and
    each core's increase of the bound by pol steps, and hardened literals
    and the bound clause by rup. A search stopped by a limit concludes
    BOUNDS with the lower bound it proved (of the cores or of the root);
    a run that overruns its time-out ends its proof after the last whole
    step with conclusion NONE.
    Linear constraints (LinearInt, LinearBool and the linear forms of LCG
    explanations), their reifications, equalities with constants,
    ChannelReif, ChannelImply, clauses, element over constants, Count,
    AtMost, AtLeast, CountBounds, Max, Min, tables, regular and MDD
    constraints, Alldifferent, Alldiff, Alldistinct, GCC, CountValues and
    CountValuesBounds are encoded (the flow reasons of the last six by a
    pol step over a violated cut of their flow network), as are In,
    BoolClause, AllEqual, AbsXeqY, MaxSimple, MinSimple, AndBoolSimple, OrBoolSimple,
    XorBool, Reified and Implies of these and of AtMost and AtLeast (the
    encoding under the literal, and for Reified the negation's under its
    negation), Not of them and of linear constraints, ConditionalSelect
    (a literal per branch that it is selected, under which y equals the
    branch), LexOrder (a literal per position that the positions before
    it are equal), and
    b -> x + c <= z and the time table of CumulativeBasic, with fixed or
    variable durations and resource uses (a time-indexed encoding); under
    --proof a cumulative with fixed durations and uses is posted as
    Cumulative, its energy and edge-finding reasons derived by a pol step
    that sums the load lines of an overloaded window with the task cover
    and order lines, any other cumulative as CumulativeBasic, and the
    redundant constraints the front end adds (unary resources, energy
    precedence, weighted completion, stocking cost, overlapping all
    different) are left out; so are diffn (as Nooverlap without its area
    and count failures, every pair of rectangles a clause of the four
    ways they are apart over order literals), int_times (as a table of
    the products), element over variables (ElementVariableFast, with a
    support literal per value and position, so that a bound of the value
    with the index open is a rup step) and
    Circuit (forward and backward positions of the nodes; its subtour
    cut reasons are derived by rup steps, position by position, over a
    strongly connected component that no arc leaves or none enters, and
    its dominator reasons are off under --proof);
    for another constraint, or a
    model over 4*10^8 characters, fzn-jacop prints "%% --proof: ..." and
    =====UNKNOWN=====. The proof derives each change of the root
    propagation as a unit from its reason, and a failure of the root
    propagation, or a clause false at the root, as UNSAT; changes without a reason (domains narrowed by
    the translation) or by a constraint that cannot be encoded narrow the
    model's domains instead. The FlatZinc translation is not certified.

103. FlatZinc regular with a set alphabet (jacop_regular_set) read the
    next states of its transition table shifted by the smallest symbol
    instead of by 1. With an alphabet such as 0..1 it crashed with an
    ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException; with one from 2 it could map
    transitions to the wrong state and report UNSAT for satisfiable
    models. Alphabets from 1 (the usual case) were not affected.

104. LCG explanations of GCC, GlobalCardinality and CountValues first try
     counting reasons: a counter's upper bound from the variables that
     exclude its value or from the lower bounds of the other counters, a
     counter's lower bound from the variables fixed to its value, and a
     value removed (or a bound moved past values) because other variables
     fixed to it reach its counter's bound. Only when none applies is the
     flow network built, which has a time share and gave the generic
     reason beyond it (93 % of GCC reasons on fillomino 7x7_0).
     -Djacop.lcg.cardinalityCounts=false turns them off. ArgMax and ArgMin
     reasons skip values outside the initial domain, where a bound across
     a wide hole gave the generic reason (47 % on tower). On the wide
     benchmark set (220 instances, 60 s) --lcg is 10 better / 6 worse and
     --lcg -f solves 140 instead of 136; on the 17 instances with these
     constraints 22 better / 6 worse over 5 runs (fillomino 7x7_0 solved,
     league 15.4 s to 8.8 s).

105. fzn-jacop breaks symmetries by default, also without --presolve:
    lex-leader constraints for variable symmetries, lex chains for
    interchangeable columns and value precedence for interchangeable
    values, as --presolve on did (--symmetry off turns it off). It is
    left out for all solutions of satisfaction problems and for models
    loaded with FlatzincLoader. When the search annotation tries the
    largest values first (indomain_max, indomain_reverse_split), the
    lex-leader keeps the largest solution of each orbit, so the search
    meets it first. Free search (-f) leaves value precedence out of
    models that minimize the largest load of a bin packing (P||Cmax),
    where it multiplied the failures of --lcg -f (pcmax n41-m7-s18:
    30,015 to 214; 20 instances, 30 s: 16 to 19 proved). The second
    given to symmetry detection now also
    bounds reading the model and building its graphs, which took up to
    7.5 s on groupsplitter and ma-path-finding. The FlatZinc test suites
    run with -Djacop.symmetry=presolve, which keeps the old behaviour.
    Symmetry constraints are posted on 431 of the 1,781 MiniZinc
    Challenge instances (2008-2025); detection takes at most 1 s,
    median 0.1 s. On the 376 of them not solved in 2 s, 30 s: fixed
    search solves 141 -> 145, -f 189 -> 194; on 140 generated colouring,
    bin packing and scheduling models 81 -> 91 and 87 -> 95.

106. LCG explanations of set constraints. What a set's domain derives
     after a set operation changes it (a cardinality bound, an element
     forced in or out by a cardinality bound, a cardinality failure) is
     explained by the operation from the elements its relation forces,
     instead of the generic reason. set_card (CardAeqX) explains by the
     set's cardinality rules with the cardinality variable, and
     array_set_element (ElementSet) by the elements or the cardinality
     that exclude an index and by the indexes left. The generic share of
     these constraints on monomatch, vaccine, gt-sort and valve-network
     falls to 0 (valve-network: 99.8 % of ElementSet reasons). On the 8
     wide-set instances with set constraints (60 s, 3 seeds per mode)
     --lcg is 6 better / 2 worse (monomatch n_5 solved in 4 s, which
     timed out; gt-sort 131 to 127) and --lcg -f 7 / 6.

107. The LCG threads of fzn-jacop -p share short learnt clauses and the
     bounds of the model's variables at the root, as atoms over FlatZinc
     names, at restarts and at the start and end of each solve. A thread
     adds the clauses of the others as learnt clauses (a clause of two or
     more atoms only when all its literals already exist there). Clauses
     hold under the model and a bound that is the cost of a solution the
     portfolio already has, so no better solution is lost. Nothing is
     shared with --presolve on or with a proof; after symmetry breaking a
     thread reads only threads that posted none or the same constraints.
     --free-config share-clauses=<size> (default 8, 0 = off) and
     share-lbd=<lbd> (default 2) set what is shared. At -f -p 4 (two LCG
     threads) it is neutral: 169 hard instances, 2 interleaved rounds,
     95 -> 95 solved; wide set 169 -> 169 solved, 29 better / 25 worse.

108. The linear relaxation (--lp on, the default) picks its simplex by size:
    the dense one below 100 rows of the model's linear constraints and the
    sparse one from 100 rows on, and also for relaxations over the dense
    size cap (org.jacop.lp.maxTableau), which before got no relaxation at
    all (roster-shifts-bool, physician-scheduling, table-layout, connect
    and radiation of the MiniZinc Challenge). With the sparse simplex the
    rows of non-linear constraints tried at the first solve are at most
    twice the model's rows plus 100. -Dorg.jacop.lp.sparseRows=N moves the
    threshold, -1 keeps the dense simplex. The sparse simplex is used only
    up to 25,000 rows and 30,000 columns (org.jacop.lp.sparseMaxRows and
    org.jacop.lp.sparseMaxColumns) and its root solve stops after 3 s
    (org.jacop.lp.sparseRootSeconds) or 4e9 work units
    (org.jacop.lp.sparseRootWork) in optimisation problems; while the
    extra rows are on trial, a sparse simplex chosen by size takes the
    pivot limit of the dense tableau, so it drops the same rows. Each LP
    deduction is cleaned after its multipliers are rounded: a multiplier
    whose row lacks the side its sign needs is dropped instead of losing
    the inequality, the inequality is divided by the gcd of its weights
    with its side rounded down, and a scale whose bounds reasoning would
    overflow a long falls back to the next smaller one
    (-Dorg.jacop.lp.clean=false turns the first two off). A ratio test
    that meets NaN stops the solve at its limit instead of throwing
    ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

109. LCG explanations of set_in_reif and array_var_set_element. In
     (x in a fixed set) and XinA (x in a set variable) explain their
     pruning, failures and entailment, so a reification of them is no
     longer generic: a value of x is ruled out by being outside the set
     (an element out of the set variable, or in it for the negation), an
     element joins the set when x is fixed to it. ElementSetVariable
     explains an excluded index by an element its set and the value
     disagree on, a membership of the value by the sets of the indexes
     left, and with the index fixed passes memberships and cardinality
     bounds between the two sets. valve-network: Reified reasons 100 %
     generic with 84 atoms to 0 % with 15; gt-sort: ElementSetVariable
     100 % generic with 75 atoms to 0 % with 2. Wide set (220, 60 s):
     --lcg 12 better / 7 worse (valve-network 90 to 111), --lcg -f
     22 / 23, solved 139 to 140.

110. New module jacop-jalp (JaLP, org.jacop.jalp): a sparse revised dual
     simplex with no dependency on jacop-core. It has a Markowitz LU of
     the basis with a Forrest-Tomlin update, dual steepest edge pricing, a
     bound-flipping ratio test, power-of-two scaling and cost
     perturbation, warm starts after bound changes and added or removed
     rows, Farkas proofs and the rows of the basis inverse. An MPS reader
     (free and fixed format, gzip) and a command line
     (java org.jacop.jalp.LpMain [-noscale] [-limit N] files) solve LP
     files. The sparse and lazy relaxations of LinearRelaxation
     (Simplex.SPARSE and LAZY, including the sparse simplex chosen by
     size) now run on JaLP, which replaces SparseDualSimplex and SparseLu
     (removed). On the osolpro LP bench (Netlib, MIPLIB 3 and
     2017, Mittelmann and JaCoP root LPs, golden references from HiGHS,
     SCIP/SoPlex and GLOP; 687 instances, 300 s) JaLP matches 636
     references, stops at the time limit on 50 and gives no wrong answer.
     On the wide set (220, 60 s) with the
     sparse simplex chosen by size, JaLP against SparseDualSimplex solves
     130 against 129 (14 better / 14 worse) and with -f 152 against 148
     (47 / 13).

111. New FlatZinc built-ins jacop_short_table_int(x, t, star), a table
     in which the value star matches any value (posted as HybridTable),
     and jacop_negative_table_int(x, t), forbidden tuples (posted as
     NegativeTable). JaCoP's mznlib uses them for the predicates
     xcsp_short_table and xcsp_negative_table of the XCSP3 bench in
     osolpro benchmarks/xcsp3, whose default decompositions are
     disjunctions of conjunctions. On the 28 instances of the XCSP3
     quick set that use them (60 s): Fillomino-6-1 solved in 17 s
     (no answer before, 24 MB FlatZinc to 1.3 MB), Tal-02 optimum in
     3 s instead of 36 s, FoolSolitaire-dec2-0-3 optimum (no answer
     before), Benzenoide-06, MaximumDensityOscillatingLife-6-4 and
     RollerSplat-05c-70 get solutions; no instance lost an answer.

112. New fzn-jacop option --gap <percent> (also in org.jacop.msc): an
     optimisation stops once the best solution is within <percent> % of
     the objective's proved bound, |best - bound| <= percent/100 * |best|,
     as the relative gap of MIP solvers. The best solution is then printed
     without "==========", unless the bound equals it, which proves it
     optimal. The bound is the objective's bound at the root before search
     and, under LCG, at every restart (with the core-guided lower bound);
     a portfolio (-p) stops on the best solution and bound of all its
     threads. With -s, each better bound is printed at once as
     %%%mzn-stat: objectiveBound=<value> in the model's objective, so the
     time a gap was proved can be read from the output.

113. LCG explanations of XmulYeqZ with one wide factor. A removed value of
     z looked for supports over x even when y was narrower, and a removed
     value of a factor over the other factor even when z was narrower, so
     a factor wider than 1,024 values made the reason generic (wordpress:
     price in 116..4600 times a 0..1 occupancy, 98k generic reasons of
     1,355 atoms on average in 30 s). The narrower range is enumerated
     now, and removals of more than 64 values are explained value by value
     while that stays under 65,536 support checks. wordpress: generic
     reasons 0, 2 atoms on average, and with --lcg the optimum 3580 is
     proved in 57 s (4406, unproved, at 60 s before). Wide set (220,
     60 s): --lcg 4 better / 4 worse, solved 117 to 118, --lcg -f 14 / 14,
     solved 138 to 140.

114. LCG explanations of LexChain with a wide variable. The explanation
     read each vector's domains by enumerating the root domains and gave
     up on a variable of more than 10,000 values, so the symmetry-breaking
     chain of wordpress (prices up to 21,278) had only generic reasons of
     11,000 to 17,000 atoms. The domains are now read from the bounds and
     the removed values on the trail. wordpress: LexChain generic reasons
     0, failures 35 atoms instead of 11,600, --lcg optimum proved in 33 s
     instead of 46 s. Wide set (220, 60 s): on the 10 instances that use
     LexChain --lcg 2 better / 0 worse, --lcg -f unchanged.

115. LCG explanations of AllEqual. A removed value or moved bound of one
     variable is explained by the same atom of another variable (one
     bound when it covers the values passed, else the removal of each
     value elsewhere), a failure by the bounds that cross or the values
     between them removed elsewhere. Before, every reason was generic
     (the domains of all variables). generalized-peacable-queens: AllEqual
     reasons 94 % generic with 9.4 atoms to 0 % with 1.0, failures 8.1 to
     2.0 atoms, 6 % more nodes in 30 s. Wide set (220, 60 s): --lcg
     6 better / 4 worse, --lcg -f 10 / 11, solved unchanged; the one
     instance using AllEqual keeps its objective in both modes.

116. Restarts under search annotations with --lcg (fixed search, the
     default mode). Before, the annotation was followed down each branch
     without restarts, so learnt clauses pruned but never reordered the
     search. Now the solver restarts and its runs take turns between the
     annotation, the SAT solver's own decisions on literals and activity
     branching, keeping the learnt clauses; activity completes an
     annotation that leaves variables unfixed. --free-config
     fixed-restarts=turns|alternate|replay|off chooses (off is the old
     behaviour), fixed-restarts-from=solution restarts only after a first
     solution. Wide set (220, 60 s, default mode, interleaved with the
     old build): 162 against 131 solved, score +163 (89 instances
     better, 12 worse, no wrong answers), 11 of 12 families better.
     Against OR-Tools CP-SAT 9.15 -p 1 -i (fixed search) the same set
     goes from level (S = -2) to JaCoP ahead (S = -176).

117. fzn-jacop ends by -t on its own on large models. The time-out was not
     kept in five places: LCG search counted the whole -t from its own
     start; free search took over from slow LCG free search even after the
     time-out (and was set up for seconds on large models); free search
     after LCG counted its limit from its first node, not from when the
     time left was read; the model was read and scanned by the detectors
     without looking at the clock; and -p answered 2 s after -t. New
     FreeSearch.Builder.deadline(epochMillis). With the watchdog off, -f,
     -t 60 s, on the 24 instances of the Challenge bench that overran:
     19 ended after 62 s (up to 90.6 s, ma-path-finding 2022, rcpsp-wet-
     diverse j90 83 s, nside HARD 77 s), now none (60.9 s at most). Wide
     set (220, 60 s, -f): solved 151 to 153, 25 better / 23 worse; the two
     solutions lost were luck on rerun. wide-quick in default mode: same
     solved and solutions. -p 4 -t 20 s on flowshop-workers 8s_ex3 answered
     at 32.3 s, now 20.2 s.

118. LCG explanations of Member. A value removed from e is explained by
     its removal from every list variable, a value removed from the last
     candidate by its removal from e and the other list variables being
     disjoint from e (their bounds when apart, else each value of the
     narrower of the two removed from the other), a failure by every list
     variable being disjoint from e. Before, every reason was generic.
     handball19 (30 s): Member reasons 63 % generic with 11.2 atoms to
     0 % with 6.3. Wide set (220, 60 s): --lcg 5 better / 5 worse, --lcg
     -f 8 / 11, solved -1 under -f; the two instances using Member keep
     their objectives in both modes.

119. KnapsackDP keeps only the bounds of the capacity while LCG records the
     changes. Each capacity it removed between the bounds (no item choice
     reaches that weight with enough profit) was a != atom whose only
     reason is every item. The item supports do not need these holes, as
     they check reachability and the profit bounds themselves. mknap2-1
     (30 s, --lcg): generic reasons 22 % to 0.2 %, 75 to 8.6 atoms per
     reason, same nodes and time. Wide set (220, 60 s): --lcg 4 better / 3
     worse, --lcg -f 8 / 15, solved -1 under -f; neither multi-knapsack
     instance changes its result.

120. The MIR cut rounds of LinearRelaxation at the root are linear in the
     size of each row. Every model row was combined as a dense unit
     multiplier over all rows and a coefficient array over all columns,
     and MirCut walked every column, so a round cost rows x (rows +
     columns). The combinations are now sparse and the cuts are the same.
     nside EASY_200_50 with --lp sparse (57,504 rows): LP time 57.7 s to
     4.1 s, same bound, cuts and nodes. Wide set (220, 60 s): cuts, root
     and cut bounds identical on all 184 instances with an LP; solved 130
     to 131 (default) and 152 to 156 (-f, timing).

121. fzn-jacop prints the objective's bound (-s, objectiveBound) whenever
     it improves during the search, not only at the root and at LCG
     restarts: under LCG at every return to the root (backjumps too, so
     fixed search, which has no restarts, gets rising bounds), in
     depth-first search without LCG at each node whose subtree is all
     that is left (DepthFirstSearch.setLastSubtreeListener), and in free
     search, also after LCG stopped as slow, at the root between runs,
     where refuted objective probes and nogoods raise it
     (FreeSearch.Builder.onBound). A search that proves its best solution
     optimal prints it as the last bound, a portfolio too. Each bound line
     comes with objectiveBoundTime, the wall-clock seconds since the JVM
     started. No bound is printed when domains were narrowed to JaCoP's
     integer range beyond the objective's worse side, since better
     solutions may have been cut off. --gap stops on these bounds as well.
     jp-encoding data1700 (-f, 60 s): bound 0 to 53273 against the
     incumbent 58599, within 10 %. Wide set (220, 60 s): no bound past a
     known optimum; the bound moves during the search on 105 optimisation
     instances in default mode (none before) and on 144 under -f (116);
     solved and objectives unchanged within noise.

122. New constraint Routes(tails, heads, literals[, demands, capacity]):
     the arcs at 1 form routes that leave node 0 (the depot) and come
     back to it, each other node is visited once or skipped by its self
     loop, and the demands on a route sum to at most the capacity. It
     keeps the paths of fixed arcs incrementally, removes an arc that
     would close a circuit without the depot or join two paths over the
     capacity, and keeps the in and out degrees (setDegrees(false) leaves
     them to other constraints). Every pruning and failure has an LCG
     explanation. The FlatZinc front end can post it as a redundant
     constraint on two-index arc models with Miller-Tucker-Zemlin load
     rows (vrp), with -Dorg.jacop.fz.routes=true; it is off by default,
     because on the 25 vrp instances of the Challenge (60 s) it leaves
     the results level: default 12 better / 9 worse (objective geomean
     0.999), -f 11 / 11 (1.006). No other Challenge instance has the
     pattern.     

123. LCG explanations of LexChain cite only the vectors and positions the
     ordering depends on. The reason of a pruning or failure used to cite
     every atom of every vector of the chain. Now the chain starts at the
     nearest vectors that still force the entry (windows of changed vectors
     around it, doubled until they suffice; vectors still at their root
     domains are skipped), and positions after the shortest prefix that
     still forces it (binary search) are left out. LexChain also prunes the
     vectors in the direction its bounds moved, so a bound pushed up the
     chain is explained by its neighbour instead of the far end. Cutstock
     (XCSP3 2025, 15 instances, 60 s): 5 to 10 solved. Wordpress12
     Offers500: 5,768 to 96 atoms per reason (--lcg -f); --lcg proves the optimum
     in 8 s instead of 19 s. Wide set (220, 60 s, osolpro #231 scoring):
     --lcg S = +32; --lcg -f S = -22, all outside LexChain except
     wordpress, where LCG is now fast enough that it no longer gives way to
     search without learning at 6 s, which proves it in 3 s (9 s to 50 s;
     with lcg-slow-rate=0 LCG proves it in 31 s instead of failing to in 60 s).
     
124. Under LCG, Regular (and the other layered-graph constraints explained
     by paths, ExtensionalSupportMdd) is encoded as clauses before the
     search and removed from the store: a literal per node and per arc
     of the layered graph (an arc groups the edges between two nodes and
     reuses a node literal when it is the only arc in or out), arcs imply
     their nodes and values, nodes imply a leaving and an entering arc,
     values imply an arc of their layer. Unit propagation then keeps the
     domains consistent and every pruning has a short clause reason,
     instead of the path explanations of LayeredGraphExplanation that
     took 80 % of pentominoes' time. jacop.lcg.graphClauses=false turns
     it off; it is skipped under --proof. 122 Challenge instances with
     regular (60 s, every solution checked): default 13 more solved, 0
     fewer, -f 7 / 2; pentominoes 05 and 07 5-20x faster, 04 solved.
     Wide set (220, 60 s): the 10 instances with regular are better or
     equal in both modes (rotating-workforce Example1242 solved in the
     default mode); solved 132 -> 134 default, 154 -> 152 -f, where the
     -f changes are all on instances without regular.
     pattern.     

125. LCG objective shaving (fzn-jacop -f, --free-config lcg-shave=on, now
     the default). The lower-bound runs that alternate with branch and
     bound assumed obj <= lb and raised the bound by 1 per proof. They now
     try obj <= lb + step - 1: the step doubles after each proof (the run
     goes on within its conflicts) and halves after a run that ran out of
     conflicts, as the non-LCG free search does since #294. A solution
     found under the assumption ends the run as before; a proof that
     comes after a solution at or below the target is optimality. New
     option lcg-lb-growth=<factor> (default 1) lets the share of the
     lower-bound runs grow after rounds without a better solution; 2 cost
     branch and bound too much on the wide set and stays off. New
     statistics lcgLowerBoundRuns and lcgLowerBoundRaised. On the 68
     instances where CP-SAT proves a 10 % gap and JaCoP -f did not (60 s):
     bound within 10 % of the incumbent on 6 instead of 2, median gap
     0.41 to 0.29 (graph-clear 0.75 to 0.20, depot-placement 0.88 to
     0.24, train.12 0.12 to 0.05). Wide set (220, 60 s, -f): 26 better /
     16 worse, solved 150 to 151, score +5, Borda +9; fixed search is
     unchanged (the options apply under -f only).

126. The FlatZinc front end merges element constraints over constant
     arrays that share one index variable into one table (HybridTable),
     the index as the last column. MiniZinc flattens a row lookup
     x[j] = t[row, j] into one element per column; each element only
     sees the index, so under LCG a removed value of x[j] was explained
     by one row != i atom per dead row (crosswords grid-05.04: learnt
     clauses of 2,551 literals on average, 45 with the table).
     TableExplanation now reads != atoms and bounds from the trail
     directly (AtomicTrail.notEqualEntry, boundEntry) instead of walking
     a column's entries. -Dorg.jacop.fz.elementTable=false turns the
     merge off; it is off with --proof. 28 of the 220 wide set instances
     have such groups. Wide set (220, 60 s): fixed search 16 better / 17
     worse, 5 / 1 on the 28 with groups (cgt -6534 to -30996, crosswords
     65 to 68, stable-goods 6368 to 5900, pillars-and-planks 23 to 12 s,
     wordpress 38 to 25 s); -f 24 / 26, 5 / 5 on the 28, solved +1, where
     repeated seeds put tppv, crosswords and diameterc-mst in the noise.

127. The LP relaxation encodes the variables of all-different-except
     constraints (alldifferent_except_0, alldifferent_except) by values
     over LP-only columns: x = sum v x_v, sum x_v <= 1 per variable and
     per value, and b = sum x_v for each reified or implied comparison
     of x with a constant. The columns are not store variables: every
     deduced inequality removes them at the bound that keeps it valid,
     so LCG reasons stay over model variables. Encodings of 100 rows and
     more use the sparse simplex and are never dropped in search.
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.valueColumns=false turns this off (domain cap
     valueColumnsDomain 128, valueColumnsMax 20,000 columns); stat
     lpValueColumns. concert-hall-cap (9 instances, 60 s): the root bound
     is the optimum on 8; proofs 2 -> 8 under -f and 0 -> 6 in default
     mode. Wide set (220, 60 s): solved 147 -> 146 under -f (noise: only
     concert-hall-cap changes its LP) and 126 -> 128 in default mode.

128. JaLP solves with the LU factors are hypersparse: a solve whose
     right-hand side is a list of nonzeros finds the reached pivots by a
     depth-first search (Gilbert-Peierls) and visits them in the order of
     the dense loop, so results are bit-identical and the simplex makes
     the same pivots. The dual simplex keeps rho, the entering column,
     the DSE vector and the rows outside their bounds as index lists, and
     the Forrest-Tomlin row elimination uses the same search. A solve
     falls back to the dense loop when its recent results were denser
     than 10% (-Dorg.jacop.jalp.hyper=0.1; 0 turns the search off).
     LP bench (659 MPS up to upTo1min, 120 s): the same pivots and
     objectives on all 621 that both solve, 627 solved instead of 621,
     geometric mean 1.25x faster on the 227 taking 0.5 s or more and
     1.79x on those taking 30 s or more (radiationm18-12-05 99.8 to
     16.7 s, neos-4722843-widden 65.5 to 16.4 s, CMS750_4 12.5 to 5.9 s). Wide set (220, 60 s): solved 146 -> 146 fixed search and
     147 -> 149 under -f, changes at the time limit only.

129. Set search and set builtins, checked by a new brute-force test
     (SetBuiltinsFuzzTest, setfuzz.trials) on random FlatZinc models of
     all set builtins and jacop_* set globals, against enumeration of
     their MiniZinc meaning, with plain search, LCG, free search and
     presolve. Fixes:
     - set_search with outdomain_min or outdomain_max decided the element
       into the set first, as indomain_min, with a warning. It now leaves
       the smallest (largest) undecided element out of the set first, as
       Gecode's SET_VAL_MIN_EXC (SET_VAL_MAX_EXC), with plain search and
       under LCG (new OutdomainSetSelect; choice SetElementChoice).
     - array_var_set_element with an array literal of set variables, such
       as [a, b], failed with "var_set_element with list of set variables
       is not available".
     - jacop_link_set_to_booleans with a Boolean variable repeated in b
       failed (ChannelBoolSet rejects repeated variables); the repeated
       one now gets an equal copy.
     - set_search with smallest (largest) chose the set with the smallest
       glb (largest lub) cardinality. It now chooses the set with the
       smallest (largest) undecided element, as Gecode's
       SET_VAR_MIN_MIN (SET_VAR_MAX_MAX). most_constrained, which used the
       smallest glb cardinality, now chooses the fewest undecided elements
       with ties broken by the most constraints, as MiniZinc defines it
       (Gecode ignores it on sets). hakank_ramsey_partition, which uses
       smallest, finds the same 18 solutions in a new order.
     indomain_min and indomain_max keep the include-first order of Gecode
     (SET_VAL_MIN_INC, SET_VAL_MAX_INC). OR-Tools CP-SAT reads indomain_min
     on sets as exclude-first (the smallest value of the element Booleans).

130. Under --lcg -f the core-guided phase no longer depends on the clock.
     Its probe, the branch and bound before it, the phase itself, its
     extensions, its "no solution of its own" stop, the lower bound's
     rate stops and the at-most-one probes took shares of the wall-clock
     time left, so the cores found differed between runs of one build
     (wide-quick, 60 s: core counts differed on 18 of 33 instances
     between two runs). They now take shares of the solver's work
     (CdclSolver.work: propagated literals plus 3 per constraint
     consistency call; CdclSolver.setWorkLimit stops the search at a
     conflict or decision), counted against -t at 2,000 per millisecond
     (LcgSolver.setTimeLimit; without -t, --free-config
     lcg-core-time=60000). A phase is still cut by the clock at 1.5x
     its share of the time left, which only happens where the work is
     much slower than the nominal rate (stat lcgCoreClockStops).
     -Djacop.lcg.workPerMs, workPerCall and clockSlack change the
     constants. wide-quick, two paired rounds: core counts differ on 6
     of 32 (all clock-capped). Wide set (220, 60 s, -f): solved 150 ->
     152, 33 better / 25 worse, 7 of 12 families better and 4 worse.

131. A parallel portfolio (-p) ends as soon as a thread proves a bound at
     the cost of the best solution, which it prints as optimal. Before,
     the threads' bounds stopped the portfolio only with --gap, so a
     proved optimum waited until one thread completed its own search.
     LCG also passes on the bounds that its lower-bound runs and the
     cores of the core-guided phase raise, not only those at the root.
     A bound under relax and reconstruct or on_restart still stops the
     search at --gap, but proves nothing. Wide set (220, -f -p 4, 60 s,
     two interleaved rounds): solved 173 to 173 and 174 to 171, 20 / 7
     and 12 / 20 better / worse, within the noise of two runs of the
     same build.

132. The linear relaxation keeps the basis of the last optimal solve at
     each level of the current branch (JaLP, the sparse simplex) and
     restores the deepest one kept when the search jumps back two or
     more levels (a restart, an LCG backjump), instead of going on from
     the basis the deeper node left. On LP-heavy -f runs the solves after
     a jump take 2 to 4 times fewer pivots (train 7.2 to 2.8, still_life2
     16.8 to 6.6). JaLP gets RevisedDualSimplex.saveBasis/restoreBasis.
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.keepBases=false turns it off; basisJump (2) and
     maxBases (32) tune it. Stats lpJumpSolves, lpJumpPivots,
     lpBasisRestores. The dense simplex (below 100 rows) is unchanged.
     Bases are not restored in the first 1,000 solves after the rows of
     non-linear constraints are added (org.jacop.lp.basisProbation),
     because those rows are dropped when early solves stop at the limit,
     and a restored basis spared them that limit: slnb7x7 kept 404
     big-M rows and took 25 s instead of 7 s.

133. The LP relaxation gets rows for more objective links: the convex
     hull of element over a constant array (lower and upper chains of at
     most 64 edges each, index domains up to 100,000 values), x div c
     through the remainder x - c*z, if-then-else (ConditionalSelect)
     as big-M rows from the root bounds, x in an interval under a
     reification, and the FlatZinc reified x = c, x = y and x != c (the
     last only with c at a bound of x).
     When not all candidate rows fit the tableau, the rows nearest to
     the objective (breadth-first through the base rows) go first; they
     used to be taken in model order and could all be cut before the
     objective's own rows. -Dorg.jacop.lp.linearize=false switches the
     rows off. tiny-cvrp medium 06 and 11: root bound 0 -> 729 and 747
     (optima 1585 and 1737). gbac, cargo, train-scheduling and
     city-position stay at 0: even an integer solve of all the rows
     gives 0 for the first three (the bound sits in bin packing,
     cumulative and disjunctive) and 256 / 309 against optima 2930 /
     10477 for city-position. Wide set (220, 60 s, paired): -f solved
     157 -> 158, 5 families better and 4 worse; default 164 -> 164, 1
     better and 6 worse, all by small objective differences at the
     time limit.
     
134. Under fixed search with --lcg (a search annotation, no -f), the
     core-guided phase of -f runs when branch and bound under the
     annotation has no solution after 15 % of the time, for 10 % of it;
     with a solution, the run goes on as before. unit-commitment L12 had
     no solution under its annotation in 60 s, while the cores'
     assumptions find one in seconds. --free-config fixed-core=<share>
     (0 = off) and fixed-core-delay=<share> set the shares. Running the
     phase under fixed search from the start instead lost proofs
     (city-position, triangular). Wide set (220, 60 s, both
     configurations at once): solved 153 to 156, 23 better / 22 worse,
     gap 0.144 to 0.142; unit-commitment no solution to 5,150,745,
     flowshop-workers 5stat_ex6 no solution to OPT in 5.9 s; ptv
     20_2b/20_3b lose their proofs and stable-goods s-d19 its solution.

135. Large neighbourhood search gets two routing neighbourhoods over the
     vehicle routes of a Routes structure: ROUTES removes the customers
     of whole routes near each other, RELATED removes customers close to
     each other by the cost of their arcs in the objective (Shaw
     removal); the run relaxes the arcs at the removed customers and the
     loads of the routes they were on, and reinserts them. AdaptiveLns
     finds imposed Routes constraints, and addRoutes(Routes) gives routes
     that are not imposed. Under -f the FlatZinc front end now runs the
     vrp detector and gives the routes it finds to LNS even though the
     Routes constraint stays off; --free-config lns-routes=0 turns this
     off. On the 25 Challenge vrp instances (-f, 60 s, three seeds) the
     mean gap to the best solution found falls from 7.0 % to 2.7 %
     (17 / 5 better / worse by median objective). No wide-set instance
     has these routes; its -f round is level (147 to 147 solved).

136. Reservoir, CumulativeOptional and CumulativeUnaryOptional explain
     their pruning and failures for lazy clause generation instead of
     taking the generic reason. Reservoir reasons over the level just
     after an event, from the precedences, shared times and time bounds
     that keep the other events before or after it, leaving out the
     changes the excess does not need. The optional cumulatives cite the
     presence of every task whose use they count, and explain an absent
     task from its compulsory part or from the room left when it would
     start. CumulativeUnary also explains a task whose 0..1 use drops to
     0 (an optional task posted as a use) by overload, edge finding or
     not-first over the other tasks, and keeps the shorter reason.
     setOptionalRules(true) (-Djacop.cumulative.optionalRules=true) adds
     Vilim's overload and edge finding over optional tasks to
     CumulativeUnary; it is off by default because it lowers the node
     rate more than it saves nodes. -Djacop.lcg.reservoirExplain=false
     and -Djacop.lcg.optionalExplain=false switch the explanations off.
     Generated reservoir scheduling kit (30 instances, 40 to 80 tasks,
     60 s): proved 26 -> 29 with model search and 24 -> 28 with -f, none
     worse. Optional kit posted with the optional classes
     (org.jacop.fz.optionalAsUse=false; 85 instances): proved 29 -> 35,
     35 better / 12 worse. With the default presence-as-use and on the
     wide set (220, 60 s) the trees stay the same where model search is
     fixed: solved 161 -> 161 and, with -f, 154 -> 154.

137. Large neighbourhood search gets three structural neighbourhoods, as in
     CP-SAT: CONSTRAINTS relaxes the variables of random constraints,
     SCHEDULING the tasks of a cumulative or unary resource that start in a
     window of time, and PACKING the rectangles of a diffn (Nooverlap) near
     a random one. SCHEDULING and PACKING also relax the variables that
     share a constraint of at most three variables with a relaxed task or
     rectangle (on cargo, the reclaim times tied to a rectangle by one
     row). OBJECTIVE now starts from the term with the largest regret (its
     cost above the cheapest value of its root domain). Under -f the
     FlatZinc front end leaves variables defined by a constraint
     (defines_var) out of the neighbourhoods, so a neighbourhood frees
     decision variables and the defined ones follow; --free-config
     lns-defined=0 restores the old set, and lns-kinds=a+b+... restricts
     the kinds. On 38 hard optimisation instances of 13 families where
     JaCoP is far behind CP-SAT (-f, 60 s, three seeds) the sum of mean
     primal gaps falls from 12.4 to 9.8 (18 / 4 better / worse; team
     assignment data1_6_24 73457 against 15561, celar scen06 3571 against
     4502). On the wide set under -f 6 of 12 families improve and 4 get
     worse (159 to 160 solved, mean gap 0.104 to 0.100);
     minimal-decision-sets australian_train5 loses its proof with
     lns-defined=1.

138. JaLP: the solve that moves the basic values after the bound flips of
     the dual ratio test is hypersparse too. The flipped columns are
     summed into a listed right-hand side, BasisLu.ftranFlips solves it
     with a density history of its own, and only the rows of its result
     change their values and are checked for the leaving row (before: a
     dense solve, a pass over all rows and a rebuild of the rows outside
     their bounds). The pivots are the same. LP bench (190 instances of
     1 to 60 s, one job per engine): geometric mean 1.05x.

139. The FlatZinc front end posts a union of singleton sets as one
     membership clause per element. MiniZinc flattens a set
     comprehension over variables, {e(i) | i in I}, into one singleton
     set per element (set_card(S, 1), set_in(e, S)) and a chain of
     set_union constraints, and under LCG every inner union of the chain
     carried membership literals of its own (gt-sort: 3,657 unions for
     11 sets). SetUnionDetector replaces a chain whose inner sets appear
     nowhere else by [v in U] <-> OR_i [e_i = v] for each element v;
     chains of fewer than 8 singletons stay as they are
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.setUnion.min), and -Dorg.jacop.fz.setUnion=false
     turns it off. gt-sort, 60 s, model search: 3 of 5 optimal (none
     before), the optimum found on all 4 instances with a known one.
     Only gt-sort changes model on the wide set.
     The [e_i = v] literals are shared by all chains, reuse the model's
     own int_eq_reif Booleans and go into one ChannelReif per element
     variable instead of one reified x = v each (gt-sort n13: 688k ->
     52k propagators; 600 s -f: 22 % less time on the 4 proved).

140. LP duals are those of the costs as given. Both dual simplex engines
     perturb the costs by about 1e-7 against dual degeneracy, and the
     duals of the perturbed costs carried that noise into the integer
     certificates of LinearRelaxation, where a large scale could turn it
     into a multiplier of a row without the side it needs; dropping it
     lost the bound. LpSolver.duals() now returns c_B B^-1 for the costs
     as given over the basis the perturbed solve ends with (JaLP:
     RevisedDualSimplex.givenDuals(), the same single BTRAN; dense: the
     slack columns of the tableau), so the pivots are unchanged and no
     primal clean-up is needed. On the 215 root LPs of the lp-bench
     jacop-root set, JaLP's certificates keep 42 of the 42 LP bounds
     that beat the column bounds (41 before; routing-flexible GCM_0007
     lost 31767 to 31765), as many as JaLP's setExact clean-up. Wide
     set (220, 60 s): solved 150 -> 150 by default, 152 -> 151 under
     -f, within the noise of the run. -Dorg.jacop.lp.givenDuals=false
     restores the old duals; -Dorg.jacop.lp.exact=true runs JaLP's
     setExact clean-up in search.

141. Under LCG an all_different over n variables whose domains hold n
     values in all adds, for each value, the clause "some variable takes
     it" over the value literals (Chuffed's alldiff_cheat). Constraints
     give such clauses through the new ImpliedClauses interface, which
     LcgSolver.encodeGraphs calls; stat lcgImpliedClauses. Off with
     -Dorg.jacop.lcg.alldiffCover=false; skipped above 16384 (variable,
     value) pairs (-Dorg.jacop.lcg.alldiffCoverLimit), since
     NumberPartitioning-200/260 (40k and 67k pairs) got slower. XCSP3
     Pentominoes (60 s): 4 -> 10 of 10 solved, Pentominoes-05-18 in 7.8 s
     (Chuffed 0.6 s). 161 XCSP3 instances with such an all_different:
     completed 76 -> 83, 22 faster / 16 slower.

142. Increasing, Among and OverlappingAlldiff give their own LCG reasons
     instead of the generic one, and Values (nvalue) gets a union-of-
     domains rule so its reasons need no replays. TilingRythmicCanons
     Increasing reasons 439 -> 2 atoms, SportsScheduling-dummy-14 Among
     reasons 170 -> 12 atoms (solved in 17 s, before not in 60 s).
     XmulYeqZ failures try a bounds-only reason and otherwise keep the
     shortest per-variable reason, not the first: Molnar failure reasons
     2,297 -> 4 atoms.

143. Element over a variable array bumps on failure the weights of the
     index, the value and only the list variables whose position is still
     in the index domain, so dom/wdeg no longer prefers variables that
     appear in every element (LangfordBin-040: solved with 195 failures,
     before not in 60 s).

     Changes 132-134 together: 88 XCSP3 instances of the families above
     (60 s): completed 47 -> 54, 22 faster / 12 slower. Wide set (220,
     60 s, default): solved 164 -> 166, 22 better / 5 worse, 6 of 12
     families better and 1 worse; -f: solved 161 -> 162, 12 better /
     14 worse, 5 families better and 4 worse (within the noise of two
     runs of one build).

144. Rounded capacity cuts in the linear relaxation, from each Routes
     with its arcs among the columns: the routes enter a set S of
     customers at least max(1, ceil(d(S) / Q)) times (or, the shorter
     form, the arcs inside S are at most |S| minus that). The sets come
     from the LP solution at the root, as the connected components of
     its support graph and sets grown greedily from each customer; up
     to 50 rounds of 50 cuts run before the MIR cuts and are dropped if
     they leave the bound where it was. -Dorg.jacop.lp.capacityCuts=false
     turns them off. The FlatZinc Routes detection (item 122) is now on
     by default, so the vrp models get them (-Dorg.jacop.fz.routes=false
     turns it off). vrp A-n34-k5: root bound 375 to 756, optimum near
     778. 25 vrp instances of the Challenge, 60 s, against develop-5.0:
     default 21 better / 4 worse (objective geomean 0.916), 3 proved
     optimal; -f 14 / 11 (0.988), 4 proved optimal. The wide set (220,
     60 s) has no routes: solved 153 -> 155 fixed search and 146 -> 145
     under -f, noise (the -f losses prove on a rerun with both jars).
     The detector no longer aborts on a bool_lin_eq whose right-hand
     side is a variable.

145. The LP relaxation (--lp) merges rows that forbid two 0/1 literals
     together (x + y <= 1, also stated as x + y - one <= 0 over a fixed
     variable, or x - y <= 0) into rows over cliques of such literals,
     on objective models. Pairwise rows let the LP take half of each set
     of shifts that share a time; the clique row takes one.
     roster-shifts-bool small (Challenge 2023): 25,803 rows become 1,791,
     the root bound is the optimum 5,281, and the proof takes 6 s
     (default) and 21 s (-f), where 60 s did not prove it before. Before,
     its 25,803 rows were over the size cap of the sparse simplex and the
     model got no LP at all. roster-shifts-bool large: bound 5,941,525 to
     56,931 (best known 56,378). The rows change the LP of 8 of the 220
     wide-set instances, with the same results in both modes. Off:
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.pairCliques=false; statistic lpPairCliques.

146. The route neighbourhoods of large neighbourhood search (ROUTES and
     RELATED) also work on giant-tour models: the successors of a
     Circuit or Subcircuit, split into routes by the links fixed at the
     root (the end depot of one vehicle followed by the start depot of
     the next), with the distances read from element constraints indexed
     by the successors. Removing customers relaxes their successors and
     those of the nodes before them; circuits over the same nodes (the
     predecessors, the tours of other scenarios) lose the same customers.
     On the 34 giant-tour Challenge instances (cvrp, vrplc, tsptw,
     stochastic-vrp, vrp-submission; -f, 60 s, three seeds) the mean gap
     to the best solution found falls from 10.4 % to 7.0 % (8 / 1 better
     / worse by median objective; cvrp 16 to 20 % lower). The wide set is
     level: -f 152 to 154 solved, fixed search (wide-quick) 48 to 47,
     the one difference on an instance without a circuit.

147. A call of the LP relaxation does less work when few bounds changed:
     LinearRelaxation sends JaLP only the column bounds that differ from
     the ones it sent last, and RevisedDualSimplex places, and moves into
     the basic values, only the columns whose bounds changed, skips the
     check of the reduced costs when no pivot was made since the last
     one, and keeps the list of columns with an infinite bound. Every
     solve gives the same pivots, values and duals bit for bit as
     before (240,000 random solves side by side, with rows added and
     removed, copies and kept bases), and the work count by which
     LinearRelaxation budgets its calls is charged as before, so the
     search is unchanged; counting only the work done instead ran other
     LP calls and made still-life-wastage 13 take 46 s instead of 30 s.
     28 LP-heavy wide-set runs (30 s, three paired runs each): LP time
     per call 0.965x by geometric mean (0.76x to 1.15x), nodes per
     second 1.00x. Wide set (220, 60 s, paired): default 164 -> 164
     solved, 7 better / 1 worse; -f 155 -> 155, 12 / 16 (an earlier
     round on another machine: 14 / 9), all by time-limit differences
     of -f's clock-based phases.

148. JaLP refactorizes its LU factors when the solves have grown more
     costly than a fresh factorization, instead of every 100 updates:
     after each pivot it rebuilds them when the recent solve work per
     pivot (a moving average) is above the average work per pivot since
     the factorization, the factorization included (Chvatal's rule). The
     work is the deterministic op count of BasisLu; the factorization
     adds the entries its pivot search looks at and a cost per row
     (BasisLu.build), fitted to measured times on 29 LP bench instances.
     The stability, drift and fill (2x) checks stay; at least 10 and at
     most 1000 updates. -Dorg.jacop.jalp.refactorWeight=0 restores the
     fixed 100 (refactorWeight 1, refactorUpdates 1000, refactorFill 2,
     refactorMin 10); RevisedDualSimplex.refactorsForWork() counts the
     refactorizations it asked for. LP bench (659 MPS up to upTo1min,
     120 s): 625 solved instead of 623, a median of 32% fewer
     factorizations, 1.11x faster on the 56 instances of 0.5 s or more
     whose pivots stay the same (radiationm18-12-05 33.3 to 14.5 s,
     neos-4722843-widden 20.8 to 10.5 s); pivot paths change on the
     others, both ways (dano3_3 and dano3_5 lost, brazil3, uccase12 and
     fome21 gained). Wide set (220, 60 s): solved 149 -> 149 fixed search
     and 146 -> 147 under -f.

149. The rows of the linear relaxation (--lp) are one pool, of which the
     simplex holds a part, with every simplex (before, only --lp lazy aged
     rows). The model's own rows stay in the simplex. Linearised rows of
     other constraints and cuts leave it after org.jacop.lp.poolAge (100)
     optimal solves in which they are inactive, and come back when a
     solution of the simplex breaks them; each return doubles the age, so
     a row the search keeps needing stays. -Dorg.jacop.lp.pool=false turns
     this off. org.jacop.lp.poolExtra and org.jacop.lp.poolCuts (both off)
     keep the linearised rows the root trial rejects, and the cuts of a
     round that does not move the root bound, in the pool instead of
     dropping them. The dense simplex can now remove rows whose slacks are
     basic. Wide set (220, 60 s): fixed search solved 163 -> 164, 7 better
     / 2 worse; -f 158 -> 160, 17 / 11 (both with pooled cuts on; with
     them off, the 34 instances where the pool moves rows are unchanged
     in fixed search and 1 / 2 under -f).

150. New module jacop-prob (org.jacop.prob) for probabilistic reasoning
     with exact probabilities: integer numerators over a common
     denominator. ProbArith compares, rescales and rounds them outward;
     Dist is a discrete distribution and ScenarioSet a finite set of
     scenarios. ScenarioChance(x, a, b, w, alpha[, ok]) states that the
     scenarios s with a[s] x <= b[s] weigh at least alpha (a chance
     constraint over scenarios). It prunes each variable by the weight
     of the scenarios a value would kill, explains its pruning for lazy
     clause generation, and with the indicators ok gives the linear
     relaxation the big-M rows of the usual expansion. ExpectedSum is
     the expectation of a sum with random coefficients. The MiniZinc
     library jacop_prob.mzn has scenario_chance and jacop_expected_sum.

151. LCG explains the failures of a linear equality (LinearIntDom, and
     SumWeight's failure reason) that its bounds do not violate. When the
     other variables' bounds leave one variable a window with no value
     in its domain, the reason is that window's removals and the bounds
     on both sides, lifted. When every window still holds a value but the
     equality has no solution (LinearIntDom's domain consistency proves
     it), the reason is every bound and the removals inside every window;
     only LinearIntDom uses that one, since Knapsack calls the linear
     reason speculatively. Before, these failures took the generic reason.
     liner-sf-repositioning tp7_4: generic LinearIntDom failures 45 % to
     0 % (--lcg) and 18 % to 0 % (--lcg -f); --lcg -f proves the known
     optimum 150674 in 41 s (before 160071 in 60 s, not proved). Wide set
     (220, 60 s, osolpro #231 scoring, two -f repetitions): --lcg S = +6,
     --lcg -f S = +9; the only decided instance with generic LinearIntDom
     failures is tp7_4 (-f +1, H1), the others are search-order noise.

152. --proof keeps the linear relaxation (--lp) on, and the VeriPB proof
     derives each of its deductions. Under --proof the relaxation is built
     by LinearRelaxation.ofModel: its rows are the linear constraints whose
     proof model is their relation as a line (LinearForms.ofModelLine:
     linear, LinearBool and the LinearExplainable ones), with no clause
     rows, table cliques, linearised rows of other constraints,
     substitution of split sums or cuts. Each bound, failure and
     infeasibility it propagates keeps its certificate (the rows and the
     exact integer multipliers it combines, divided by their gcd), and the
     proof writes the reason as a pol step over those rows' lines (the
     <= line for a positive multiplier, the >= line for a negative one)
     followed by the rup step of the clause. The relaxation's deductions
     are not divided by the gcd of their weights under --proof, which
     changes no bound they propagate. The pol steps are deleted at the
     next restart. Before, --proof turned --lp off.

153. The LP cut rounds at the root add exact Gomory cuts over the rows and
     their slacks. A tableau row gives the integer equality
     sum (lambda A)_j x_j - sum lambda_i s_i = 0 with s_i = a_i x; its MIR
     is taken over the columns and the slacks (bounded by the row sides
     and the column bounds) and the slacks are substituted back, which is
     the textbook Gomory mixed-integer cut computed in longs, so no float
     rounding enters a cut. The old aggregate MIR cuts stay. Cuts whose
     weights are too large or too far apart are no longer dropped: small
     weights leave the cut at their bounds and the rest is divided by the
     least power of two that fits, each weight rounded to the bound nearest
     the LP point with the remainder moved to the right side, which keeps
     every integer point. trucking 05 root bound 282.2 to 287 (the
     optimum). -Dorg.jacop.lp.slackGomory=false and
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.reduceCuts=false switch them off. Wide set at 60 s:
     default 162 to 162 solved (7 families better, 0 worse), -f 152 to 154.

154. Under --lcg without -f, the output variables that a search
     annotation leaves open are decided after it in input order, lower
     half first, as fixed search without --lcg does, instead of by
     activity branching. On connect, whose annotation covers about 760
     of the variables, activity branching wandered: 0018 290M to
     177-287k, 0083 99M to 925k, 0086 204.8M to 224,674 with the
     search completed in 15-27 s instead of running into the 60 s limit
     (not reported optimal: 325 of its domains reach beyond JaCoP's
     integer range). --free-config fixed-completion=activity gives the
     old completion. Wide set (220, 60 s, fixed search, interleaved):
     solved 119 to 119, with solutions 189 to 189; besides connect,
     flowshop-workers 5stat_ex6 49 to 24 s, ptv pax_period 20_3b 18 to
     51 s and network_50_cstr 2204150001 12 to 22 s.

155. Diffn's area check also tests the rectangles that overlap a
     rectangle's window with dual feasible functions (new class
     org.jacop.util.DualFeasibleFunctions): for every pair f1, f2 of
     the functions u^(k) and U^(e) of Fekete and Schepers and f_CCM1 of
     Carlier, Clautiaux and Moukrim, a packing needs
     sum f1(w_i) f2(h_i) <= f1(W) f2(H) (S. P. Fekete and J. Schepers,
     A general framework for bounds for higher-dimensional orthogonal
     packing problems, Math. Methods Oper. Res. 60(2):311-329, 2004;
     New classes of fast lower bounds for bin packing problems, Math.
     Program. 91(1):11-31, 2001; J. Carlier, F. Clautiaux and
     A. Moukrim, New reduction procedures and lower bounds for the
     two-dimensional bin packing problem with fixed orientation,
     Comput. Oper. Res. 34(8):2223-2250, 2007). The functions are exact
     integer maps, with parameters taken from the item sizes, and the
     test is skipped when Steinberg's condition proves the set packable
     (A. Steinberg, A strip-packing algorithm with absolute performance
     bound 2, SIAM J. Comput. 26(2):401-409, 1997). Binpacking's f_CCM1
     bound uses the same code. The area check now runs when twice the
     minimum area exceeds the free space. Under --lcg an area-check
     failure is explained by the rectangle and the rectangles with a
     part in its window instead of by all rectangles.
     -Dorg.jacop.diffn.dff=0 (off), 1 (default), 2 (always) and
     -Dorg.jacop.diffn.areaReasons=false give the old behaviour. All 120
     Challenge instances that post Diffn, 60 s: default 64 to 66 solved
     (accap a3 and a5 optimal), -f 65 to 64 and 63 to 63 in two rounds
     (accap a4 and a5 solved, others lost and won by search changes).
     Wide set: default 166 to 166 (5 families better, 1 worse), -f
     158 to 158 and 161 to 159, within the noise of the same build run
     twice (only 11 of the 220 instances post Diffn).

156. JaLP gets a primal simplex, RevisedDualSimplex.solvePrimal(limit),
     and setCost(c) to change the objective while the factored basis is
     kept: the basis stays primal feasible, so the primal simplex goes on
     from it where the dual simplex has to repair its dual feasibility.
     Composite phase 1 (the sum of infeasibilities, priced every pivot),
     Devex pricing, a two-pass Harris ratio test with bound flips of the
     entering column, bound perturbation after 30 degenerate pivots and
     bound shifting of infeasibilities below 1e-5; the dual simplex
     cleans up when the restored bounds leave the basis infeasible and
     gives the infeasibility proof. Nothing in the solver calls it yet; it
     is for RINS and LP diving. LpMain -primal and
     -reobjective (zero objective first, then the real one) run it. LP
     bench (659 LPs, 120 s): no wrong answer; the dual simplex makes the
     same pivots as before on all 631 it solves; after an objective
     change the primal solves 616 against the dual's 627 in about the
     same time (0.94 on solves over 0.5 s), and from scratch 610 against
     631, 1.27 times slower on solves over 0.5 s.

157. Local search (--free-config ls=...) keeps an all different over n
     variables whose domains share n values as a permutation: it starts
     from a matching and moves those variables only by swaps, so the
     constraint is no longer evaluated (an implicit constraint, as in
     Comet's constraint-based local search). Other all different
     constraints count their values, which makes the delta of a move
     O(1), and one walk step in four tries only the variables whose
     value another one takes. --free-config ls-perm=0 turns the
     permutations off. Costas arrays, local search only, 60 s: n <= 12
     solved before, n <= 16 now. Wide set at 60 s, -f ls=0.1: 159 to
     160 solved, with solutions 190 to 190; ls stays off by default.

158. Under --lcg, GCC and CountValues explain an upper bound of a counter
     by the lower bounds of the other counters (the variables they take
     leave too few for this value) before they try the flow network.
     The reason was written but not called, so these bounds went to the
     flow, which on nmseq (where the counters are also list variables)
     ran out of its time share: 47 to 65 % of the GCC reasons were
     generic. nmseq 202 and 395, fillomino 7x7_0: generic GCC reasons
     to 0 %, average reason 59-67 to 7-9 atoms; nmseq 395 is solved in
     44 s (60 s did not suffice), fillomino 7x7_0 --lcg 23 to 11 s.
     Wide set (220, 60 s, paired, scored by section 7 of the scoring
     rules): --lcg S = +4, -f S = +6 over two repetitions.

159. New constraint XorSystem (org.jacop.constraints): Gauss-Jordan
     elimination over GF(2) for a system of parity constraints. The rows
     are bit-packed and kept in reduced row echelon form over the unfixed
     variables; a pivot change adds one row to the others, so nothing is
     undone on backtracking. A row with one unfixed variable fixes it and
     a fixed row with the wrong parity fails, which gives full
     propagation of the conjunction. Under LCG the reason is the fixed
     values of the other variables of the combined row. The FlatZinc
     layer builds one redundant system per connected component of
     array_bool_xor, bool_xor, bool_not, bool_eq, bool_eq_reif and
     bool_ne_reif (with at least one row of three or more variables);
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.xorSystem=false switches it off. A system that finds
     fewer than 10 failures in 10,000 calls switches itself off, as
     CryptoMiniSat disables its matrices. References: C.-S. Han and
     J.-H. R. Jiang, When Boolean Satisfiability Meets Gaussian
     Elimination in a Simplex Way, CAV 2012, LNCS 7358, 410-426;
     T. Laitinen, T. Junttila and I. Niemela, Classifying and Propagating
     Parity Constraints, CP 2012, LNCS 7514, 357-372 (arXiv:1406.4698);
     M. Soos, S. Gocht and K. S. Meel, Tinted, Detached, and Lazy CNF-XOR
     Solving and Its Applications to Counting and Sampling, CAV 2020,
     LNCS 12224, 463-484; M. Soos, K. Nohl and C. Castelluccia, Extending
     SAT Solvers to Cryptographic Problems, SAT 2009, LNCS 5584, 244-257.
     parity-learning (7 instances, 60 s): 2 to 7 solved by default and
     1 to 7 with -f, each in 1-8 s instead of 34 s or a timeout.
     speck-optimisation: unchanged within seed noise. Wide set (220):
     only parity-learning 52_26_6.1 and speck easy_3 get a system.

160. New constraint DifferenceCycles over difference constraints
     x - y <= c that hold always or when a 0/1 literal takes a value. It
     fails as soon as the ones that hold close a cycle of negative
     weight and, under LCG, explains the failure by the literals of the
     cycle alone, so a job shop or RCPSP/max model whose precedences are
     chosen by 0/1 variables no longer creeps round such a cycle by
     bounds propagation (10^8 rounds over 0..3*10^8 in the regression
     test). It keeps a feasible potential and repairs it from the head of
     each new arc with a Dijkstra search over reduced costs (Ramalingam,
     Song, Joskowicz and Miller, Algorithmica 23(3), 1999; Cotton and
     Maler, Fast and flexible difference constraint propagation for
     DPLL(T), SAT 2006, LNCS 4121), which backtracking keeps valid; the
     first potential comes from Bellman-Ford with Tarjan's subtree
     disassembly (Cherkassky and Goldberg, Math. Programming 85(2),
     1999). Only arcs inside a strongly connected component are kept.
     The FlatZinc front end posts it, as a redundant constraint, when a
     reified or half-reified difference (int_lin_le/eq_imp/reif,
     int_le/lt_imp/reif) lies on a cycle; it extends the posting-time
     check of the always-true ones (DifferenceBoundsDetector).
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.differenceCycles=false switches it off. Theory
     propagation of implied literals and two-variable inequalities
     (TVPI, Hochbaum and Naor, SIAM J. Computing 23(6), 1994; UTVPI,
     Schutt and Stuckey, INFORMS J. Computing 22(4), 2010) are not
     covered. Posted on 27 of the 220 wide instances; slow_convergence
     has no conditional differences and is not touched. Wide set at
     60 s: default 166 to 166 solved, -f 160 to 160; of the outcomes that
     changed, 11 of 12 (default) and 19 of 21 (-f) are on instances where
     nothing is posted (run-to-run noise). cyclic-rcpsp 10-30% faster
     with up to 2.8x fewer failures, rectangle-packing rpp30 1.1 to
     0.7 s, rcpsp_max psp_d_244 8.5 to 10.2 s.

161. The clique rows of tables (TableCliques) and new value encodings of
     GCC, count, alldifferent, alldistinct and element use LP-only
     columns [x = v] (LpColumn) instead of store Booleans channelled with
     ChannelReif, so they add no variables or constraints to the store.
     Tables of up to 6 variables (org.jacop.lp.tableCliqueArity) are
     projected on their pairs. The value encodings (x = sum v [x = v],
     sum [x = v] = 1, counters, at most one per value, y = sum a[v] [x = v])
     share one set of columns per variable and reuse the model's own
     Booleans; they are tried at the first solve and kept only when they
     raise the root bound or make it infeasible, and when they are not
     kept the other extra rows are tried without them. In the simplex an
     LP-only column is bounded by the domain of its variable.
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.valueEncodings=false turns the new encodings off,
     valueEncodingsDomain (64) and valueEncodingsMax (20,000) cap them;
     the stat lpTableBooleans is now lpTableColumns. Infeasibility and
     dual certificates use the simplex bounds of the LP-only columns, and
     their LCG explanations add the facts x != v or x = v behind those
     bounds. Wide set (220, 60 s): default 164 to 163 solved (3 families
     better, 4 worse), -f 156 to 154 (3 / 5); the instances that change
     have the same LP in both versions and swap outcomes when re-run,
     except sudoku_fixed p28, where one more LP failure changes the
     search path.

162. Root reduced-cost fixing for the linear relaxation (SCIP's
     prop_rootredcost). The inequalities of the duals of the last optimal
     solve at the root level are kept; they are combinations of the rows,
     so they hold everywhere, and whenever the bound of the objective
     improves at the root level (a restart, or a backjump under LCG) they
     are propagated again and fix the columns whose reduced cost no
     longer fits in the gap. Statistic lpRootPrunings;
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.rootReducedCosts=false turns it off. Re-solving the
     relaxation at restarts, with new cut rounds, is there too but off by
     default (-Dorg.jacop.lp.restartSolves=true): it solves only when
     bounds other than the objective changed at the root, starts from the
     basis of the last root optimum and backs off when it deduces nothing.
     Wide set (220, 60 s, paired): root reduced costs prune on 17
     instances by default and 6 under -f; default 10 better / 3 worse
     (wmsmc-int proved in 11 s instead of 13 s), -f 14 / 11, solved
     unchanged within noise. With re-solves on: -f 156 solved against
     157 and 158 for two base runs, and ATSP instance10 lost its proof
     by default (LP time 0.4 s to 3.8 s).

163. Flow model of Regular and ExtensionalSupportMdd in the linear
     relaxation (Cote, Gendron and Rousseau, "Modeling the regular
     constraint with integer programming", CPAIOR 2007): one LP-only arc
     column per group of edges with the same level and value on a path of
     the layered graph (the value column itself when the group is a single
     edge), a row sum arcs = [x = v] per group, one unit of flow out of the
     source and conservation at every inner node. The flow polytope of a
     layered graph is integral. The arc columns are bounded by the domain
     of their variable (LpColumn.arc) and are not used to guide the
     search. The rows come after the value encodings and are kept only when
     they raise the integer bound of the objective above that of the rows
     before them. -Dorg.jacop.lp.flowEncodings=false turns them off. The
     value encodings of item 161 are no longer tried on models without an
     objective, where they cannot raise a bound. Wide set (220, 60 s,
     paired): default 161 to 161 solved (5 families better, 5 worse), -f
     162 to 162 (6 / 3); the LP differs only on the 8 satisfaction models
     that lose their value columns and on 2 peaceable-queens instances
     where the flow rows are built but not kept, and none of the outcomes
     that changed is on those. On the 55 Challenge instances with Regular
     or MDD and an objective, the flow rows rarely fit in the sparse
     simplex (handball: none of 1664) and, without that cap, raise the
     integer root bound only on peaceable queens (pq_n12 72 to 71).

164. Sequence (sliding among) as a global for local search and lazy
     clause generation, and found where a model states it window by
     window. AmongSeq, the domain consistent propagator of Sequence on the
     cumulative-sums network of Brand, Narodytska, Quimper, Stuckey and
     Walsh (CP 2007; the constraint is due to Beldiceanu and Contejean
     1994, Regin and Puget CP 1997, and van Hoeve, Pesant, Rousseau and
     Sabharwal CP 2006), now
     - explains its pruning under LCG by a path of the network that would
       close a negative cycle with the removed value, citing only the
       memberships on that path (fewest first), and a failure by a
       negative cycle; before, it had replayed or generic reasons, and
       adding it to car sequencing cut nodes per second tenfold;
     - implements Violation (the sum over the windows of the distance of
       their counts to min..max), and local search keeps the count of
       every window, so a move is evaluated in O(q), steps repair a
       violated window, and swaps keep the class counts, as Comet's
       differentiable sequence constraint (Van Hentenryck and Michel,
       Constraint-Based Local Search, 2005).
     The FlatZinc front end posts a redundant Sequence over a chain of at
     least three window constraints sum(y[i..i+q-1]) <= c (>= c, = c)
     with unit coefficients over 0/1 variables shifted by one position,
     as MiniZinc gives for forall-window sums (car sequencing, CSPLib
     prob001). The windows are still posted. With
     -Djacop.fz.sequenceLift=true a sequence whose y[i] are elements
     A[x[i]] of one constant 0/1 array is posted over the x[i] instead
     (the classes of car sequencing); it helps local search (LS only,
     CSPLib cars at 20 s: 0 -> 4 of 82) but costs default search (30
     against 36 solved over the 0/1 variables), so it is off.
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.sequence=false turns the detection off. Default
     options, 60 s: CSPLib cars (79 instances) 39 -> 44 solved; the 29
     Challenge instances with window chains (trucking, still_life,
     roster-shifts-bool, roster-sickness) unchanged; wide set 167 -> 168
     solved by default and 163 -> 163 under -f, with chains on 4 of its
     220 instances (the other changes are run-to-run noise).
     The window rows a posted sequence covers are marked implied (see
     165), unless a row defines a variable, so local search evaluates
     the sequence alone; propagation, LCG and the linear relaxation keep
     the rows. LS only, CSPLib cars at 20 s: 3 -> 5 of 82 solved; default
     options unchanged (cars 31 -> 31 at 20 s, the 34 other instances
     with window chains identical at 60 s).

165. Local search (--free-config ls=...) no longer evaluates the
     constraints JaCoP adds itself. The redundant constraints of the
     detectors are marked implied when they are imposed
     (Store.imposingImplied, Store.isImplied) and left out, since they
     only add propagation. The symmetry breaking constraints of
     --symmetry are left out too, and a solution is first moved onto a
     symmetric one that satisfies them: lex chains over interchangeable
     columns sort the columns, value precedence renames the values in
     the order of their first use (Law and Lee, Constraints 11(2-3),
     2006), and a lex-leader constraint x <=lex g(x) of a generator g
     (Crawford, Ginsberg, Luks and Roy, KR 1996) replaces a solution s
     that breaks it by s o g; the store checks the result. This keeps
     the constraint model of the problem, as in Comet's constraint-based
     local search (Van Hentenryck and Michel, MIT Press 2005).
     --free-config ls-implied=0 and ls-sym=0 turn it off; statistic
     lsCanonicalized. Local search only, 60 s, 54 local-search
     instances: 19 to 24 with a solution (cutstock type04 and type10,
     nsp 7, 60 and 87); local search steps 4x faster on golfers 5_3_6
     and 23x on cars 65_09. Wide set at 60 s, -f ls=0.1: 162 to 161
     solved, with solutions 192 to 192, within the noise of free search.

166. New constraint PrecedenceCost (org.jacop.constraints): sum(i)
     f[i](s[i]) <= cost under precedences s[j] >= s[i] + d over an
     acyclic graph, where each f[i] is convex piecewise linear, given as
     earliness and tardiness terms e * max(0, b - s) + t * max(0, s - b).
     The minimum of the sum over the bounds of the starts and the
     precedences is computed exactly as the dual of a convex-cost network
     flow (successive shortest paths with potentials; the potentials are
     an optimal schedule) and becomes the minimum of the cost; start
     times are filtered by the reduced cost of a shortest residual path
     to or from the root, and the precedences are propagated on bounds.
     Under LCG the reason is the flow certificate: only the bounds whose
     dual multiplier is positive, weakened as far as the slack allows.
     The FlatZinc layer posts it, as a redundant constraint, for sums of
     int_max(0, s - due) and int_max(0, due - s) terms over start times
     linked by difference constraints (rcpsp-wet, rcpsp-wet-diverse). It
     is on with the model's search and off under -f;
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.precedenceCost=true or false sets it in both modes.
     rcpsp-wet and rcpsp-wet-diverse (15 instances, 60 s, default
     search): 9 of 10 rcpsp-wet solved as before, 7 of them faster
     (j60_36_8 11.6 to 6.8 s), j90_10_10 unsolved either way; the
     rcpsp-wet-diverse j60_19_6-3 objective (maximised) 7,400 to 30,600
     in three runs. Under -f it lost on j90_48_4 (3.4 to 7.2 s) and the
     diverse j30_44_8-4, hence off there. References: R. K. Ahuja,
     D. S. Hochbaum and J. B. Orlin, Solving the Convex Cost Integer
     Dual Network Flow Problem, Management Science 49(7), 950-964, 2003;
     P. Chretienne and F. Sourd, PERT scheduling with convex cost
     functions, Theoretical Computer Science 292(1), 145-164, 2003;
     J.-N. Monette, Y. Deville and P. Van Hentenryck, Just-In-Time
     Scheduling with Constraint Programming, ICAPS 2009.

167. The XorSystem detector also reads tables (jacop_table_int,
     jacop_table_bool and short tables): the parity constraints a table
     implies on its 0/1 variables, the equations of the affine hull over
     GF(2) of its tuples allowed by the domains, become rows of the
     system. A table that is an XOR gives its row, a table whose tuples
     span every 0/1 point gives nothing. On the MiniZinc Challenge and
     XCSP3 benchmarks no table builds a system: the cryptanalysis models
     (PRESENT difference table, a + b + c != 1 truncated XORs of AES)
     span the whole space, and code-generator, is and Nonogram imply only
     isolated 2-variable rows. XCSP3 2025 Cryptanalysis gets systems from
     its bool_xor (8 per instance) with no change in time or answers.

168. Under --lcg, EinA (set_in_reif with a set variable)
     explains what the set's cardinality derives inside the constraint:
     when the cardinality bound leaves no room for the element, or when
     the other members already fill it, the reason cites the
     cardinality and the other memberships of the set's mirror
     (SetExplanation.explainCardinality, as ElementwiseExplainable
     does), instead of falling back to a generic reason. On vaccine v857
     every generic reason came from these (28,010 of 97,168); now none,
     and -f 22.7 s to 17.7 s. Of the 37 wide-set instances with
     set_in_reif, vaccine is the only one with generic Reified reasons.
     Wide set (220, 60 s, paired, scored by section 7 of the scoring
     rules): --lcg S = +7, -f S = -2 over two repetitions, with every
     decided instance outside the instances whose reasons changed.

169. ChannelBoolSet (jacop_link_set_to_booleans and the set unions found
     by SetUnionDetector)
     sets the Boolean of an element to 0 when the element leaves the
     set's least upper bound; before, the Boolean kept 1 in its domain.
     Under --lcg it explains its own atoms and failures: a Boolean
     and its membership literal explain each other, and the cardinality
     bounds and memberships derived from them are explained by counting
     the elements in and out of the set (the members fixed at the root
     counted as constants), instead of a generic reason. On gt-sort
     n9_ub10_75.0 the generic reasons go from 73,899 of 74,913 to none
     and the average reason from 41 to 13 atoms (--lcg) and from 22 to
     6 atoms (-f). Wide set (220, 60 s, paired, scored by section 7 of
     the scoring rules): --lcg S = +1, -f S = -7 over two repetitions,
     from one instance (cgt) that posts no set constraint.

170. Lattice methods for linear equalities.
     - LinearIntDom finds the supports of an equality by bitset sumsets
       when that is estimated cheaper than enumerating them: a forward
       pass of shifts gives the reachable partial sums after each
       variable, a backward pass keeps those that still reach the
       right-hand side, in O(sum |D_i| * W / 64) for a window of W
       partial sums. It stays domain consistent on long equalities with
       small coefficients, where the enumeration (product of the domain
       sizes up to 10^7) fell back to bounds. This is the knapsack dynamic
       program of Trick (Annals of OR 118, 2003) and the sumset view of
       subset sum of Koiliaris and Xu (SODA 2017; ACM TALG 15(3), 2019)
       and Bringmann (SODA 2017), on bitsets rather than FFT.
       -Dorg.jacop.constraints.LinearIntDom.sumset=off|auto|always.
     - New class LinearEqualities and org.jacop.util.IntLattice: the
       integer solutions of a system A x = b as x0 plus a lattice basis,
       by a column Hermite normal form (Hermite 1851; Kannan and Bachem,
       SIAM J. Computing 8(4), 1979), and LLL reduction (Lenstra, Lenstra
       and Lovasz, Math. Annalen 261, 1982) with floating-point
       Gram-Schmidt as in Schnorr and Euchner (Math. Programming 66,
       1994). The FlatZinc front end (LinearSystemDetector) gives it the
       int_lin_eq constraints: each is divided by the GCD of its
       coefficients (infeasible when it does not divide the constant),
       equalities that only define a variable are set aside, and each
       remaining component is solved over the integers, which proves it
       infeasible or gives every variable a congruence x = x0 (mod g)
       whose other values are removed from domains of at most 256 values
       (larger domains only get their bounds rounded); the variables the set-aside
       equalities define get the congruence of their sum.
     - Market split systems (at least two equalities of five or more
       variables, a coefficient of at least 10, domains of at most 100
       values) are reformulated as Aardal, Hurkens and Lenstra do (Math.
       of OR 25(3), 2000): x = x0 + sum_k lambda_k q_k over the reduced
       basis, with x0 moved near the centre of the box (Babai,
       Combinatorica 6, 1986). The linear relaxation takes these rows in
       and lambda is searched first, last basis vector first, middle
       value first. Without the relaxation the lambda search is
       slower than the x search, so it is only done with the relaxation
       on (the default). On 20
       market split instances (Cornuejols and Dawande, IPCO 1998;
       m = 3..6 equalities, n = 10(m-1) binaries, 60 s) solved 8 -> 18
       by default and 6 -> 18 with -f; m = 5 takes 2-5 s against
       time-outs, and 2 of the 5 m = 6 instances stay open. The column
       Hermite normal form reduces each row by its smallest entry
       (nearest-multiple subtraction rather than pairwise extended
       Euclid, whose multipliers grew U to millions of bits on
       lights_out) and gives up on a component beyond 256 bits.
       -Dorg.jacop.fz.lattice=false turns the detector off;
       -Dorg.jacop.fz.latticeReformulate=auto|on|off. Wide set (220,
       60 s, paired): congruences prune on 16 instances and no wide
       instance is reformulated; solved 168 -> 167 by default (one evm
       proof at 59.4 s lost) and 160 -> 161 with -f, instances 6 better
       / 6 worse and 13 / 13, the rest of the differences being on
       instances whose model does not change. ptv pax_period_20_3b is
       proved in 28 s instead of 58 s (4.4 s instead of 7.4 s with -f),
       harmony canon in 18 s instead of 23 s (36 s instead of 48 s).

171. Scheduling objective bounds. WeightedCompletion takes resource
     uses and a capacity (new WeightedCompletion(s, d, r, w, m, cost));
     with m > 1 it bounds sum w * (s + d) by the bound of Eastman, Even
     and Isaacs for identical machines (W. L. Eastman, S. Even and
     I. M. Isaacs, Bounds for the optimal scheduling of n jobs on m
     processors, Management Science 11(2), 1964), extended to release
     dates, resource uses and fixed tasks: a fluid relaxation of the
     capacity left by the fixed tasks, where at every moment the released
     task with the largest w / (r * d) takes it all, and each task adds
     half its duration (the mean busy time bound of Goemans; A. S. Schulz,
     Scheduling to minimize total weighted completion time: performance
     guarantees of LP-based heuristics and lower bounds, IPCO 1996, LNCS
     1084, gives the same bound as LP inequalities). Without releases it
     is LB1 / m + (m - 1) / (2m) * sum w * d. A free task's start is
     bounded by the bound of the others plus its own completion, an
     overload of the fixed tasks fails, and under --lcg the constraint
     explains with the start bounds and cost.max() instead of replay.
     New constraint LateCount(s, d, due, late) on a unary resource: the
     number of tasks ending after their due date is at most late, bounded
     by the algorithm of Moore and Hodgson (J. M. Moore, An n job, one
     machine sequencing algorithm for minimizing the number of late jobs,
     Management Science 15(1), 1968) with the release dates dropped and
     the tasks that must be on time kept on time by tightening the other
     due dates; a task that cannot be late (on time) without raising the
     bound above late.max() gets its start bounded. Propagation for late
     jobs with release dates follows the line of Ph. Baptiste, C. Le Pape
     and L. Peridy, Global constraints for partial CSPs: a case-study of
     resource and due date constraints, CP 1998, LNCS 1520. The FlatZinc
     front end posts both as redundant constraints: WeightedCompletion on
     cumulatives of capacity above 1 that are not unary
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.weightedCompletion.parallel=false turns it off), and
     LateCount where a sum in the objective counts tasks of a unary
     resource through bool2int of int_le_reif or int_lt_reif on a start
     or a later end (-Dorg.jacop.fz.lateCount=false).
     Random sets at 30 s (osolpro benchmarks/scheduling-bounds): on 60
     1|r_j|sum U_j instances (n 10 to 60) optima proved go from 49 to 58
     by default and from 48 to 58 with -f (Chuffed 33, CP-SAT 36); the
     mean time at n = 40 drops from 11.5 s to 1.9 s. On 72 P|r_j|sum w C
     instances (m 2 to 4, n 10 to 40) the best objective is better on 18
     and worse on 7 by default (optima 44 to 43); with -f it is better
     on 7 and worse on 16, against 10 and 12 between two runs of the
     same jar, so a small loss (optima 42 to 41 in two rounds). Wide set
     at 60 s: the detection fires on cargo challenge16 (172089 to 155318
     by default) and code-generator gobmk (proved optimal in 16.5 s
     with -f); solved 168 = 168 by default and 163 to 164 with -f, and
     the other differences are on instances where nothing is posted.

172. Tables in the linear relaxation. The rows TableCliques makes from a
     table (clique rows of its pair conflicts) now come with the convex
     hull of the table (Balas, "Disjunctive programming", Annals of
     Discrete Mathematics 5, 1979; the extended formulation of Bergman,
     Cire, van Hoeve and Hooker, "Decision Diagrams for Optimization",
     2016, section 2.2): one LP-only tuple column per live tuple, or the
     value columns of a position that tells the tuples apart (the index
     of a 2-D element), sum = 1, [x = v] = sum of the tuples with v for
     small domains and x = sum t_x lambda for the others. A Boolean that
     implies values on two or more positions (AndBool in the store, binary
     clauses under LCG) is tied to the tuples that agree with them. The
     table rows are tried at the first solve like the value encodings,
     with their own share of the sparse size cap, and kept only when they
     raise the bound or make the relaxation infeasible. The tables that
     ElementTableDetector merges from elements sharing an index are now
     registered too, so table-layout's width/height pairs reach the LP.
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.tableEncodings=false turns the hulls off;
     org.jacop.lp.tableTuples (2000) and org.jacop.lp.tableTupleColumns
     (20000) limit them. Root bounds: table-layout 2011 rises on all five
     instances (en-760 1344 to 1427, optimum 1604; en-1000-1615 1855 to
     2201), depot_placement u159_4 126 to 252, vrplc9_5_10_s2 26 to 28.6,
     is A3PZaPjnUz 47104 to 50805; physician-scheduling only 1140 to 1145.
     A 5 s probe of the wide set shows the LP differs on 30 of 220
     instances; those 30 at 600 s, paired: solved 23 = 23 by default (3
     better, 0 worse; vrplc9_5_10_s2 proved in 35 s instead of 77 s), 24
     = 24 with -f (1 better, 3 worse, all three within the spread of runs
     at other time limits; evm-super-compilation 121 s to 58 s).

173. Local search measures a table (HybridTable) by its distance to the
     nearest tuple, the number of columns that must change, instead of
     0/1 (violation degrees, Van Hentenryck and Michel, Constraint-Based
     Local Search, MIT Press 2005); tables over 50,000 cells stay copy
     checks. When one column of a table holds a distinct value in every
     tuple and all cells are equalities, the other columns are defined
     from it as invariants (as in Comet), so the table always holds; it
     is listed as K:HybridTable and --free-config ls-keys=0 turns it off.
     An exact-class CountValuesBounds gets the O(1) value counters of the
     all different, with a penalty outside its bounds. Local search only,
     60 s, 54 local-search instances: 24 to 25 with a solution (cars
     65_09, solved on 4 of 4 seeds against 0 of 3, 2.2x more steps).
     Wide set at 60 s, -f ls=0.1: 163 to 164 solved, with solutions 192
     to 192.

174. ConvexAllocation: a bound for sum f_i(x_i) <= cost with
     sum x_i = total and convex costs f_i (ConvexCost: weighted maxima
     of affine pieces, their squares, or tables). The minimum of the
     separable convex allocation is found by the greedy on marginal
     costs (Hochbaum, "Lower and upper bounds for the allocation problem
     and other nonlinear optimization problems", Mathematics of
     Operations Research 19(2), 390-409, 1994); it generalises the
     bounds of Spread (Pesant and Regin, "SPREAD: a balancing constraint
     based on statistics", CP 2005, LNCS 3709) and Deviation (Schaus,
     Deville, Dupont and Regin, "The deviation constraint", CPAIOR 2007,
     LNCS 4510). The minimum bounds cost, the marginal cost lambda gives
     reduced costs that bound every x_i, and for up to 64 variables
     (-Djacop.convexAllocation.exact) each bound of x_i is exact over
     the integer intervals (bounds(Z)), found by walking x_i away from its
     minimiser while the others follow greedily. Under LCG it explains
     its prunings with the Lagrangian bound at lambda.
     ConvexAllocationDetector posts it in FlatZinc next to the model's
     own constraints for objectives that sum abs, squares or maxima of
     affine terms of variables whose sum is fixed or a variable (unit
     int_lin_eq of 3 or more variables, or bin_packing loads). It is on
     by default without LCG and off under LCG
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.convexAllocation=true/false overrides): with LCG a
     stronger cost bound cost gbac most of what the alternating restarts
     find (2020 UD10 417 to 8052). Of the MiniZinc Challenge instances
     2008-2025 it fires on gbac only. Random balance and gbac instances
     (50, 30 s, --lcg off): solved 13 to 35, square_b12 14760 to optimum
     43 in 0.9 s, every squared dead zone solved within 3.4 s; wide set (220, 30 s, --lcg off): 89 to 89 solved, gbac UD3
     57738 to 57174, and with -f 117 to 120 solved, gbac 968 to 785,
     the other changes being run-to-run noise.

175. Local search searches the machine sequences of disjunctive scheduling
     models with critical-path neighbourhoods, the scheduling abstraction
     of Comet's constraint-based local search (Van Hentenryck and Michel,
     "Scheduling abstractions for local search", CPAIOR 2004). Machines are
     cumulative constraints with fixed durations and demands on which no
     two tasks fit together; precedences are rows s[a] + lag <= s[b]
     between their starts. A tabu search moves operations of the blocks of
     one critical path: NS swaps the first two and last two operations of
     each block (Nowicki and Smutnicki, "A fast taboo search algorithm for
     the job shop problem", Management Science 42(6), 1996), N1 any two
     adjacent ones (van Laarhoven, Aarts and Lenstra, Operations Research
     40(1), 1992), N4 moves one to the front or back of its block
     (Dell'Amico and Trubian, Annals of Operations Research 41, 1993).
     Moves are ranked by the estimates of Taillard (ORSA Journal on
     Computing 6(2), 1994) and Dell'Amico and Trubian from heads and tails,
     with arc tabu and aspiration. Every better schedule is checked in the
     store; when the cost is not the makespan or the store rejects the
     schedules, the general local search takes over. LocalSearch.Builder
     .scheduling(kinds), --free-config ls-sched=ns+n4 (default), n1, 0;
     statistic lsSchedule=tasks/machines/iterations. Local search itself
     stays off by default (ls=0). 46 JSPLIB job shops and 5 Challenge open
     shops, 60 s: local search alone cuts the mean gap to the best known
     makespan from 30.81 % to 0.91 % (51 better, 0 worse); -f with
     ls=0.1 from 2.62 % to 1.04 % (28 / 0). Wide set (220), -f ls=0.1:
     161 to 162 solved, 2 families better, 4 worse; only openshop tai20-4
     (optimal in both) and mspsp hard_01 (gives way at once) have such a
     graph, so the rest is run-to-run noise.

176. New redundant constraint HornFlow (org.jacop.constraints.cumulative)
     for cumulative scheduling: the preemptive relaxation checked by a
     maximum flow from tasks to elementary intervals, after W. A. Horn,
     Some simple scheduling algorithms, Naval Research Logistics
     Quarterly 21(1) (1974) 177-185, and A. Federgruen and H.
     Groenevelt, Preemptive scheduling of uniform machines by ordinary
     network flow techniques, Management Science 32(3) (1986) 341-349;
     the conditions are those of A. Shioura, V. A. Strusevich and N. V.
     Shakhlevich, Generalizing Horn's conditions for preemptive
     scheduling on identical parallel machines via network flow
     techniques, Networks 83(3) (2024) 527-546. Each task arc carries
     r_j|I| and each interval arc C|I|, so it covers any resource use;
     compulsory parts are taken out of the intervals first
     (useCompulsoryParts, on by default). A failure is explained under
     LCG by the min cut, as energy over a union of intervals U:
     sum_j r_j max(|cp_j & U|, p_j - |W_j \ U|) > C|U|, with tasks left
     out and bounds lifted while the slack allows. FlatZinc option
     -Dfz_cumulative_flow=false|unit|true (default false) adds it to
     unit-use cumulatives or to every cumulative. Checks: the flow equals
     the cut condition enumerated over every U and is sound against brute
     force (HornFlowTest); 30,000 LCG solves with every explanation
     checked, 30,382 own explanations, none generic (HornFlowLcgTest);
     a case in LcgFuzzTest and in ConsistencyChecker. Cumulative's own
     rules already refute almost every root the flow refutes (0 of 20,000
     random instances with edge finding on); in search 8 of 500 unit-use
     instances get smaller trees. Benchmarks (60 s) show no gain: 52
     unit-use Challenge instances, 25 to 23 solved by default and 25 to
     24 with -f; wide set (220, 29 with a cumulative), 167 to 167 and
     159 to 160, changes within noise. Hence off by default.

177. The FlatZinc front end finds vehicle routes written as a giant tour
     (circuit(x) over the customers and a start and an end node per
     vehicle, with each end node's successor fixed to the next start
     node: cvrp, vrplc, stochastic-vrp and vrp-submission of the
     Challenge; P. Kilby and P. Shaw, Vehicle Routing, Handbook of
     Constraint Programming, 2006) and posts a redundant Routes (item
     122) over new arc Booleans b[i,j] <-> x[i] = j between customers,
     with one depot arc out of and into each customer and their degree
     rows. A quantity accumulated along the tour (T[x[n]] >= T[n] + q[n]
     with q >= 0, such as the load) gives the demands and the capacity,
     so the linear relaxation gets the rounded capacity cuts of item 144
     (J. Lysgaard, A. N. Letchford and R. W. Eglese, Mathematical
     Programming 100(2), 2004). Each element d = c[x[i]] over a constant
     array gets the row d = sum c[j] b[i,j], and every accumulated
     quantity the row sum T[x[n]] = sum T[m] over the nodes, which holds
     because x is a permutation: it ties an objective written as the
     arrival times at the end nodes (cvrp) to the arc costs. Root LP
     bounds go from 0 to 34 on cvrp simple2 (optimal, proved in 224
     nodes), 0 to 446 on P-n16-k8 (optimum 450), 0 to 661 on A-n37-k5
     and 0 to 461 on vrp-submission A_4v_22l (best known 481); vrplc
     9_5_10_s1 61 to 146 (optimum 351, time windows). The new Booleans
     are branched on last under lazy clause generation, and the route
     neighbourhoods of LNS keep using the circuit itself.
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.giantTour=false turns the detection off. 34
     giant-tour instances of the Challenge, 60 s: optima proved 14 to 19
     by default and 14 to 20 with -f (A_4v_22l 820 to 481 optimal), 13
     better / 9 worse by default (objective geomean 0.976) and 13 / 5
     with -f (0.987); vrplc13_11_20_s4 finds no solution in 60 s in
     either mode (its first solution comes after 46 s without the
     detection). Wide set (220, 60 s): the detection fires on the 4
     giant-tour instances only; solved 166 to 164 by default and 156 to
     157 with -f, all changes outside them are run-to-run noise
     (vrplc9_5_10_s2 finds its optimum 329 at 9.7 s instead of 17 s
     but no longer proves it in 60 s by default).

178. The redundant constraints the FlatZinc layer adds next to the
     model's own are marked implied in the store (Support.implied), so
     local search does not evaluate them: the CumulativeUnary and the
     pairwise disjunctions of a cumulative, the cumulative projections
     of a diffn (with the variables of their spans), the all different
     of a circuit, subcircuit or assignment (unless the model states the
     same all different), the OverlappingAlldiff of overlapping
     all_different pairs, the bin packing conflict cliques, HornFlow
     and PrecedenceCost. Before, only the constraints of the detectors were
     marked (item 165). Store.isImposingImplied is new.

179. The experimental cut-based conflict analysis of --lcg
     (-Djacop.lcg.linear=learn, off by default) also uses the
     explanation of a constraint that has no linear relation when all its
     atoms are on 0/1 variables: the clause is then an exact linear row
     (antecedent literals sum to at most their number minus one, or the
     conclusion holds), used as the start of a failure or as a reason.
     -Djacop.lcg.linearClauses=false turns it off. On the 249 mznbench
     instances, 10 s, failures ending in a learnt sum went from 6.7% to
     7.0% (--lcg -f) and 5.9% to 6.6% (--lcg); most element, table and
     scheduling explanations cite several integer bounds and stay out of
     reach. With learning on, clause rows on against off, 30 s, 18 pairs:
     cryptanalysis kb128_n5_obj17 finds a solution in 5.6 s instead of
     none, steiner-triples_13 none instead of one at 21 s, the rest
     within noise. %% lcg-linear also reports rowConstraints and
     rowsMillis (root rows made and the time taken).

180. Bug fix: under --lcg the reason of WeightedCompletion on a capacity
     above 1 left out the holes a pruned bound skipped. When the latest
     start computed for a task (or the cost's minimum) fell on a removed
     value, the domain moved past it, and the reason (start bounds and
     cost.max()) did not imply the recorded bound; LcgFuzzTest found it.
     The reason now adds the != atoms of those holes.

181. Energy cuts of cumulative resources in the LP relaxation
     (org.jacop.constraints.lp.EnergyCuts). For every Cumulative of an
     objective model, LinearRelaxation separates at the root the
     mean-busy-time inequalities of a set S of tasks released at t or
     later, sum e_j (S_j + p_j/2) >= tE + E^2/(2C') with e_j = r_j p_j,
     E = sum e_j and C' = min(C, sum r_j), in the integer form
     sum e_j S_j >= tE + ceil((E^2 - C' sum e_j p_j)/(2C')), their
     mirror for deadlines, and for tasks with 2r_j > C the one-machine
     inequality sum p_j S_j >= tP + (P^2 - sum p_j^2)/2. Durations and
     uses are their root minimums and the time bounds the root bounds,
     so every cut is global. For each release date the tasks are sorted
     by their LP mid points and the most efficacious prefix is taken.
     References: M. Queyranne, Structure of a simple scheduling
     polyhedron, Mathematical Programming 58 (1993) 263-285; L. A. Hall,
     A. S. Schulz, D. B. Shmoys and J. Wein, Scheduling to minimize
     average completion time: off-line and on-line approximation
     algorithms, Mathematics of Operations Research 22(3) (1997)
     513-544; M. X. Goemans, M. Queyranne, A. S. Schulz, M. Skutella and
     Y. Wang, Single machine scheduling with release dates, SIAM Journal
     on Discrete Mathematics 15(2) (2002) 165-192. The cuts run in the
     root rounds of the capacity cuts and are kept only if they raise the
     bound; stat lpEnergyCuts, switch -Dorg.jacop.lp.energyCuts=false.
     Bin packing adds the assignment rows load_b = sum_i w_i [bin_i = b]
     (L. V. Kantorovich, Mathematical methods of organizing and planning
     production, 1939, Management Science 6(4) (1960) 366-422) as tried
     LP rows over value columns, off by default
     (-Dorg.jacop.lp.binpackingRows=true): on gbac the LP bound stays 0
     with them, as fractional assignments balance the loads, and their
     value columns turn the auto LP guide on although the rows are dropped
     (2017 gbac UD8 at 60 s: 89 without, 342 with them).
     Root bounds go from 0 to 52-1619 on six cargo instances (2013
     challenge04 0 to 379, optimum 714) and from 66 to 90 on trains12;
     gbac stays at 0 since fractional assignments balance the loads, and
     cargo 2017 gains nothing (its pad durations have root minimum 0).
     Checks: every cut holds for every schedule of 300 random resources,
     enumerated (EnergyCutsTest); optimum kept by DFS and by LCG with
     the explanation checker on random scheduling models
     (LinearRelaxationTest). Benchmarks at 60 s: 34 cargo, gbac and
     train-scheduling instances, 9 to 10 solved by default and 10 to 10
     with -f (gap 0.200 to 0.177, cargo 2013 challenge08 29361 to 25751,
     challenge10 16883 to 14087); wide set (220), 167 to 168 and 161 to
     161, 7 families better and 3 worse in both modes, all changed
     instances but one without kept cuts (noise).

182. DiffnK explains its pruning and failures under --lcg by the boxes
     of the rule that fired, instead of by the bounds of all boxes:
     a pairwise separation by the origin and length bounds of the two
     boxes (with the holes skipped by the pruned bound), a volume or fit
     failure by the boxes it counted, and the exclusion of a
     non-strict box by the atoms that make it zero-sized or absent. The
     average reason drops from about 149 to about 15 atoms on
     products-and-shelves (-Dorg.jacop.diffnk.reasons=false restores
     the generic reason).
     Diffn has a new energetic check on strips (setEnergetic,
     -Dorg.jacop.diffn.energetic=0|1|2, off by default): for each
     dimension and each strip [t1, t2) with t1 an earliest or latest
     start and t2 an earliest or latest end, the minimal parts of the
     rectangles inside the strip must pack into the strip, tested with
     the dual feasible functions of item 155 (mode 1) or by area only
     (mode 2) (F. Clautiaux, A. Jouglet, J. Carlier and A. Moukrim, A new
     constraint programming approach for the orthogonal packing problem,
     Comput. Oper. Res. 35(3):944-959, 2008; S. P. Fekete and
     J. Schepers, Math. Methods Oper. Res. 60(2):311-329, 2004). Its
     failures are explained by the strip rectangles' bounds that place
     them in the strip and the extent.
     At 600 s (three rounds each, default and -f search, same jar with
     the generic reason as base) the five 2025 products-and-shelves
     instances all reach the optimum in every round: ps-50-09 is proved
     in 109-125 s by default and 240-326 s with -f, where the base finds
     the optimum but proves nothing in 600 s; ps-25-01 goes from 154-180
     s to 28-35 s (default) and from 374-394 s to 24-27 s (-f); ps-25-03
     from 60-72 s to 5-7 s; ps-50-06 from 34-39 s to 14-18 s. The
     energetic check stays off: on the 70 hardest Challenge Diffn
     instances at 240 s it solves 28 -> 18 (mode 1) and 28 -> 25
     (mode 2), and on the 25 carpet-cutting instances at 600 s mode 2
     solves 12 -> 11 with 8 better and 8 worse.

183. The automatic LP guide of the FlatZinc front end
     (--free-config lcg-lp-branching=auto) turns on only when the simplex
     has value columns. Before, it also turned on for the columns of
     tried value encodings that the root trial dropped. New
     LinearRelaxation.simplexValueColumns() and stat
     lpSimplexValueColumns. This turns the guide off on 43 wide-set
     instances. At 600 s the same number are solved in both modes;
     with -f tower 2025 goes from 111 to 24 s and spot5 28 from 25 to
     12 s, but vrplc9_5_10_s2 goes from 17 to 150 s
     (lcg-lp-branching=on restores the guide there).

184. Under --lcg, AndBoolSimple and OrBoolSimple (bool_and and bool_or of
     two Booleans) and XplusYeqC (x + y = c) explain their own atoms and
     failures instead of a replayed or generic reason. A result of AND
     fixed to 0 cites the operand at 0, a result fixed to 1 both
     operands, an operand cites the result and, when needed, the other
     operand (OR by duality), including the negated and the entailed
     constraint under Reified. x + y = c walks the values of the atom
     against their mirrors c - v in the other variable, like the shift
     explanations of equalities (ShiftExplanation.explainMirror). On opd
     flener_et_al_15_350_100 the AND reasons stop being replayed (388k)
     or generic (54k) at the same length (1.9 atoms); on cgt
     cgt_5_h27_r0.05_s0.8_0 the x + y = c reasons go from 39 atoms, a
     third generic, to 1 atom. On the 46 wide instances that post these
     constraints (240 s, paired, scored by section 7 of the scoring
     rules): --lcg S = +14, -f S = +1 over two repetitions.

185. Symmetry detection in fzn-jacop builds the graph of a constant array
     once per content: an array whose elements are all constants (a table
     of tuples, given by name or written out) becomes one key shared by
     every constraint that uses the same contents, instead of one vertex
     per position for each use, and named int arrays are read and their
     constants interned once. Huge constant tables now fit in the 1 s
     detection budget (org.jacop.fz.symmetryTime): on the 15 groupsplitter
     instances graph building was cut off by the deadline on all 15, now
     it completes on all in 0.12-0.33 s CPU and orbitopes are posted on 6.
     Tables with equal contents get the same colour, so constraints over
     the same table are interchangeable. Of the 165 Challenge instances
     with the slowest detection, graph deadlines drop from 136 to 103 (the
     rest are graphs of 100k-1.3M vertices, not tables); on the wide set
     10 of 220 instances post different symmetry constraints and the rest
     post the same. Benchmarks (600 s): groupsplitter 10 to 10 solved by
     default (u15g5pref0 309 to 412) and 10 to 9 with -f (u12g2pref1
     proved at 549 s before, not by 600 s now); the 10 changed wide
     instances 8 to 8 in both modes.

186. The dense simplex (below 100 rows) keeps and restores bases too,
     like JaLP in 132: DualSimplex gets saveBasis/restoreBasis, which
     copy the tableau, the basis and the edge norms, and refuse a basis
     saved before rows were added or removed or in another copy. Since a
     save copies the whole tableau, the linear relaxation saves dense
     bases only at the first denseBasisDepth (3) levels below the level
     of the first LP solve (org.jacop.lp.denseBasisDepth, -1 turns dense
     saves off). Keeping the LU factors with a JaLP basis was measured
     and not done: refactorizations after restores take 1 to 14 % of the
     LP time (JFR, train_3, still_life2, rcpsp-wet j90), and saves
     outnumber restores about 20 to 1, so copying the factors at each
     save would cost more than it saves. Tuning at 240 s on 26 LP-heavy
     -f instances: denseBasisDepth 3 solves 20 (sparse only 19) with 226
     s of LP time instead of 319 s; basisJump 4 and maxBases 8 or 128 are
     no better, so they stay 2 and 32. On the 73 wide-set instances that
     restore dense bases (600 s, base and candidate in the same round):
     by default 66 to 66 solved, 2 families better and 1 worse; with -f
     63 to 64 solved (blocks-world 16-4-83 proved optimal in 414 s), 5
     families better and none worse; city-position is slower with -f
     (city-5-06 222 s to 307 s).

187. Capacity cuts of a Routes (item 144) are also separated in search:
     every few optimal LP solves after the root (from 4, doubled up to
     1024 while nothing is violated, halved when cuts are found), up to
     10 cuts go into the row pool, where they age out like other cuts.
     The mandatory customers are fixed at the root, so these cuts hold
     for the whole search. New directed multistar cuts over the same
     sets: Q x(in(S)) >= d(S) + sum over outside customers j of
     d_j (x(j->S) + x(S->j)), after N. R. Araque, L. Hall and T. L.
     Magnanti, Capacitated trees, capacitated routing and associated
     polyhedra, CORE discussion paper 9061 (1990), and A. N. Letchford,
     R. W. Eglese and J. Lysgaard, Multistars, partial multistars and the
     capacitated vehicle routing problem, Mathematical Programming 94
     (2002) 21-40; the separation follows J. Lysgaard, A. N. Letchford
     and R. W. Eglese, A new branch-and-cut algorithm for the capacitated
     vehicle routing problem, Mathematical Programming 100 (2004)
     423-445. A multistar cut is added only for a set whose rounded
     capacity cut holds: when both competed, the multistar cuts took the
     slots of the capacity cuts and the root bound fell.
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.capacitySearch=false and
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.multistarCuts=false turn them off; statistics
     lpCapacitySearchCuts and lpCapacitySeparations. Checks: every cut
     keeps every set of routes of 3,000 random graphs found by
     enumeration (CapacityCutsTest), and a CVRP keeps its optimum with
     the LCG explanation checker on. 25 vrp instances of the Challenge,
     240 s, the default (both on) against both off: -f 13 better / 6
     worse (objective geomean 0.996), proofs faster (A-n32-k5 171 to
     58 s); default 9 / 7 (0.999), A-n32-k5 proved. Search cuts alone
     against both off: -f 13 / 6 (0.997), 2 more proved near the limit;
     default 9 / 6 (1.000). The multistar cuts are level (against search
     cuts alone, -f 8 / 10, default 2 / 2). At 60 s the search cuts gave
     default 16-18 / 2-4 (geomean 0.95); the gain shrinks at 240 s.
     Energy cuts (item 180) stay at the root. Framed capacity cuts are
     not separated.

188. All-different rows in the LP relaxation (--lp and the automatic LP).
     A permutation (an all-different of n variables over exactly n
     values) whose variables have no value encodings (domains over 64
     values, or encodings off) gets the extended formulation of the
     permutahedron by a sorting network: each comparator of Batcher's
     odd-even merge network gives a free LP-only column pair with
     p + q = a + b, p <= a, p <= b, and the outputs are fixed to the
     sorted values; O(n log^2 n) rows and columns instead of the 2^n
     facets, after M. X. Goemans, Smallest compact formulation for the
     permutahedron, Mathematical Programming 153 (2015) 5-11. The rows
     are tried at the first solve and kept only when they raise the
     bound. With value encodings the assignment polytope already gives
     the convex hull, so -Dorg.jacop.lp.alldiffNetwork=all is needed to
     add the network there (=false turns it off;
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.alldiffNetworkMax caps the comparators, 5,000).
     Every all-different of an optimisation model also separates Hall
     cuts at the root, after the capacity and energy cuts: sorting the
     LP values gives, for every prefix of k variables, the cut sum >= the
     k smallest values of the union of their domains (and <= the k
     largest), the inequalities of H. P. Williams and H. Yan,
     Representations of the all_different predicate of constraint
     satisfaction in integer programming, INFORMS Journal on Computing
     13 (2001) 96-103, and J. Lee, All-different polytopes, Journal of
     Combinatorial Optimization 6 (2002) 335-352, for which sorting
     finds the most violated cut of each size. They are kept like the
     capacity cuts; -Dorg.jacop.lp.alldiffCuts=false turns them off.
     New LpColumn kind: a free column (no CP variable), which an LCG
     explanation removes at its bounds without facts. Statistics
     lpFreeColumns, lpSimplexFreeColumns, lpAlldiffCuts and
     lpAlldiffBound. Checks (AlldifferentRowsTest): the network sorts
     every 0-1 input for n up to 16, every permutation is a point of the
     rows and the LP optimum of random costs is the best permutation;
     no Hall cut cuts off any solution of random domains, nor a convex
     mix of them; random models keep their optimum with the relaxation,
     also under LCG with the explanation checker (LinearRelaxationTest).
     Reach: none of the 38 all-different optimisation instances of the
     wide set changes (their permutations are value encoded). Hall cuts
     raise the root bound of ghoulomb (34.1 to 38.1, 42.6 to 49.4),
     lot-sizing pigment30b (0 to 190) and team-assignment. On those 35
     instances at 600 s, base against new: default 26 to 27 solved,
     -f 22 to 22, same objectives except two team-assignment instances
     whose root LP is the same in both (search timing); no wrong answer.

189. The LCG explanation checker (-Djacop.lcg.check=true) and the
     explanation minimality sampler (-Djacop.lcg.minimality) skip a
     constraint whose copy needs variables outside its scope, such as
     DifferenceCycles, whose scope holds only its literals. Before, the
     sampler stopped the solver with "... is not a variable of the
     constraint" (whirlpool with -Djacop.lcg.minimality); the checker
     counts such reasons as skipped. Solving is unchanged.

190. fzn-jacop prints the statistics of JaCoP's own components (lcg*,
     presolve*, lp*, components*, lns*, ls*, symmetry*, probe*,
     portfolio*, ...) only in verbose mode: with -s -v. -s alone prints
     the standard FlatZinc statistics (and objectiveBound with
     objectiveBoundTime); components*, lcgSlowConflicts and
     portfolioSharedClauses, which -s printed before, now need -v too.
     --search-statistics stays, as -s -v without the -v lines after each
     solution, and so does -s -debug.

191. JaSAT puts all assumptions of a solve call on one decision level and
     keeps that level at restarts, after R. Hickey and F. Bacchus,
     Speeding Up Assumption-Based SAT, SAT 2019, LNCS 11628, 164-182.
     Before, each assumption opened its own level with its own CP
     fixpoint, and every restart undid and rebuilt them all; the
     core-guided phase of --lcg -f solves under many assumptions. The
     assumptions are still assigned one at a time, each propagated before
     the next: assigning them all and propagating once made the LCG
     explanations about 7 times longer (ptv). A conflict on the
     assumption level gives the core directly. A restart backtracks to
     the root only when vivification or elimination is due; LcgSolver
     exports bounds, presolves and refreshes the objective bound only at
     the root, since bounds on the assumption level hold only under the
     assumptions. CdclSolver.setAssumptionsOnOneLevel(false) and
     --free-config lcg-assume-level=0 give the old behaviour. Counter:
     on 34 optimisation instances of wide-quick, -f, 240 s, propagating
     assumption decisions took a median 3.3 % of the SAT work, above
     10 % on 13 (ptv up to 76 %). wide-quick, 240 s, 2 paired reps: -f
     52 solved against 51 in both (work-task-variation seed-8 proved in
     about 60 s), 2 families better and 0 worse in both, time geomean
     0.967; default mode 51 against 51, neutral (geomean 1.013). Wide
     set, -f, 600 s, 1 rep: 177 solved in both, 16 instances better and
     10 worse, 6 families better and 1 worse; 12 open objectives better
     and 2 worse (unit-commitment L12 3.33M to 1.49M, gbac UD3 11949 to
     6771). CdclFuzzTest covers both settings with up to 24 assumptions.

192. Constraints that hold for every value left mark themselves entailed,
     so later changes of their variables no longer wake them: XlteqY,
     XplusClteqZ, XplusYlteqZ, XneqY, Min, Max, MaxSimple,
     ElementVariableFast, Not, SumBool with !=, and CountValues /
     CountValuesBounds. Before, they kept being called after they held
     (1.1 million calls in 30 s on word-equations, 2.8 million on accap
     with -f). Search heuristics that count active constraints, such as
     most_constrained, can choose differently.

193. Values (MiniZinc nvalue) bounds the number of values from below by a
     clique of pairwise different variables. At the root it reads the
     pairs of its variables that the other constraints force to differ
     (x != y, alldifferent, x = y + t or |x - y| = t with 0 not in t,
     x = y + c with c != 0, and linear constraints of those forms) and
     finds a large clique K of that graph (greedy, then Tomita and Seki's
     branch and bound with a node limit), so count >= |K|. This is the
     independent-set lower bound of AtMostNValue (Bessiere, Hebrard,
     Hnich, Kiziltan and Walsh, Constraints 11(4), 2006) with the
     difference constraints added, as in Fages and Lapegue (CP 2013;
     Artificial Intelligence 212, 2014), whose filtering is applied too:
     when count <= |K|, a variable outside K takes a value of a clique
     variable it is not linked to. Under --lcg both have explanations.
     On the XCSP Rlfap instances that minimise the number of frequencies
     the clique equals the optimum on graph-01, graph-02, graph-09 and
     graph-14; at 600 s, with the models' search and with -f, JaCoP now
     proves graph-01, graph-02, graph-09 and scen-02 optimal in 6-430 s
     (base: none of them), and the lower bound on the other five rises
     from 5-8 to 8-20. Instances where the variables of nvalue have no
     difference constraints between them are unchanged.
     -Djacop.values.cliques=false turns it off.

194. JaLP presolves the first solve of a problem and starts the whole
     problem from the postsolved basis, the reductions and basis
     postsolve of E. D. Andersen and K. D. Andersen, Presolving in linear
     programming, Mathematical Programming 71 (1995) 221-245: empty,
     singleton and redundant rows (and row bounds the column bounds
     imply), fixed, empty and dominated columns (dual fixing), implied
     free column singletons and doubleton equations. The reduced problem
     is solved with the same perturbed costs; the postsolve maps its
     basis back, computing the duals of the removed rows (section 4),
     and the dual simplex finishes on the whole problem, so duals,
     Farkas rays, inverse rows and the search are those of the whole
     problem and correctness never depends on the presolve. A chunked
     root solve (the linear relaxation calls the simplex 50 pivots at a
     time) keeps the reduced solve going across calls. Also fixed: in
     exact mode, a pivot limit during the removal of the perturbation
     put it back at the next solve, which could cycle.
     -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolve=false turns it off. LP bench (659 LPs,
     120 s): 0 wrong, 638 to 644 solved, pivots geomean 0.68 (jacop-root
     0.44, miplib2017 0.82), solve time 1.15x faster (shifted geomean,
     191 LPs of 0.5 s or more). Wide set: 81 of 220 instances get a
     reduced root LP; at 600 s on those, solved 57 to 57 by default and
     58 to 58 with -f, and the instances that differ also differ between
     two runs of the same build, except unit-commitment L12-T48G15L10 by
     default, where the different optimal root vertex leads the search
     to a poor first solution (with the cost perturbation at 2e-6 or
     5e-7 it does as well as without presolve).

195. New constraints ExactCover and TableCover for exact cover problems,
     with the DL+ rule of Chabert and Solnon: when every option left for
     an element a that must be covered also covers an element b, the
     options that cover b but not a are removed. ExactCover(x, sets, n)
     selects options x[o] = 1 covering the elements sets[o] so that every
     element 0..n-1 is covered once; it keeps the number of options of
     every element and the selected option, restored on backtracking as
     the dancing links of Algorithm X, so it also does what the
     equalities sum(x[o] : e in sets[o]) = 1 do. TableCover(groups,
     tuples, exact) is a tiling written as tables over groups of
     variables (the cells of a piece and its placements) under an all
     different: every tuple covers its group and its values, the values
     at most once, or exactly once when exact. Under --lcg both explain
     what they propagate by the options or tuples out of the domains
     (a removal by DL+ at element a: the options of a that share no
     element with it). The FlatZinc front end posts a redundant
     TableCover when the variables of an all different are split into
     the variable lists of tables (XCSP3 Pentominoes), by default
     without LCG only: on the 14 XCSP3 Pentominoes at 240 s it takes
     --lcg off from 6 to 14 solved (13 in about a second), and -p 4,
     whose third thread runs --lcg off, from 143 s to 25 s in total;
     under LCG its reasons cost more than its pruning saves on half of
     them. -Dorg.jacop.fz.tableCover=true|false sets it in both modes.
     Redundant ExactCover on the equalities x_1 + ... + x_k = 1 over 0/1
     variables is opt-in, -Dorg.jacop.fz.exactCover=true: it changed
     nothing on the instances it reaches. References: M. Chabert and
     C. Solnon, A Global Constraint for the Exact Cover Problem:
     Application to Conceptual Clustering, Journal of Artificial
     Intelligence Research 67 (2020) 509-547; D. E. Knuth, Dancing
     Links, in Millennial Perspectives in Computer Science (2000)
     187-214; D. E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, Volume 4B,
     Section 7.2.2.1, Addison-Wesley, 2022.

196. LP cut rounds above the dense tableau cap and finer Gomory
     multipliers. When the dense simplex has no room for a round of
     cuts (m+50 rows by m+50+n columns would pass maxTableau, 4M
     entries), LinearRelaxation moves the root LP to a JaLP simplex
     over the same rows and runs the cut rounds there; it goes back to
     the dense tableau, at the same optimum and without the cuts, when
     they do not raise the bound of the integer objective (mqueens n11:
     cuts reach 0.9999, the bound stays 1). Switch
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.sparseCuts=false, stat lpSparseCuts. The rows of
     the basis inverse that weight the exact slack Gomory and aggregate
     MIR cuts are scaled to integers at 2^36
     (-Dorg.jacop.lp.gomoryScale) instead of 2^20, with 2^20 as the
     fallback on overflow: once the cuts of the first round are rows
     with weights up to 2^24, multipliers rounded at 2^20 are too coarse
     and no MIR divisor is violated in later rounds. The slack Gomory
     MIR now tries the weight of the basic column first, the divisor of
     the textbook Gomory mixed-integer cut
     (-Dorg.jacop.lp.gomoryDivisor). Reference: R. E. Gomory, An
     algorithm for integer solutions to linear programs, in Recent
     Advances in Mathematical Programming, McGraw-Hill (1963) 269-302;
     H. Marchand and L. A. Wolsey, Aggregation and mixed integer
     rounding to solve MIPs, Operations Research 49(3) (2001) 363-371.
     Root bounds on the wide set (16 of 169 objective instances move):
     hoist PU_2_8_1 gap closed 0 to 77 % (the float GMI probe reaches
     77 %), radiation i8-7 59 to 100 %, racp j30_26 13 to 17 %, wmsmc
     38 to 39 %; connect 0086_02 25 to 19 % and unit-commitment L12 33
     to 32 % (another cut path). Checks: optimum kept by DFS and by LCG
     with the explanation checker with the move forced on every model
     (LinearRelaxationTest), and the cuts of both scales valid on random
     models. Benchmarks at 600 s: hoist, radiation, racp, mqueens and
     other moved instances (17), 13 to 13 solved by default and with
     -f; wide set (220), 178 to 177 solved, 3 families better and 5
     worse, and none of the 16 instances that changed makes a cut or
     moves to JaLP (timing noise).

197. LexChain gives its own LCG reasons on more chains. The FlatZinc front
     end shares one variable per constant, so the vectors of
     SocialGolfers (lex_chain over rows that hold the same constants)
     counted as having repeated variables, and LexChain refused every
     reason (the generic reason of all its arguments: 373 atoms on
     SocialGolfers-08-04-10, 94 % of its reasons). Variables fixed at
     the root no longer count as repeated. A value of x[i] that lies
     between the lower chain from x[0] and the upper chain from x[m-1]
     on neither side alone, but is ruled out by both together, now gets
     a two-sided reason, and a failure where the two chains cross is
     explained by the window of vectors around the crossing. Reasons on
     SocialGolfers-08-04-10 go from 373 to about 7 atoms, generic 94 %
     to 0 %. Reference: M. Carlsson and N. Beldiceanu, Arc-consistency
     for a chain of lexicographic ordering constraints, SICS Technical
     Report T2002-18 (2002). Checks: AtomicTrailTest (repeated constant,
     both sides), LcgFuzzTest.lexChainReasonsAgreeWithPairwiseSearch
     (LexChain against pairwise LexOrder with the explanation checker on).

198. The FlatZinc front end restricts the result of int_times to the
     products of its factors' values when there are at most 4096 value
     pairs (-Dorg.jacop.fz.productDomains=false turns it off). Molnar
     (XCSP3 2022) multiplies squares into triple products in 64..262144;
     with interval domains every product variable had 1,000-3,000 bound
     literals under --lcg and each conflict ran about 2,300 SAT
     propagations along those chains. The same search needs 2,198
     instead of 20,380 literals; Molnar-3-8 is proved in 28 s instead
     of 70 s and Molnar-4-4 in 26 s instead of 52 s. CP-SAT needs as
     many conflicts there; the gap is the cost per conflict.

199. The default search under --lcg branches first, with dom/wdeg and
     the smallest value, on the index variables of variable element
     constraints when every search variable is an index or a list
     variable of one (stat lcgElementPositions). LangfordBin (XCSP3
     2025) places numbers with element constraints from positions p to
     cells v; dom/wdeg on the cells bumped every list variable on each
     element failure and never found a solution, while on the positions
     LangfordBin-039 to 240 are solved in 0.6 to 9 s (none of these in
     240 s before) and 010 is proved infeasible in 7 s instead of 16 s.

200. The MiniZinc Challenge image (jacop-flatzinc/src/main/minizinc/
     Dockerfile) trains the JVM's ahead-of-time cache (Java 25) on the
     aot-training models with the image's own JVM and the classpath of
     fzn-exec, and fzn-exec starts from it. On the 33 instances of the
     2026 Challenge that JaCoP finishes in under 5 s the median run takes
     0.27 s instead of 0.47 s; on a loaded machine (four runs on four
     cores) zephyrus 14__6__10__3 took 2.2 s instead of 2.9-3.4 s. The
     Challenge compares times in whole seconds.

201. The linear relaxation makes generalized subtour elimination cuts of
     the forests of Tree and Path (MiniZinc tree, dtree, path, dpath) at
     the root, for an objective: for a set S of nodes and a node k of S,
     the selected edges inside S are at most the selected nodes of S
     other than k (Lucena and Resende, Strong lower bounds for the prize
     collecting Steiner problem in graphs, Discrete Applied Mathematics
     141, 2004; Padberg and Wolsey, Trees and cuts, Annals of Discrete
     Mathematics 17, 1983). A most violated cut for each k is a maximum
     weight closure found with one maximum flow (Picard, Management
     Science 22, 1976). A model with trees also takes all its other rows
     into the relaxation and keeps them in search, and under -f the
     solution of the relaxation guides the annotation's runs
     (lcg-lp-branching=auto). On the 2026 Challenge's surface-based-tsp
     the root bound is now the optimum on all five instances (st70: 490
     before, 675; ch130: 3,744 before, 6,112). At 240 s, two runs each,
     -f proves st70 in 1.5-1.8 s (before: not in 240 s) and berlin52 in
     1.6-2.4 s (15 s); fixed search proves st70 in 22-23 s (not in 240 s)
     and berlin52 in 6-8 s (13 s). -Dorg.jacop.lp.treeCuts=false turns
     the cuts off; org.jacop.lp.treeRounds (200) bounds their rounds.

202. New global UnaryEarlinessTardiness bounds a sum of earliness and
     tardiness costs of tasks on unary machines by the assignment
     bound of Sourd and Kedad-Sidhoum: each task is split into unit
     operations assigned to time slots of capacity one, with unit costs
     whose sum never exceeds the task's cost, and the minimum cost
     transportation (successive shortest paths over at most 64 blocks
     of slots) gives the bound. It filters start bounds by reduced
     costs of the dual, relaxes given precedences s_j >= s_i + d in
     the Lagrangian way with multipliers from a subgradient search at
     the root, and explains its failures and prunings under --lcg by
     windows weakened against the stored dual. Exact solves are
     screened (old flow still optimal, dual under the current windows,
     greedy repair) and capped by a deterministic work budget. The
     FlatZinc front end adds it next to PrecedenceCost on unary
     resources when -Dorg.jacop.fz.unaryEarlinessTardiness=true; it is
     off by default, since it raises proven bounds but not the
     solutions found in a time limit. On 36 one-machine and job-shop
     earliness/tardiness instances (240 s) the mean gap between bound
     and best solution goes from 84 % to 39 % on the one-machine
     family under the model search and from 60 % to 28 % with -f; on
     job shops with costs on every operation from 100 % to 88 % under
     the model search. On the rcpsp-wet and rcpsp-wet-diverse Challenge
     instances (600 s) it proves j30_27_5-wet-diverse-4 in 152 s (not
     proved in 600 s without it) and lowers the upper bound of
     j30_44_8-wet-diverse-4 under -f from 19020 to 17632. References: F. Sourd and S. Kedad-Sidhoum, The
     one-machine problem with earliness and tardiness penalties,
     Journal of Scheduling 6(6), 533-549, 2003; F. Sourd, New exact
     algorithms for one-machine earliness-tardiness scheduling,
     INFORMS Journal on Computing 21(1), 167-175, 2009; M. L. Fisher,
     The Lagrangian relaxation method for solving integer programming
     problems, Management Science 27(1), 1-18, 1981; M. Held, P. Wolfe
     and H. P. Crowder, Validation of subgradient optimization,
     Mathematical Programming 6, 62-88, 1974. Checks:
     UnaryEarlinessTardinessTest (bound against brute force, with and
     without precedences; filtering keeps every supported start),
     GlobalConstraintBruteForceTest, UnaryEarlinessTardinessLcgTest and
     LcgFuzzTest (explanation checker on), PrecedenceCostFuzzTest.

203. Presolve tabling (--presolve-tabling) no longer runs out of memory
     on groups over wide domains. Its enumeration listed every value of
     the variable it branched on into an array before the node limit
     could stop it; with auxiliary variables of about 10^9 values this
     took gigabytes per level (work-task-variation, 2025 MiniZinc
     Challenge: out of memory at 3 GB within 2 s). It now gives up on a
     group as soon as a domain is larger than the nodes left; such a
     group reached its node limit before too, so it gets no table either
     way.

204. Local search keeps more of its state incrementally, as Comet's
     invariants do (L. Michel and P. Van Hentenryck, A constraint-based
     architecture for local search, OOPSLA 2002, 83-100; P. Van
     Hentenryck and L. Michel, Constraint-Based Local Search, MIT Press
     2005). A trial move that changes many variables (a swap, or a
     variable with the variables it defines) updates sums, counters and
     windows as it goes but measures each touched constraint once at its
     end, and undoes by restoring the sums of the touched rows at once.
     Element constraints (new SelectingViolation, implemented by
     ElementVariableFast) read a list variable only while their index
     selects it, as the element invariant of Comet; domain rows of defined
     variables are measured directly. A list variable that only element
     constraints list[index] = y read (the pair matrix of the social
     golfers model) is not moved by the search: after every move it takes
     the value of y asked for with the most weight by the element
     constraints selecting it, a functional invariant (CBLS, ch. 5), so a
     golfer move needs no second move of a matrix cell (--free-config
     ls-follow=0 turns this off; statistic followers in the
     LocalSearch line). -Djacop.ls.verify=true checks the incremental
     state against a fresh computation after every step. 54 CBLS
     instances, local search only, 600 s: 28 to 30 solved (golfers 5_3_6
     in 1.4 s, none before; costas 17 in 203 s; mqueens n20; one wwtpp
     lost), costas 16 in 4 s instead of 18 s, better objectives on gfd,
     largescheduling, liner and train. Wide set, -f with ls=0.1, 600 s:
     178 to 178 solved, mean gap 0.074 to 0.075; a repeat of the 11
     instances that differed shows 8 as run-to-run noise. Worse in both
     runs: triangular n10 is proved in 270 s instead of 50 s (local search
     finds the optimum 20 later, at 26 s instead of 9 s, and the
     core-guided phase then lowers the bound one value at a time),
     ihtc kletzander i09 (7810 instead of 6530 to 6730; local search
     gives only the first solution, LNS does the rest) and trains18 (387
     instead of 386).

205. Free search (-f) under --lcg leaves every decision to the SAT
     solver when the model is a CNF: all its variables are 0/1 and every
     constraint became clauses (Regular and MDD under --lcg, change
     #447). Before, the runs took turns between the annotation (or
     dom/wdeg), the SAT solver's literals and activity branching, which
     on such a model are only slower SAT decisions. On nonograms
     (regular over 1..2 cells, stat lcgPureClauses) at 600 s with two
     seeds: MiniZinc Challenge 2026 hard_03 is solved in 53-56 s (388 s
     and unsolved before), hard_02 in 6-23 s (34-39 s), 2013 dom_14 in
     4-22 s (79-129 s); of 29 nonogram instances 19 runs are faster, 1
     slower, the rest equal. Not for wider domains: pentominoes, whose
     regular constraints become clauses too, lost up to 4x with it.
     -Djacop.lcg.pureClauseLiterals=false turns it off.
     
206. Bug fix: when LCG free search gives way to free search without
     learning (lcg-slow-rate), the regular and MDD constraints that went
     to the SAT solver as clauses are imposed in the store again. Before,
     free search without learning ran without them, printed solutions
     that violate them and could claim a wrong optimum: on XCSP3 2025
     FAPP-aux-18-0350 with large tables posted as MDD, every solution
     after the switch broke about 140 of its 1,425 tables.

207. Opt-in TSP mode of the FlatZinc front end: with
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.tsp=true, a pure travelling salesman model, one
     circuit(x) with no fixed successor and a tour length
     sum(i)(d[i, x[i]]), gets the arc Booleans, cost rows and Routes of
     the giant-tour detection (item 177) with its
     first node as the depot. The linear relaxation becomes the subtour
     elimination relaxation (Dantzig, Fulkerson and Johnson, Operations
     Research 2, 1954) with subtour cuts and new blossom and comb cuts
     (CombCuts: Padberg and Rao, Odd minimum cut-sets and b-matchings,
     Mathematics of Operations Research 7, 1982; Groetschel and Padberg,
     On the symmetric travelling salesman problem, Mathematical
     Programming 16, 1979; separation as in Letchford, Reinelt and
     Theis, Mathematical Programming 116, 2008). Use it for a pure TSP;
     it is off by default because only such models gain. 42 TSPLIB
     instances (14 to 152 cities, osolpro benchmarks/tsp), 600 s:
     proved 20 -> 23 with the model's search and 24 -> 30 with -f
     (kroA100, gr120, pr152, rat99, eil101, st70; time geomean of the
     common proofs 0.93 and 0.67); the bound gap to the optimum on the
     25 instances of 70 cities or more falls from a median of 3.4 % to
     0 % (max 11.9 % to 0.8 %), and from 0.7 % to 0 % with -f. Blossom
     and comb cuts are separated at the root, and by default only for
     Routes without a capacity: on 59 CVRP instances they were level
     (osolpro #322). -Dorg.jacop.lp.combCuts=true|false forces them on
     or off, -Dorg.jacop.lp.combSearch=true also separates them in
     search, -Dorg.jacop.fz.tspArcs (default 40000) limits the arcs.

208. JaLP's presolve also removes parallel rows and merges parallel
     columns (E. D. Andersen and K. D. Andersen, Presolving in linear
     programming, Mathematical Programming 71 (1995) 221-245, sections
     3.4 and 3.6). A row that is a multiple of another gives its bounds
     to the kept row; columns that are multiples of each other in every
     row and in the cost become one column with the summed bounds. The
     basis postsolve gives a removed row its dual and a removed column a
     value at a bound; where the merged bounds of rows or columns meet,
     the sign of the dual or of the reduced cost tells which original
     bound holds. The postsolved basis keeps the steepest-edge weights
     of the reduced solve for the columns that stay basic (neos2 stalled
     in degenerate pivots after the clean-up of the cost perturbation
     with unit weights). Also fixed: a row skipped by the ratio test and
     still violated at the end stopped the solve at its pivot limit on an
     unbounded problem; now it does only when its proof holds on fresh
     factors. -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveParallel=false turns the parallel
     reductions off. LP bench (659 LPs, 120 s, against the presolve
     without them): 0 wrong, 640 solved in both, pivots geomean 0.98
     (jacop-root 0.97, miplib2017 0.96; neos-3555904-turama 8,824 to
     2,680, mzzv42z 25,294 to 7,088, but dano3mip 57,825 to 103,410);
     solve time about the same. Wide set: the parallel rules reach the
     root LP of 72 of 240 instances; at 600 s on those, solved 46 to 46,
     5 families better and 2 worse. Reproducible in three runs:
     ATSP instance10_0p25 proved in 16 s instead of 60 s, accap
     instance5 in 73 s instead of 100 s, still-life-wastage 13 in 44 s
     instead of 80 s, stable-goods s-d19 7016 instead of 6636; worse
     unit-commitment L12-T48G15L10, ptv pax_period_20_3b and
     fox-geese-corn 17, which with the cost perturbation at 2e-6 do as
     well as or better than before: the search follows a different
     optimal root vertex.

209. Under --lcg the objective lower-bound search (lcg-lb) starts
     before the first solution: each round assumes the objective at its
     root lower bound, one run per round and without shaving, until a
     solution is found. Before, those runs waited for a first solution.
     A run that neither raises the bound nor finds a solution halves the
     share of the next run until the bound goes up again, so the search
     for a first solution keeps its time. XCSP3 ButtonsScissors-00 and
     -02, where every first solution lies near the optimum (0.2 s with
     the objective at most 9, the optimum, no solution in 15 s with at
     most 13), are proved optimal in 10-60 s; neither had a solution in
     600 s (00 in one of two runs).

210. The default search under --lcg, for a model without a search
     annotation, branches in input order with the smallest value instead
     of dom/wdeg when it has more than 50,000 search variables
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.lcgDomWdegLimit, stat lcgInputOrder): dom/wdeg
     scans every variable at each decision, about 1 ms a decision on
     the 160,000 cells of XCSP3 AztecDiamond-200. AztecDiamond 200 to
     300, Takuzu 040 and 050, WordDesign1 065 and NonTransitiveDice
     30-30-4 now have a solution in 12 to 75 s (none in 600 s before,
     AztecDiamond-300 in 527 s); RamseyPartition-equ-3-0600 takes 200 s
     instead of 57 s to prove unsatisfiable.

211. Cumulative with constant durations and resource uses (the time table
     of CumulativeBasic, Cumulative and CumulativeUnary) sweeps as the
     scalable sweep of Letort, Beldiceanu and Carlsson ("A Scalable Sweep
     Algorithm for the cumulative Constraint", CP 2012): at each change of
     the profile it touches only the tasks whose resource use crosses the
     room left under the limit, instead of every task under pruning. Its
     explanations under --lcg read the load of the compulsory parts at a
     time point from a profile built once per explanation, O(log n)
     instead of a sum over all tasks. Pruning and reasons are unchanged.
     On largescheduling instance-1600-2 (1600 tasks) the search makes
     2.1x the nodes in 60 s: the old explanations stalled it for most of
     the run after 8 solutions. At 600 s, over the 71 bench instances
     with a cumulative or disjunctive constraint (34 of the wide set and
     a topic set with 100+ tasks), the same 32 are solved; without -f
     the objective is better on 19 and worse on 2, with -f better on 16
     and worse on 12; the 10 largescheduling instances run 1.3x to 9.9x
     the nodes per second.

212. Bug fix: a search with a child search (as the FlatZinc front end
     builds for integer then Boolean variables) that timed out while the
     child was enumerating solutions ended with the child's part of the
     last solution and the master's part of an earlier one. Assigning that
     pair at the end of labeling threw FailException, so Fz2jacop -a -t
     printed =====UNSATISFIABLE===== after the solutions instead of
     =====TIME-OUT===== (seen on XCSP 2023 KidneyExchange-4-041_c23 with
     --default-search input, in about half the runs). The master now
     records its part before it stops.

213. The local-search thread (ls) of the parallel portfolio moves up from
     seventh to second place among the extra threads, ahead of default and
     free-chs-luby, so -p 3 and -p 4 run it in place of free-chs-luby
     (-p 4: the options given, lcg-lits, ls, default). The thread runs
     local search for up to half the time while it improves within 5 s,
     then CP free search from its solution. With -f -p 4 at 600 s on 4
     cores, against develop-5.0 324b78d21 in the same round: the wide set
     (220) is neutral (185 to 186 solved, 15 better / 15 worse), job shops
     9 of 17 solved instead of 7 (la38, ta71 proved; ta51 126 to 4 s), and
     the local-search set (54) gives cutstock type04 a solution and better
     objectives to cutstock type10 and largescheduling; on the wide set
     cargo challenge16 152917 to 125910 and ihtc-2024 marte i01 5159 to
     4388. -p 4 without -f is neutral (wide-quick, 240 s, 53 to 54 solved).
     Sequential -f keeps local search off: it adds 1 to 3 s to every easy
     instance (wide-quick 1 better / 18 worse at 240 s). Data in osolpro
     benchmarks/ls-default.

214. Activity branching under --lcg bumps every integer variable whose
     atoms conflict analysis resolved or minimised away, as MiniSat bumps
     every variable the analysis meets (N. Een, N. Sorensson, An
     Extensible SAT-solver, SAT 2003), not only the variables of the
     learnt clause; each variable once per conflict. On the 220 instances
     of the wide set at 600 s: fixed search 179 solved instead of 178, 22
     instances better and 9 worse, no family worse (2019 liner-sf-
     repositioning tp7_4 proved optimal, celar graph11 309218 -> 239859);
     -f 178 solved in both, 19 better and 14 worse, 7 families better and
     3 worse. -Djacop.lcg.bump=learnt restores the old bumping. Reason-
     side bumping (J. H. Liang, V. Ganesh, P. Poupart, K. Czarnecki,
     Learning Rate Based Branching Heuristic for SAT Solvers, SAT 2016)
     was tried and lost on top of it.

215. Hall cuts of item 188 with value copies, and in search. The
     Williams-Yan cuts now also come from all-different constraints with
     exceptions (alldifferent_except_0, AlldifferentExcept) and from
     global cardinality constraints (GCC, CountValues,
     CountValuesBounds): for the k variables of a prefix S of the
     sorted LP values, each counted value v enters the greedy sum of the
     k smallest (or largest) values at most min(u_v, |S holding v|)
     times and at least max(0, l_v - |variables outside S holding v|)
     times, and an excepted value any number of times: the extremal
     points of the multiset permutahedron, after R. Rado, An
     inequality, Journal of the London Mathematical Society 27 (1952)
     1-6, with H. P. Williams and H. Yan (2001) and J. Lee (2002) as in
     item 188. An all-different whose variables are not all LP columns
     now keeps its cuts over the columns among them (two or more);
     before, it was left out. -Dorg.jacop.lp.alldiffVariants=false turns
     the copies off. Hall cuts are also separated in search, as the
     capacity cuts of item 187 (every 4 to 1024 optimal solves, up to
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.alldiffSearchCuts=10 cuts into the row pool), with
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.alldiffSearch=true; they are off by default, as at
     240 s they cost lot-sizing pigment20a and cable-tree-wiring A022
     their optimum. Statistics lpAlldiffLists, lpAlldiffVariantLists,
     lpAlldiffSearchCuts and lpAlldiffSeparations. Checks
     (AlldifferentRowsTest): no cut with copies cuts off a solution of
     random domains or a convex mix of them; random models with an
     all-different-except-0 or a GCC keep their optimum, also under LCG
     with the explanation checker (LinearRelaxationTest), and also after
     the rows of the other constraints are dropped in search. Reach (429
     Challenge optimisation instances with these constraints, 20 s): 243
     have Hall lists, 99 of them with copies. The root bound of
     physician-scheduling rises from 570 to 670, 1145 to 1245 and 570 to
     620 (twice), and of vaccine v11 from 119 to 118 and v857 from 203 to
     202 (maximisation); the 21 instances whose root changes, at 600 s:
     default 12 to 12 solved, one better objective (physician
     instance18_real 7750 to 6915), -f 12 to 12 with differences of
     search timing only; no wrong answer.

216. New constraint ColouredSpanningTreeBound (org.jacop.constraints.graph)
     for spanning trees with limits on the number of edges taken from
     disjoint edge sets (colours). Such trees are the common bases of a
     graphic and a partition matroid (Edmonds 1970). At every node it
     checks that a common base exists with Cunningham's augmenting paths
     (SIAM J. Comput. 1986), bounds the cost from below with the
     Lagrangian relaxation of the colour limits (Held and Karp 1970),
     which is exact since the matroid intersection polytope is integral
     (see also Gabow and Tarjan 1984 for one colour), and removes edges
     by reduced costs on the Lagrangian tree (Sellmann CP 2004, Regin et
     al. CPAIOR 2010). The FlatZinc front end posts it as an implied
     constraint for weighted_spanning_tree (jacop_wst) with sums of edge
     variables with all coefficients 1 that restrict the tree
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.colouredTree=false turns it off). On 30 generated
     colour-limited and rainbow spanning tree instances with 30 to 150
     nodes (osolpro benchmarks/matroid-intersection) JaCoP proves all 30
     instead of 15 in 600 s, e.g. rb_100_s1 optimum 758 in 26 s (best
     774 before) and cc_60_12_s1 in 19 s instead of 495 s. No MiniZinc
     Challenge or wide-set model combines weighted_spanning_tree with
     such limits, so the default benchmarks are not affected.

217. ColouredSpanningTreeBound and SpanningTreeBound give their own LCG
     reasons, and ColouredSpanningTreeBound a solution hint. The reasons
     follow the cycle and cut optimality conditions of minimum spanning
     trees (Tarjan, Sensitivity analysis of minimum spanning trees, Inf.
     Process. Lett. 14, 1982): a lower bound on the cost cites only the
     excluded edges cheaper than an edge of their tree path and the
     selected edges that a cheaper edge could replace; the removal or
     selection of an edge adds the edges of its tree path or of its
     fundamental cut and the maximum of the cost. A coloured tree rebuilds
     its bound with the multipliers of its last calls; the absence of a
     common base keeps the generic reason. The hint is the Lagrangian
     tree made a common base and improved by edge swaps, given when its
     weight meets the lower bound of the cost (so it is optimal); the
     FlatZinc front end tries the values of such hints first when the
     model has no search annotation and puts them in the saved phases
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.constraintHints=false turns it off). On rb_100_s2
     under --lcg the generic share of reasons drops from 0.48 to 0.06 and
     the average reason from 128 to 41 atoms. On the 39 instances of
     osolpro benchmarks/matroid-intersection at 600 s the total time
     drops from 1121 s to 506 s (all proved before and after), e.g.
     ccd_100_20_s3 34 s instead of 486 s, cc_150_30_s2 2.4 s instead of
     52 s, and five instances 1-1.5 s instead of 19-20 s.

218. Values (MiniZinc nvalue) reads its difference graph once more at its
     first call below the root, from the root constraints and domains.
     Before, it read the graph only at root calls, so a difference
     constraint imposed after its last root call was missed when nothing
     woke it again at the root, for example with a count fixed in its
     domain; the clique bound and the filtering of Fages and Lapegue
     (CP 2013; Artificial Intelligence 212, 2014) then did nothing. The
     filtering also computes the union of clique domains once for all
     variables linked to the same clique variables: 6 to 28 unions per
     call instead of 180 to 900 on the XCSP Rlfap instances, with the
     same pruning.

219. With lazy clause generation (--lcg), -Djacop.lcg.clauses=Class,...
     gives small constraints of the named classes (or all) to the SAT
     solver as clauses in place of propagators; default none. The
     clauses come from the constraints' pseudo-Boolean encodings:
     clauses as they are, linear relations by the order encoding
     (Tamura, Taga, Kitagawa and Banbara, Compiling finite linear CSP
     into SAT, Constraints 14(2), 2009) with the direct encoding of
     two-variable equalities, and 0/1 sums by a unary counter (Sinz,
     Towards an optimal CNF encoding of Boolean cardinality constraints,
     CP 2005). A constraint becomes clauses only within
     jacop.lcg.clauses.limit (256 clauses), .dom (64 values) and
     .counter, and constraints over the objective's definition stay
     propagators, since the core-guided phase reads them. On the wide
     benchmark set at 240 s the links (FzXeqC, FzXneqC, Reified,
     Implies, Not, XeqC-like, ChannelReif, ChannelImply, In) and small
     Boolean constraints (AndBoolSimple, OrBoolSimple, XorBool, SumBool,
     LinearBool) raise conflicts per second by 16-27 % but change the
     solved counts little, so the option stays off.

220. jacop-prob: ChanceSum(x, w, c, alphaNum, alphaDen) states
     Pr(sum(i) w[i] * x[i] <= c) >= alphaNum / alphaDen for independent
     random weights w[i] (Dist), x[i] >= 0 and a capacity variable c.
     It never lists the joint scenarios: a convolution over the loads
     0..max(c) of the items at their minimum gives the probability as
     an interval of outward-rounded doubles, and a comparison with
     alpha that the interval leaves open is decided on BigInteger
     numerators. It fails, lowers each max(x[j]) to the largest value
     that keeps the probability (domain consistent for 0/1 x), raises
     min(c) to the alpha-quantile, explains its pruning for lazy
     clause generation (lower bounds of x, largest expected load
     first, and max(c)), and gives the linear relaxation rows from the
     Cantelli inequality (mean - c <= k * sigma, k = sqrt((1 - alpha) /
     alpha), with tangents of the square root). jacop_prob.mzn has
     chance_sum(x, value, prob, den, c, alpha). On the ccki family of
     osolpro benchmarks/prob: the 8 small instances match brute force
     (under 0.6 s); of the 6 large ones, whose 3^30 to 5^100 joint
     scenarios no expansion can write, n30 are proved in 8 s and n60,
     n100 have solutions at 240 s (the rows cut the n60 bound from 1202
     to 976 and the n30 proofs from 15-24 s to 8 s).

221. LateCount uses release dates. For each earliest start t as origin,
     every task may do up to t - est of its processing before t on a
     machine of its own (a relaxation), and Moore and Hodgson bounds the
     late tasks among the rest from t on; the best origin gives the
     bound and the filtering, the earliest start of all the bound of
     before. With new LateCount(s, d, due, late, true) it also enforces
     the dominance rule of Baptiste, Peridy and Pinson (European J.
     Operational Research 144(1), 2003): when p[i] <= p[j], r[i] + p[i]
     <= r[j] + p[j], due[i] - p[i] >= due[j] - p[j] and r[i] + p[i] <=
     due[i], task i is on time whenever task j is. The rule keeps an
     optimum only when nothing else constrains the tasks, so the
     FlatZinc front end turns it on only then: each start occurs only in
     the unary resource and in its lateness test, the tests count with
     one weight, the domains are intervals and leave room for the late
     tasks at the end (-Dorg.jacop.fz.lateCount.dominance=false turns it
     off; -Dorg.jacop.lateCount.releases=false the release bound). On 60
     random 1|r_j|sum U_j instances (n = 40 to 150), 240 s, the optima
     proved go from 49 to 60 with the model's search and from 54 to 60
     with -f, no objective worse. On the 11 instances the base does not
     prove, the release bound alone proves 11 and the dominance alone 7.

222. The LP-guided search decisions (lcg-lp-branching) see the root
     solution of the linear relaxation also when the search that uses
     them is set up after the root propagation, as free search is: the
     relaxation kept its solutions only from then on, and when no solve
     in search is optimal the guide had no solution to follow.
     surface-based-tsp 2026 ch130, whose root solution after the tree
     cuts is the optimum, is proved in 7-12 s under -f instead of being
     unsolved at 600 s; the other 16 instances of the wide set where the
     guide is on keep their proofs and objectives (stable-goods s-d19 -f
     7385 instead of 7485 at 600 s). Fixed search, whose activity runs
     follow the root solution kept from the first solve in search, is
     unchanged.

223. JaLP pivots are about 1.1x faster with the same arithmetic. The full
     passes of ftran and btran over U read the pivot row, position, pivot
     and column of each slot of the order in sequence, with the columns of
     U in one flat array laid out in that order (a Forrest-Tomlin column
     is appended), and the rows of L are indexed by step; the
     factorization no longer allocates per step, and the bound-flipping
     ratio test computes each breakpoint ratio once instead of in every
     pass. On the LP bench (659 LPs, 120 s) every LP has the same pivots,
     objective and factorizations; sgm 1.11x on the 199 LPs of 0.5 s or
     more, 1.15x on those of 5 s or more (map10 1.5x, map16715-04 1.55x,
     i_n13 1.29x).

224. Under lazy clause generation (--lcg), a value excluded by a bound
     is explained by the earliest bound of the variable beyond it,
     instead of the variable's latest bound. Explanations that say
     "x cannot take v" (Count, the counting reasons of GCC, Element,
     linear reasons over holes, ...) cite weaker atoms from earlier
     levels, so learnt clauses are more general: on peacable_queens 8
     under -f, a third of the Count reason atoms could be weakened to
     an earlier one before, none now. The earliest bound is found by
     binary search over the variable's bound entries, whose values
     only tighten. -Djacop.lcg.lift=false keeps the latest bound. On
     the osolpro wide set at 600 s, --lcg: 5 very hard instances
     better (cargo, blocks-world, ihtc kletzander and marte,
     rcpsp-wet-diverse), none worse; --lcg -f, two repetitions: 6 very
     hard instances better (gbac, tdtsp, neighbours, blocks-world,
     cargo, work-task-variation), 1 worse (java-routing trip_8_3).

225. Dichotomic bound search, off by default. --free-config
     lcg-bisect=<gap> makes LCG free search probe the objective: it
     assumes obj <= lb + (best - 1 - lb) / 2 under a conflict limit, and
     a probe that fails on that assumption alone proves obj > target
     (a root clause and the objectiveBound); a probe out of conflicts
     halves the step, a proof doubles it again (at most half the gap).
     Within the gap it runs branch and bound. --free-config
     portfolio-bisect=<gap> gives this search to the second thread of
     -p (without the core-guided phase and without clause sharing:
     its clauses cost jp-encoding its proofs in the other threads),
     in place of the last thread of the default layout. At -p 4, 240 s,
     on 68 MiniZinc Challenge instances with an open bound gap
     (osolpro benchmarks/bound-worker): the gap to the best known
     solution averages 0.139 instead of 0.167 at 60 s, but at 240 s
     the bound is better on 8 and worse on 6 (proved 42 against 44,
     within run-to-run noise); tsptw n20w180.004 is proved only with
     it, while spot5 1401 and community-detection Zakhary.s12.k4 lose
     the bound of the DFS thread it replaces. Neutral on the 43
     objective instances of wide-quick. OR-Tools CP-SAT runs similar
     probes in its objective_shaving workers.

226. The LP relaxation (LinearRelaxation) now has the weight and profit
     rows of Knapsack (MiniZinc jacop_knapsack, and the surrogate
     knapsacks KnapsackDetector posts), as linearised rows tried at the
     root. Before, a model stated with knapsack constraints had no LP
     row of them: on MiniZinc Challenge multi-knapsack the root LP was
     empty or far from the optimum (mknap2-10: 1101197, optimum 624319)
     and is now at the optimum. On the 15 multi-knapsack instances of
     the Challenge (600 s), total time 95 s -> 38 s, and with -f 109 s
     -> 38 s (mknap2-2: 32 s -> 12 s); no other wide-set instance gets
     a different LP. -Dorg.jacop.lp.knapsackRows=false turns it off.
     Lifted cover cuts of the knapsack rows (sequence-independent
     lifting, Gu, Nemhauser and Savelsbergh, J. Comb. Optim. 4, 2000)
     were tried as well and are not included: the MIR cuts already
     reach the same root bounds on the MiniZinc Challenge instances.

227. Feasibility pump for first solutions of free search, off by
     default. --free-config pump=1 (with -f, with or without --lcg)
     runs the pump of M. Fischetti, F. Glover and A. Lodi ("The
     feasibility pump", Mathematical Programming 104, 2005) at the root,
     over its own linear relaxation with the value encodings, and gives
     its last rounding as the hint the first run follows: each round
     rounds every variable to the value its columns [x = v] prefer, then
     the primal simplex of JaLP finds the LP point closest to that
     rounding in L1 distance over the 0/1 columns, leaving out the
     FlatZinc defined variables; a repeated rounding is perturbed as in
     that paper. pump-max=<ms> (default a tenth of the time left, at
     most 30 s) and pump-iterations=<n> (default 200) bound it. The
     pump's relaxation also has the table hulls keyed by a variable of
     up to 64 values (TableCliques.rows(tables, implied, hullDomain);
     the default LP keeps 16), and rows over the value columns for
     CountValuesBounds and for b <-> x in S (Reified/Implies of In)
     (Linearizations.valueRows(..., sets)); without them the option
     Booleans of car sequencing are tied only to the mean of the class
     and no rounding is ever a solution. On 34 MiniZinc benchmark car
     sequencing instances (17 cars, 17 carseq) at 240 s, -f solves 31
     with the pump against 10 without (with --lcg off, 30 against 4);
     SCIP finds these solutions with its feasibility pump at the root.
     On the wide set it is not a default: 186 -> 185 solved at 240 s,
     10 better and 32 worse, the pump's time spent on optimisation
     models whose first solution is easy (osolpro
     benchmarks/feasibility-pump).

228. Bug fix: the MiniZinc predicate channel(x, b) of JaCoP's library
     paired b with the values of dom(x) instead of with the indices of b,
     although it is documented as x = i <-> b[i]. With dom(x) = 2..6 and
     b indexed 1..5, b[1] was true for x = 2; when dom(x) and index_set(b)
     had different sizes (holes in dom(x), or a narrower or wider domain),
     the model was rejected with "Status array size ... has not equal
     size as number of values". channel(x, b) now posts x in index_set(b)
     and channels over index_set(b). Found by the all-solutions suite
     (random variants of its channel model).

229. Free search (-f) that continues after LCG gave way runs its large
     neighbourhoods from LCG's best solution. They waited for free
     search's own first solution, which must improve on LCG's; on
     saeling 2026 j0050_m003 (makespan) it never came and no
     neighbourhood ran: 1350 instead of 1442 at 600 s (CP-SAT free
     reached 1392 in the 2026 Challenge). On the 25 wide-set instances
     where LCG gives way, 600 s, -f: 13.5 vs 12.5 pair points, 6
     families better and 5 worse. -Dorg.jacop.lns.shift=true adds shift
     neighbourhoods (the tasks after a window of time may start
     earlier); off by default, as it loses on the wide set.

230. WeightedCompletion on a resource of capacity above 1 (P|r_j|sum w_j
     C_j) bounds the cost with the parallel inequalities of the prefixes
     of Smith's order (A. S. Schulz, "Scheduling to minimize total
     weighted completion time: performance guarantees of LP-based
     heuristics and lower bounds", IPCO 1996, LNCS 1084), each prefix S
     bounded by integral (e(S) - min(A, B)) dt with A the work of the
     fluid schedule of S and B the work of its tasks' earliest
     processing, so the rows also hold the earliest completions. The
     greedy dual of the rows (as for Queyranne's polytope, Math.
     Programming 58, 1993) makes it a sum over the prefixes, at most
     twice the cost of the fluid bound it replaces, which it never
     goes below. Under --lcg the reasons of the parallel bound lower the
     minima of the free starts towards their root values as far as the
     bound still holds (checked by recomputing it), and a pruned start
     no longer cites its own minimum. On 36 random P|r_j|sum w_j C_j
     instances (n = 20 to 40, 2 to 4 machines) the proven bound is
     higher on 23 at 240 s in both modes; the mean gap goes from 3.4 %
     to 2.9 % (model search) and from 1.3 % to 1.1 % (-f), with the
     same objectives on average. 2017 cargo challenge16 (wide set,
     600 s) improves from 136790 to 125504 (model search) and from
     137787 to 128330 (-f). -Dorg.jacop.weightedCompletion.prefixRows
     and ...weakReasons=false turn the two parts off.

231. The FlatZinc front end posts a domain-consistent Alldistinct (Régin,
     "A filtering algorithm for constraints of difference in CSPs",
     AAAI 1994) instead of the bounds-consistent Alldiff (Puget, "A fast
     algorithm for the bound consistency of alldiff constraints", AAAI
     1998) for an all_different over the size limits (more than
     org.jacop.fz.alldiffDomainSize values or more than 100 dense
     variables) when at least half of its variables are element indexes
     or table variables and it has at most a quarter more values than
     variables. Elements and tables make holes inside the domains, e.g.
     a cost bound cost[i, x[i]] <= z in a bottleneck assignment, which
     bounds consistency does not see. On 72 random bottleneck
     assignments (30 to 250 agents) the optima go from 58 to 72 at 600 s
     in model search and with -f. In the MiniZinc Challenge 2008-2026 the
     rule changes 4 of 1,881 instances (orthorio). Lifting the limits
     altogether, or dropping the condition on the values, lost on the
     Challenge instances they reach (cgt, crosswords, ma-path-finding,
     cable-tree-wiring). -Dorg.jacop.fz.alldiffChannel=false keeps the
     old rule, =loose drops the condition on the values, =all lifts the
     limits.

232. Fixed search (no -f) with --lcg follows the search annotation again,
     as the fixed search (fd) category of the MiniZinc Challenge requires
     ("FD search solvers must follow the search strategy defined in the
     problem"): the solver no longer restarts (--free-config
     fixed-restarts=off is the default, was turns), so no run branches on
     the SAT solver's literals or on activity, and the core-guided phase
     no longer runs for an objective without a first solution
     (fixed-core=0 is the default, was 0.1). Learnt clauses still prune
     the annotation's tree. fixed-restarts=turns|alternate|replay and
     fixed-core=<share> keep the 5.0 behaviour for experiments. The
     propagations statistic under --lcg now counts JaSAT's propagations
     as well as the calls of the store's propagators: a model whose
     constraints became clauses (nonogram) printed only the root's calls.

233. Bug fix: the FlatZinc front end rejected weighted_subcircuits when
     its arguments shared a variable or an equal constant, for example
     tour[1] = tour[2] or two tour costs fixed to 0 ("Constraint of type
     WeightedSubcircuits has parameter list, tour, tourCost and cost that
     contains repeated values."), so a satisfiable model reported an
     error. Repeated variables of tour, tour_cost and cost are now
     replaced by copies constrained to be equal. Found by the
     all-solutions suite (random variants of its weighted_subcircuits
     model).

234. fzn-jacop prints the running objective bound (%%%mzn-stat:
     objectiveBound and objectiveBoundTime, one stat block per better
     bound) only in verbose mode, with -s -v or --search-statistics.
     With -s alone these blocks came out many times during the search
     and hid the solutions; -s now prints only the solutions and the
     final statistics. --gap still stops at the same bound.

235. JaLP (RevisedDualSimplex) gives the right answers in five cases found by
     an exact-arithmetic differential fuzzer (osolpro
     benchmarks/jalp-allsolutions), now also run in the build as
     LpSolverExactTest (dense DualSimplex, JalpSimplex and RevisedDualSimplex
     checked against a rational simplex: status, objective, primal values,
     duals and Farkas rows).
     - It no longer answers UNBOUNDED when only its cost perturbation pulls
       a column without cost to an infinite bound (min x1 with x1 >= 1 and a
       free column in no row was UNBOUNDED).
     - An UNBOUNDED answer no longer leaves the artificial bounds at 1e14
       for the next solve: after a cut that bounded the problem the optimum
       was 4.93 instead of 5. They start again at 1e6 with each new answer.
     - solvePrimal with a limit of a few pivots per call answered LIMIT
       forever with the exact clean-up (setExact), and on an infeasible
       problem, where each call's primal phase 1 undid the dual simplex
       pivots of the call before.
     - solve with a limit of one pivot never ended when a row was left out
       as round-off; leaving a row out no longer counts against the limit.
     Behind JaCoP's relaxation (JalpSimplex) an UNBOUNDED answer counts as a
     limit, solvePrimal is not called, and only LP-only columns can have
     infinite bounds, so JaCoP lost at most pruning; the stand-alone solver
     (LpMain) could give a wrong status or objective.

236. IntVar keeps the size of its domain once counted, until the domain
     changes (the same refresh that keeps its bounds), so getSize() no
     longer walks the intervals of the domain at each call. dom/wdeg
     and the other domain-size heuristics read the size of every
     unfixed variable at every decision: on 2013 celar graph11 with
     -f --lcg off the search runs about 2x more nodes per second. The
     search does not change. Wide set (600 s, paired) on the instances
     where size counting takes at least 1 % of a 60 s profile: same
     solved and objectives (7 model-search, 15 -f instances); celar
     -f proved the optimum in 139 s instead of 152 s in that run.

237. Under --lcg, LateCount explains its pruning itself instead of
     with every atom of its variables. A reason holds the late tasks
     (start > due - p), the tasks that must be on time (start <= due -
     p), the minima of the starts lowered towards their root values by
     a common fraction (1, 1/2, ...) as far as the bound from the same
     origin still implies the atom, and the weakest upper bound of late
     that still fails; the dominance pairs and overlapping fixed
     tasks have reasons of their own. On 1|r_j|sum U_j (osolpro
     benchmarks/scheduling-bounds, 600 s): the 60 instances with 40 to
     150 jobs are proved in a mean 6.8 s instead of 17.6 s (-f: 2.5 s
     instead of 12.4 s, 60 proved instead of 59), and of 24 new ones
     with 200 and 300 jobs 23 are proved instead of 16 (-f: 24 instead
     of 23), with no objective worse. -Dorg.jacop.lateCount.reasons=false
     restores the generic reason.

238. The core-guided phase of --lcg -f also gives its time to branch and
     bound when its lower bound stalls: at three times its rate over
     the second half of the phase's work it would not meet the best
     solution before the end of the run. The rate since the phase's
     start stays high after a fast first rise (often from a far initial
     bound) and kept the phase to the end of the run. The test starts
     after a tenth of the run's work after the phase's start, as some
     phases stall for a while and then prove the optimum
     (work-task-variation 2025 seed-8). On the 25 instances of the 2026
     Challenge (atp-stage2, community-detection) and the wide set where
     it fires, 600 s, -f: 14.6 vs 10.4 pair points, 5 families better
     and none worse; javarouting trip_7_2 proves its optimum in 428 s
     instead of 566 s, community-detection 2026 in 379 s and 260 s
     instead of 408 s and 293 s. -Djacop.lcg.coreStall=0 turns it off.

239. ChanceTable (jacop-prob): p = sum(s, w[s] * [x satisfies scenario s])
     over scenario tables, the one-stage stochastic CSP of Walsh
     ("Stochastic constraint programming", ECAI 2002) with the
     decisions taken before the random variables are seen. Scenario s
     holds when x is a tuple of each of its tables; a table shared by
     scenarios (one constraint for one value of its random variable) is
     stored and checked once. Filtering is the support counting of a
     compact table (Demeulenaere et al., "Compact-Table: efficiently
     filtering table constraints with reversible sparse bit-sets", CP
     2016) weighted by scenario: a value whose supporting scenarios weigh
     less than the lower bound of p is removed, the upper bound of p is
     the weight of the scenarios still alive and its lower bound the
     weight of those whose tables contain every tuple of the domains. It
     explains its pruning for lazy clause generation by the removed
     values that kill the tuples of the heaviest scenarios. FlatZinc:
     chance_table in jacop_prob.mzn.

240. ProbMdd (jacop-prob): p = floor(scale * Pr(T)) for a layered
     decision diagram of decision levels (edges labelled by the values
     of one variable) and chance levels (edges with probability
     numerators), the compiled form of stochastic constraint problems
     and probabilistic programs (Babaki, Guns and De Raedt, "Stochastic
     constraint optimization using propagation on ordered binary
     decision diagrams", StarAI 2017; Latour et al., "Combining
     stochastic constraint optimization and probabilistic programming",
     CP 2017). Two passes in the (+, max) semiring bound p and remove
     the values whose edges carry too little mass; with all decisions
     fixed, Pr(T) is computed exactly on big integers. The passes use
     doubles rounded outward. Lazy clause generation gets the removed
     labels of the other levels and the bound of p. FlatZinc: prob_mdd
     in jacop_prob.mzn.

241. Symmetry breaking in the FlatZinc front end also posts Schreier-Sims
     cuts x_b <= x_j: for each variable b that a generator moves first in
     the lex-leader order, every j in the orbit of b under the generators
     that fix all variables before b (Liberti and Ostrowski, "Stabilizer-
     based symmetry breaking constraints for mathematical programs",
     J. Global Optim. 60, 2014; Salvagnin, "Symmetry breaking inequalities
     from the Schreier-Sims table", CPAIOR 2018, LNCS 10848). The
     lexicographically smallest (or largest) solution of each orbit
     satisfies them, so they keep what the lex-leader constraints, lex
     chains and value precedence keep. Cuts already implied by the first
     positions of the posted constraints are left out, which is nearly all
     of them: of the 345 MiniZinc Challenge instances where symmetry
     breaking fires, 29 get any. zephyrus_15_10 is proved in 1.6 s instead
     of not at all in 600 s. -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetry.schreierSims=off turns
     them off; --search-statistics prints symmetrySchreierSimsCuts.

242. LateCount bounds a weighted sum of late tasks (1|r_j|sum w_j U_j)
     with new LateCount(starts, durations, due, weights, late). The lower
     bound of late is the larger of two bounds. The first is the sum of
     the c smallest weights of the undecided tasks, where c is the
     number of late tasks of Moore-Hodgson on the relaxed problem. The
     second is a linear relaxation: each task may be on time for part of
     its processing, within the capacity of every due-date prefix. These
     capacities are nested, so they form a polymatroid and the greedy
     order by weight per unit of processing is optimal (Lawler and
     Moore, "A functional equation and its application to resource
     allocation and sequencing problems", Management Science 16(1),
     1969; Edmonds, "Submodular functions, matroids, and certain
     polyhedra", 1970). Each step of the greedy takes the smallest slack
     of the suffix in a segment tree with lazy addition.
     A task is filtered late or on time without solving the relaxation
     again where the answer cannot change. That is the case when
     Moore-Hodgson already puts the task late and the relaxation gives
     it no on-time processing, or when Moore-Hodgson keeps it on time
     and the relaxation gives it all of its processing. The new bound
     then comes from prefix sums of the smallest weights. Under --lcg,
     the weighted bound explains its pruning like the count.
     The FlatZinc front end now passes the weights of the objective to
     the LateCount it detects. Before, it counted the late tasks.
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.lateCount.weights=false counts them again. Paired
     runs at 600 s on 60 generated weighted instances (osolpro
     benchmarks/scheduling-bounds, lcw), counting against weights:
     - model search: 12 -> 33 optima, mean time 481 -> 284 s, 39
       objectives better and 2 worse;
     - -f: 36 -> 58 optima, mean time 263 -> 27 s, 11 objectives better
       and 1 worse.

243. New constraint SequenceMdd: a relaxed multivalued decision diagram over
     the order in which a circuit visits its nodes, with arrival and
     departure times (in[j] >= out[i] + travel[i][j] on the arc i -> j,
     out[j] >= in[j] + delta[j]) and optionally a sum of arc costs, after
     Cire and van Hoeve, Multivalued Decision Diagrams for Sequencing
     Problems, Operations Research 61(6):1411-1428, 2013. Each call compiles
     the diagram top-down (width 64, nodes with the same visited set merged
     exactly, the costliest or latest merged beyond the width) and bottom-up
     (latest departures, least cost to the end); it removes successor values
     on no path, bounds the arrival and departure times and the cost, and
     the return time to the depot. The FlatZinc front end posts it as a
     redundant constraint on circuit(succ) or circuit(pred) with time chains
     arr[succ[i]] = dep[i] + time[i, succ[i]] (or the predecessor form of
     tsptw) on circuits of up to 32 nodes. MiniZinc Challenge tsptw
     n20w160.001 and n20w140.005 are proved in 7 s under the model's search
     (not proved in 240 s before). -Dorg.jacop.fz.sequenceMdd=false turns
     it off; org.jacop.fz.sequenceMddWidth and org.jacop.fz.sequenceMddNodes
     set the width and the largest circuit.

244. GlobalCardinality (MiniZinc global_cardinality and count with
     ::domain) keeps the value lists of its variables between executions
     and builds again only the lists of the variables that changed (or
     whose level was removed), instead of every list at every execution.
     Pruning and search are unchanged; on the 2026 warehouse instances,
     where the lists took about 60 % of the run, fixed search explores
     1.1x to 4.2x more nodes in 600 s.

245. Large linear relaxations. A relaxation of an optimisation model over
     the size caps of the sparse simplex (25,000 rows or 30,000 columns),
     which was dropped, is kept up to 1,000,000 rows and 250,000 columns:
     it is solved at the root only, its root solves (first solve, trial of
     further rows, cut rounds) share a tenth of the time limit instead of a
     pivot budget of about ten pivots, and it is not solved again in
     search. Under -f its root solution guides the decisions when it is
     large by its rows and has at least 10,000 columns (the guide lost on
     triangular, 36,000-101,000 rows over 408-782 columns). A large
     relaxation whose root bound does not cut the domain of the objective
     is removed after its root solve: woken in search for nothing, it
     halved the conflicts of LCG under -f on cable-tree-wiring A073 (bound
     -198), which then found no solution in 600 s. On the store x
     warehouse MIP form of the 2026 warehouse model (117,000 rows after
     pair cliques; JaLP solves its root LP in 9.7 s, HiGHS 1.15.1 in
     9.3 s), 600 s fixed search: olal41 71,811 -> 47,050, olil4 58,139 ->
     38,354, oly20 705,184 -> 177,745. -Dorg.jacop.lp.large=false keeps
     the old behaviour; see properties.txt.

246. The default search branches on set variables in input order, smallest
     element in first, as JaCoP 4 did: free search (-f, or a model
     without search annotation) picked the set with the largest failure
     count and degree, and the branching that LCG free search alternates
     with left the sets out. gardner_dinner (35 triples of 15 guests, no
     annotation) is solved again in 266 nodes without LCG and 2,242 with
     it; 5.0 found no solution in 30 s. --free-config set-heuristic=afc
     restores the old choice. org.jacop.msc declares --symmetry, so
     minizinc --solver org.jacop --symmetry off model.mzn works.

247. VarIntMap, the map from variables to positions that LexOrder, LexChain,
     LinearBool, GlobalCardinality, Nooverlap, ExtensionalSupportStr and
     the restart nogoods of free search use, hashed a variable by its store
     index. Boolean variables are not in the store's array and all have
     index -1, so every boolean started probing from the same slot and a
     lookup scanned all the booleans of the map. They are now hashed by
     their name. The symmetry breaking LexOrder of still-life-wastage 13
     (1,369 positions over booleans) spent 15-20 % of the search in this
     scan; it now solves in 43 s instead of 54 s with the same nodes.

248. JaSAT (CdclSolver) keeps the decision level, reason, trail position
     and analysis mark of a SAT variable in one 16-byte record instead of
     four arrays, so an assignment writes one cache line and conflict
     analysis reads one line per variable, as Kissat and CaDiCaL do. Same
     search; pure SAT runs (CdclMain) are about 3 % faster.

249. The LCG explanations of table constraints (HybridTable) share one
     array of entry marks per atom trail (AtomicTrail.newEntryMark)
     instead of each keeping its own array as long as the trail. The
     explanations and the search are unchanged; the peak heap of table
     models with many tables drops, for example from 445 MB to 198 MB
     live heap on work-task-variation seed-8 under -f at 60 s.

250. The FlatZinc front end reformulates positive tables exactly when it
     posts them. A free column (the table is the product of the other
     columns and the values of the column) becomes a unary domain, so a
     Cartesian table becomes unary domains only; a column that a single
     other column determines (functional dependency, Cambazard and
     O'Sullivan, Reformulating Positive Table Constraints Using
     Functional Dependencies, CP 2008, LNCS 5202) is split off as a binary
     table, or an equality when the columns are equal; tables over the
     same variables are intersected into one; short tables drop columns
     of stars only or of one value. The parts share at most one variable,
     so propagation is as strong as on the table, and LCG explanations
     name fewer variables. 120 of 392 table instances checked change
     (7 wide-set instances; XCSP AircraftLanding, CarSequencing,
     ChampionsLeague, DC-skinny, FAPP, Nonogram, Ortholatin, Pentominoes,
     SportsScheduling, Subisomorphism, WordSquare). On 55 of them at
     600 s: default 29 -> 30 solved, geomean time 0.88 on the 28 both
     solve (0.998 without CarSequencing, whose single-seed differences
     are seed spread); -f on the 27 optimisation ones 12 -> 13 solved.
     FAPP-aux-20 is proved instead of running out of memory.
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.tableReformulation=false turns it off; see
     properties.txt.

251. New org.jacop.core.TimeStampInt, a TimeStamp specialised for int
     values (int arrays, no Integer boxing), replaces TimeStamp<Integer>
     in all 41 places that used it (AbstractBoolVector, BoolClause,
     AbstractChannel, LexOrder, MinOfSums, SumWeight, Alldistinct,
     AbstractMinMax, the cumulatives, InputOrderSelect, ...). Search is
     unchanged. With --lcg off and fixed search at 240 s, the 20
     instances that use it most are 1.6 % faster in nodes per second
     (road-cons road_11 4-5 %, zephyrus 3-4 %); a base against base
     round on the same instances varies by about 1 %.

252. TimeIndexedBound, a dual bound of the objective from a time-indexed
     linear relaxation of the cumulative and disjunctive resources
     (Pritsker, Watters and Wolfe, "Multiproject scheduling with limited
     resources: a zero-one programming approach", Management Science
     16(1), 1969), with interval-indexed buckets where the horizon is
     long (Queyranne and Schulz, "Polyhedral approaches to machine
     scheduling", TU Berlin report 408, 1994) and aggregated precedence
     rows over cumulative columns (Christofides, Alvarez-Valdes and
     Tamarit, "Project scheduling with resource constraints: a branch
     and bound approach", EJOR 29(3), 1987). It takes the rows of the
     linear relaxation and the bounds at the root, is solved by the
     revised dual simplex, and turns the duals (or a Farkas ray) into an
     exact Lagrangian bound on big integers, so the bound is safe
     whatever the floating-point error. It is solved again when the
     objective limit drops or the root bounds change, within a share of
     the run time and backing off after solves that prove nothing new.
     Lazy clause generation explains the bound by the objective limit
     and the root bounds the solve assumed. Off by default; FlatZinc:
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.timeIndexed=true. On 39 MiniZinc Challenge instances
     with cumulative that the relaxation reaches (600 s, model search)
     the bound rises on 16 and falls on none: cargo challenge07 4 to
     168 (best 328), ihtc-2024 i03 230 to 5338 (best 10660), racp
     j60_29 963 to 978; solved counts do not change.

253. LazyBitSetDomain, for domains of 65,536 values or more, keeps its holes
     as sorted intervals and found the interval of a value by binary search
     on every query. It now tries the interval found last and the next one
     first. A time table sweep probes the starts of each task in increasing
     order, mostly in already removed ranges, so the binary search took 30 %
     of the time of largescheduling instance-1600-2 with free search; that
     instance now explores 1.6-1.8x more nodes in 240 s. Pruning is the same.

254. The domain trail of the variables with more than 64 values
     (LazyBitSetDomain) keeps each entry in the space its kind needs: a
     saved word of the bit set takes 24 bytes instead of 56 and the state
     saved at the first change of a level 48 bytes instead of 56. Entries
     are of variable length in an int and an object stream, both still
     grown in segments without copying. Search is unchanged. The live heap
     at its peak drops on deep searches over wide domains: debruijn_binary
     03_08 3.17 GB -> 0.86 GB (solved in 4.5 s at -Xmx2G; it ran out of
     memory at -Xmx3G before) and 17 % faster, 04_06 806 MB -> 423 MB.
     Elsewhere the speed is unchanged (identical search, 11 wide-set
     instances, 6 runs each: 1.003 against 0.997 between two base runs).

255. LCG free search (--lcg -f) skips the core-guided phase after branch
     and bound when the phase's probe (its first 2 % of the time) stopped
     without a solution of its own and branch and bound found one: the
     rest of the phase stopped the same way after a third of its time
     and only delayed branch and bound. At 600 s on 29 instances, two
     seeds each: fast-food ff58 is proved in 24 s instead of 95 s,
     gt-sort n9 in 57 s instead of 323 s, city-position 5-06 in 48 s
     instead of 322 s, and city-position 7-04 (no proof before) in
     104 s. The probe still runs, so models where cores close the gap
     at once (proteindesign, ptv, spot5, celar, work-task-variation)
     keep their proofs. -Djacop.lcg.coreProbeSkip=false restores the old
     behaviour; see properties.txt.

256. fzn-jacop and the MiniZinc Challenge image start the JVM with
     -XX:+UseTransparentHugePages on Linux: the heap is backed by 2 MB
     pages, which cuts the TLB misses of JaCoP's randomly read clause
     arena, watch lists and domains. The same search takes about 5 % less
     time (32 wide-set instances, 6 paired reps, every rep 0.93-0.96x;
     a copy of the same jar 0.99-1.01x), and wide-quick at 240 s solves
     the same instances 7 % faster in wall time in both modes, with about
     4 % more peak memory. The kernel must have transparent huge pages in
     madvise or always mode; otherwise the JVM runs as before. The option
     exists only in the Linux JVM, so the launcher adds it only there.
     -XX:+UseCompactObjectHeaders was measured again and is not set: no
     gain, and an AOT cache made without it is rejected.

257. Fix: when the time given to symmetry breaking ran out while the
     Schreier-Sims cuts were posted (on by default since 241), the check
     was not caught and the solver stopped with SymmetryBreaking$OutOfTime
     before search, printing no solution. The cuts posted so far are now
     kept, as when MAX_CUTS is reached. Seen with -f on a loaded machine:
     steelmillslab bench_19_6 and skill-allocation 5d_1 (every run),
     cryptanalysis kb128, ptv 20_3b and 20_2b, word-equations 03,
     oocsp_racks, rcpsp-wet-diverse j30_27_5-4 and whirlpool d29 (some
     runs).

258. Inferred redundant cumulatives (on by default;
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.inferredCumulatives=false turns them off), after A. Sidorov, On Inferring Cumulative
     Constraints, CP 2026 (arXiv 2602.15635). At each time point a
     cumulative is a knapsack inequality over the tasks that run then; a
     cover of these inequalities (tasks that cannot all run together) is
     lifted by sequential lifting (M. W. Padberg, A note on zero-one
     programming, Operations Research 23, 1975) to sum(pi_i) <= pi_0 over
     more tasks, which holds at every time point and is posted as
     Cumulative(starts, durations, pi, pi_0), or CumulativeUnary when
     pi_0 = 1. Covers of 2 and 3 tasks and of the longest and shortest
     tasks per resource use are lifted over all fixed-capacity cumulatives
     together and over the pairs of tasks that precedences or disjunctions
     keep apart (the conflict graph of org.jacop.fz.disjunctiveCliques,
     Sidorov, Marijnissen and Demirovic, CP 2025); the five
     with the largest capacity bound sum(d_i * pi_i) / pi_0 that no input
     cumulative dominates are posted, and only when that bound exceeds the
     capacity bound of every input cumulative and clique
     (.useful=false posts them anyway; those mostly add propagation and
     slowed LCG). org.jacop.presolve.InferredCumulatives does the inference
     on its own. On rcpsp 2013 instance 13 one inferred cumulative of 18
     tasks with capacity 3 has capacity bound 78, the optimum: model search
     proves it in 0.6 s instead of 6.8 s, -f in 0.7 s instead of 47 s.
     test-scheduling t30m20r3-20 is proved in 0.7 s (open after 600 s
     without). 31 of 100 cumulative instances get one; on them, at 240 s,
     model search solves 24 instead of 23 and -f 28 as before in 327 s
     instead of 365 s, with no instance lost (also at 600 s). On by
     default after a round with the default jar: of the 240 wide-set
     instances 21 run the inference (at most 1 s, largescheduling) and 4
     get an inferred cumulative; on the 31 instances, model search 24
     solved instead of 23, -f 28 as before in 282 s instead of 354 s.

259. Decomposition bound (off by default, -Dorg.jacop.fz.decomposition=true).
     Before search, the FlatZinc front end looks for blocks of variables
     that only counting (CountValuesBounds) and linear constraints link,
     as in the connected-component detection of Gamrath and Luebbecke
     (SEA 2010): a worker's shift sequence, a person's roster. It bounds
     the objective by the Lagrangian relaxation of the linking rows
     (Geoffrion, Mathematical Programming Study 2, 1974), with column
     generation: a JaLP master over the blocks' solutions found so far,
     with Wentges' smoothing of the duals, and for each block a pricing
     problem, its own constraints with the reduced cost as objective,
     solved by the solver itself as in Ha, Quimper and Rousseau (CP 2015):
     by branch and bound in the store, or, for a block it cannot finish
     there, by lazy clause generation in a copy of the block. Each bound is
     certified in integer arithmetic from duals rounded to multiples of
     1/K, so it holds whatever the simplex's accuracy, and the best one
     bounds the objective at the root. physician-scheduling instance10
     (MiniZinc Challenge 2021): root bound 1245 -> 2980 = the optimum in
     about 7 s; work-task-variation (2025) has no root bound and gets one.
     The statistics are dbBlocks, dbRows, dbIterations, dbColumns,
     dbBound, dbPricingTimeOuts and dbTime.

260. SequenceMdd takes time-dependent travel times: stepTravel[i][j][s] is
     the travel time on the arc i -> j when i is left in step s (time
     out[i] / stepLength), and waiting is allowed, so the arrival is the
     earliest t' + travel(t') over the departures t' still possible
     (an extension of Cire and van Hoeve, Operations Research 61(6),
     2013). The FlatZinc front end also detects
     tours given by an inverse pair (jacop_assignment) whose order is
     proved by a position array, with the travel time read from a table
     over (predecessor, departure step), as in the MiniZinc Challenge
     tdtsp model. On the 15 tdtsp instances of 2015, 2017 and 2026, 600 s:
     the ten proved instances are proved 1.1x-2.9x faster under the
     model's search and 1.1x-10x faster with -f, and the five 20-node
     instances end with higher lower bounds and, where the model names
     the objective, better solutions (2026 inst_20_56_00 22,450 ->
     17,579; -f 16,358 -> 14,962). -Dorg.jacop.fz.sequenceMdd=false turns
     it off.

261. Binpacking (MiniZinc bin_packing_load and its variants) wakes only
     the bins an item left or was fixed in, read from the values the item
     lost at the current level, instead of every bin of the item's previous
     domain, and keeps for each bin the items that may still be in it, so
     that a bin is no longer processed by a scan of all items (the list is
     restored when a level is removed; a bin that more than half of the
     items may still be in is scanned in full). Pruning is unchanged. On the
     2025 ihtc-2024-marte instances, where Binpacking took about half of
     the run, fixed search explores 1.06x to 5.9x more nodes in 600 s.

262. Cuts in search keep their period only while they move the bound.
     The capacity cuts of item 187 and the Hall cuts of item 215 were
     separated more often whenever a violated cut was found, even when
     adding it left the objective of the re-solved LP where it was; now
     the period halves only when the cuts change the LP objective (by more
     than 1e-6 relative) or make the LP infeasible, and doubles (up to
     1024) otherwise. -Dorg.jacop.lp.searchCutGate=false restores the old
     rule. The energy cuts of item 181 can also be separated in search,
     from the current LP values over the bounds of the first solve (so
     they hold for the whole search), under the same period and gate, up
     to 10 cuts per separation, with -Dorg.jacop.lp.energySearch=true;
     off by default. Statistics lpSearchSeparationsMoved,
     lpEnergySearchCuts and lpEnergySeparations. Check
     (LinearRelaxationTest): random scheduling models keep their optimum
     with energy cuts in search, also under LCG with the explanation
     checker. Measured (base and candidate in the same round, 2 jobs
     each): in default mode search cuts fire on 5 of the 240 wide
     instances, all routing; on these and 8 vrp/vrplc instances at 600 s,
     separations fall by about half, solved 4 to 4 by default and with
     -f, objectives 2 better and 2 worse by default and 4 better and none
     worse with -f, dual bounds equal by default and 2 higher and 2 lower
     with -f. Wide-quick at 240 s: 44 to 44 solved,
     2 families better, none worse. With Hall and energy cuts in search
     on, 97 topic instances at 240 s: 30 to 30 solved (default), 44 to 47
     (-f), and cable-tree-wiring A022 ends as without them (the same
     objective by default, optimal in 112 s instead of 100 s with -f).
     Hall and energy cuts in search stay off by default: they leave the
     dual bounds where they were.

263. JaLP no longer cycles on LPs with large costs or huge artificial
     bounds. A reduced cost past the dual tolerance (1e-9) but within
     1e-14 of the sizes of its terms is taken as zero: with costs near
     1e7 such values are a few units in the last place and took either
     sign as the basis changed, so the dual simplex flipped two parallel
     columns to an artificial bound and back without end and the primal
     simplex let two columns enter in turn after every refactorization
     (Dantzig-Wolfe master LPs of work-task-variation, osolpro
     benchmarks/decomposition-bounds). Both simplexes also keep hashes of
     their last 32 bases: after 20 pivots back to one of them the dual
     simplex leaves the row out and, if any row is still outside its
     bounds on fresh factors at the end, stops at LIMIT rather than
     return a point outside a row; the primal simplex hands over to the
     dual one. That ends a cycle of the exact LP fuzzer at LIMIT (seed
     36935: free columns at bounds of 1e12, the slacks of two parallel
     rows swapped forever). Both reproducers solve in 113 and 1,818
     pivots, before they ran until the pivot limit.

264. TSP edge elimination: before search, a pure symmetric travelling
     salesman model (one circuit with no fixed successor, the minimised
     objective only in obj = sum(i) cost[x[i]] + c, symmetric costs) loses
     the arcs that are in no optimal tour, proved from the distances alone
     with the 2-opt compatibility of Jonker and Volgenant ("Nonoptimal
     edges for the symmetric traveling salesman problem", Operations
     Research 32(4), 1984) and the close point and main edge elimination
     of Hougardy and Schroeder ("Edge elimination in TSP instances",
     WG 2014; see also Cook, Helsgaun, Hougardy and Schroeder, "Local
     elimination in the traveling salesman problem", Mathematical
     Programming Computation, 2024). The rules use strict improvements,
     so every optimal tour stays and no triangle inequality is needed.
     It leaves 5 to 12 arcs per city on 42 TSPLIB instances in 0.1 to
     0.6 s. At 600 s it proves 16 optima instead of 14 with the model's
     search (proof time geomean 0.43) and 31 instead of 29 in the TSP
     mode with -f (0.69); with plain -f it proves 25 instead of 27
     (0.72 on the instances both prove; 24 instead of 22 at 240 s).
     org.jacop.constraints.TspEdgeElimination; FlatZinc in
     GiantTourDetector; System property org.jacop.fz.tspElimination=false
     turns it off, org.jacop.fz.tspEliminationMs (default 2000) limits it.

247. The LCG explanation of a table (HybridTable) breaks ties between
     columns that kill a tuple at the same cost by the narrowest bounds
     of the column, not by the lowest column index, so the learnt clause
     no longer depends on the order of the table's columns. On the 28
     XCSP Charlotte and Fortress instances 21 are solved in 240 s instead
     of 16, and 22 instead of 18 in 600 s (Fortress2-05, -08, -09 and -10
     in 3 to 27 s instead of the time limit).

265. Symmetry breaking in fzn-jacop (org.jacop.fz.SymmetryBreaking) now
     handles set variables and the value symmetries hidden in MiniZinc's
     encoding of set comprehensions.
     - A set variable is a vertex of the model graph coloured by its
       domain, joined to one vertex per undecided element (Puget,
       "Automatic detection of variable and value symmetries", CP 2005),
       so symmetries may exchange set variables; this only in models
       whose search annotation branches on set variables, since the
       symmetries it reveals in integer searches slowed valve-network
       down. The set variables a
       symmetry moves get Booleans "e not in s" (ChannelBoolSet), which
       take part in the lex-leader constraints (Crawford, Ginsberg, Luks
       and Roy, "Symmetry-breaking predicates for search problems", KR
       1996). Set variables at the front of the lex-leader order that
       the group permutes in every way are ordered by one lex chain
       (Flener et al., "Breaking row and column symmetries in matrix
       models", CP 2002). The two operands of set_union, set_intersect,
       set_symdiff, set_eq, set_ne and jacop_disjoint can be exchanged,
       and a chain of set unions whose intermediate sets are used
       nowhere else is one n-ary union.
     - When the plain value spaces give no value symmetry, a second try
       puts set variables into the space of their elements (membership
       and the set operations keep holding after the elements are
       renamed), joins groups of variables whose values overlap, and
       lets the generators move the other variables of a space with its
       values while fixing its variables of the search annotation, which
       get the value precedence chains (Law and Lee, "Global constraints
       for integer and set value precedence", CP 2004). This finds the
       interchangeable teams of 2026 workforce-alloc, flattened from
       {t | t in Team where exists(...)} into a variable with domain
       {0, t}, a singleton set and a chain of unions per team.
     At 600 s, monomatch n3 (UNSAT) and workforce-alloc 202 and 24_56
     are now proved with the search annotation and with free search,
     and workforce-alloc 71 with free search. -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetrySets=false and
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetryEncoded=false turn the two parts off.

266. fzn-jacop -p reads the model once: the portfolio parses the file into
     one syntax tree and each thread builds its own store from it, instead
     of every thread reading and translating the file. LCG threads of -p
     also take the values of a better solution shared by another thread as
     their phases when they import its bound (solution-guided search, Beck,
     "Solution-guided multi-point constructive search for job shop
     scheduling", JAIR 29, 2007). At 240 s with -f -p 4, two rounds each,
     the 62 instances of the wide-quick set score 64.7 against 59.3 pair
     points (26 better, 6 worse) and the 65 instances of the 2026 Challenge
     gap set 66.2 against 61.8 (36 better, 32 worse). --free-config
     portfolio-one-parse=0 and lcg-shared-phase=0 turn the two parts off.

267. Root probing under --lcg, off by default (-Djacop.lcg.probe=share of
   the time left, for example 0.02). It only adds facts: each value of each
   0/1 variable, then each value of each domain of at most 64 values
   (-Djacop.lcg.probe.domain), is assumed and propagated by the clauses and
   the constraints; a value that fails is removed at the root (failed
   literals, J. W. Freeman, PhD thesis, University of Pennsylvania, 1995),
   and an atom that every value of a variable implies holds at the root
   (lifting, I. Lynce and J. Marques-Silva, ICTAI 2003; M. W. P.
   Savelsbergh, ORSA Journal on Computing 6(4), 1994). Probing every value
   is singleton consistency at the root (R. Debruyne and C. Bessiere,
   IJCAI 1997). Passes repeat while they add facts, and probing stops when
   a quarter of its budget found nothing (-Djacop.lcg.probe.barren). With
   -Djacop.lcg.probe.early=true it runs before the linear relaxation is
   built, so that --lp has the probed domains and, as clique rows, greedy
   maximal cliques of the conflicts x = a, y = b that probing found (A.
   Atamturk, G. L. Nemhauser and M. W. P. Savelsbergh, Conflict graphs in
   solving integer programming problems, EJOR 121(1), 2000). On the
   wide set at 600 s it raises the root LP bound of 76 of 179 optimisation
   instances with a relaxation (4 looser) and the final dual bound of 27
   (1 looser), for example cable-tree A041 -50 to 255400, spot5 28 0 to
   168610, physician-scheduling 1245 to 1745; it solves 167 against 166
   instances, and 189 against 191 with -f, the probing seconds costing
   time on easy instances.
   -Djacop.lcg.probe.delay probes between runs of branch and bound instead,
   once that share of the time limit has passed. LcgSolver.probeRoot is the
   API; one LcgFuzzTest run in four probes before solving.

268. LcgSolver.release attaches again, also above the root level, the
   constraints that became clauses: removeConstraint only marks them
   entailed there, and the mark stayed (Store.clearEntailed), so early
   root probing lost the regular constraints of handball19 (elitserien
   2014) and reported the objective 0 as optimal against the optimum 3.

269. Linear overlap factoring at FlatZinc posting, on by default
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.linearOverlap=false turns it off), after the presolve rule

270. With the decomposition bound on (-Dorg.jacop.fz.decomposition=true)
   free search (-f) starts from the columns of its master and searches
   its blocks as a neighbourhood of large neighbourhood search. At the
   end of column generation the restricted master (one column per block,
   the linking rows, the least cost) is solved for at most a second
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.decomposition.primalMs, 1000) by lazy clause
   generation, the restricted master heuristic of C. Joncour, S. Michel,
   R. Sadykov, D. Sverdlov and F. Vanderbeck, Column generation based
   primal heuristics, Electronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics 36 (2010)
   695-702. Its solution, or else the column of largest weight in the
   fractional master of each block, gives the first values of the
   search. The new AdaptiveLns kind BLOCKS relaxes whole blocks, chosen
   by roulette on how far their heaviest column is from 1, so the
   fractional blocks first (--free-config lns-blocks=0 turns it off);
   AdaptiveLns.addGroups gives the groups.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.decomposition.primal=false turns both off. On
   physician-scheduling instance10 the first solution is the optimum
   2980, proved in 18 s instead of 34-180 s (median of 3 at 240 s).
   Results: osolpro benchmarks/decomposition-primal.

271. Automatic dominance breaking at presolve (off by default,
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.dominance=true), after J. H. M. Lee and A. Z. Zhong,
   Automatic Generation of Dominance Breaking Nogoods for a Class of
   Constraint Optimization Problems, IJCAI 2020, 1192-1200, and Exploiting
   Functional Constraints in Automatic Dominance Breaking for Constraint
   Optimization, CP 2022, LIPIcs 235, 31:1-31:17 (JAIR 78, 2023).
   org.jacop.presolve.DominanceNogoods looks, for every value of one
   variable and every pair of values of two variables, for another
   assignment of the same variables that keeps every constraint they
   take part in satisfied and strictly improves the objective; such
   assignments are nogoods, posted as removed values, clauses on 0/1
   variables or tables of the allowed pairs. Linear rows, monotone
   constraints, implications, reified linear rows and clauses are checked
   by deltas; variables defined by linear equalities and by functional
   constraints (b <-> x = c, element) follow the move. Only strict
   improvements are used, so the nogoods keep every optimal solution
   that symmetry breaking keeps. Budgets: 1 s, 200,000 nogoods, domains of
   at most 16 values. It fires on 7 of the 187 optimisation instances of
   the wide set and on 18 of 582 Challenge instances (trucking,
   shortest_path, spot5, pattern-set-mining, table-layout, maximum-dag,
   aircraft-disassembly, unit-commitment). On those 39 instances at 600 s
   (default search) it solves trucking_13 in 3 s instead of not at all,
   halves the nodes of trucking, and finds better solutions on trucking_10
   and six spot5 instances, with no worse result; the presolve takes at
   most 0.9 s.

272. Linear overlap factoring at FlatZinc posting, off by default
     (-Dorg.jacop.fz.linearOverlap=true), after the presolve rule
     FindBigHorizontalLinearOverlap of OR-Tools CP-SAT (CpModelPresolver,
     9.15; the rule has no separate publication). When two or more
     int_lin_eq, int_lin_le or int_lin_ne constraints contain the same
     sub-sum sum(b_i * x_i) of at least 4 terms (.minSize), each times an
     integer factor k, the sub-sum gets a new variable z = sum(b_i * x_i)
     and every such row uses k * z instead, so the sum is propagated once
     and its bounds are shared. The largest proportional overlap of each
     row with another row is taken greedily (.maxOcc, .work bound the
     search). z is a split sum, so the linear relaxation (--lp) and LCG
     use the original rows. Reified and half-reified rows are left alone
     (.reified=true includes them): there the shared sum is propagated at
     every node while the reified rows are passive, which made
     pattern-set-mining and bnn-planner 3-4x slower. Off under --proof.
     It fires on 52 of the 240 wide-set instances. On those, at 600 s,
     model search solves 38 as before with init+solve time 0.945x, -f 41
     instead of 39 (kitchen 2026 and javarouting trip_7_2 proved) with
     0.959x; vaccine v857 2.2x, harmony canon 1.5x and blocks-world 16-4-83
     1.5x faster, network_50 and roster-shifts-bool slower (other trees).
     It does not fire on the other 188 wide-set instances, which are
     unchanged.

273. The CDCL solver reads the clock every 64 rounds of the LCG
   propagator's fixpoint instead of every 1,024. Rounds of a fixpoint
   with creeping bounds can be slow: on vrp-submission toy_D and
   D_3v_25l_w_reload (2021, arrival times up to 460,588) the core-guided
   probe ran minutes past its share and kept the whole -f run at 600 s
   without a solution, ending past -t. Now toy_D proves its optimum 107
   in 38 s and D_3v_25l_w_reload proves 146 (best known 207) under -f
   at 600 s; wide-quick at 240 s is unchanged in both modes.

274. SequenceMdd compiles its diagram without allocating: the arcs of the
     current domains are read once per call into bit masks, each diagram
     node keeps a mask of the last nodes of its paths, and the nodes of the
     next layer are found by a Zobrist hash of their visited sets in an
     open-addressing table instead of a HashMap of copied masks. The time
     bounds are read once per call. The diagram, the pruning and the search
     trees are unchanged; at 600 s over the 21 tsptw, stochastic-vrp and
     tdtsp instances it proves, the time to proof is 0.87x with the search
     annotation and 0.84x with free search (geometric means), tsptw
     n20w180.004 293.6 s -> 152.7 s and 188.9 s -> 86.7 s. Above 32 nodes
     (-Djacop.sequenceMdd.replayNodes) lazy clause generation takes the
     generic reason instead of replaying the constraint, whose replays cost
     83 % of its time on 60-node circuits. The FlatZinc limit of 32 nodes
     stays: on vrplc (40 to 60 nodes) the diagram leaves the search tree as
     large as before, so it cannot pay for itself there.

275. Three cardinality constraints of the Global Constraint Catalog, all
     propagated with a feasible flow (lower and upper arc bounds) whose
     residual strongly connected components give the values that some
     solution uses:
     - SymmetricGCC(x, rowCount, colCount): a 0/1 matrix x whose row r
       has rowCount[r] ones and whose column c has colCount[c] ones
       (symmetric_gcc / symmetric_cardinality of the catalog). Domain
       consistent on the cells and bounds consistent on the counts, after
       I. Kocjan and P. Kreuger, "Filtering methods for symmetric
       cardinality constraint", CPAIOR 2004, LNCS 3011, 200-208, with
       the flow model of J.-C. Régin, "Generalized arc consistency for
       global cardinality constraint", AAAI 1996.
     - CardinalityMatrix(x, values, rowCount, colCount): value values[k]
       occurs rowCount[r][k] times in row r and colCount[c][k] times in
       column c of x (colored_matrix of the catalog), with one such flow
       per value linking rows and columns, after J.-C. Régin and C. Gomes,
       "The cardinality matrix constraint", CP 2004, LNCS 3258, 572-587.
     - Same(x, y) and UsedBy(x, y): y is a permutation of x, or a
       sub-multiset of it, domain consistent with the flow network of N.
       Beldiceanu, I. Katriel and S. Thiel, "Filtering algorithms for the
       Same constraint", CPAIOR 2004, LNCS 3011, 65-79 (and the UsedBy
       algorithm of their report MPI-I-2004-1-001).
     The MiniZinc library of fzn-jacop defines same, used_by,
     symmetric_gcc and cardinality_matrix with them (the row and column
     sums and cardinalities are posted as well). With
     -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetricGcc=true the FlatZinc front end also finds
     0/1 matrices whose rows and columns are bounded sums (as MiniZinc
     flattens them) and posts a redundant SymmetricGCC; it is off by
     default: on the 76 Challenge instances (vrp, solbat, amaze, kitchen,
     roster-shifts-bool, ma-path-finding) where it fires, at 240 s, it
     never changed the sequence of solutions of the model search and
     only slowed it down (vrp, amaze).

276. Memory of the FlatZinc front end and of constraint scopes, found by an
   object-level heap census of the largest models: the parse tree is
   released before the search (the parser held it for the whole run, 29 %
   of the heap of nside EASY_200_50; a portfolio building from one shared
   tree keeps it), a name is one String for all its uses (950 -> 90 MB on
   ma-path-finding g32_p20_a10), a declaration drops its annotations and
   array literal once its variable is made, and a scope of up to 8
   variables is an array instead of a LinkedHashSet (830 MB on
   ma-path-finding g32_p20_a10). Live heap at the peak: rcpsp_max d
   902 -> 502 MB, rcpsp-wet-diverse j60 1304 -> 853 MB, nside 584 -> 431 MB;
   ma-path-finding g32_p20_a10 under -f now reaches the search within 4 GB
   and rcpsp-wet-diverse j90_48_4 is read and posted.

277. IntervalDomain keeps its intervals in one int array (min and max of
     interval i at 2i and 2i+1) instead of an array of Interval objects,
     so walking a domain reads one array and the domain operations
     allocate no Interval. The public field intervals is removed: use
     domain.leftElement(i) and domain.rightElement(i), or getInterval(i)
     where an Interval object is wanted. AffineDomain now overrides
     leftElement and rightElement. Search is unchanged (same nodes and
     solutions in fixed search); 60 s x 3 runs of 12 instances, base and
     candidate side by side: +1.9 % nodes/s on average (cgt +4.6 %,
     monomatch +4.1 %, liner +3.8 %), base against itself +0.1 %.

278. Diffn (and Diff2, Nooverlap) does less repeated work with the same
   pruning. The profile sweep of a rectangle runs per dimension, only when
   that rectangle or a compulsory part in that dimension changed since its
   last sweep; the events of compulsory parts that did not change are kept
   between builds; and the area check skips the dual feasible functions
   for a window whose rectangle sizes passed them the last time. On 81
   instances posting Diffn (carpet-cutting, cargo, accap, code-generator,
   stripboard, pillars-and-planks, rectangle-packing, perfect_square,
   workforce-alloc, ...) at 240 s with the default search the solutions
   are the same and the speed geomean is 1.04 (rpp21 1.18x, carpet-cutting
   up to 1.48x nodes).

279. JaSAT (CdclSolver) clause headers take three ints instead of four: the
   saved position of the search for a new watch moves into the free high
   16 bits of the flags word, so a clause of three literals takes 6 ints
   instead of 7. Same search (the simplification schedule still
   counts the old header size); pure SAT runs (CdclMain) are about 2 %
   faster.

280. A domain declared beyond JaCoP's integer range, which the FlatZinc
   front end narrows to the range, no longer blocks the claim of
   optimality or unsatisfiability when the model shows the narrowing
   harmless. The model's linear rows (int_lin_eq, int_lin_le, int_eq,
   int_le, int_lt) are read as declared and their bounds propagated in
   64-bit arithmetic, with the objective bounded by the best solution
   (feasibility-based bound tightening: M. W. P. Savelsbergh, Preprocessing
   and probing techniques for mixed integer programming problems, ORSA
   Journal on Computing 6(4), 1994). A narrowed variable is harmless when
   these bounds keep it in range, when it occurs in no constraint, or when
   it occurs only in int_lin_le rows that the end of the range satisfies
   (variable locks: T. Achterberg, Constraint Integer Programming, PhD
   thesis, TU Berlin, 2007). 2021 connect 0086_02 (329 domains
   0..1000000000) is now proved optimal in 15 s; before, the search
   completed at the optimum and printed no ==========.

281. LCG free search (-f) uses target phases in its activity runs before the
   first solution: a decision on an integer variable takes the value that
   variable had in the deepest assignment (longest SAT trail at a conflict)
   reached since the last restart, as CaDiCaL does for Boolean variables
   (A. Biere, M. Fleury, "Chasing Target Phases", Pragmatics of SAT 2020).
   -Djacop.lcg.targetPhase=true|false turns it on or off in every mode, and
   -Djacop.jasat.lubyUnit sets the Luby restart unit (default 512). On the
   99 of 240 wide-set instances where it fires, at 600 s with -f, the same
   75 are solved, 10 instances are better and 5 worse, 5 families better
   and 1 worse, and the time geomean on instances solved by both is 0.972
   (blocks-world 16-4-83 416 -> 113 s, community-detection Sampson 558 ->
   191 s, workforce-alloc 202 131 -> 42 s; pure satisfaction losses on
   monomatch n5 3.8 -> 10.3 s and oocsp racks 030 10 -> 16 s).

282. Lazy clause generation explains the pruning of SymmetricGCC,
     CardinalityMatrix, Same and UsedBy (entry 274) with their own reasons
     instead of the generic one. The network is rebuilt from the domains
     before the atom with the atom negated; it has no feasible flow, and
     the set of nodes reachable from the source after the max flow is a
     cut whose arcs break Hoffman's condition (the upper bounds leaving it
     sum to less than the lower bounds entering it). The reason cites only
     the bounds and removed values that give those arc bounds, after
     G. Downing, T. Feydy and P. J. Stuckey, "Explaining flow-based
     propagation", CPAIOR 2012, LNCS 7298, 146-162. LcgFuzzTest posts the
     four constraints (600,000 runs with the explanation checker: clean).
     With -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetricGcc=true on the 76 instances where it
     fires, at 240 s: the share of generic reasons falls from 4.6 % to
     0.5 % (model search) and 6.3 % to 0.5 % (-f), the mean reason size
     from 35 to 7 literals under -f, nodes/s +5.8 % under -f; at 600 s
     the three vrp proofs of -f take 125-208 s instead of 147-500 s.

283. The FlatZinc front end with -a and -cs (complementary search) labels
   the output variables first and then completes the other variables
   once, as without -cs. Before, -cs labelled every variable under -a and
   printed an output solution again for each assignment of the variables
   that are not output (for example 388 solutions for the 102 of the
   mznlib all_different_opt test, 580950 for the 5700 of geost_zero_size).

284. The sum propagators do less work with the same pruning. SumInt over
     more than three variables moves fixed variables behind the free ones
     on the trail (as LinearInt does), its == case loops only until the
     bounds stop moving, and SumInt and LinearInt skip a pruning pass when
     the slack is at least the largest variability of a free term.
     SumWeight skips the divisions for terms that cannot lose a value,
     SumBool counts its bounds in one pass, LinearIntDom fuses the two
     bitset passes of its backward sweep, and LinearExplanation finds the
     trail entry of each term once instead of up to three times. On 27
     instances posting sums that time out in 60 s, with a search order
     independent of identity hash codes, nodes/s rise 1.4 % (soccer-
     computational 1.25x, unit-commitment 1.13x, ihtc 1.05x); solved
     instances run as fast as before. Search is unchanged except where the
     new SumInt term order gives other LCG reasons (accap).

285. Search splitting for -p (opt-in, --free-config portfolio-split=N):
   N LCG threads of the portfolio divide one search tree in place of the
   last N settings, while the other threads keep searching the whole tree.
   A part of the tree is a cube of atoms [x >= v], [x = v] over FlatZinc
   names, which a thread solves under assumptions in branch and bound; a
   busy thread splits its cube on its first decision below the assumptions
   when another waits, keeps the decision and gives away its negation
   (CP-SAT's shared tree workers; C. Chu, C. Schulte and P. J. Stuckey,
   CP 2009; Cube and Conquer, M. Heule et al., HVC 2011; E. Ehlers and
   P. J. Stuckey, CPAIOR 2016). Learnt clauses hold without the
   assumptions, so clause and bound sharing go on. On wide-quick at 240 s
   with -f -p 4, two split threads beside two portfolio threads were
   better in 4 families and worse in none in both of two rounds, with
   optimality proofs about 5 % faster (geometric mean 0.94 and 0.95);
   four split threads lost proofs that need core-guided or lower-bound
   search (spot5 28) and were 17 % slower. ExternalPropagator gets
   notifyTopDecision, called before each decision at the assumption level.

286. The FlatZinc front end posts partner constraints for counts that cannot
   be one (CountPartnersDetector). When every value of x[i] is counted
   over a list x with jacop_among(x, {v}, n) or jacop_count(x, v, n) and n
   cannot be 1..k-1, a value taken by x[i] is taken by k - 1 others; the
   model's disequalities x[i] != x[j] (int_ne, int_lin_ne, all-different)
   leave x[i] few partners P(i), and sum(j in P(i)) (x[i] = x[j]) >= k - 1
   is posted (a clause for k = 2, x[i] = x[j] for a single partner), as an
   implied constraint (B. M. Smith, Modelling, Handbook of Constraint
   Programming, 2006, ch. 11). Under LCG the counts explain each pruning
   with all other variables of the list (50-70 literals on XCSP3
   ButtonsScissors), the partner clause with a few. ButtonsScissors 2025
   at 600 s with -f: solutions on all 11 instances where 07-09 had none,
   09 proved optimal (21) in 95 s, 00-03 proved 2-10x faster. It fires on
   no instance of the wide MiniZinc set. -Dorg.jacop.fz.countPartners=false
   turns it off.

287. The linear relaxation is much tighter on models with half-reified value
     literals (MiniZinc int_eq_imp, JaCoP ChannelImply) beside reified ones
     of the same variable, as XCSP 2025 TankAllocation1 (the cargo of each
     tank, with x = empty reified for the objective). The half-reified
     literals join the variable's value clique, sum b + e <= 1, and a
     literal that doubles a reified one gets b <= e, as CP-SAT's presolve
     detects half-reified value encodings (-Dorg.jacop.lp.impliedValues=false
     turns this off): root bound 16880 -> 1799 on TankAllocation1-0000
     (optimum 1619). The dense dual simplex no longer stalls in the cut
     rounds: slacks have no cost and no perturbation, and the rows of cuts
     made thousands of zero-length pivots up to the round's limit; a slack's
     cost is now shifted as it leaves the basis, scaled by the root of its
     row's largest weight so that the integer certificate of the duals keeps
     the LP bound (cost shifting, A. Koberstein, "The dual simplex method,
     techniques for a fast and stable implementation", PhD thesis,
     Paderborn, 2005; -Dorg.jacop.lp.slackShift=false). The root cut rounds
     go on after the first five while each moves the bound by 1 % of what
     the rounds before it did (up to 50 rounds and 1 s more,
     -Dorg.jacop.lp.cutRoundsMax, -Dorg.jacop.lp.cutExtraSeconds), and a
     round of Gomory cuts over the slacks that stops at its pivot limit is
     taken again with MIR cuts only. MIR cuts (H. Marchand and L. A.
     Wolsey, "Aggregation and mixed integer rounding to solve MIPs",
     Operations Research 49(3), 2001) are also separated in search from
     the rows of the root, as the capacity cuts are, with a period that
     halves when they move the bound, off by default
     (-Dorg.jacop.lp.mirSearch=true; on a knapsack their separations took
     the LP's work budget and the search 7 times more nodes). New
     statistics lpMirSearchCuts, lpMirSeparations, lpCutLimits. Cut bound
     on TankAllocation1-0000 1768 -> 1676.

288. DifferenceCycles takes arcs x - y <= c - s * o whose weight depends on
   the bound of an offset variable o (y >= x + d with a variable duration
   or travel time), and literals of any domain: the arc holds when the
   literal is fixed to its value. The FlatZinc front end adds the arcs of
   three-variable unit rows (int_lin_le/eq over x, y and an offset), of
   int_max/int_min (m >= x) and of array_var_int_element (z = a[j] when
   the index is j), so cycles through element successors and variable
   travel times, as in routing and cyclic scheduling, fail at once
   instead of creeping by bounds propagation. As before, the constraint
   is posted only when a conditional arc lies on a cycle: offset arcs
   alone (costas-array, road-cons, celar) cost more than they gained.
   On the MiniZinc Challenge
   2023 yumi-static instance p_4_GG_GG_yumi_grid_setup_7_7, -f proves the
   optimum 457 in 104 s instead of 232 s (CP-SAT 19 s). This is the
   precedence reasoning with variable offsets of CP-SAT (Perron, Didier
   and Gay, The CP-SAT-LP solver, CP 2023) and the global difference
   constraint propagation of Feydy, Schutt and Stuckey (PPDP 2008), used
   for cycle detection only. -Dorg.jacop.fz.differenceOffsets=false and
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.differenceElements=false turn the new arcs off.

289. Lazy clause generation under -f with a linear objective: when the
     objective's root bound (from the linear relaxation, say) is above the
     lower bound the core-guided phase starts from, a search for a solution
     at that bound, under an assumption, runs before the core-guided probe
     (0.2 % of the work left, at most 1,000 conflicts). A solution found
     there is optimal; without one the activity and target phases are
     restored and the schedule goes on as before. On nfc (Challenge 2022,
     cyclic staffing over a network flow) the relaxation's bound is the
     optimum, but the probe delayed branch and bound by 0.67 % of the time
     limit: at 1200 s the five instances take 0.35-0.46 s instead of
     2-14 s (CP-SAT proves them at the root too). It fires on 55 of the
     187 optimisation instances of the wide set and finds the optimum on
     14-17 of them; at 600 s over two seeds proofs stay at 87 of 110, the
     proved instances take 0.89x and 0.78x the time (geometric mean),
     families 3 better / 2 worse and 4 / 1. -Djacop.lcg.boundDive=0 turns
     it off; statistics lcgBoundDives and lcgBoundDiveSolutions.

290. Large neighbourhood search under -f (free search with lazy clause
   generation) fixes and relaxes the model's own decision variables again
   on models where MiniZinc defines the integer copy of a Boolean by
   bool2int(b, i) :: defines_var(i). The front end merges i into b, and
   the defines_var annotation then marked b itself as defined, so b was
   left out of the neighbourhoods. On speck-optimisation (2023)
   very_hard_5 that left 1 of 12,213 variables to LNS, every
   neighbourhood was the whole model and LNS made no progress; it now
   works over 2,541 variables. 32 of the 187 optimisation instances of
   the wide benchmark set get a different set of LNS variables. When
   fewer than 16 variables are not defined by a constraint (a few roots
   that define the rest by chains, as in pattern-set-mining), the
   neighbourhoods take every variable, as they did when none was left.

291. LCG free search (-f) that makes few conflicts gives way to free search
   without learning only when it has also stopped making progress, and the
   rate below which it is slow is 300 conflicts/s instead of 50. At the
   check (lcg-slow-at, 0.1 of -t) a search under lcg-slow-rate goes on while,
   during the last lcg-slow-stall share of the time limit (default 0.05), it
   raised the bound or its best cost fell by lcg-slow-gain of itself
   (default 0.005); otherwise it gives way as before. Free search without
   learning can improve the best solution but never raises the bound, so a
   search whose bound still rises keeps learning: on ma-path-finding LCG
   makes about 14 conflicts/s on 650,000 variables while core-guided search
   raises the bound to the optimum. At 600 s with -f on the 17 instances
   where the check at 300 fires (15 of the 187 wide-set optimisation
   instances and unit-commitment L10, L15): unit-commitment L10 2.97M ->
   1,703,450 (the optimum), L12 3.05M -> 1.49M, L15 96.0M -> 43.2M, ihtc
   kletzander i09 8,310 -> 7,135; stable-goods s-d19 (maximised) 7,304 ->
   7,186. Statistic lcgSlowDeferred counts the checks that let a slow LCG
   go on.

292. The FlatZinc front end tabulates small flattened expressions: the
   constraints joined by variables that MiniZinc introduced (not output,
   not in the solve item, not aliased), over at most three other
   variables, become one table of the tuples that extend to a solution,
   when the group holds a division, a modulo or an element over a
   variable array. These propagate to bounds while their relations have
   holes all over the box: x mod y = 0 \/ y mod x = 0 flattens into two
   int_mod, two int_eq_imp and a clause that prune almost nothing between
   x and y. XCSP3 solvers keep such expressions whole (C. Lecoutre,
   Constraint Networks, 2009) and Savile Row tabulates such subproblems
   (Ö. Akgün, I. P. Gent, C. Jefferson, I. Miguel, P. Nightingale and
   A. Z. Salamon, CP 2018; J. J. Dekker, G. Björdal, M. Carlsson,
   P. Flener and J.-N. Monette, Constraints 22, 2017). XCSP 2025
   ChainReaction at 600 s: 9 of 14 solved, 6 before (30-035 proved
   unsatisfiable in 26 s, where the fastest 2025 solver took 87 s). On
   the wide set it fires on 4 of 240 instances, within a few percent of
   before. Off with -a (unless org.jacop.fz.intensionTable=force) and
   --proof; org.jacop.fz.intensionTable=false turns it off.

293. The FlatZinc front end proves parity infeasibilities such as
   Langford's problem L(2, n) for n = 1, 2 (mod 4) (C. D. Langford, Math.
   Gazette 42, 1958; parity proof R. O. Davies, Math. Gazette 43, 1959)
   before search. An all_different constraint over n variables whose
   domains together hold exactly n values takes every value, so the sum
   of its variables is the sum of those values; that equality now joins
   the int_lin_eq system that LinearSystemDetector reduces to its Hermite
   normal form, which proves 2 S + 104 = 325 infeasible on XCSP
   LangfordBin-013. The all-different constraints are the model's own and
   those AlldifferentDetector finds, which now also uses disequalities
   implied by other constraints: x[p] = y and x[q] = z over the same array
   with disjoint domains of y and z give p != q (shifted by the index
   bases), and a*x - a*y = c with c != 0 gives x != y. A clique of
   implied disequalities only is recorded for the sum, not posted
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.alldifferentImplied=post posts its Alldiff too, which
   made the solvable LangfordBin instances much slower; =off does not
   collect implied disequalities); -Dorg.jacop.fz.permutationSums=false
   leaves the sums out. XCSP 2025 LangfordBin-010/013/014 are proved
   infeasible in 0.5 s (before: 5.9 s, none in 240 s, none in 240 s); CP-SAT
   proves them with zero-half cuts of its LP worker. Of 1,998 wide-set
   and XCSP instances screened, the model changes on 4 (those three and
   gfd-schedule, where implied disequalities enlarge a detected clique;
   same results at 600 s). Posting the implied cliques changes 81 and
   slows the solvable LangfordBin instances.

294. Lazy clause generation explains the atoms of nine recent globals
     with their own reasons instead of the replayed or generic one:
     - MultiInterDistance, Spread, Deviation and StockingCost by the box of
       bounds in which the pruned value has no solution, relaxed bound by
       bound to the root while it stays infeasible (deletion filter, J. W.
       Chinneck and E. W. Dravnieks, "Locating minimal infeasible
       constraint sets in linear programs", ORSA J. on Computing 3(2),
       1991); StockingCost checks the box with its greedy bound (V. R.
       Houndji, P. Schaus, L. Wolsey and Y. Deville, "The StockingCost
       constraint", CP 2014), and an upper bound at the due date needs no
       reason;
     - KnapsackDP by the reasons of Knapsack (KnapsackExplanation);
     - IncreasingNValue by the neighbouring variable's bound for the order
       (N. Beldiceanu, F. Hermenier, X. Lorca and T. Petit, "The
       increasing nvalue constraint", CPAIOR 2010) and an empty reason for
       the count's trivial bounds;
     - InterDistance by the compulsory part of another task for a removed
       value and by the detectable precedences, edge finding and not-first
       rules of a unary resource for bounds (UnaryExplanation, after P.
       Vilím, "Filtering algorithms for the unary resource constraint",
       2008, and A. Schutt, "Improving scheduling by learning", 2011);
     - CircuitCost and SubcircuitCost by the reduced costs of the minimum
       (maximum) assignment over the domains before the atom: only the
       removed arcs with a negative reduced cost are cited (F. Focacci, A.
       Lodi and M. Milano, "Cost-based domain filtering", CP 1999). The
       Hungarian method is warm-started from the duals of the previous
       explanation and only the rows whose variables changed between the
       two trail positions are rebuilt.
     The explanations of InterDistance (-Djacop.lcg.interDistanceReasons=
     true) and of CircuitCost and SubcircuitCost (-Djacop.lcg.
     assignmentReasons=true) are off by default. Of the 29 Challenge
     instances where these globals fire (mario, vrplc, train-scheduling,
     rect-euler), at 240 s with both on: free search is 1.11x faster in
     geomean over the 22 instances both prove (mario up to 1.6x), but
     vrplc9_5_10_s3 and s2 free search and trains12 are slower, and the
     model's search is 0.76x (mario, vrplc: same nodes, the cost of the
     explanations). The other constraints never fire on these instances.
     LcgFuzzTest has the property lcg.fuzz.recent to post these globals in
     half of the constraints (1.2 million runs with the explanation
     checker, with and without holes: clean).

295. Tables as clauses under LCG (experiment, off by default):
   -Djacop.lcg.tableClauses=direct makes each HybridTable (no restrictions
   between columns, at most 100 tuples, 64 values per column and 5000
   clauses, .tuples/.dom/.limit) clauses of the SAT solver in place of
   its propagator and TableExplanation, as regular and MDD constraints
   already are. A table with two unfixed columns becomes the support
   clauses of its values, [x = v] -> OR [y = w] over the w paired with v
   (I. P. Gent, Arc consistency in SAT, ECAI 2002), with no new literal;
   a larger one the support encoding with a literal per tuple
   (F. Bacchus, GAC via unit propagation, CP 2007), which HybridTable
   already wrote for proofs. Both give domain consistency by unit
   propagation. "support" uses the second encoding for every table,
   "binary" encodes only the two-column tables. Tables over the
   objective's definition stay propagators, as in jacop.lcg.clauses: as
   clauses they lost spot5, Fortress and HCPizza (s = 5 x tables) by up
   to 8x. On 67 table instances (28 wide, 39 XCSP) at 600 s, each
   configuration on its own core: default 52 = 52 solved, time 1.00,
   nodes 0.98; -f 57 -> 58, time 1.04 (base against base repeat 1.01).
   Robust gains (3 seeds and both modes): TSPTW 1.8-2x, pillars-and-planks
   1.6-1.9x, code-generator 1.5x, IHTC, SchedulingOS, BeerJugs; losses
   SportsScheduling-dummy, Eternity, PegSolitaire, Spot5-0412 (2-3x).
   Statistics lcgTablesAsClauses, lcgTableClauses, lcgTableTupleLiterals.

296. The FlatZinc front end divides an objective declared beyond JaCoP's
   integer range by the factor its defining equation forces on it,
   instead of narrowing its domain to the range. When the objective z
   occurs in c*z + sum(a_i*x_i) = b, z is a multiple of g = G / gcd(G, c)
   with G = gcd(a_i, b); z is then held as z/g, its coefficient in every
   linear row is multiplied by g, and the output multiplies its value
   back. This is the objective scaling of the CP-SAT presolve
   (PresolveContext::CanonicalizeObjective in Google OR-Tools, which
   divides the objective by the gcd of its coefficients). It applies only
   when the objective, and the variables declared equal to it, occur in
   no constraint but linear rows and fit the range once divided. Of the
   13 Challenge instances (2008-2026) with domains beyond the range it
   fires on the 3 large 2023 unit-commitment ones (obj 0..2147483646,
   g = 5): with fixed search L12 and L15 now find solutions (2113098330
   and 2143644175 at 600 s, CP-SAT 2117495245 and 2146624175 at 1200 s in
   the Challenge), where narrowing cut off every solution the search
   order reaches; with -f L10 reaches the optimum 1703450 in 3 of 3 runs
   (before 2551340-3519410) and L15 46758125 (before 103430755).

297. The FlatZinc front end posts the sums implied by linear equalities
   with unit coefficients that partition the variables of an
   all_different. When sum(X_1) = s_1, ..., sum(X_k) = s_k split the
   variables of all_different(A) into disjoint blocks (the rows, or the
   columns, of a grid), s_1 + ... + s_k is the sum of the values of A,
   which lies between the sums of the |A| smallest and |A| largest
   values of the union of their domains (a constant for a permutation).
   It posts that sum over the s_i, and over the union of several
   partitions, and offers each to AlldiffSum, which then also sees that
   the s_i are different when the model says so. A linear relaxation of
   the all_different gives the same aggregation (H. P. Williams and
   H. Yan, "Representations of the all_different predicate of
   constraint satisfaction in integer programming", INFORMS Journal on
   Computing 13(2), 2001). It fires on 32 XCSP 2023-2025 instances and
   on no MiniZinc Challenge instance; at 600 s Heterosquare-fair-10
   218 s -> 4 s, fair-15 and hard-20 solved in 10 s and 0.4 s
   (unsolved before), AntimagicSquare-11/12 162 s -> 35 s and
   122 s -> 10 s; 30 of the 32 solved instead of 28.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.partitionSums=false turns it off.

298. JaLP's dual simplex no longer factorizes the basis again to check the
   rows it left out as round-off when its factors have had no update since
   the last factorization: the duals and basic values are computed again on
   those factors, which gives the same pivots. In free search of MiniZinc
   Challenge 2023 unit-commitment L10 the check ran about 38,000 times in
   120 s and took 462 microseconds each with a new factorization (15 % of
   the run), 130 to 150 microseconds without.

299. Under --lcg, an all different constraint over as many values as
   variables (a permutation, up to 32 variables), whose unfixed variables
   are in no other constraint, becomes clauses of the SAT solver in place
   of its propagator: x[i] = v excludes x[j] = v, and each value is taken
   by some variable (the extended encoding of Lynce and Ouaknine, "Sudoku
   as a SAT problem", ISAIM 2006; CP-SAT expands permutations the same
   way). Unit propagation finds the forced values and places, though not
   the larger Hall sets of the propagator, and needs no explanations. On
   the 18 25x25 sudokus of Gecode's examples under their fixed search
   (MiniZinc Challenge sudoku_fixed) at 600 s: 14 solved as before, 1.6x
   faster on them (13 of 14), 3,942 instead of 2,245 conflicts per second;
   at 1200 s sudoku_fixed p21 and p27 are solved. Permutations that share
   variables with other constraints keep the propagator: there its Hall
   sets made -f proofs 3-4x slower without it (traveling-tppv, vrplc,
   open-stacks). -Djacop.lcg.alldiffClauses=false turns it off; statistic
   lcgPermutationsAsClauses.

300. The FlatZinc front end finds tilings of the cyclic group Z_n by
   translation, A + B = Z_n, written as an all different over the n terms
   (b + a) mod n, and posts the redundant exact cover over the
   characteristic vector c of B: c counts the base variables per value
   (CountValues), and every value is covered by exactly one c[v - a]
   (E. M. Coven and A. Meyerowitz, J. Algebra 212, 1999; the SAT encoding
   of G. Auricchio, L. Ferrarini, S. Gualandi, G. Lanzarotto and
   L. Pernazza, CPAIOR 2022 and Constraints 29, 2024). The vector joins
   the default search. XCSP 2025 TilingRythmicCanons with -f at 600 s:
   12 of 14 solved, 3 without the cover, among them 1-18, 1-22 and 1-33
   that no 2025 solver solved. It fires on no wide-set instance. Off
   under --proof; org.jacop.fz.translationTiling=false turns it off.

301. The target phases of LCG free search (entry 280) are reset at every
   restart to the values of the longest SAT trail at a conflict seen so far
   in the search, instead of being kept from the run before: the best
   rephasing of CaDiCaL (A. Biere, "CaDiCaL, Lingeling, Plingeling,
   Treengeling and YalSAT Entering the SAT Race 2019"; A. Biere, M. Fleury,
   "Chasing Target Phases", Pragmatics of SAT 2020). On the 99 of 240
   wide-set instances where target phases fire, at 600 s with -f, 75 -> 74
   solved, 10 instances better and 5 worse, 4 families better and 2 worse,
   and the time geomean on the 74 instances solved by both is 0.940
   (vrplc9_5_10_s2 113 -> 25 s, sudoku_fixed p28 28 -> 9 s,
   traveling-tppv circ20fnonbal 24 -> 12 s, oocsp racks 030 12 -> 7 s;
   community-detection Sampson s10 k3, 264 or 558 s in base runs, is not
   proved in 600 s, workforce-alloc 202 55 -> 139 s).
   -Djacop.lcg.targetBest=N resets every N restarts, 0 never (default 1).

302. Lazy clause generation adds unary (sequential) counters for small
   counting constraints outside reification (SumBool, LinearBool, Count,
   CountBounds, CountValues, CountValuesBounds, AtMost, AtLeast) once
   they take part in conflicts. A propagator explains a count by the
   literals of its variables only, so every learnt clause restates a
   whole count; the counter literals [first i terms sum to at least j]
   let learnt clauses state partial counts, as the auxiliary variables
   of a SAT encoding do (Sinz, Towards an optimal CNF encoding of Boolean
   cardinality constraints, CP 2005; Abio, Nieuwenhuis, Oliveras,
   Rodriguez-Carbonell and Stuckey, To encode or to propagate? The best
   choice for each constraint in SAT, CP 2013). As in lazy decomposition
   (Abio and Stuckey, Conflict directed lazy decomposition, CP 2012) a
   constraint stays a propagator until it explained 100 conflicts
   (-Djacop.lcg.counters.uses) and becomes clauses at the next restart;
   -Djacop.lcg.counters=eager encodes them all before search,
   -Djacop.lcg.counters=false turns this off. ClauseEncoder now encodes
   CountValues and CountValuesBounds (global_cardinality) with the count
   of the other values. On the XCSP 2025 EFPA instances with q >= 4 the
   -f proofs and solutions take 0.36-0.59 of the time at 600 s
   (EFPA-4-5-4-11 484 s to 263 s, 2.6 to 1.1 million conflicts); on the
   38 MiniZinc Challenge instances of the wide set where counters fire,
   35 = 35 solved, timing within noise.

303. Lazy clause generation explains the pruning and failures of Geost
   itself instead of replaying it (replay gave up and left 97 % of its
   reasons generic). A bound of a coordinate is explained by the region
   the sweep (Beldiceanu, Carlsson, Poder, Sadek and Truchet, "A Generic
   Geometrical Constraint Kernel in Space and Time for Handling
   Polymorphic k-Dimensional Objects", CP 2007, LNCS 4741, 180-194) moved
   past, a removed shape by the whole origin box: the region is covered by
   the compulsory parts of other objects (the cells each covers at every
   origin and with every shape it may take), the outside and holes of the
   allowed area, and the holes of the object's own domain; the reason
   cites the bounds that delimit the region, the shapes left and the
   bounds and shapes of the obstacles the cover needs. Objects whose
   times vary are left to replay. -Dorg.jacop.geost.reasons=false gives the
   old behaviour. LcgFuzzTest has a Geost case with polymorphic objects,
   areas, times and shape domains with gaps (80,000 runs with the
   explanation checker: clean). On 40 pentomino and polyomino strip
   packings at 600 s, generic reasons fall from 97 % to 13 % and reasons
   from 36 to 15 literals; -f solves 32 instead of 25 (geometric mean time
   0.54 on those both solve), model search 19 as before (time 0.89).

304. Reified and implied linear equalities and disequalities
     (int_lin_eq/ne_reif/imp with weights other than 1 and -1) fix their
     0/1 variable when the gcd of the weights of the free terms does not
     divide the constant minus the fixed terms: -x0 - 3 * x1 != -1 holds
     for x0 = 2 whatever x1 is, but the 0/1 variable stayed free because
     entailment was read from the bounds of the sum only. Lazy clause
     generation explains it by the fixed terms. Found by the new
     ConsistencyChecker case ReifiedLinear (nodes with a free b that has
     no support: about 420 per 1000 instances before, 86 after); the 6
     wide-set instances that post such constraints run as before at 600 s
     (same nodes where solved; pennies-opt n8, proved by the base at
     598 s, times out at the limit).
     ConsistencyChecker has 17 more cases, for the constraints the wide
     set posts most, and LcgFuzzTest has -Dlcg.fuzz.focus to check the
     explanations of chosen constraints at volume.

305. Under lazy clause generation a reified or implied In(x, dom) counts as
   entailed only when the bounds of x lie in dom, not when every value
   left in x does. The full test needs a reason with every value removed
   between the bounds, which on the XCSP 2025 RollerSplat models (plan
   variables with 411 values) made learnt clauses long. RollerSplat-07b-50
   proves its optimum in 379 s instead of 503 s at 600 s and 07c-70 is
   proved in 560 s (not proved before); with z <= 41 the 07b proof takes
   205 s and 147k nodes instead of 393 s and 254k. Search without LCG is
   unchanged; -Djacop.lcg.inEntailByBounds=false restores the full test.
   ElementVariableFast keeps a residual support per index (Lecoutre and
   Hemery, "A Study of Residual Supports in Arc Consistency", IJCAI 2007,
   125-130), so the search is the same and about 4 % faster on RollerSplat.
   On the 40 wide-set instances with set_in_reif or set_in_imp, at 600 s,
   no family is worse with -f or with model search.

306. Under -f with --lcg, the core-guided phase ends sooner when its lower
   bound slows down. An extension of the phase now also needs the bound to
   meet the best solution at its rate over the second half of the phase,
   not only at its rate since the start, which counts the fast rise from a
   far initial bound. After 120 s of the phase's own work, the stall test
   projects a rate that falls from the third to the fourth quarter of the
   phase as a geometric series instead of linearly. On chessboard3 (MiniZinc
   Challenge 2023) each core cost more than the last and the phase took
   about 1,050 s of a 1,200 s run: -f now proves it in 447-473 s (4 runs),
   where it was unproved at 1,200 s. Of 187 optimisation instances of the
   wide benchmark set at 600 s, the rules reach 5; javarouting trip_7_2 is proved in
   255-349 s instead of 284-419 s, kitchen 2026 linear in 177-318 s
   instead of 194-364 s, the others are unchanged.
   -Djacop.lcg.coreExtendRecent=false and -Djacop.lcg.coreDecay=false turn
   the two rules off; -Djacop.lcg.coreDecayAfterMs sets the 120 s.

307. New constraint AllDifferentLists(x): the rows of x are pairwise
   different tuples, the allDifferent constraint over lists of XCSP3
   (Boussemart, Lecoutre, Audemard and Piette, "XCSP3-core", arXiv
   2009.00514, 2020). It hashes the fixed rows and the rows with one
   variable left: two equal fixed rows fail, and a row with one variable
   left loses the values that would make it equal a fixed row, which is
   unit propagation on the pairwise decomposition for all pairs at once.
   A call handles only the rows fixed since the last one. Under --lcg it
   explains a removed value by the two rows and a failure by the two
   equal rows. JaCoP's MiniZinc library defines
   fzn_xcsp_all_different_lists with it, so the XCSP3 translations of
   radsz/xcsp-competition no longer get the pairwise decomposition:
   m(m-1)/2 disjunctions for m rows, 3.0M int_lin_ne_imp and 3.0M
   Booleans (a 685 MB FlatZinc file, 4.7 GB to flatten) on XCSP 2025
   LotteryDesign-32-6-6-2-1000. On the 12 LotteryDesign instances of
   XCSP 2025 (the only ones of 2022-2025 with the constraint), -f, 600 s:
   11 solved instead of 9 (1000 and 2000 tickets, out of memory before),
   the other 9 2-10x faster.

308. Under --lcg, a table that compresses to a few tuples of value sets
   becomes clauses of the SAT solver in place of its propagator, as
   CP-SAT does in presolve (cp_model_expand.cc: tuples are merged into
   tuples of value sets first, then the compressed table is encoded).
   A table of plain values whose valid tuples compress to at most 16
   tuples of value sets, at least 4 times fewer than before, over
   columns of at most 256 values each, is written as the support clauses
   of its compressed form (Bacchus, "GAC via unit propagation", CP 2007),
   or with two unfixed columns as the support clauses of their values
   with no literal per tuple (Gent, "Arc consistency in SAT", ECAI 2002).
   Unit propagation keeps the propagator's domain consistency. Frame
   axioms of planning models such as the move tables of XCSP PegSolitaire
   (140 tuples, 4-6 after compression) woke their propagator on every
   literal of their columns while most calls pruned nothing; as clauses
   only the watched literals are visited, 2.1x the conflicts per second.
   On the 23 instances where it fires (11 of the 240 of the wide set and
   the 12 PegSolitaireTable instances of XCSP 2025), 600 s, -f and
   default: no instance lost, the solved ones 1.29x (-f) and 1.36x
   (default) faster (geomean), PegSolitaireTable 3-3-0 327 -> 109 s,
   0-3-0 189 -> 83 s, 2-2-0 15 -> 4 s, 1-4-0 8 -> 19 s.
   Tables over the objective's definition keep their propagator.
   -Djacop.lcg.tableClauses.compressed=false turns it off
   (.tuples, .ratio, .dom and .input set the limits); statistic
   lcgCompressedTablesAsClauses.

309. New constraint ConjunctionSum(terms, weights, sum): sum is at least
   the weighted number of conjunctions of value literals (x = a, x != a)
   that hold. Besides the terms that hold it counts, for each variable,
   the least cost over its domain of the terms whose other literals all
   hold, and removes the values whose cost would take the bound above
   the maximum of sum: the partial forward checking bound of weighted
   constraint satisfaction (E. C. Freuder and R. J. Wallace, Partial
   constraint satisfaction, Artificial Intelligence 58, 1992; J. Larrosa,
   P. Meseguer and T. Schiex, Maintaining reversible DAC for Max-CSP,
   Artificial Intelligence 107, 1999). Terms watch two variables that
   are open for them, as clauses watch two literals, and the bound is
   kept incrementally; it explains its pruning for lazy clause
   generation. The FlatZinc front end posts it as a redundant constraint
   on a minimized objective that is a sum of bool2int(array_bool_and)
   over int_eq_reif and int_ne_reif literals, as in the XCSP 2025
   BlockModeling problems, when every variable of a literal has at most
   4 values (-Dorg.jacop.fz.conjunctionSum.maxValues);
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.conjunctionSum=false turns it off. A linear sum over
   the indicators counts a term only once all its literals hold, so
   before the lower bound stayed near zero. On the 8 BlockModeling
   instances with 2 or 3 blocks, -f at 600 s proves all 8 optima instead
   of 4 (kansas-02 in 5 s instead of 480 s, kansas-03 83 instead of an
   unproved 88, karate-02 and karate-03 in 15 s and 548 s instead of
   unproved, politicalactor-03 in 6 s instead of 408 s, transatlantic-03
   in 6 s instead of 130 s). With 5 or 10 blocks the least costs stay
   zero until most nodes are placed and the reasons cost more than the
   bound gains, so it is not posted there.

310. -Dorg.jacop.fz.binaryEqAlias=true (off by default) makes a 0..1
   integer x an alias of the Boolean b when int_eq_reif(x, 1, b) states
   x = b, as bool2int(b, x) does, instead of keeping both variables and an
   equality. MiniZinc writes a Boolean grid used in sums as 0..1 integers
   channelled to Booleans this way: on ma-path-finding g16_p10_a30
   (MiniZinc Challenge 2022) 141,000 such pairs, which drops the
   propagators before search from 462,000 to 325,000. A search variable
   that becomes an alias is searched as its Boolean, in the integer
   variable order, and under --lcg the alias gets no literals. At 240 s,
   -f, 3 seeds on 5 ma-path-finding instances the result is mixed:
   g24_p20_a10 reaches its optimum in 3 of 3 seeds at about 68 s instead of
   1 of 3, the others gain or lose with the seed, so it stays off.

311. The linear relaxation gets the prime implicates of tables over 0/1
   variables as clause rows (Quine, "The problem of simplifying truth
   functions", American Mathematical Monthly 59, 1952; McCluskey, Bell
   System Technical Journal 35, 1956): a minimal partial assignment that
   no tuple extends gives the row sum x (literals x = 0) + sum (1 - x)
   (literals x = 1) >= 1. On the Fortress problems of XCSP 2025 (walls
   that enclose points of a grid) the reachability tables then make the
   relaxation the vertex cut linear program, whose bound is the optimum
   (Menger's theorem); before, these short tables gave the relaxation no
   row. Fortress1-03..10 at 600 s, with -f and in default mode: 8 optima
   proved in 2-5 s, none before (Gurobi, CP-SAT and Z3 prove them in
   4-23 s in the competition); Benzenoide-06 is solved in the same time. The rows are tried at the first solve, for an
   objective, and kept only when they help. A table gets them when its
   free columns are 0/1 (at most 10, -Dorg.jacop.lp.tableClauseArity), it
   has at most one prime implicate per free column
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.tableClausesPerColumn; parity and counting tables such
   as xor or the rule of life have many, whose rows only slowed the
   simplex), and its convex hull over tuple columns is not among the
   rows already, which implies every clause; a model with more than
   10,000 such rows gets none (-Dorg.jacop.lp.tableClauseRowsMax). Of the
   XCSP 2022-2025 and wide-set optimisation instances only Fortress1 and
   Benzenoide-06 get them. -Dorg.jacop.lp.tableClauseRows=false turns
   them off; statistic lpTableClauses.

312. The FlatZinc front end finds bin packing constraints whose items fall
   into groups that fill every bin with exactly one item: the bin
   variables of an all_different constraint, as many as there are bins,
   with values among the bins. For each group it posts the dual
   viewpoint (redundant modelling with channelling, Cheng, Lee and Wu,
   "Increasing constraint propagation by redundant modeling: an
   experience report", Constraints 4(2), 1999): the position of the item
   each bin gets (Assignment), the weight of that item (ElementInteger)
   and each load as the sum of these weights, the weights placed for good
   and the load of the other items. The bin packing constraint bounds a
   load by all items that may still go into the bin; the sums also see
   that the bin gets one more item of every group, the lightest at least
   and the heaviest at most (CP-SAT's presolve tightens the loads the
   same way). In MiniZinc Challenge team-assignment (one player of each
   board in every team) under the model's search, which it follows
   exactly (the same solutions in the same order): data1_6_6 is proved
   in 4 s instead of 173 s, 2018 data2_4_15 in 86 s (unproved in 600 s),
   and at 600 s 2022 data3_4_31 reaches 26441 instead of 8363 (CP-SAT
   9.15 with the model's search 23434); 9 of the 10 instances are better,
   data3_5_31 is worse at 600 s (22357 instead of 29357) and better at
   1200 s (32357 instead of 31357). It fires only on
   team-assignment among the 1,781 Challenge instances of 2008-2026.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingPermutations=false turns it off.

313. Under free search (-f) the search follows the LP solution
   (lcg-lp-branching auto) also for an optimisation problem whose linear
   relaxation is a set partitioning, packing or covering model:
   LinearRelaxation.setRows() holds when every model row other than the
   objective's has unit weights of one sign over 0/1 columns (fixed
   columns are constants) and there are at most 0.1 rows per column
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.setRowsShare). Their root solutions are almost integral
   and the root bound is often the optimum, as in the LP-based branching
   of set partitioning solvers (Ryan and Foster, An integer programming
   approach to scheduling, 1981; Barnhart et al., Branch-and-price,
   Operations Research 46(3), 1998). XCSP 2025 BusScheduling, 12
   instances at 600 s: 9 -> 12 optimal (c2 600 -> 16 s, r3 unsolved ->
   205 s, r5 feasible 30 -> optimal 29 in 91 s, r5a 306 -> 41 s). It turns
   on for 5 instances of the wide benchmark set (jp-encoding,
   prize-collecting, hitori, skill-allocation, community-detection) and
   for 22 MiniZinc Challenge instances of those problems, neutral on both.
   The FlatZinc statistics print lpSetRows.

314. JaLP can separate zero-half ({0,1/2}-Chvatal-Gomory) cuts in its root
   cut rounds (Caprara and Fischetti, "{0,1/2}-Chvatal-Gomory cuts",
   Math. Programming 74, 1996), with the heuristic of Koster, Zymolka
   and Kutschka ("Algorithms to separate {0,1/2}-Chvatal-Gomory cuts",
   Algorithmica 55, 2009) that CP-SAT and SCIP use: the integer rows
   tight within 0.1 at the LP solution are eliminated over GF(2) on
   bitsets, and each set of rows whose sum is odd on the right side and
   violated by at least 0.1 is aggregated exactly and rounded with the
   MIR of MirCut and divisor 2; cuts of efficacy below 0.02 are dropped.
   It is off by default (-Dorg.jacop.lp.zeroHalf=true turns it on): it
   fires on 36 of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark set and raises
   the integer root bound on 10 of them (racp j30 461 -> 467,
   steiner-tree +1.3 %, triangular 36 -> 34) and lowers it on 2
   (hoist, unit-commitment). On the 41 wide instances where it changes
   anything it solved the same instances at 240 s and 600 s and was
   within the noise on time (geomean 1.03). On TankAllocation1 of XCSP
   2025 (-f, 600 s) it proves 1600 in 31-35 s and 2591 in 55-132 s (two
   runs each), which the base proves in neither run. The statistic
   lpZeroHalfCuts counts the cuts added.

   MIR cuts made in search (-Dorg.jacop.lp.mirSearch=true, off) are now
   scaled down to weights of at most 2^16
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.mirSearchWeight). Their weights reached 2^24 with
   right sides near 1e10, and on such rows the dense tableau gives Farkas
   multipliers of about 1e-7 with the wrong sign, so most infeasible LP
   solves gave no certificate and pruned nothing: on the 60-item
   knapsack of PortfolioSplitTest search cuts timed out after 90 s; now
   the optimum takes 18,292 nodes and 9 s (42,356 nodes and 1.5 s
   without search cuts, so they stay off). -Dorg.jacop.lp.cutWeight
   caps the root cuts the same way (off by default; 2^20 removes every
   unproven solve of smelt and maximum-dag, within the noise on time).
   The statistic lpUnproven counts infeasible LP solves without an
   integer certificate.

315. Under free search (-f) with lazy clause generation, a variable whose
   chain of linked bound literals [x >= v] -> [x >= u] has reached 64
   literals gets no new links from short reasons: a reason of up to 3
   literals that holds a transient bound literal of such a variable is
   used once from the reason stack instead of being kept as a clause,
   and so is a conflicting propagation's reason when it needs no
   backtrack. Each linked literal is a step that every bound change of
   the variable walks; on MiniZinc Challenge 2023
   multi-agent-graph-coverage (arrival times 0..405) the kept reasons
   linked about 260 literals per variable and 81 % of all SAT assignments
   were chain steps. CP-SAT keeps integer bounds out of its clauses (its
   integer reasons are expanded to Boolean ones in conflict analysis).
   On the 65 instances of the wide benchmark set where it fires, two
   paired rounds at 600 s: 6 and 4 families better, 0 and 2 worse, one
   more proof in both (community-detection Sampson.s10.k3), runtime on
   solved instances 0.987 pooled; rnd_n10_p1500 43-48 -> 22-26 s, wwtpp
   ex05160 14 -> 6 s, hoist PU_2_8_1 and multiple-constant-multiplication
   XU07_28 slower (search changes). In the default search it lost on 5
   families and won on none, so it is off there.
   --free-config lcg-chain-limit=<n> sets the limit under -f and
   -Djacop.lcg.chainLimit=<n> in every search; -Djacop.lcg.reasonUnkept=false
   turns it off. -Djacop.lcg.expandUnkept=true also resolves such
   literals out of learnt clauses (off: slower on liner-sf-repositioning
   and work-task-variation). The FlatZinc statistics print
   lcgUnkeptReasons and lcgExpandedLiterals.

316. Under --lcg, a permutation (a domain consistent all different over as
   many values as variables) whose variables are terms of the objective's
   sum also becomes clauses (item 293): the linear forms that define the
   objective (its sum and the blocks of a split sum) no longer count as
   other constraints of its variables
   (-Djacop.lcg.alldiffClauses.objective=false turns this off). On the
   25x25 sudokus of sudoku_opt (MiniZinc Challenge 2022), whose objective
   is a signed sum of all 625 cells, the 75 all different propagators had
   stayed; with clauses the same fixed search runs about 2x faster
   (p20 26 -> 11 s, p23 364 -> 203 s; 9 -> 11 of 18 puzzles proved at
   600 s). It turns on for no instance of the wide benchmark set.
   New option -Dorg.jacop.fz.firstFailTie=span (off by default) breaks the
   ties of first_fail between the variables with the fewest values by the
   smallest max - min + 1 (new comparator org.jacop.search.SmallestSpan),
   as OR-Tools CP-SAT's CHOOSE_MIN_DOMAIN_SIZE ranks them. The variable
   chosen still has the smallest domain. On sudoku_opt p23 it gives the
   tree of CP-SAT's fixed search (668k -> 74k failures, as CP-SAT's 69k);
   on the 102 wide instances with a first_fail search it makes 18 worse
   and 9 better at 600 s, so it is not the default.

317. AllDifferentLists(x, except) takes excepted tuples (XCSP3 allDifferent
   over lists with except): two rows may be equal when their tuple is one
   of them. A fixed row with an excepted tuple is not hashed, so it neither
   fails nor prunes; explanations are unchanged. JaCoP's MiniZinc library
   defines fzn_xcsp_all_different_lists_except with it (the
   radsz/xcsp-competition include has the pairwise decomposition). No
   main-track XCSP3 instance of 2022-2026 uses the except form. Tests:
   SingleConstraintTest and LcgFuzzTest compare it with the decomposition;
   ConsistencyChecker has AllDifferentLists cases (no wrong pruning over
   230,000 nodes; forward checking, so no consistency level is claimed).

318. DifferenceCycles finds the arcs that a literal fixed to a value makes
    hold by binary search over the literal's arcs sorted by value, and keeps
    a flag for each conditional arc in its potential, so that neither the
    literal events nor the potential repair test the literals of all arcs.
    Search and failures are the same in fixed search. The constraint itself
    took 19.6 % of solver time on word-equations (MZNC 2024) and now 5.1 %
    (1.27x more nodes per second at 60 s, 1.11x at 600 s). On the 85
    wide-set instances that post it, at 600 s, both runs solve the same
    instances (57 by default, 62 with -f) and the times and timed-out
    objectives are within noise.

319. fzn-jacop searches before an expensive root LP. When the first solve
   of the linear relaxation is large (its rows and the rows of the other
   constraints that it tries, times those rows and the columns: at least
   1,000,000 tableau entries, -Dorg.jacop.fz.lpProbeSize), a fixed search
   first runs for 200 ms (-Dorg.jacop.fz.lpProbe, 0 turns it off) with the
   relaxation suspended. If it has not finished by then, it starts again
   from the root, where the relaxation is solved as without the probe:
   under LCG by a one-time restart of the SAT core to level 0
   (CdclSolver.restartAt, LcgSolver.atRootAfter), which keeps the learnt
   clauses, and otherwise by a RestartSearch of two runs
   (ProbeCalculator), which keeps the bound of the best solution. Easy
   instances no longer pay for a root LP and cut rounds that they do not
   need: nside MEDIUM_5_3 of the 2026 Challenge spent 1.3 s in them and
   0.04 s in search. Free search (-f) is unchanged. On the 62 instances of
   the wide benchmark set where it fires, 600 s, fixed search: 33 proved
   both ways, 0.927 time geomean on them (hoist PU_2_8_1 19.2 -> 4.3 s,
   nside MEDIUM_5_3 3.6 -> 1.9 s, harmony canon 179 -> 117 s); orthorio
   challenge_12 loses its only solution, found at 400 s without the probe.

320. New constraint CostNetworkBound (org.jacop.constraints.cfn): a lower
   bound of an objective that sums unary and binary cost tables, the form
   of weighted constraint satisfaction problems (frequency assignment,
   satellite photograph selection, protein design). At the root the cost
   network is reparametrised by equivalence-preserving transformations:
   variables tied by bijective hard constraints (|x - y| = k) are merged,
   then block coordinate ascent of the dual of the local polytope (MPLP;
   A. Globerson and T. Jaakkola, NIPS 2007), in scaled integers, so the
   bound is exact for any rounding, moves costs to the unary functions
   (soft arc consistency and virtual arc consistency, M. C. Cooper, S. de
   Givry, M. Sanchez, T. Schiex, M. Zytnicki and T. Werner, Artificial
   Intelligence 174 (2010) 449-478; H. Montalbano, S. de Givry and G.
   Katsirelos, arXiv 2509.17706, 2025). During search the bound is node
   consistency of the reparametrised network with forward checking of
   its binary costs, and a value whose cost lifts the bound past the
   objective's other bound is removed; reasons for lazy clause generation
   cite the removals of the cheaper values (and the values of the fixed
   neighbours), lifted by the slack. -Dorg.jacop.fz.costNetwork=true
   (default false) finds such objectives in FlatZinc (tables and
   expressions over at most two variables per term) and posts the bound
   when its root bound is above the objective's root bound after the
   linear relaxation and some cost is binary. On the MiniZinc Challenge
   proteindesign12 instances at 600 s, -f proves 13 of 15 instead of 9
   (geometric mean time 0.17 on those both prove) and default search 10
   of 15 at 240 s instead of 3; on celar graph and scen instances the
   proven bound rises from 0 to the network's (graph11 2712, scen07
   21023, graph05 221 = optimum) with default search at 240 s. SPOT5
   (unary costs only) and CELAR6/7 SUB (root bound 0) are left alone by
   the gate. Of the 187 optimisation instances of the wide benchmark set,
   the detector reads cost terms in 52 and 1 passes the gate.

321. Under --lcg, a table's explanations remember the values the reason
   already covers: a tuple with such a value is dead at no cost, where
   each tuple re-evaluated its values and their alternatives before. The
   reasons are unchanged. On the crosswords grid-05.04 instance
   explanations took 39 % of the time and the search runs 1.18x the
   nodes in 240 s. On the 49 wide-set instances with a table at 600 s,
   default search solves 32 instead of 31 (time 0.985 on both solved)
   and -f 37 = 37 (time 0.992).

322. TimeIndexedBound gets arc-flow blocks for bin packing
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.arcFlow=true, off by default): each bin of a Binpacking
   constraint is a network over its reachable loads, with an arc per
   distinct item weight in decreasing order (so a multiset of items is one
   path) and a sink arc per load value, carrying one unit of flow; its flow
   projects onto the convex hull of the bin's knapsacks (J. M. Valerio de
   Carvalho, Exact solution of bin-packing problems using column
   generation and branch-and-bound, Annals of Operations Research 86
   (1999) 629-659; M. Delorme and M. Iori, Enhanced pseudo-polynomial
   formulations for bin packing and cutting stock problems, INFORMS
   Journal on Computing 32(1) (2020) 101-119; column elimination, A.
   Yamin, W.-J. van Hoeve and T. Ralphs, arXiv 2602.22589). The FlatZinc
   front end (LoadFunctionDetector) finds the variables the model defines
   from a load alone (int_lin_eq over two variables, plus, minus, times,
   max, min, abs, pow, element over constant tables) and gives them exact
   rows y = sum(L) g(L) [load = L] over the sink arcs, which carry the hull
   to the objective: the squared distance of a load to its band in gbac,
   the loss table of a slab in steelmillslab. Without these rows the flow
   moves nothing. Arcs and columns are capped (-Dorg.jacop.lp.arcFlow.arcs,
   .columns); the solves get their own pivot and time limits (.firstMs,
   .solveMs, .pivotsPerColumn). The FlatZinc statistics print tiArcs.
   Two fixes apply to the time-indexed bound too: a solve is used only
   while the current bounds lie within the root bounds it assumed (free
   search goes back above the level where they were captured), and a
   failure is explained by the objective below the solve's bound instead
   of the objective limit the solve assumed; a reused solve had assumed
   a looser limit, often the root one, and the probes of core-guided lower
   bound runs then learnt an unsound clause (a wrong optimum of gbac
   reduced_UD9 under -f). On the 46 instances of the MiniZinc Challenge
   gbac, mapping, steelmillslab, team-assignment, ihtc and kidney-exchange
   problems and the 7 wide benchmark set instances that post bin packing,
   at 600 s with model search, the proven bound rises on 17 and falls on
   none, solved 21 = 21 (gbac mean gap 0.932 -> 0.323; gbac
   reduced_UD2 and reduced_UD6 bounds reach the optimum, UD4 0 -> 324,
   steelmillslab bench_2_19 0 -> 34); with -f at 240 s it rises on 11 and
   falls on 5 (gbac instances whose first solve does not finish), solved
   28 = 28.

323. After the search before an expensive root LP (317), the first solve of
   the linear relaxation keeps the rows of the other constraints as a
   first solve at the start of that search would: the trial compares the
   bounds with and without them at the variable bounds the search started
   from, not at those it ended with. Facts found by the short search could
   make 1,859 extra rows of mqueens n11 raise the bound from 0 to 1, and
   keeping them cost 12 s of LP in search (14.2 s against 3.2 s).

324. The dominance nogoods of Lee and Zhong (org.jacop.fz.dominance, 5.0
   entry on DominanceNogoods) are on by default for fixed search; they stay
   off under -f and --proof, and org.jacop.fz.dominance=true/false
   overrides. On the 39 MiniZinc Challenge instances where they fire
   (trucking, spot5, shortest_path, pattern-set-mining, unit-commitment,
   table-layout, maximum-dag, aircraft-disassembly), at 600 s fixed search
   proves 26 instead of 24 (trucking 13 in 6 s, 14 in 4 s) and improves 10
   objectives, 1 worse by 0.006 %; under -f they are neutral or worse (31 =
   31 proved, 3 instances better, 6 worse, time 1.078), so -f keeps them
   off. The pass runs on 156 of the 240 wide-set instances, 0.06 s on
   average and at most 0.58 s; on easy instances solved at the root it
   adds about 0.1 s. Its memo of moves is now an array instead of a boxed
   map (pattern-set-mining-k2 audiology 0.30 s -> 0.12 s, same nogoods).
   References: J. H. M. Lee and A. Z. Zhong, Automatic Generation of
   Dominance Breaking Nogoods for a Class of Constraint Optimization
   Problems, IJCAI 2020; Exploiting Functional Constraints in Automatic
   Dominance Breaking for Constraint Optimization, CP 2022.

325. The FlatZinc front end removes the control b of a half-reified
   constraint b -> C when b appears nowhere else but as a positive literal
   of clauses, as bounded variable elimination does in SAT preprocessing
   (Een and Biere, Effective preprocessing in SAT through variable and
   clause elimination, SAT 2005) and as CP-SAT's presolve does with
   enforcement literals (Perron, Didier and Gay, The CP-SAT-LP solver, CP
   2023). When C is a literal x = t or x != t of a 0/1 variable x, b is
   replaced by that literal in its clauses and b -> C is dropped. When b's
   only clause is l \/ b, the clause is dropped and not l -> C is posted,
   which for a positive literal l is the new constraint ImpliesNot(l, C) (l
   = 0 => C) or XplusClteqZImpliedNot. Solve-item and output variables are
   kept. On ma-path-finding g16_p10_a10 this removes 92,560 of the 186,511
   variables and posts 96,846 instead of 140,792 constraints and 3,040
   instead of 49,410 clauses. It fires on 90 of the 240 instances of the
   wide benchmark set (MiniZinc Challenge 2008-2026); at 600 s it solves 68
   of them as before (seat-moving sm-10-13-00 proved, cable-tree-wiring
   A041 not within 600 s), time geomean 0.99 on the instances both solve;
   with -f it solves 74 as before, 4 % faster in geomean (vrplc9_5_10_s2
   112 -> 30 s, still-life-wastage 13 127 -> 83 s; steelmillslab bench_19_6
   13 -> 23 s). -Dorg.jacop.fz.enforcementInlining=false turns it off; it
   is off under --proof and when all solutions are asked for.

326. The FlatZinc front end checks the pigeonhole count of agreements
   (M. Plotkin, IRE Trans. Information Theory 6 (1960) 445-450; B. M.
   Smith, "Symmetry and search in a network design problem", CPAIOR 2005,
   on counting arguments for codes and designs). Where a group of m
   variables over q values has an equality or difference indicator for
   every pair, at least r C(b+1,2) + (q-r) C(b,2) of the pairs are equal
   (m = bq + r). The detector sums these bounds and the model's rows that
   bound agreements from above (int_lin_le over the indicators) with all
   multipliers one; when the sum is infeasible the model fails at load.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.agreementCount=true (default), false turns it off,
   rows also posts the bounds as constraints (slower: with the root LP
   they make the LP run at every node). On the XCSP 2024 Hamming
   instances at 600 s, 20-10-05-09 and 30-15-07-14 (no 2024 solver solved
   it) are proved unsatisfiable in 1.4 and 2.9 s and 20-10-03-08 in 2.5 s
   instead of 41 s; the detector also fires on EFPA, TravelingTournament,
   SocialGolfers and three wide-set instances without changing the search.

327. New constraint BinaryBitTable(x, y, tuples): a table of two variables
   kept domain consistent with bit-sets, AC3-bit with residues (C.
   Lecoutre, J. Vion, Enforcing arc consistency using bitwise operations,
   Constraint Programming Letters 2 (2008) 21-35). The supports of each
   value are a bit-set over the values of the other variable; a revision
   costs about a word per value and trails only the domains, where the
   compact table trails the words of its tuple set. Under LCG it explains
   a removal by the removals of the supports, with the bound atoms of the
   other variable for supports beyond its bounds. The FlatZinc front end
   posts it for positive tables of two variables whose ranges span at most
   4096 values, and for negative ones whose domains hold at most 1M pairs
   (on the pairs they allow). On XCSP 2024 Subisomorphism it searches the
   same tree as the compact table at 3.4 times the nodes a second without
   LCG and 1.9 times with LCG; FRB 3x. -Dorg.jacop.fz.binaryBitTable=false
   turns it off.

328. The XorSystem detector of the FlatZinc front end also reads linear
   equations whose odd-coefficient terms are 0/1 variables or variables of
   a single parity: sum(a_i * x_i) = s gives the GF(2) row of the x_i with
   odd a_i, its right-hand side the parity of s. Lights Out puzzles (each
   cell's neighbour sum in {1, 3, 5}) are then solved by Gauss-Jordan
   elimination at the root (M. Soos, K. Nohl and C. Castelluccia,
   Extending SAT solvers to cryptographic problems, SAT 2009; Lights
   Out: K. Sutner, Linear
   cellular automata and the Garden-of-Eden, Math. Intelligencer 11
   (1989) 49-53). XCSP 2024 LitPuzzle at 600 s with -f: 9 of 10 proved
   instead of 5 (sizes 20, 25, 40 and 50 in 1-21 s), LitPuzzle-30 376
   instead of 414. A plain sum of 0/1 variables equal to a constant is left
   to the other propagators. Of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark
   set, it fires on whirlpool d29 and d30 only (unsolved both ways).

329. Under free search an objective that the core-guided phase cannot take
   (no linear definition: the index of an element, a maximum, a product)
   goes, after 100,000 conflicts of branch and bound with lower-bound
   runs, to dichotomic search on its bound until the bound meets the best
   solution, as CP-SAT's objective shaving does (L. Perron, F. Didier and
   S. Gay, The CP-SAT-LP solver, CP 2023). XCSP 2024 FoolSolitaire at
   600 s: 17 of 17 proved instead of 13. On the 27 instances of the wide
   benchmark set where it can fire (-f, 600 s) nothing is solved or lost
   and no family gets worse. -Djacop.lcg.bisectUncored=false turns it
   off, -Djacop.lcg.bisectUncoredAfter sets the conflicts; statistic
   lcgBisectUncored.

330. The LU factorization of JaLP takes a column singleton as its pivot
   whatever its size against its row (Suhl and Suhl 1990): such a
   column eliminates nothing below it. Before, a triangular basis with
   many small column singletons took a quadratic pivot search: 8 s per
   factorization on the LP relaxation of MIPLIB 2017 app1-2 (the solve
   from over 120 s to 14 s). On the 687 LPs of the LP bench 644 = 644
   are solved; on the wide-set instances where the change applies, -f
   connect 0086_02 takes 7 s instead of 52 s, wordpress Offers500 15 s
   instead of 221 s.

331. The route neighbourhoods of free search (ROUTES, RELATED) now run on
   models whose successors are numbered from 0, such as the XCSP3 VRP_LC
   models through MiniZinc: there the subcircuit is posted on copies
   succ + 1 that are not search variables, and AdaptiveLns built no
   routes. It maps such copies back to the successors and reads the arc
   costs from the elements on them. Customers that must share a route (a
   label element label[succ[v]] = label[v] with two labels equal, such as
   a pickup and its delivery) are removed together by Shaw removal. On the
   10 VRP_LC instances of the XCSP 2024 competition with -f, 240 s, two
   seeds, 11-05-15-s4 reaches 332 instead of 352/346 and 13-11-20-s4 606
   instead of 634/606; at 600 s 11-05-15-s3 332 instead of 340; the
   others are equal. On the 13 wide-set instances with a circuit the
   change does not fire and the results are identical.

332. Under free search (-f) with lazy clause generation and a search
   annotation, once a first solution is known each turn of runs at
   restarts is the annotation, six runs of the SAT solver's own decisions
   on literals, then activity branching, instead of one run of each
   (--free-config lcg-literal-runs-solved=<n>, default 6;
   lcg-literal-runs=<n> before a solution, default 1). Literal runs can
   prove much faster than the others: on MiniZinc Challenge 2023
   multi-agent-graph-coverage rnd_n10, literal decisions alone prove the
   optimum with 34k-49k conflicts in 8-10 s (CP-SAT 9.15 -f -p 1: 31k,
   9 s) against 89k-111k conflicts in 26-32 s for the turns; first
   solutions keep the old turns, since literal runs alone lose them on
   cargo, linear-to-program and p1f. Wide benchmark set at 240 s: 186 ->
   189 solved, 5 families better and 3 worse, runtime on solved instances
   1.03 (instances solved within 5 s slower); 600 s on the 118
   optimisation instances not solved within 5 s by both: 78 -> 81 solved
   (celar graph11, crosswords grid-05.04, physician-scheduling
   instance10), 7 families better and 2 worse, runtime on solved
   instances 0.88 (vrplc9 112 -> 18 s, javarouting trip_7_2 363 -> 139 s,
   workforce-alloc 202 130 -> 45 s; steelmillslab bench_19_6 12 -> 30 s,
   evm-super-compilation 49 -> 108 s).

333. -Djacop.virtualClock=true (default false) runs the clock of lazy
   clause generation on its work: the time limit, the shares of the -f
   phases, the time of the neighbourhood runs, the delayed root probe
   and the check of LCG's speed (lcg-slow-rate, which runs in the search
   instead of a thread that sleeps) count jacop.lcg.workPerMs (2000) of
   the solver's work per millisecond, so a run makes the same choices
   and stops at the same point whatever the load of the machine. It is
   meant for benchmarks of search changes: the wall time of a run then
   differs from its limit (median 1.04 times the wall-clock run on the
   wide-quick set), and the watchdog waits 10 times the time-out
   (jacop.virtualClock.wallFactor). Two runs of the 62 wide-quick
   instances at 240 s with -f, two at a time, ended identically (nodes,
   failures, work, answer) on 58 instances, against 45 with the wall
   clock. The rest come from time limits outside LCG: symmetry detection
   (ptv, steelmillslab) and search without learning after LCG gave way
   (celar graph11, whirlpool d29).

334. Free search with lazy clause generation (-f) no longer decides only
   0/1 variables before a first solution: once 2,000 conflicts pass
   without a solution, its activity runs take turns at restarts with and
   without the 0/1 variables first, until the first solution
   (--free-config lcg-booleans-first=until-solution, the new default;
   lcg-booleans-first-after sets the conflicts; on restores the old
   order). On the XCSP 2024 traveling tournament with 24 to 40 teams,
   Booleans first decided the whole home/away pattern before any
   opponent and found no solution in 600 s; now the first one comes in
   30 to 60 s. Adaptive LNS has a new kind LINES, which relaxes the
   scopes of random all-different constraints (such as the rounds of a
   round-robin schedule) with the variables that small constraints tie to
   them alone.

335. New constraint ArgMaxMonotone(x, f, first, index, offset): index is the
   first position k of the largest f[k](x[k]), for nondecreasing functions
   f[k] given as tables. It runs the ArgMax rules (Gange and Stuckey, CP
   2020) through the functions, in the spirit of views (Schulte and Tack,
   CP 2005), and under LCG explains with the bounds of the x[k]. The
   FlatZinc front end posts it for an arg_max whose values are defined by
   y = m * v + c and v = e[p] over a constant array: MiniZinc flattens
   arg_max(t)(a[h, t] * e[p[t]]) with one variable per value, whose bounds
   ArgMax explained, so learnt clauses about one arg_max did not apply to
   another. On the 15 tower instances of the MiniZinc Challenge (2020,
   2022, 2025), the only Challenge model with arg_max, -f at 600 s proves
   10 instead of 9 (tower_070_070_15_070-08 in 250 s, unproved before;
   learnt clauses 72 -> 38 literals) and 6 others about twice as fast.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.argMaxFunctions=false turns it off; it is off under
   --proof.

336. In free search with lazy clause generation, the runs of the default
   branching of a model without search annotation place the rectangles
   of its diffn constraints first, one at a time, bottom-left: the
   rectangle that can start first in the first dimension (ties to the one
   that can start first in the second) gets its origins and lengths set
   to their smallest values, then the next (the bottom-left rule of
   B. S. Baker, E. G. Coffman and R. L. Rivest, "Orthogonal packings in
   two dimensions", SIAM Journal on Computing 9(4), 1980; one rectangle
   at a time as in H. Simonis and B. O'Sullivan, "Search strategies for
   rectangle packing", CP 2008). Set times on the cumulative starts
   follows, as before. The rectangles are placed until the first
   solution (--free-config rect-placement=first, the default; always in
   every such run, off never). On the coal stockyard of XCSP 2024 Cargo,
   where stockpiles are rectangles of days by pad positions with a
   variable stay, set times alone found no solution of Cargo-16 and 20 in
   600 s and one of Cargo-19 at 220 s; with the placement, 132,939,
   114,274 and 112,584 at 600 s, below the best of the 2024 competition
   (248,754, 295,975 and 256,274 in 1800 s), and Cargo-12 24,404 instead
   of 27,966; the other 12 Cargo instances end the same. Placing in every
   run lost Cargo-07's proof and Cargo-10's best solution. None of the 14
   wide-set models with diffn is affected: all have a search annotation.
   A satisfaction model, where until the first solution is every run,
   does not place by default (XCSP 2023 SquarePackingSuite-23 and -27
   found no solution in 600 s with it, 146 s and 20-60 s without).

337. Symmetry breaking in the FlatZinc front end breaks the conditional
   symmetry of interchangeable values in a sequence ordered by
   precedences (new class org.jacop.fz.ConditionalValueSwaps), as in the
   patience game Black Hole (I. P. Gent, C. Jefferson, T. Kelsey, I.
   Lynce, I. Miguel, P. Nightingale, B. M. Smith and S. A. Tarim, "Search
   in the patience game 'Black Hole'", AI Communications 20(3), 2007;
   conditional symmetry: I. P. Gent, T. Kelsey, S. A. Linton, I.
   McDonald, I. Miguel and B. M. Smith, "Conditional symmetry breaking",
   CP 2005). The model has inverse(x, y), tables on x whose relation is
   the same after two values a and b are exchanged, and precedences
   y[u] < y[v]. Exchanging a and b in a solution keeps it a solution when
   the predecessors of the value moved forward still come before it and
   the successors of the value moved back still come after it, so
   y[b] < y[a] -> OR(u before a) y[u] > y[b] \/ OR(s after b) y[s] < y[a]
   is posted for a the value the lex-leader order prefers (Crawford,
   Ginsberg, Luks and Roy, KR 1996). The constraints are lex-leader
   constraints over x in the order of the other symmetry breaking
   constraints, whose first variables are those of the search
   annotation: with int_search(x, input_order, indomain_min, complete)
   the search finds the same first solution as without them, takes the
   same decisions, and fails sooner, so fixed search still follows the
   annotation. On the 25 black-hole instances of the MiniZinc Challenges
   2009-2025 at 600 s, fixed search solves 25 instead of 17
   (2025 layout_3 40 s, layout_14 17 s, layout_17 9 s, all three unknown
   before; 2009 instance 19 proved unsatisfiable in 38 s); the 17 that
   both solve take 2.1x less time in geomean, and the 12 satisfiable ones
   among them print the same first solution. It fires on 2 of the 240
   instances of the wide benchmark set, both black-hole. It is used for
   satisfaction problems that ask for one solution, not under free search
   (-f), which solves all 25 within 13 s without it and was up to 5.8x
   slower with it on the 9 hard ones; -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetrySwaps=false turns it off. The
   statistic symmetryValueSwaps counts the pairs of values.

338. Lazy clause generation follows a search annotation (or the default
   branching of a model without one) with fewer decisions. Such a run
   decides its variables in a fixed order, so after a backjump it took
   again every decision between the backjump level and the conflict:
   gardner_dinner made 2,242 decisions for 90 conflicts, against 266 nodes
   without learning. The runs that follow the annotation now backtrack one
   level after a backjump over more than 10 levels, assert the learnt
   literal on the level kept, and assert it again if a later backtrack
   goes below it (chronological backtracking: A. Nadel and V. Ryvchin,
   "Chronological Backtracking", SAT 2018, LNCS 10929; the missed lower
   implications handled lazily as in A. Moehle and A. Biere, "Backing
   Backtracking", SAT 2019, LNCS 11628; the threshold is lowered from
   their 100). gardner_dinner takes 193 decisions. Backtracking one level
   after every conflict lost the only solution of 2026 orthorio
   challenge_12 in 600 s, since with first_fail a short backjump reorders
   the decisions after the learnt literal. --free-config lcg-chrono=<n>
   sets the threshold (0 always, -1 never); the statistic
   lcgChronoBacktracks counts these backtracks.
   When the annotation has fixed all its variables, the run also takes the
   assignment as a solution if the printed variables and the objective
   are fixed, every variable left has no constraint that is not entailed,
   and every clause is satisfied; the variables left take their smallest
   values. Before, activity decisions went on over the variables left: on
   a_card_trick_1 (a 4 x 13 card layout with set channels), 1,014
   decisions on 0/1 variables that no constraint used after the 29 of the
   annotation, against 37 nodes without learning. It now takes 29. It is
   off with neighbourhood search, presolve and proof logging; the
   statistic lcgCompletedModels counts these solutions. On the 240
   wide-set instances at 600 s, default search solves 170 instead of 169
   and finds a solution of 203 instead of 202, 7 families better and none
   worse (ptv pax_period_20_3b proved in 486 s, unit-commitment
   L12-T48G15L10 a first solution, physician-scheduling instance10
   10810 -> 9015); free search (-f) solves the same 193, 3 families
   better and 2 worse.

339. Symmetry breaking renames the elements of set variables: set
   variables that only set operations, set comparisons other than the
   lexicographic ones and cardinalities constrain (set_union,
   set_intersect, set_diff, set_symdiff, set_eq, set_ne, set_subset,
   set_superset and their reified forms, disjoint, set_card), with no
   constant set among their arguments, form a set value space, whose
   values may be permuted together in all its sets. Their elements join
   one vertex per value in the variable graph, so the automorphisms find
   the value symmetry next to the symmetry of the set variables, and the
   card-symbol matrix of a model with both gets lex chains on its rows
   and its columns (double lex, P. Flener, A. Frisch, B. Hnich, Z. Kiziltan,
   I. Miguel, J. Pearson and T. Walsh, "Breaking row and column symmetries
   in matrix models", CP 2002, LNCS 2470). As for the set symmetries of
   item 241, only models whose search annotation branches on set
   variables; -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetrySetValues=false turns it off. Of the
   65 Challenge instances with set variables it fires on the 5 of 2021
   monomatch (Dobble cards), where it proves the 3 UNSAT instances in
   under 3 s with and without -f (before, at 600 s: n3 in 50 s with -f
   and 155 s without, the two n4 unknown) and finds the n5 and n6
   solutions in 2.0-3.5 s with -f (n6 without -f 85 -> 14 s).

340. Satisfaction models get a second linear relaxation, solved once at
   the root and then removed, that can refute them. It holds the value
   encodings of the counting, all-different and element constraints
   (with the count bounds of global_cardinality), the model's linear and
   clause rows restated over the value columns, and the pair conflicts
   merged into cliques (Atamturk, Nemhauser and Savelsbergh, Conflict
   graphs in solving integer programming problems, EJOR 2000). A control
   b of a half-reified b -> x != c (MiniZinc's translation of a
   disequality in a disjunction) is bounded by 1 - [x = c], and the z of
   not z -> x != c by [x = c], so the clause (x != c) \/ (y != c) becomes
   [x = c] + [y = c] <= 1. Such counts are pigeonhole arguments, which
   resolution and so lazy clause generation refute only in exponentially
   many steps (Haken, The intractability of resolution, TCS 1985). The
   main relaxation keeps these encodings only for an objective. The
   social golfers 08-04-11 of the XCSP 2024 competition (32 players in 8
   groups of 4 for 11 weeks: each player meets 3 others a week, 33 in
   all, but there are 31) were UNKNOWN at 600 s and are now refuted at
   the root in under 2 s; only LP-based solvers proved them in the
   competition. The root solve is capped at 2,500 pivots
   (org.jacop.lp.rootRefutationPivots). It fires on 353 of the 792 XCSP
   2022-2025 satisfaction instances (median 0.4 s more on the 2024 ones
   both versions solve) and on 15 of the 53 satisfaction instances of
   the wide benchmark set, with the same answers and, under -f, the same
   search trees. -Dorg.jacop.lp.rootRefutation=false turns it off;
   --search-statistics prints lpRefutationRows, lpRefutationCliques,
   lpRefutation (refuted, feasible, limit), lpRefutationTime and
   lpRefutationPivots.

341. The FlatZinc front end merges common subexpressions before posting
   (as CP-SAT's presolve does, DetectDuplicateConstraints: Perron, Didier
   and Gay, The CP-SAT-LP solver, CP 2023; and the duplicate-row presolver
   of MIP solvers: Achterberg, Bixby, Gu, Rothberg and Weninger, Presolve
   reductions in mixed integer programming, INFORMS Journal on Computing
   32(2), 2020). Two constraints that define their outputs by the same function
   of the same arguments (reified comparisons, Boolean connectives,
   int_times/plus/min/max/div/mod/abs, reified linear constraints,
   elements) give one output, the other becomes its alias; a plain
   equality of two variables (int_eq, bool_eq, x - y = 0, a conjunction
   of one literal) aliases them; a repeated constraint is posted once, and
   a linear constraint adds the coefficients of a repeated variable. It
   repeats until nothing changes (at most 6 rounds). It changes the model
   of 56 of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark set
   (community-detection rnd_n100: 5,190 -> 211 constraints, 6.6 -> 4.1 s).
   At 600 s against the build with enforcement inlining it solves 44 of
   them as before in default mode (time geomean 1.007, cable-tree-wiring
   A041 finds a worse best value on another search tree) and 48 as before
   with -f (geomean 1.002). -Dorg.jacop.fz.cse=false turns it off; it is
   off under --proof.

342. JaLP chooses the leaving row of the dual simplex by gap^1.2 / weight
   instead of gap^2 / weight (dual steepest edge) on LPs shaped like
   multicommodity flows: at least 5000 columns, at most 2.2 nonzeros per
   column and at most 4 columns per row (the less sensitive dual steepest
   edge of Q. Huangfu, High performance simplex solver, PhD thesis,
   University of Edinburgh, 2013, section 3.3). Squaring the gap favours
   dense rows of the tableau, whose pivots lose the hypersparse solves.
   On the 659 LPs of the LP bench at 120 s: 643 -> 644 solved, 0 wrong,
   solve time 0.98 overall and 0.83 on the Mittelmann LPs, 18 LPs faster
   and 1 slower (pds-30 39 -> 20 s, pds-50 112 -> 57 s, pds-60 proved in
   74 s, the n37 network design LPs 0.45-0.8; snp-02-004-104 1.9 slower).
   Without the shape gate it is 1.13 slower over the bench. On the 11
   wide-set runs where it applies (600 s) nothing is solved or lost.
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.linearScore=on|off overrides the choice.

343. The -p portfolio can split one depth-first search over its threads by
   work stealing with recomputation (Schulte, "Parallel search made
   simple", TRICS 2000; Chu, Schulte and Stuckey, "Confidence-based work
   stealing in parallel constraint programming", CP 2009): a thread that
   runs out of work takes an open right branch of another thread, given as
   the decisions that lead to it, replays them in its own store and
   searches below; the best bound is shared. With free search and at least
   4 threads two threads do this by default, CP search (--lcg off) on the
   model's annotation in place of the last two settings; option
   --free-config portfolio-dfs-split=<threads> sets their number, 0 turns
   it off. On the 240 instances of the wide set at 600 s (-f -p 4): 203
   solved instead of 200, 7 families better, 3 worse; MiniZinc Challenge
   2022 generalized-peacable-queens n9_q5 is proved in 484 s (not proved
   in 600 s before, CP-SAT -p 4 264 s) and n8_q3 in 31 s instead of 53 s.
   A split thread runs the propagation of the root before its first cube,
   so the linear relaxation keeps root reduced costs from the root and not
   from that cube. Statistics portfolioDfsSplitThreads, portfolioDfsSplits,
   portfolioDfsCubesClosed, portfolioDfsSplitWaitMillis.

344. The FlatZinc front end rewrites a variable s defined by a sum
   s = sum(i) a[i] * x[i] that is used otherwise only in half-reifications
   b -> s = m or b -> s <= m, where m is the least value of the sum: each
   becomes the clauses not b \/ x[i] = v[i], with v[i] the bound at which
   the term is least, and the sum is no longer posted (an output s gets its
   value from the terms when a solution is printed; under presolve the sum
   stays, woken only when a term is fixed). This is the presolve rule that a
   linear constraint at its minimum activity fixes every term at its
   minimum (CP-SAT, OR-Tools cp_model_presolve.cc), under the enforcement
   literal. XCSP3 translations write it as or(ne(x, v), eq(aux, 0)) with aux
   the sum of the variables around x; in SameQueensKnights (XCSP 2024) the
   2,450 to 11,250 sums of up to 300 terms took 80 % of the time. At 600 s
   all 15 instances improve or stay: b-10 is proved optimal (490 s), and 8
   instances reach values above the best of the 2024 competition (b-75 133
   instead of 94, field best 86; b-70 125 instead of 110, field 115). It
   fires on none of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark set.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.zeroSum=false turns it off; it is off under --proof.

345. A root solve of the linear relaxation of an optimisation model in the
   sparse simplex (the first solve, or the trial of the extra rows such as
   value encodings) that stops at its pivot or work budget now goes on
   within half of the time a large relaxation gets for its root solves
   (org.jacop.lp.largeRootShare of the time limit). Such a solve gave no
   bound and its rows were dropped. On Drinking-010000 of the XCSP 2024
   competition (13,142 rows and 12,570 extra rows) the trial stopped at its
   budget and the bound stayed 0; it now takes 0.8 s more and the bound is
   1,000, the optimum, as Drinking-020000, a large relaxation, already had.
   It fires on none of the optimisation instances of the wide benchmark
   set. -Dorg.jacop.lp.rootExtendShare=0 turns it off; the statistic
   lpRootExtendTime gives its time.

351. Value symmetry breaking also looks at index variables of element
   constraints: when no value symmetry is found on the model as written,
   each element constraint x = A[k] is read as k selecting a position of
   A, so that permuting the values of k permutes the arrays it selects
   from (and with them their rows). A value symmetry found on k is broken
   by value precedence (Law and Lee, CP 2004; Puget, CP 2005, "Breaking
   all value symmetries in surjection problems"). The pass gets at most
   half of the time left for symmetry detection. On the XCSP 2026
   competition it proves HIPP (haplotype inference by pure parsimony)
   I0-gus-15, I1-gus-14, I2-gus-21, brown-I0-bro-15, brown-I1-bro-14 and
   brown-I2-bro-21 unsatisfiable in 1.5 to 12 s at 600 s (none before;
   I2-gus-21 was proved by no competition solver), and Antichain2 085 and
   086 in 58 and 82 s (198 and 244 s before). It fires on CoveringArray
   (XCSP 2023) and Wordpress (XCSP 2024) and on 3 of the 240 instances of
   the wide benchmark set (wordpress, open-stacks, stable-goods).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetryIndexValues=false turns it off.

352. The FlatZinc front end posts an "exists k" pattern of XCSP3
   translations, an index variable k used only by element constraints
   r_i = A_i[k] whose results are used only by one linear relation
   C(r_1, ..., r_n), as the disjunction over the positions p of
   C(A_1[p], ..., A_n[p]): half-reified linear constraints b_p ->
   C(A_1[p], ...) and the clause of the b_p (at most 64 positions,
   org.jacop.fz.indexDisjunction.max). k and the r_i are no longer posted
   and get their values from the first position that holds when a
   solution is printed. Before, the element constraints only propagated
   bounds of the r_i through k. On Antichain2 of the XCSP 2026
   competition (with the value symmetry above) 100-3-10 is solved in 37 s
   instead of 172 s and 120-3-10 in 85 s instead of 162 s. It fires on none
   of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark set or of the XCSP 2022-2025
   instances. -Dorg.jacop.fz.indexDisjunction=false turns it off; it is
   off under --proof and presolve.

337. The linear relaxation gets the rows of an assignment model for
   conjunctions b_v <-> s /\ [x = v] of a Boolean s with the value
   Booleans of a variable x: sum_v b_v = s (<= s when the values do not
   cover the domain of x), and y = d + sum_v (v + c - d) b_v for
   y = if s then x + c else d (ConditionalSelect). Under lazy clause
   generation the conjunctions are read from their clause rows. Count over
   0/1 variables gets its exact row. Before, these were big-M rows only,
   which let a fractional s keep every b_v at 0. On ihtc-2024-kletzander
   (MiniZinc Challenge 2025) the root bound rises from 0 to 9,151 on
   test03 (optimum 9,385, proved with -f in 5 s at 600 s, before never)
   and to 13,639 on test05 (bound 13,798 at 600 s, before 4,128). Of the
   240 instances of the wide benchmark set they reach 10, with no change
   in solved instances at 600 s. -Dorg.jacop.lp.selectedValues=false and
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.booleanCount=false turn them off.

338. Free search (-f) with lazy clause generation that has no solution and
   makes fewer than 30 conflicts per second at lcg-slow-at gives way to
   free search without learning (--free-config lcg-slow-unsolved=<rate>,
   0 = off). Before, a slow LCG without a solution never gave way. On
   ihtc-2024-marte i14 (MiniZinc Challenge 2025) LCG makes about 10
   conflicts per second and found no solution in 1200 s; now -f finds
   24,783 at 600 s and 18,747 at 1200 s (CP-SAT par 16,165 in the
   Challenge). Fixed search (without -f), which also runs under LCG with
   the decisions of the search annotation, gives way in the same case to
   the annotation's search without learning; with a solution it keeps
   LCG. On i14 it finds 35,036 at 600 s, where it found none before
   (Gecode fixed search 35,626 in the Challenge). Of the 240 wide
   instances each fires on one more (rcpsp_max psp_ubo500_33, no solution
   either way).

339. Binpacking skips the sort and uses a closed form of Shaw's NoSum
   test (CP 2004) and one elimination and commitment pass when the items
   not yet placed have the same weight, with the same pruning.


340. Under -f, a model whose cost is the makespan of a disjunctive
   schedule (unary resources and precedences between the task starts,
   such as job shops, also with decomposed disjunctions) first runs the
   critical-path tabu search of local search alone, for half of the time
   left until it stalls for 5 s, without --free-config ls. On the 50x10
   job shops swv11-swv14 (240 s) the makespans drop by 2-13 % and beat
   CP-SAT; open shops (no precedences) and portfolio workers are left out.
   --free-config ls-auto=0 turns it off. Nowicki and Smutnicki, A fast
   taboo search algorithm for the job shop problem, Management Science
   1996; Dell'Amico and Trubian, Applying tabu search to the job-shop
   scheduling problem, Annals of OR 1993.

341. The linear relaxation rounds the row it proves its bound with: each cut
   round adds the mixed-integer rounding (MIR) cut of the rows weighted by
   their LP dual values, without the objective's own rows (a Chvatal-Gomory
   cut with the LP's multipliers, V. Chvatal, Edmonds polytopes and a
   hierarchy of combinatorial problems, Discrete Math. 4, 1973; MIR of an
   aggregated row as in H. Marchand and L. A. Wolsey, Aggregation and mixed
   integer rounding to solve MIPs, Oper. Res. 49(3), 2001). On covers with
   near-equal costs this row counts the sets a cover needs: MiniZinc
   Challenge 2021 wmsmc-int batch_0_case_115_instance_0 has root bound
   509,697 instead of 500,353 (optimum 509,704) and -f proves it in 10 s
   instead of not in 600 s. -Dorg.jacop.lp.dualAggregate=false turns it off.

342. The FlatZinc front end folds a variable z that is an affine function
   of another variable x into the linear constraint that uses it (free
   column substitution of MIP presolve: Achterberg, Bixby, Gu, Rothberg
   and Weninger, Presolve reductions in mixed integer programming,
   INFORMS Journal on Computing 32(2), 2020; as CP-SAT's presolve does:
   Perron, Didier and Gay, The CP-SAT-LP solver, CP 2023). z is defined
   by int_times(c, x, z), int_plus(x, c, z) or a two-term int_lin_eq
   with a unit coefficient on z, is not an output or solve variable, its
   domain allows every value of the function, and it occurs in one
   linear constraint besides its definition: the term a*z becomes a*p*x,
   a*q moves to the constant and the definition is not posted. A z in
   several rows stays, since with lazy clause generation it is a shared
   sub-term whose literals carry learning from row to row (code-generator
   mips_gobmk of the 2019 Challenge: 7 such variables in 49 rows, 34 ->
   63 s when folded; -Dorg.jacop.fz.affineFoldingRows=n allows n rows).
   It fires on 4 of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark set: nside
   EASY_200_50 folds 54,504 products by constants into its sums
   (propagators 61,579 -> 7,075), the same search tree in 85 -> 46 s at
   240 s; the other three are unchanged within noise.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.affineFolding=false turns it off; it is off under
   --proof.

343. The routes neighbourhood of the adaptive LNS (route removal on
   circuits and subcircuits) also frees the links between every route
   and its depots, so that a route can move to another vehicle.
   With identical vehicles and symmetry breaking such as "vehicle k
   unused implies vehicle k + 1 unused", a route other than the last
   could not be emptied while the routes after it kept their vehicles.
   XCSP 2024 VRP_LC-10-05-20-s3 at 600 s finds 267, 288 and 269 over
   three seeds instead of 288, 289 and 288 (the better solutions use
   one vehicle fewer; the 2024 competition best was 277), and MiniZinc
   Challenge 2026 vrplc10_5_15_s4 with -f 207 (the best known), 209 and
   216 instead of 216 three times. -Djacop.lns.depotLinks=false turns it off.

344. NetworkFlow prunes its arcs without the pivots of the network simplex
   when it can, with the same pruning. A residual arc (u, v) whose
   shortest residual cycle back from v to u has room for the flow the
   cost limit allows (limit / cycle cost, or all its capacity at cost 0)
   is decided by that shortest path alone, for arcs of any capacity, not
   only of capacity one; the other arcs are probed by successive shortest
   paths with node potentials instead of dual pivots (Ahuja, Magnanti and
   Orlin, Network Flows: Theory, Algorithms, and Applications, Prentice
   Hall 1993, ch. 9). The probing of arcs took 74 % of the time of -f on
   MiniZinc Challenge 2021 mapping ring_3. A test compares the domains
   with the pivot probes over 300 random networks and dives (20,000 when
   checked by hand). On the 30 mapping and nfc instances of the Challenge
   (2015-2022) at 600 s: -f proves the same 30, 0.85 the time (geometric
   mean, instances over 1 s; ring_3 106 -> 51 s, mesh_star_mpeg 87 -> 35 s,
   ring_mp3 41 -> 17 s, mesh_star_2 388 -> 248 s), default mode proves
   the same 27, 0.86 the time. -Dorg.jacop.netflow.generalArcs=false and
   -Dorg.jacop.netflow.shortestPaths=false bring back the probes.

345. Under lazy clause generation a constraint failure first hands the SAT
   solver the atoms its fixpoint recorded and had not pushed yet, and only
   then the conflict, whose clause cites those atoms instead of their
   explanations (as with literals created on demand in lazy clause
   generation, T. Feydy and P. J. Stuckey, "Lazy clause generation
   reengineered", CP 2009). Conflict analysis then resolves them lazily
   and can stop at one of them; before, each was replaced by its own
   explanation at once, down to atoms the solver had, and the conflict
   clauses of an Alldiff failure on ghoulomb had 406 literals on average
   (XplusYeqZ 187). Fixed search on ghoulomb makes 2x the conflicts in
   30 s. On the wide benchmark set with -f at 600 s (240 instances): 21
   better, 16 worse, families 5 better, 1 worse, geomean 0.934 over the
   144 solved by either (mapping mesh_star 128 -> 21 s, vrplc9_5_10_s2
   118 -> 24 s, community-detection Sampson 374 -> 83 s; fast-food ff58
   32 -> 114 s, and work-task-variation seed-8 lost its proof, a core
   phase that is cut by the clock and times out in 2 of 3 runs on the
   base too). On the 62 wide-quick instances at 240 s, -f: families
   7 better, 2 worse, geomean 0.80 (base against base 0.95); default
   mode: 4 better, 2 worse, geomean 0.90, one more solved. It is off with
   --proof; -Djacop.lcg.pushBeforeConflict=false turns it off
   (LcgSolver.setPushBeforeConflict). The statistic lcgHeldFailures
   counts the failures held back. With it, the shared tree of
   portfolio-split splits on the next decision below the first one when
   the first is on the objective, which is not shared; otherwise a
   knapsack whose first decision was always the objective never split.

346. CountValues and CountValuesBounds read again only the domains whose
   size changed since their last call, with the same pruning, and
   Assignment queues changed variables in arrays instead of a hashed set
   and map. On the 31 wide instances that post one of them (fixed
   search, 600 s) time is 0.976 of before over the 18 solved by both;
   ptv pax_period_20_2b 33.1 -> 28.9 s, and black-hole 15 and 19 search
   6.6 % and 3 % more nodes.
346. New float constraint PowPeqR(x, c, z), x^c = z for a constant c > 0:
   hull consistent for any sign of x with an integer c (an even c keeps the
   hole around 0, an odd c the sign), and for x >= 0 with a real c. The
   FlatZinc front end posts it for float_pow with a constant exponent (a
   negative one as 1 / x^-c), which MiniZinc emits for x^2.0 and pow; 425 of
   the 747 instances of the osolpro float set use it. Benhamou and Older,
   "Applying interval arithmetic to real, integer and boolean constraints",
   J. Logic Programming 32(1), 1997.

347. New float constraint HyperbolicPeqR(x, f, z) for f = sinh, cosh and
   tanh, hull consistent with outward rounding; the FlatZinc front end posts
   float_sinh, float_cosh, float_tanh and their inverses (the inverse as the
   same constraint with the arguments swapped) instead of the MiniZinc
   library decompositions through exp and ln, which use x twice and are not
   hull consistent. Benhamou, Goualard, Granvilliers and Puget, "Revising
   hull and box consistency", ICLP 1999.

348. New float contractor FloatCid (org.jacop.floats.search), 3B shaving and
   constructive interval disjunction (3BCID) run after propagation at every
   search node as a ConsistencyListener. The FlatZinc front end runs it on
   the float search variables of satisfaction problems (10 shaving slices,
   every variable at every node); -Dorg.jacop.floats.cid=s3b[,variablesPerNode]
   sets it and -Dorg.jacop.floats.cid=off turns it off. At 600 s it solves
   52 of the 59 satisfaction instances of the osolpro float set instead of
   45 (geometric mean of the times 0.92). It is off for optimisation, where
   it is slower. Lhomme, "Consistency techniques for numeric CSPs", IJCAI
   1993; Trombettoni and Chabert, "Constructive interval disjunction", CP
   2007.

349. Float propagation can skip narrowings smaller than a given part of the
   domain width (a weak fixpoint, against slow convergence):
   -Dorg.jacop.floats.minReduction=r or FloatDomain.setMinReduction(r),
   default 0 (off). Lhomme, IJCAI 1993.

350. New float contractor FloatNewton (org.jacop.floats.constraints), the
   interval Newton method on the square system formed by the float
   equations of a model (linear equalities, sums, products, powers, exp,
   ln, sin, cos): a Hansen-Sengupta step with the inverse of the midpoint
   Jacobian as preconditioner and Gauss-Seidel, evaluated in interval
   arithmetic with outward rounding, after propagation at every search node.
   The FlatZinc front end runs it, ahead of FloatCid, on the float search
   variables of satisfaction problems; -Dorg.jacop.floats.newton=false turns
   it off and =true also runs it for optimisation. At 240 s it solves 52 of
   the 59 satisfaction instances of the osolpro float set instead of 50 with
   FloatCid alone (geometric mean of the times 0.90).
   Hansen and Sengupta, "Bounding solutions of systems of equations using
   interval analysis", BIT 21(2), 1981.

347. Free search (-f) probes the root before lazy clause generation search
   by default: failed literals on the 0/1 variables and on the values of
   small domains, for 2 % of the time left and at most 1 s (new
   -Djacop.lcg.probe.cap=ms; -Djacop.lcg.probe=0 turns probing off, and
   an explicit -Djacop.lcg.probe=share keeps its uncapped budget as
   before). On java-routing of the MiniZinc Challenge (2013, 2021) it
   finds the parity of the positions of the stops and the stops that
   cannot follow each other, which propagation does not, and proves 8 of
   10 instances in 240 s instead of 5 (2.6 times faster over three
   seeds). The 62 wide-quick instances (-f, 240 s) take 2,609 s instead
   of 2,866 s in all and solve 52 instead of 51 (work-task-variation
   seed-8); easy instances where probing finds nothing are up to a few
   seconds slower, as the literals it makes move the search. Model
   search does not probe by default. The failed probes do not count in
   the weights of dom/wdeg, afc or activity (counted, they kept XCSP 2022
   BlockedQueens-56 from a solution in 600 s, 11-31 s without).

348. A solve of the linear relaxation in search that stops at its pivot or
   work limit now bounds the objective by its dual multipliers. They are
   not optimal but still valid: the exact certificate (Neumaier and
   Shcherbina, Safe bounds in linear and mixed-integer linear programming,
   Mathematical Programming 99, 2004) turns any multipliers into a sound
   bound and the same bounds reasoning as an optimal solve. Such solves
   used to give nothing. They are rare (0.1 % of the solves of the wide
   benchmark set, on 43 of 184 runs with a relaxation), but where they
   happen they prune: at 600 s on those runs the best solution of ptv
   pax_period_20_3b goes from 4324 to 3860 and of surface-based-tsp ch130
   from 8202 to 8032, and vaccine v857 is proved in 19.9 s instead of
   26.4 s. -Dorg.jacop.lp.anytimeBound=false turns it off; with -s -v the
   statistics lpAnytime, lpAnytimePrunings and lpAnytimeFailures give the
   stopped solves and what they pruned.

349. New redundant constraints at FlatZinc posting (PairCoverDetector): when
   pairs of items must share bins of bounded capacity (0/1 x[g][i], rows
   sum(i) x[g][i] <= c, demands sum(g) b[g] >= k with b[g] -> x[g][i] /\
   x[g][j]), each item i is in at least ceil(D(i) / (c - 1)) bins, D(i)
   its demanded pairs (B. M. Smith, Symmetry and Search in a Network
   Design Problem, CPAIOR 2005). On the 16 XCSP 2026 Sonet2 (SONET ring)
   instances at 600 s, 6 -> 12 proved: 3-4, 5-5 and 6-5 optimal in 2-4 s,
   6-4 (unsolved in the competition), 7-4 and 8-4 infeasible at the root.
   It fires on none of the wide set; -Dorg.jacop.fz.pairCover=false turns
   it off.

350. SumWeight reads the whole queue of changed variables and reuses its set
   between calls; it stopped at the first variable already behind its
   pointer, which never left a value unpruned (100,000 WakeUpFixpointTest
   trials with a new SumWeight case). ElementInteger with an index smaller
   than its domain pruning limit unions the values of the index directly
   instead of walking the groups of duplicate values. On the 45 wide
   instances that post one of them (fixed search, 600 s) time is 1.011 of
   before over the 30 solved by both, inside the noise (0.978 at 240 s),
   and the 15 unsolved search 0.7 % more nodes; physician-scheduling
   instance10 11010 -> 10835 and unit-commitment L12-T48G15L10 reach better
   objectives, none worse.

351. Under -f the set partitioning relaxations whose rows linear overlap
   factoring (item 269) rewrote get LP-guided
   branching again: the factoring turns the set rows into rows over the
   shared sums, which LinearRelaxation.setRows does not recognise, so the
   detector now checks the model's rows before it rewrites them. CSPLib bus
   driver scheduling (crew schedules, 160-199 rows over up to 19,091
   shifts) at 120 s with -f: r3 and r5 are proved optimal (16 and 29) in 29
   and 28 s, unsolved in 240 s before, c1a in 39 s (70 s); CP-SAT 9.15
   proves them in 25-63 s. It fires on none of the wide set; the statistic
   lpGuided (-s -v) shows the decision.

352. The FlatZinc front end removes from each difference t = x - y (any
   a*x - a*y + b*t = c) the value that x - y != d would give, for every
   disequality of the model, stated or implied, that AlldifferentDetector
   collects, and raises abs(t) to the smallest absolute value left in t,
   which AbsXeqY's bounds consistency misses when t has values on both
   sides of 0. A difference of two all-different variables is not 0, so in
   the CSPLib all-interval series the absolute differences start at 1 and
   their all-different ranges over exactly n-1 values: n = 40, 60 and 100
   found no solution in 240 s (default and -f) and now solve with 0
   failures, as CP-SAT does through the same presolve reduction. It fires
   on none of the wide set. Not with --proof.

353. New constraint MaxCliqueBound(x, conflicts, sum): x are 0/1
   variables, no two variables in conflict are both 1, and sum <= x[1] +
   ... + x[n]. It bounds sum by a greedy coloring of the vertices that can
   still be 1 (classes of pairwise conflicting vertices, kept as bit sets
   in decreasing degree order) as maximum clique algorithms do: E. Tomita
   and T. Seki, "An efficient branch-and-bound algorithm for finding a
   maximum clique", DMTCS 2003, LNCS 2731; E. Tomita, Y. Sutani, T.
   Higashi, S. Takahashi and M. Wakatsuki, "A simple and faster
   branch-and-bound algorithm for finding a maximum clique", WALCOM 2010,
   LNCS 5942; P. San Segundo, D. Rodriguez-Losada and A. Jimenez, "An
   exact bit-parallel algorithm for the maximum clique problem", Computers
   & Operations Research 38(2), 2011; as a constraint, J.-C. Regin, "Using
   constraint programming to solve the maximum clique problem", CP 2003,
   LNCS 2833. Under LCG its reason cites only the zeros that do not join a
   color class, or a vertex at 1 in conflict with them. The FlatZinc front
   end posts it as a redundant constraint on a maximized sum of 0/1
   variables with pairwise conflicts (x[i] + x[j] <= 1, bool_clause or
   array_bool_and of two); -Dorg.jacop.fz.maxClique=false turns this off.
   A model that is maximum clique and nothing else is solved by branch and
   bound without LCG unless --lcg is given (-Dorg.jacop.fz.maxClique.lcg=true
   keeps LCG). Of the 15 Clique instances of the XCSP 2026 competition it
   proves 6 at 600 s (brock200-4 in 14 s), where develop-5.0 proved 2. The
   LP pair cliques test conflicts on bit sets, so p-hat1500-1 builds its
   model in 35 s instead of more than 300 s, with the same cliques.

354. The tabulation of small flattened expressions (item 292) reaches
   groups whose outer variables follow from one of them. Its small search
   now infers through clauses, implications and reifications (a clause
   with one unknown literal and the others false; a false relation makes
   its literal false; a true literal of an equality gives the unknown
   term), so a link of the XCSP 2026 Collatz chain, x mod 2 != 0 \/
   y = x div 2 and x mod 2 = 0 \/ y = 3x + 1, is enumerated over x alone
   instead of the x * y box. A group is tabulated when one branch of that
   search multiplies the domain sizes of the outer variables it branches
   on to at most the rows limit, which for a group with an outer variable
   of more than 10,000 values is that domain size, up to 100,000. Groups
   with the same constraints over variables with the same domains share
   one tabulation and the index of one table, so more groups fit in the
   node budget. On the 23 instances of the XCSP 2022-2026 competitions
   where it changes the model, at 600 s: Collatz 12 of 12 decided in
   1-8 s, 1 before (2 to 12 of the 23 solvers of 2026 decided each); Dominoes
   (2023) 11 of 11 solved in 7-102 s, 2 before, among them the three
   45-* instances that no 2023 solver solved, and a solution checked by
   the XCSP3 SolutionChecker for 40-150, which sat4j+roundingsat claimed
   unsatisfiable. On the wide benchmark set it changes one instance
   (code-generator mips_gobmk, one more table).

355. Lazy clause generation explains an entailed reified <= or >= linear
   constraint (int_lin_le_reif, int_lin_le_imp and the like) from one
   bound lookup per term: before, it read both bounds of every term and
   then looked up the entry of the bound it cites again. The reasons and
   the search are the same. bnn-planner navigation_5x5_10s, where these
   reasons took 22 % of the time, solves in 5.35 s instead of 6.29 s;
   over the 53 wide instances with int_lin_le_reif or int_lin_le_imp, the
   instances solved with the same search in at least 10 s take 0.94x
   (default) and 0.93x (-f) of the time at 600 s.

356. -Djacop.virtualClock=true also covers free search without learning
   after LCG gives way (-f): its time limit, the time LCG left, counts
   jacop.virtualClock.propagationsPerMs (1000) propagations per
   millisecond, and the probes of the value impact heuristic take their
   share of the propagations instead of the time. Two runs of the 62
   wide-quick instances at 10 s with -f, three at a time, end identically
   on 58 to 61 of them (55 before). The rest are symmetry detection
   (whirlpool), presolve timers (a few propagations on monomatch and
   work-task-variation) and, in one of three censuses, mapping.
   FreeSearch.Builder.virtualClock turns it on for one search.

357. LCG branch and bound under -f can jump below the best solution
   (--free-config lcg-jumps=1, off by default; LcgSolver.setJumps):
   after a streak of quick improving solutions (jacop.lcg.jumpAfter, 4,
   each within jacop.lcg.jumpFast, 20, conflicts of the one before) the
   run stops and probes obj <= best - step under a limit of
   jacop.lcg.jumpConflicts (100) conflicts, doubling the step after
   every solution and halving it after every refutation, which proves
   obj >= target + 1 as a clause. The core-guided phase steps the same
   way. Models with hundreds of tiny improvements gain: MiniZinc
   Challenge 2021 connect 0086_02 25.5 -> 13.5 s and carpet-cutting
   mzn_rnd_test.01/.02 about 2x faster (600 s, -f). On the wide set at
   600 s with -f it is neutral (13 instances better, 12 worse) and the
   35 where jumps fire take 1.6 % more time in total (city-position
   city-7-04 265 -> 401 s, peacable_queens 8 loses its proof), hence
   off by default. Statistics lcgJumpRuns, lcgJumpSolutions,
   lcgJumpsRaised and lcgJumpLimits.

358. Float optimisation in fzn-jacop is faster and proves more optima.
   Once branch and bound has found 1000 solutions
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.floatCrawl) improving by a few ulps, the objective is
   bisected between a lower bound and the best cost, each step with a
   budget of nodes (-Dorg.jacop.fz.floatStepNodes, 100000, or the nodes
   used so far) after which branch and bound takes over again; steps far
   below the best cost are tried only under such a budget. Under free
   search (-f) bisection starts at once (-Dorg.jacop.fz.floatBisectStart)
   and each solution restarts the search (-Dorg.jacop.fz.floatRestart).
   The lower bound comes from a new LinearFloatRelaxation
   (org.jacop.floats.constraints) of the linear float rows, McCormick
   envelopes of products (G. P. McCormick, Mathematical Programming 10
   (1976) 147-175) and secant and tangent rows of squares, solved by JaLP
   with exact duals and made safe by the bound of A. Neumaier and O.
   Shcherbina (Mathematical Programming 99 (2004) 283-296), so rounding
   never cuts off a solution; an infeasible relaxation is confirmed by a
   Farkas certificate. The LP point is probed as a candidate solution
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.floatLpPoint). -Dorg.jacop.fz.floatBisection=false and
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.floatLp=false turn the parts off. On 46 float
   optimisation instances (Ibex benchmarks, hakank, jacop-benchmarks) at
   600 s, three at a time, default search proves 25 optima instead of 18
   and -f 25 instead of 17; no instance with float variables is in the
   wide benchmark set.

359. Free search with lazy clause generation on an optimisation model
   whose search variables all lie in one cardinality with fixed counts
   (jacop_count_values or global_cardinality posted as CountValuesBounds
   with equal bounds) first dives without learning through those
   variables in model order, largest value first, for at most 5 % of the
   time limit; the values of its first solution start the default
   branching of LCG. On the slot model of bin packing (x[i][j] the weight
   of the j-th item in bin i) this fills bin after bin with the largest
   item that fits, the packing of first fit decreasing (D. S. Johnson,
   A. Demers, J. D. Ullman, M. R. Garey and R. L. Graham, "Worst-case
   performance bounds for simple one-dimensional packing algorithms",
   SIAM Journal on Computing 3, 1974). LCG made about 30 conflicts a second
   there and found no solution of 8 of the 15 BinPacking instances of the
   XCSP 2024 competition in 240 s; with the dive all 8 have one (n3c2w1a
   91, the best of the field; n3c3w1a proved optimal in 4 s), and the
   other 7 are unchanged. It fires on none of the instances of the wide
   benchmark set. Under LCG the reifications and the objective
   live in SAT clauses, so the dive does not print its solution: the first
   run of LCG completes it. --free-config slot-dive=off turns it off and
   slot-dive-share sets its share of the time; the statistics slotDiveSlots
   and slotDiveTime give its size and time.

360. Under lazy clause generation the FlatZinc front end adds to a
   cumulative whose tasks have variable durations and fixed resource
   uses, and at most 4000 (task, time point) pairs, its time-resource
   decomposition: at each time point t the sum of the resource uses of
   the tasks with s <= t < s + d is at most the capacity (Schutt, Feydy,
   Stuckey and Wallace, Why cumulative decomposition is not as bad as it
   sounds, CP 2009). The cumulative propagators take the earliest end of
   a task as its earliest start plus its shortest duration; when another
   constraint bounds the end, such as a stockpile that stays until it is
   reclaimed, they miss the part of the task between its latest start
   and its earliest end, which the learnt clauses over the start and end
   literals of the decomposition capture. XCSP 2024 Cargo at 600 s, two
   seeds: Cargo-22 proved in 4 s and Cargo-10 in 154 and 62 s, which no
   run proved before (CP-SAT, one thread: 0.5 and 59 s); Cargo-06 152
   and 5 s instead of 332 and 132 s, Cargo-05 4 s instead of 19 s. Of the
   240 wide instances it fires on two (stack-cuttingstock d3,
   stripboard), unchanged. -Dorg.jacop.fz.cumulativeTimeIndexed=false
   turns it off, =true posts it without lazy clause generation too, and
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.cumulativeTimeIndexedPairs=n sets the size limit.

361. Large neighbourhood search under -f keeps to the variables that no
   constraint defines when there are at least 3 of them, instead of 16
   (item 290). Below 16 the neighbourhoods took every variable, which on
   models with a few roots that define the rest, such as city-position
   (8 and 12 roots), made each neighbourhood relax variables that the
   fixed roots determine anyway. Found by bisecting a -f loss of the
   second integration round (city-position city-5-06, 4 seeds: 26-42 s
   before item 290, 66-98 s after). On the 10 wide instances with 3 to
   15 roots, -f at 240 s, two seeds: city-position city-5-06 108/93 ->
   48/29 s, city-7-04 proved in 107/126 s (before unproved at the
   limit), fast-food ff58 47/28 -> 19/28 s, median-string p2_10_8-0
   16/15 -> 11/12 s, the other six equal; total time 819 -> 409 s.
   pattern-set-mining (1 and 2 roots) and atp-stage2 (2) still take
   every variable.

362. The core-guided phase of free search (-f) under lazy clause
   generation probes the objective's own bound when its cores raise the
   lower bound by about one each, as cores of two terms with large
   domains do (a makespan and a tardiness that rise together). After 32,
   64, 128... cores, if the cores' recent rate would need more than four
   times as many cores again to meet the best solution, bounds of the
   objective at doubling distances above the lower bound are assumed
   with 100 conflicts each, and each one refuted becomes a clause. The
   phase hands over to branch and bound once the probes leave less than
   an eighth of the cores' gap; the probes' work is left out of the
   phase's rates. MiniZinc Challenge 2021 ATSP instance5 is proved in
   1.2 s instead of 2.3 s (1,978 cores of +1 before, 32 now); on the 13
   of 187 wide optimisation instances where it fires, -f at 600 s takes
   0.86 of the time with the same proofs (kitchen linear 305 -> 166 s).
   -Djacop.lcg.coreObjectiveProbe=false turns it off.

363. The FlatZinc front end detects tilings of 0..n-1 by m arithmetic
   progressions of k terms with pairwise distinct gaps (perfect rhythmic
   tilings, T. Johnson, "Tiling in my music", The Experimental Music
   Review, 1996; N. Alon, M. Debski, J. Grytczuk, J. Przybylo, "Perfect
   rhythmic tilings", arXiv 2603.02786): strictly increasing starts, an
   all_different over the gaps and terms t = s + j g given by int_lin_eq
   chains, offset sums or the columns of tables. A model that places the
   terms with element constraints on a cell array gets the implied
   all_different over the terms. Free search (-f) of a satisfaction model
   for one solution then branches as Knuth's algorithm X with the leftmost
   column (D. E. Knuth, "Dancing links", 2000): each start at its smallest
   value, its gap at a random value whose terms fall on free cells, under
   Luby restarts of 100 failures and without LCG. On the 24
   PerfectRythmicTiling instances of the XCSP 2026 competition JaCoP finds
   15 tilings at 600 s instead of 9, among them 1-4-20, which no solver
   of the competition solved; total time 6,947 s instead of 10,499 s. It
   fires on none of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark set or of the
   XCSP 2022-2025 FlatZinc instances. -Dorg.jacop.fz.progressionTiling=false
   turns the detection off, -Dorg.jacop.fz.progressionTiling.search=false
   the search only.

364. ElementVariableFast (MiniZinc element with a variable array) skips
   the calls woken by array positions the index can no longer select, and
   for a wake from a few selectable positions it checks residual supports
   (the changed positions, the bounds of the value and, for values spanning
   fewer than 64, each value) instead of scanning the whole index domain.
   On XCSP 2026 TelecomScheduling, where each task selects its day in the
   team array of every agent (100-200 days), the scans took 70-85 % of the
   time; at 600 s B-10 now reaches 178920 and B-01 1045380, where JaCoP
   found no solution before, and A-10 104404 instead of 140534 at 240 s.
   On the 82 wide instances with an element over a variable array the
   same instances are proved (56 by default, 62 under -f, 600 s) and the
   total time is unchanged (1.005 and 1.012, the same search under the
   virtual clock).
   A list wake then rechecks only the values whose residue is a changed
   position: 11 % more nodes per second on TelecomScheduling-B-10, the
   same pruning.

365. Free search (-f) of a resource-constrained project scheduling model
   (a makespan over CumulativeBasic resources with fixed durations,
   demands and capacities, and precedence rows) runs a project schedule
   heuristic, org.jacop.search.ls.ProjectSchedule: the serial schedule
   generation scheme (R. Kolisch, Serial and parallel resource-constrained
   project scheduling methods revisited, EJOR 90, 1996), forward-backward
   improvement (K. Y. Li and R. J. Willis, An iterative scheduling
   technique for resource-constrained project scheduling, EJOR 56, 1992;
   V. Valls, F. Ballestin and S. Quintanilla, Justification and RCPSP: a
   technique that pays, EJOR 165, 2005) and the activity list genetic
   algorithm of S. Hartmann (A competitive genetic algorithm for
   resource-constrained project scheduling, NRL 45, 1998), with shift
   moves and restarts. A short burst runs first; its best schedule is
   checked in the store, printed and given to lazy clause generation as
   its first solution (LcgSolver.setIncumbent), so LNS starts from it.
   Then the algorithm continues in slices as a new LNS kind (PROPOSALS),
   also in free search without learning after the lcg-slow hand-over.
   Models whose resources are all machines are left to local search.
   On the 20 PSPLIB j60/j120 instances of the MiniZinc benchmarks at
   600 s, -f is at or below CP-SAT (240 s) on 14, before 7; the mean gap
   to CP-SAT goes from +0.97 % to -0.80 % on j120 and from +1.49 % to
   +0.71 % on j60 (j120_31 216 -> 204, CP-SAT 208). On the 35 instances
   where it fires, 16 are better and 8 worse (proof times of 18 proved
   both ways: geometric mean 1.013). Of the 240 wide instances it fires
   on one. --free-config sgs=0 turns it off; sgs-share (0.03, share of
   the time limit), sgs-max (60000 ms), sgs-patience (20000 schedules)
   bound the first burst and sgs-lns (20 schedules per task, at least
   1000) the slices.

367. The XOR system of the FlatZinc front end (XorSystemDetector, Gauss-
   Jordan elimination over GF(2)) also takes the parity rows of linear
   equations over 0/1 variables only, and of equations written as two
   opposite inequalities a x <= k and -a x <= -k (as OPB files do).
   Exactly-one rows and other cardinalities of value 1 or n - 1 stay out.
   Together these rows give the parity arguments of even colouring
   models (Markstrom, "Locality and hard SAT-instances", JSAT 2, 2006;
   PB competition evencolouring and ec_* families): the sum of all vertex
   rows is odd, so the slacks of an optimisation model must sum to an odd
   number and an exact model has no solution. In the PB competition
   evencolouring_opt_linear_random6regular_nvert_071 and _unit_..._351
   are proved optimal in 1.5 s and 3.7 s (before not at 600 s, CP-SAT
   5 s), ec_stargrid_04_021 and _06_012 unsatisfiable in 1.5 s (before
   and CP-SAT unknown at 240 s), ECgrid5x40split, ECgrid5x130split and
   ec_random_rational_random8reg_split_750 unsatisfiable in under 2 s
   (before unknown). It changes 15 of 222 PB and 9 of 240 wide instances.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.xorZeroOne=false turns it off.

368. Free search picks its dom/wdeg variable from an indexed heap kept up
   to date by domain changes (Store.domainWatcher) instead of scanning
   every search variable and counting unfixed scope variables at each
   decision. The choice is the same variable. On TelecomScheduling-B-10
   (XCSP 2026, about 5000 search variables), where the scan was 40 % of
   the time, -f makes 73 % more nodes in 120 s. Over 16 wide instances
   that hand over from LCG to plain CP search and 3 TelecomScheduling
   instances at 600 s the total time goes from 10815 to 10569 s, with
   BACPj-01-08 proved; on wide-quick at 240 s it is neutral (2829 vs
   2839 s, same 52 solved). -Dorg.jacop.search.free.domWdegScan=true
   restores the scan.

369. Free search (-f) of a model without a search annotation branches on
   the variables that no defines_var annotation names before the defined
   ones: under lazy clause generation until the first solution, and
   always in free search without learning (after LCG gives way). On
   XCSP 2026 Feedback (a permutation x, penalties z = max(0, x[i] - x[j])
   per arc, minimise their sum) the default search fixed the defined
   penalties, which pins differences of x and fails deep on the
   all_different; P1000-15000 and P1000-25000 had no solution in 600 s
   and now reach 2424015 and 4008836, and P1000-05000 finds its first
   solution in 8 s instead of 105 s. On 18 other XCSP families where the
   order changes, 6 are better and 6 worse at 600 s; on wide-quick under
   -f (240 s) 2 groups are better and 1 worse (proof time noise).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.definedLast=always keeps the order after the first
   solution, false restores the old order. The Boolean of a half
   reification (int_lin_ne_imp and the other *_imp constraints) is not
   counted as defined, as the constraint does not fix it: XCSP 2023
   WordDesign1 words-065/075/085 had no solution in 600 s, now 24-64 s.

370. The FlatZinc front end posts the aggregated flow of a regular
   constraint whose sequence is counted by cardinality constraints, one
   per residue class i mod p of its positions (RegularFlowDetector): the
   automaton's layers of one class are merged, the cardinalities give the
   number of arcs per value, and flow conservation links consecutive
   classes (flow formulations of regular: G. Pesant, CP 2004;
   M.-C. Cote, B. Gendron and L.-M. Rousseau, CPAIOR 2007). Rotating
   workforce scheduling (MiniZinc Challenge 2018, 2019, 2022) is the
   case: one regular over the cyclic schedule and a global cardinality per
   weekday. 2019 Example1370, which CP-SAT proves infeasible in 38 s and
   JaCoP did not in 600 s, fails at the root; Example1174 and 2018
   Example1780 (210 s) are proved in about 2 s, by default and with -f.
   It fires on 15 of the 127 Challenge instances with a regular
   constraint, all of them rotating workforce, and the solved satisfiable
   ones keep their times. -Dorg.jacop.fz.regularFlow=false turns it off;
   --proof does not post it.

371. CostNetworkBound (-Dorg.jacop.fz.costNetwork=true) cites each atom of a
   lazy clause generation reason once and propagates incrementally.
   A reason cited one atom per cheaper value of a fixed variable and the
   bounds of a neighbour once per edge, so on celar scen07 a reason held
   about 6,100 atoms of which 215 were distinct; it now cites the bound
   atoms of the cheaper values outside a variable's bounds (for a fixed
   variable the nearest cheaper value on each side) and each neighbour
   once. During search only the variables whose domain or fixed
   neighbours changed are evaluated again, and the received binary costs
   of a variable are read once. Conflicts per second on scen07 rise
   about 8 times. On the 19 MiniZinc Challenge instances where the bound
   posts (celar, proteindesign12, one community-detection), at 600 s,
   default search proves 14 instead of 13 (1UBI.13p.9aa 184 s), the
   1HZ5.12p.19aa instances in 78-81 s instead of 280-307 s (total time
   0.79), and improves the objective of all three celar instances; -f
   proves 16 instead of 15 (total time 0.87).

372. The FlatZinc front end posts the LYM inequality of vectors that are
   pairwise incomparable in the product order (every ordered pair u, v
   has "u[c] < v[c] for some c", as a clause of literals that imply
   u[c] < v[c] or as two element constraints with the same index whose
   results are in a < b): sum_i 1/N(rank(x_i)) <= 1, where N(r) is the
   number of elements of rank r of the product of the chains of the
   coordinates. Products of chains are LYM posets (L. H. Harper,
   J. Combin. Theory A 17, 1974; W. N. Hsieh and D. J. Kleitman, Stud.
   Appl. Math. 52, 1973), so the inequality is implied; for subsets it is
   Lubell's (J. Combin. Theory 1, 1966) and gives Sperner's theorem (Math.
   Z. 27, 1928) and its multiset form (de Bruijn, van Ebbenhorst
   Tengbergen and Kruyswijk, 1951). It is posted as one rank variable and
   one element of scaled weights per vector and a sum, only when it
   removes a rank at the root; with more vectors than the largest rank the
   model fails at the root. On Antichain1 and Antichain2 of the XCSP 2026
   competition (-f, 600 s) it decides the six instances at Sperner's bound
   that JaCoP did not decide before: 127-09 and 253-10 (C(9,4) + 1 and
   C(10,5) + 1 subsets) and Antichain2-142-6-03 are proved unsatisfiable
   in 12, 60 and 7 s, and 126-09, 252-10 and 141-6-03 are solved in 19, 78
   and 79 s. Over the 24 Antichain instances 24 are decided instead of 18,
   in 325 s instead of 4,126 s. The other UNSAT instances fall to 1-4 s (Antichain1-071-08
   86 -> 3 s, Antichain2-086-4-05 73 -> 2 s). It fires on none of the 240
   instances of the wide benchmark set or of the XCSP 2022-2025
   instances. -Dorg.jacop.fz.antichain=false turns it off; it is off under
   --proof.

373. A LinearInt (MiniZinc int_lin_eq/le with a variable sum) of 200 or
   more terms keeps the bounds of its sum incrementally: the store tells
   it about every bound change of its variables and a consistency call no
   longer rescans all terms, which dominated the cost sums of assignment
   models (advisors in the sense of Lagerkvist and Schulte, CP 2007).
   Terms are sorted by their initial variability so a pruning pass stops
   at the first term within the slack. Pruning is unchanged. On the 2012
   ROADEF machine reassignment instances (cost sums of 1000-2004 terms)
   nodes per second go up 1.9x in default search and 1.7x under -f;
   a1_1 -f at 600 s reaches 44,311,581 (before 45,404,327, best known
   44,306,501). -Dorg.jacop.linearIncrementalTerms=n sets the threshold,
   0 turns it off. Binpacking also skips its elimination and commitment
   tests (Shaw, CP 2004) when the load range is at least as wide as the
   heaviest candidate, where no test can prune; this was 88 % of the time
   of a1_5 under -f.

374. The core-guided phase of lazy clause generation trims each core: it
   solves again under the core's own literals, with at most 1000
   conflicts a call and up to 3 calls while the core shrinks, and relaxes
   the smallest core it got (core trimming in RC2: Ignatiev, Morgado and
   Marques-Silva, RC2: an Efficient MaxSAT Solver, JSAT 11, 2019). A
   smaller core raises the lower bound by as much and leaves its other
   soft literals to later cores. Under -f it fires on 34 of the 240 wide
   instances; at 600 s on those the total time is 5988 s instead of
   6153 s (0.973) with the same 26 proved: workforce-alloc 202 50 s
   instead of 97 s, neighbours-new-6 83 s instead of 158 s, city-position
   7-04 88 s instead of 174 s, pattern-set-mining ionosphere 7 s instead
   of 14 s; city-position 5-06 43 s instead of 27 s, kitchen linear 170 s
   instead of 143 s. Two earlier rounds before entry 362 gave 0.937 and
   0.983. On the 62 wide-quick instances at 240 s: -f 3 families better,
   none worse; fixed search unchanged.
   -Djacop.lcg.coreTrim=n sets the calls per core, 0 turns it off
   (LcgSolver.setCoreTrim); the statistic lcgCoreShrunk counts the
   literals it took out of cores.

375. JaLP's presolve also removes forcing rows and fixes dominated columns
   (Andersen and Andersen, "Presolving in linear programming",
   Mathematical Programming 71 (1995), sections 3.2 and 3.5; part of the
   Gurobi catalogue of Achterberg, Bixby, Gu, Rothberg and Weninger,
   "Presolve reductions in mixed integer programming", INFORMS J. Comput.
   32 (2020)). A row whose activity reaches its bound only with every column
   at one of its bounds fixes those columns; the postsolve gives the row
   the dual that keeps their reduced costs feasible and makes the column
   that sets it basic. Bounds on the duals from the row sides and from
   column singletons without a bound fix columns whose reduced cost cannot
   be 0. -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveForcing=false and
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveDominated=false turn them off. They fire on
   149 of the 687 LPs of the LP bench (pivots 0.92x there, time 0.96-0.97x
   above 0.5 s, MIPLIB 2017 0.93-0.94x) and on 17 of 184 JaCoP runs that
   build an LP, where results stay the same.

376. The linear relaxation takes the rows over the terms of the objective on
   the sparse simplex when they do not fit the dense tableau. A model
   stated in clauses and elements has few rows of its own (CSPLib
   warehouses, prob034: 2), so the dense simplex was chosen and the clause
   rows b[i,j] -> open[j] and the value encodings of the element costs did
   not fit it; the root bound of cap131 stayed at 626,300 (optimum
   915,059). Now the rows at distance at most 1 from the objective, and
   the rows over no other columns, go to the sparse simplex when they are
   at most org.jacop.lp.sparseMaxRows (25,000): cap131 root bound 914,788,
   free search proves the optimum in 4 s instead of not in 600 s (CP-SAT
   4.5 s), and traveling-tppv circ20fnonbal (MiniZinc Challenge 2022) is
   proved UNSAT in 45 s instead of 208 s. -Dorg.jacop.lp.nearRows=false
   turns it off; the statistic lpNearRows gives the rows taken.

377. Symmetry breaking posts value precedence in models whose own
   lex-leader constraints (lex_chain_lesseq over two vectors, lex_lesseq)
   hid that their values are interchangeable. Each such constraint
   X <=lex sigma(X) must compare a prefix of one common order, with sigma
   a permutation of the variables that keeps their domains, fixes the
   objective and maps every other constraint to one of the model; the
   lexicographically smallest solution of an orbit of all symmetries then
   satisfies them and the precedence chain in that order (Law and Lee,
   CP 2004; Walsh, CP 2006). XCSP 2026 ColoredGrid-09 (9 disjoint
   9-queens solutions, UNSAT) is refuted in 3.7 s, no answer at 300 s
   before; ColoredGrid-08 6.3 -> 0.5 s. It fires on no wide-set instance.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetryModelLex=false turns it off.

378. Free search (-f) of an optimisation model without a search
   annotation, after slow lazy clause generation gave way without a
   solution, takes turns until its first solution with runs that fix the
   search variables in the model's order, least value first
   (FreeSearch.Alternation.EVERY_RESTART_UNTIL_FIRST_SOLUTION,
   --free-config first-input=<runs>, one run in 2, 0 = off): the root
   bounds can make dom/wdeg start at the far end of a chain (GolombRuler:
   the last mark first). LCG slower than lcg-slow-rate without a solution
   at half the time limit now gives way too (lcg-slow-unsolved-late,
   default 0.5). XCSP 2026 GolombRuler-a3-32: 1348 at 600 s, no solution
   before; on 26 XCSP 2025/2026 instances where it can fire, two more
   with a solution and 7 better against 3 worse (seed spread); neutral on
   the wide set.

379. The FlatZinc front end posts the reified comparisons of one variable
   with constants (int_le_reif, int_lt_reif with a constant on either
   side) and their half-reifications (int_le_imp, int_lt_imp) as one
   ChannelOrder per variable with two or more of them, in place of one
   Reified or Implies each, as presolve rule 5 (OrderLiterals) already
   did under --presolve on. ChannelOrder gets implied items
   (b => x <= t, b => x > t), a violation for local search and a
   pseudo-Boolean encoding for proofs; the linear relaxation and the
   dominance nogoods read it as the reifications it replaces. On the
   41 wide instances where it fires (2,596 channels for 21,321
   constraints) at 600 s the same instances are proved; the proved
   instances take 0.976 of the time by default and 0.736 under -f
   (2026_kitchen 288 -> 174 s, neighbours-new-6 152 -> 98 s), total time
   0.997 and 0.982. -Dorg.jacop.fz.orderChannels=false keeps the
   reifications, -Dorg.jacop.fz.orderChannels.implied=false keeps the
   half-reifications.

380. ArgMax and ArgMin bound the values at positions that the index can
   no longer take: such a position may not reach the extreme over the
   positions the index can still take (strictly for positions after
   them, since the first extreme wins). ArgMax and ArgMin now reach
   domain consistency in the consistency checker (bounds(Z) before), and
   so does ArgMaxMonotone, which the FlatZinc front end posts for
   maximum_arg over monotone functions of shared variables; the checker
   also gains XlteqY, XneqY and BinaryBitTable cases at domain
   consistency. The LCG explanations are the existing ones. The upTo5sec
   arg models give the same answers and node counts; the one wide
   instance with maximum_arg (tower) fails 10 % less often (30155 ->
   27287) at the same time.

381. The core-guided phase of LCG free search keeps the 0-1 terms of a split
   objective (lcg-split) also when their sums span up to twice fewer
   levels (-Djacop.lcg.coreSpanRatio=2); wide terms keep the sums as
   before. A long run's root propagation tightens the sums beyond their
   terms' bounds, and the cores then ran on a few wide sums. The probe
   skip of the phase (jacop #597) no longer fires when the
   probe raised the lower bound at least as far as it is still below the
   best solution (-Djacop.lcg.coreProbeHalfWay=false restores it). Stats
   lcgCoreTermsBySpanRatio and lcgCoreProbeHalfWays. On 23 MaxSAT
   Evaluation 2024 instances where JaCoP had a gap to CP-SAT, -f at 600 s
   proves 11 instead of 9 (xai-mindset2 iris 8 s, packup-rand892 263 ->
   8 s) and raises the bounds of logic-synthesis max1024 (154 -> 256 of
   259) and optic-gen (45 -> 100); total time 9374 -> 8328 s. On the 11
   wide-set instances where it fires (none in default mode), -f at 600 s
   proves the same 10, total time 974 -> 897 s.

382. With -Dorg.jacop.lp.restartSolves=true, the cut rounds of the
   linear relaxation at restarts can use the bounds tightened at the root
   level since the first solve, as SCIP separates again over the
   presolved problem after a restart (T. Achterberg, Constraint Integer
   Programming, PhD thesis, TU Berlin, 2007):
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.restartBounds=true (off by default). The proving side
   of the objective goes back to its first-solve bound, so the cuts can
   move the LP bound below the objective cap, and the solve reads the
   pool of aged-out rows. These cuts hold only under the root-level
   bounds: when the search backtracks below the LP root level the
   relaxation stops and its ground check skips them; proof logging
   (--proof) does not use them. Statistics lpRestartBoundCuts,
   lpRestartCutBound, lpRestartBoundsLost. -Dorg.jacop.lp.extraLimits
   (3) sets how many pivot-limit hits drop the extra rows and cuts;
   with zero-half cuts on TankAllocation1 under -f they were dropped on
   8 of 9 instances, which left the restart relaxation without them.
   At 600 s with -f on the 41 wide instances with LP cuts, total time is
   5705 s for the base, 5054 s with extraLimits=1000000 (roster-shifts
   s100_e20 proved in 3 s, base timeout) and 5046 s with restart bounds
   too, 33 proved against 32; dual bounds improve on 7 of the 8 still
   open (train 57764 -> 67043, pigment20a 1033 -> 1043, unit-commitment
   1320705 -> 1335120). On TankAllocation1 (9) with zero-half: chemical
   2667 -> 2459 (optimum 2296), 2000 1821 -> 1730, 0400 1386 -> 1329,
   1600 proved in 19 s instead of 52 s.

383. Free search (-f) on satisfaction models gives LCG away to free search
   without learning when learning does not cut the search: at 10 % of -t
   (then each time the elapsed time doubles) the conflicts so far jump
   back fewer than 1.3 levels on average and their learnt clauses have
   an LBD of at least 0.35 of the conflict depth, so the clauses act like
   chronological backtracking. Before, only optimisation searches gave
   way (lcg-slow-rate). --free-config lcg-sat-probe=on|report|off,
   lcg-sat-at=<share of -t> (0 gives way at once), lcg-sat-jump and
   lcg-sat-lbd set it; with -s it prints lcgSatProbe. Fixed search keeps
   LCG, since the annotation backtracks about one level a conflict
   whatever learning does. On the 35 satisfaction instances of the wide
   set and the XCSP competitions where -f LCG runs for more than 10 s, at
   600 s in two repeats it solves 20 -> 25 and 21 -> 26, total time 0.73
   and 0.78 (Subisomorphism g11-g30 and g11-g44, AnotherMagicSquare-04
   proved UNSAT, FRB-45-21-3 solved). The first thresholds (1.5 levels,
   LBD 0.4) lost on unseen instances (137 XCSP 2026 CSP and CSPLib
   satisfaction instances, 240 s: solved 48 -> 42 and 47 -> 42); the
   current ones were fitted on both sets with a cross-validation split by
   problem family (37 instances where a rule fires, 600 s, two repeats:
   probe off 21 and 20 solved, first rule 14 and 12, current 19 and 19).

384. The LP relaxation keeps the value encodings of the model's variables
   (sum b_v = 1, x = sum v b_v) when the other rows do not all fit its
   first-solve trial. Before, they came after thousands of linearized rows
   and missed the trial: on the ROADEF 2020 maintenance planning model the
   relaxation could leave every Boolean at 0, all its duals were zero and
   it never pruned. Now they go first, are tried alone first, and get the
   time of a large root solve (10 % of -t). New clique rows over value
   Booleans: value Booleans of one variable that each imply a Boolean a
   give sum b_v <= a (implications lifted over a clique of the conflict
   graph, A. Atamturk, G. L. Nemhauser, M. W. P. Savelsbergh, Conflict
   graphs in solving integer programming problems, European Journal of
   Operational Research 121(1), 2000), and set packing rows over such
   Booleans are restated over the b_v. The sparse simplex takes up to
   50,000 columns (was 30,000). ROADEF 2020 at 600 s with -f: dual bound
   A_01 31,697,304 -> 31,804,427 (optimum 31,820,680), A_06 20,116,179 ->
   21,448,097 (optimum 21,498,698). 36 of the 240 wide instances are
   reached, with the same results. -Dorg.jacop.lp.leadEncodings=false and
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.valueCliques=false turn them off.

385. Time windows for vehicle routes written as a giant tour (MiniZinc
   VRPTW models: arrival a[v] = T[succ[v]], a[v] >= T[v] + s[v] + d[v]).
   The FlatZinc front end reads a lag for every arc from these rows and
   removes the successors that no arrival time can reach (time window
   reduction, M. Desrochers, J. Desrosiers, M. Solomon, A new optimization
   algorithm for the vehicle routing problem with time windows, Operations
   Research 40(2), 1992). When some customer has a window of its own
   (narrower than the start and end times of the tour imply), it also
   gives the windows to Routes, whose LP separates infeasible path
   (tournament) cuts over paths of the LP support that no vehicle can
   follow in time (N. Ascheuer, M. Fischetti, M. Groetschel, Solving the
   asymmetric travelling salesman problem with time windows by
   branch-and-cut, Mathematical Programming 90(3), 2001), and posts the
   new constraint ArcTimes(x, offset, t, lag): x[v] = w implies
   t[w] >= t[v] + lag[v][w], with LCG explanations. Solomon R101 with 100
   customers is proved optimal in 81 s (17 s with -f), unproved in 600 s
   before. -Dorg.jacop.fz.timeWindows=false turns this off, =all gives
   cuts and ArcTimes to every giant tour; -Dorg.jacop.fz.arcTimes=false
   and -Dorg.jacop.lp.pathCuts=false turn off the propagator and the cuts.

386. A satisfaction model stated in clauses over 0/1 variables, with no
   value encodings for the root refutation, gets a root-only refutation
   LP over its clause rows when its binary clauses hold at-most-one
   structure: unit propagation and the substitution of equivalent
   literals (strongly connected components of the binary implication
   graph, Aspvall, Plass and Tarjan, IPL 8(3), 1979) first, then the pair
   conflicts merged into clique rows (Atamtuerk, Nemhauser and
   Savelsbergh, EJOR 121, 2000). Pigeonhole formulas, which resolution
   and so lazy clause generation refute only in exponentially many steps
   (Haken, TCS 39, 1985), are refuted at once. Of the SAT Competition
   2024/25 CNFs of the SAT gap screen, pigeon-hole, mutilated-chessboard
   and grandtour-puzzle (UNKNOWN at 600 s) and fpga-routing (203 s) are
   refuted at the root in under 4 s; on the 13 CNFs where it can change
   the result, 600 s, default and -f, 20 -> 26 runs solved, total time
   5,984 -> 1,599 s. On the 12 wide instances where it runs the answers
   are the same (rubik pays about 0.5 s). -Dorg.jacop.lp.clauseRefutation
   =false turns it off; org.jacop.lp.clauseRefutationCliques (1) and
   org.jacop.lp.clauseRefutationPivots (1,000) set its gate and its pivots.

387. The core-guided phase of LCG can exhaust its cores, off by default
   (-Djacop.lcg.coreExhaust=K, LcgSolver.setCoreExhaust). After a core
   of at least two literals is relaxed, the outputs "at least j of them
   false" of its new totalizer, j = 2, 3, ..., are each assumed alone,
   at most K of them and at most 1000 conflicts a call, and relaxed as
   unit cores while they are refuted (core exhaustion in RC2: Ignatiev,
   Morgado and Marques-Silva, RC2: an Efficient MaxSAT Solver, JSAT 11,
   2019). The bound rises at once by what a run of cores over the same
   literals would give. With K = 8 under -f, on the 32 of 87 MaxSAT
   Evaluation 2024 instances where it fires, at 600 s, the total time is
   5593 s instead of 5757 s (0.971) with 26 proved instead of 24
   (quantum-circuit realamprandom 118 s, causal-discovery Bands 522 s and
   mqc 10tree825p 582 s instead of unproved, close_solutions teams16 14 s
   instead of 26 s, mqc 10tree510p 7 s instead of 15 s; frb20-11-1
   unproved instead of 318 s, mqc 10tree320p 341 s instead of 228 s).
   Lower bounds rise on mpe network-10 (74137 -> 85304), RBAC
   mse20 (90519 -> 91991) and inconsistency-measurement (35 -> 38), and
   fall on minimize-5gons (30 -> 27). Two earlier rounds, on develop
   before the core span ratio rule, gave 0.944 in total. On the 62
   wide-quick instances at 240 s under -f the total is 1.009 with 3
   families worse (is A3PZaPjnUz 4 -> 18 s, work-task seed 8 20 -> 49 s),
   so it stays off. The statistic
   lcgCoreExhausted counts the outputs it proved.

388. ElementVariableFast finds the list positions of a variable that woke
   it in a table indexed by the variable's store index when the indexes of
   its list span few values, instead of by binary search. On MiniZinc
   Challenge 2023 multi-agent-graph-coverage rnd_n75 1086 elements share
   two lists of 543 variables, so every change of a variable wakes 543
   elements, and the binary searches took 16 % of the solving time: the
   same search runs in 0.76-0.87 of the time. On the 18 wide instances
   where the wakes take at least 1 % of the time the total time is 1.004
   with identical search (virtual clock, 2 rounds).

389. Maximum clique (item 353). MaxCliqueBound covers the vertices that the
   coloring puts beyond sum.min() - 1 classes one at a time: by the
   re-coloring of MCS (E. Tomita, Y. Sutani, T. Higashi, S. Takahashi and
   M. Wakatsuki, "A simple and faster branch-and-bound algorithm for
   finding a maximum clique", WALCOM 2010, LNCS 5942), then by MaxSAT
   reasoning on the color classes as soft clauses, a failed literal test
   by unit propagation (C.-M. Li and Z. Quan, "An efficient
   branch-and-bound algorithm based on MaxSAT for the maximum clique
   problem", AAAI 2010; C.-M. Li, Z. Fang and K. Xu, "Combining MaxSAT
   reasoning and incremental upper bound for the maximum clique problem",
   ICTAI 2013). When all are covered it fails; when one is left, that
   vertex is in every clique large enough and is set to 1. The vertices
   are colored in the minimum width order of MCS. Its LCG reasons cover
   both (a zero joins a class of an inconsistent subset only when the
   derivation falsifies it first). The properties
   org.jacop.maxClique.renumber, .maxsat, .force and .degeneracy set to
   false turn the parts off. A model that is maximum clique and nothing
   else, without a search annotation (or under -f) and without LCG, is
   searched as MCQ and MCS do: the vertex of the last color class first,
   at 1 first (org.jacop.search.MaxCliqueSelect), after a short first run
   whose incumbent is the best greedy clique from every vertex; its
   pairwise conflicts are not posted, since the bound enforces them
   (their propagators took more than half of the time, and the sequence
   detector posted AmongSeq on chains of them).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.maxClique.search=false keeps the default search and
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.maxClique.pairs=true posts the conflicts. Of the 15
   Clique instances of the XCSP 2026 competition it proves 10 at 600 s
   instead of 6 (brock400-4 in 149 s, brock400-2 in 369 s, p-hat0700-2,
   p-hat1500-1), brock800-2 and brock800-4 reach their optima (24, 26,
   before 19), and the summed time goes from 5769 s to 3610 s;
   p-hat1500-2 and p-hat1500-3 end one below (64 and 93).

390. Under --lcg with -f, LCG free search counts as slow, and gives way to
   free search without learning once it stops making progress, also when
   it makes fewer conflicts per second than 5 times the average length of
   its learnt clauses (--free-config lcg-slow-literal, 0 = off), not only
   below lcg-slow-rate (300). A conflict that learns a clause of hundreds
   of literals prunes little, so learning costs more than it saves and
   large neighbourhood search runs a third as often. On the CSPLib TTPPV
   instances (traveling tournament with predefined venues, prob068) LCG
   learns 210 to 450 literals per conflict, the bounds of the 400 travel
   terms of the objective, at 490 to 990 conflicts per second. At 600 s
   the switch fires on the 10 circ20 instances and 2 of the 4 feasible
   circ14 ones: the sum of the 17 best objectives goes from 12,816 to
   12,526 (CP-SAT 9.15, -p 1: 12,428), 8 of 10 circ20 better. Of the 240
   wide instances it reaches 4 by its first check; cargo challenge16
   goes from 132,309 to 115,263, the other three have no solution of
   either version at 600 s.

391. New constraint XmulYeqZSaturated(x, y, z): z = x * y saturated at the
   ends of JaCoP's integer range, z = max(MIN_INT, min(MAX_INT, x * y)).
   The FlatZinc front end posts it for int_times(x, y, z) when the
   declared domain of z reaches beyond the range (and holds every product
   of the declared factors) and z is otherwise only compared, by int_eq,
   int_ne, int_le or int_lt and their _reif and _imp forms, with constants
   or variables declared strictly inside the range, and is neither
   printed nor the objective. Such a comparison has the same truth value
   for a product beyond the range and for the end of the range, so the
   end stands for all of them (a saturated sentinel, as in interval
   arithmetic: R. E. Moore, Interval Analysis, Prentice-Hall, 1966) and
   no solution is lost; before, XmulYeqZ over the narrowed z removed every
   pair of factors whose product did not fit, so a completed search could
   claim nothing. MiniZinc compiles tree_vals[i] = tree_vals[l] *
   tree_vals[r] of the 2022 Challenge model arithmetic-target to 21 such
   products declared 0..4722438400: at 600 s its three large instances
   (4108, 6872, 8657) are now proved optimal (7) in 12-31 s of search in
   default mode and 10-15 s with -f; before, the search completed at 7
   and printed no ==========. Of the 1,781 Challenge instances, only these
   three have such a product. It is off with --proof.

392. The root reduced costs of the LP relaxation now prune in search. They
   were propagated only at the level of the first LP call, which search
   never revisits, so they never fired; now each branch propagates them
   again once the bound of the objective changes. Two-literal nogoods of
   the dominance presolve are only tried as LP rows instead of taken as
   model rows: 84,897 of them made the LP of a 5,000-item knapsack too
   large to solve in search. On the PB24 OPT-LIN knapPI knapsacks at 600 s
   similar_weights is proved in 67 s instead of 158 s and profit_ceiling in
   140 s instead of 190 s. On the 240 wide instances at 600 s, 196 instead
   of 193 are solved by default and 198 instead of 193 with -f, total time
   0.959 and 0.953.

393. Large neighbourhood search under -f has a new kind, RINS
   (relaxation induced neighbourhood search, E. Danna, E. Rothberg and
   C. Le Pape, Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MIP
   solutions, Mathematical Programming 102(1), 71-90, 2005). It relaxes
   the variables whose value in the best solution differs most from
   their value in the root solution of the linear relaxation, and fixes
   the others. It is used when the model has a linear relaxation;
   --free-config lns-rins=0 turns it off, and AdaptiveLns.setLpValues
   gives the values. On the 34 wide-set optimisation instances with a
   relaxation that -f leaves open, at 240 s it finds a better objective
   on 12, 11 and 10 of them and a worse one on 7, 5 and 8 over three
   seeds (gbac UD3 2414 -> 515, 9736 -> 1015 and 7242 -> 915; warehouse
   olil4 66713 -> 62918); at 600 s 6 better and 5 worse (gbac UD3
   317 -> 173, kletzander i09 6735 -> 4765, cargo challenge16
   129091 -> 154593). On 42 quicker relaxation instances it is neutral
   (36 solved by both, total time 1.002 at 600 s).

394. Under --lcg a linear constraint over 0/1 variables that is equivalent
   to a few clauses over its own literals becomes those clauses: its prime
   implicates, the minimal covers of the threshold function (Balas and
   Jeroslow, Canonical cuts on the unit hypercube, SIAM J. Appl. Math.
   23(1), 1972), when there are at most 2 per variable
   (-Djacop.lcg.clauseShaped=N, 0 = off). An at most one constraint over
   n literals is its n (n - 1) / 2 exclusions, "at most 3 of 4" a single
   clause. Before, such sums stayed propagators with lazy counters, which
   become clauses only after 100 uses. The PB instance vdw_k4_l4_n360
   (85,680 "at most 3 of 4" sums) becomes pure CNF and is solved in 63 s
   (no solution in 600 s before; CP-SAT 9.15: 49 s). On the 117 of 222
   PB screen instances where it fires, at 600 s, 81 -> 83 solved and the
   total time goes from 24,092 to 22,394 s (0.930); on the 47 of 240
   wide instances where it fires, 44 -> 44 solved, total time 1.010
   (default) and 0.965 (-f). New statistic lcgClauseShaped.

395. The XorSystem detector of the FlatZinc front end reads parities written
   as clauses (bool_clause), as CryptoMiniSat finds the XORs of a CNF
   (M. Soos, K. Nohl and C. Castelluccia, "Extending SAT Solvers to
   Cryptographic Problems", SAT 2009). A set of at most 8 variables
   (jacop.fz.xorClauseLength) whose clauses, also shorter clauses that
   subsume some of them, exclude every point of one parity gives a row.
   A set whose clauses exclude half of the points with an algebraic
   normal form of degree 2 gives a row over the variables and products
   m = a and b (the linearisation of Bosphorus, D. Choo, M. Soos, K. M.
   A. Chai and K. S. Meel, ICTAI 2019), which reads XORs of disjunctions
   (a or b = a + b + ab); a product that occurs in one row only is left
   out, and an AND gate x = ab stands for its product. Gauss-Jordan
   elimination then refutes Tseitin formulas, which take resolution
   exponentially long proofs (A. Urquhart, "Hard examples for
   resolution", J. ACM 34(1), 1987). A system of plain rows read from
   clauses whose rows and variables form a tree is not posted: unit
   propagation on its clauses already reaches what Gauss-Jordan
   elimination would (C. Beeri, R. Fagin, D. Maier and M. Yannakakis,
   "On the desirability of acyclic database schemes", J. ACM 30(3),
   1983); the XORs of adders and multipliers are such trees.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.xorClauses=false turns the clauses off,
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.xorQuadratic=false the degree-2 rows. On the 90 SAT
   Competition 2024/25 CNFs of the SAT screen (cnf2fzn FlatZinc, 240 s)
   it fires on 41; tseitin-formulas, mutilated-chessboard, or_randxor
   and subset-cardinality are refuted at posting (none decided before),
   random-graph-xorsat and binary-tree-parity are solved in 1 to 3 s
   (unknown before; Kissat 164 s and unknown), and the default search
   solves 13 of the 41 instead of 7, -f 11 instead of 5. On the 240
   wide instances it fires on 2013 rubik 6-cube and 7-cube only, which
   stay solved in 2 to 5 s (0.3 s slower).

396. Core exhaustion (386) is on by default, for 8 outputs per core
   (-Djacop.lcg.coreExhaust=K, 0 = off), and gives up once the first
   output of 16 cores per output it proved, plus 16, was not refuted
   (-Djacop.lcg.coreExhaustPatience=P, LcgSolver.setCoreExhaustPatience,
   0 = never). A failed test costs conflicts and changes the solver's
   phases and learnt clauses: on is A3PZaPjnUz, where no output is ever
   refuted, it kept the core-guided phase from the objective probes that
   prove the optimum (1.7 s -> 13 s); with the patience rule 1.8 s.
   Under -f at 600 s, on the 14 wide instances with objective terms
   that are not all 0-1 where exhaustion fires, two rounds give a total
   time of 2760 s instead of 3008 s (0.918) with 25 proved instead of 24:
   lot-sizing pigment20a is proved in one round (370 s) and its bound
   rises in the other (1014 -> 1070); diameterc-mst c_v15 (23 -> 18 s),
   prize-collecting 32-4-8-2 (26-30 -> 22-26 s), median-string and
   kidney-exchange are faster in both, accap instance5 (46-49 -> 62-67 s)
   and gt-sort n9 (54-59 -> 60-68 s) slower in both. On the 32 MaxSAT
   Evaluation 2024 instances where exhaustion fires, against exhaustion
   without the patience rule, 5510 s instead of 5625 s (0.980), 25 proved
   in both (it was 0.919 and 26 against no exhaustion). On the 62 wide-quick
   instances at 240 s under -f the total is 0.997 (6632 s instead of
   6649 s), the same 36 proved; the two that exhaustion alone slowed
   down (is A3PZaPjnUz, work-task seed 8) did not move.

397. The LP relaxation keeps the definitions of the shared sums of linear
   overlap factoring that its rows still use. It replaces those sums by
   their definitions only up to depth 8 but dropped every definition, so
   the deeper sums of nested sets were free columns: CSPLib bus driver
   scheduling c1 had root bound 4.4 instead of 25.4 (optimum 26). With -f
   at 600 s on the 20 instances it reaches (12 bus driver scheduling, 8 of
   the 240 wide instances): 15 -> 18 proved (c1 10 s, c2 20 s, r5a 193 s),
   total time 3,389 -> 1,898 s; fixed search at 240 s 11 -> 13 proved.
   The factoring is the horizontal overlap rule of the CP-SAT presolve
   (L. Perron, F. Didier, CP-SAT, OR-Tools 9.15).

398. Free search (-f) also searches before an expensive root LP (319).
   When the first solve of the relaxation is large, a solver of its own
   runs for 200 ms (-Dorg.jacop.fz.lpProbe) with the relaxation suspended.
   Its answer is final when it finishes. Otherwise its prunings are undone,
   the relaxation is solved at the root and free search starts as before,
   from the probe's best solution as phase and bound. Free search takes
   choices at its start from the solved relaxation (its solution as a
   guide, the core-guided schedule from the root bound), so the probe does
   not run inside it. nside MEDIUM_5_3 of the 2026 Challenge: 2.3 -> 0.5 s.
   At 600 s with -f, base and change pinned to a core each: the 104
   Challenge instances of the upTo5sec, upTo30sec and upTo1min sets where
   it fires 1895 -> 1851 s in total, the 50 wide instances where it fires
   12355 -> 11763 s and 30 -> 31 proved (roster-shifts-bool s100_e20_c8
   proved in 14 s). -Dorg.jacop.fz.freeLpProbe=false turns it off.

399. The LP relaxation states the value encodings of the model's Booleans
   (sum b_v = 1, x = sum v b_v, and y = sum a[v] b_v for an element
   y = a[x]) in place of the convex hull rows of the elements they cover,
   since the encoding is that hull. The encodings count with the model's
   rows when the simplex is chosen by size: when they make 100 rows or
   more, the sparse simplex takes them all, outside the share of the
   other extra rows, and keeps them in search instead of dropping them at
   the pivot limit of the dense tableau. On a generalized assignment model
   (OR-Library gap, job j to agent a[j], b[i,j] <-> a[j] = i, capacity rows
   over the b, cost by element) the encodings are the assignment and cost
   rows of its LP: before, their dense trial with 836 hull rows stopped at
   the pivot limit and kept none (gapb 5x100 root bound 1,569, now 1,831,
   optimum 1,843), or the LP stalled after 33 solves in search (gapd
   10x200). OR-Library gap b-d at 600 s: 3 -> 7 of 18 proved (total time
   0.81) and 2 -> 7 with -f (0.79), a better incumbent on 16 of 18 and 14
   of 18. It fires on 8 of the 240 wide instances, with the same results.
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.encodingRows=false turns it off; the statistic
   lpEncodedElements gives the elements left to their encodings (":sparse"
   when they chose the sparse simplex).

400. The core-guided phase of LCG (MaxSAT-like objectives) looks for its
   at-most-one groups of soft literals (Ignatiev, Morgado and
   Marques-Silva, RC2, JSAT 11, 2019) once more after it hardens soft
   literals. A conflict soft
   clause heavier than the gap to the best solution is hard only once a
   solution is known, and only then puts its two literals in one group;
   before, the groups were built while it was still soft and never again,
   and the cores raised the bound by one each. Weighted MaxSAT
   frb_wt-frb20-11-1 (independent set, 20 domain cliques) is proved in
   4 s instead of 416-477 s by default and 186-200 s with -f;
   drmx-cryptogen threshold128 is proved in 275 s by default (before
   not at 600 s). The statistic lcgCoreRegroups counts it.

401. Bin packings that minimize the bins used find packings at the bound.
   (a) Free search packs the items of a bin packing whose objective
   bounds every item's bin (bin[i] <= bins) into as many bins as the
   objective's lower bound, by bin completion with first-fail item
   choice, completions that pack the items with the fewest completions
   first, and randomized restarts, org.jacop.search.ls.BinCompletion
   (R. E. Korf, A new algorithm for optimal bin packing, AAAI 2002;
   C. P. Gomes, B. Selman and H. Kautz, Boosting combinatorial search
   through randomization, AAAI 1998). The packing is checked in the
   store, printed and given to lazy clause generation as its first
   solution, which only has to prove it optimal. --free-config
   bin-completion=off turns it off, bin-completion-share (0.05 of the
   time limit, at most 30 s) bounds it.
   (b) Symmetry breaking posts a value precedence over the bins also when
   the model empties the bins above the objective (b > bins ->
   load[b] = 0, flattened into bool_clause, int_eq_imp and int_le_imp):
   like bin[i] <= bins these constraints are left out of the value graph,
   since relabelling the bins by first use (Y. C. Law and J. H. M. Lee,
   Global constraints for integer and set value precedence, CP 2004)
   keeps the bins above the objective empty. The chain orders the items
   by decreasing weight, as first fit decreasing opens the bins.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetryEmptiedBins=false turns it off.
   On the OR-Library binpack5-8 Falkenauer triplets (E. Falkenauer, A
   hybrid grouping genetic algorithm for bin packing, Journal of
   Heuristics 2, 1996) and shuffled copies, all 16 are proved optimal in
   3-16 s at 600 s, where 6 were (CP-SAT proves the originals in
   2-33 s); of the 8 binpack1-4 instances 3 are proved instead of 1
   (u250_00 and u250_10, which CP-SAT does not solve in 240 s) and the
   4 largest get a solution, where they had none. Total time over the
   24 falls from 10707 to 3252 s (-f: 10759 to 3366 s). Neither part
   fires on the 240 wide instances.

402. JaSAT can rewrite a formula by bounded variable addition before its
   first variable elimination (org.jacop.jasat.cdcl.Factorizer,
   CdclSolver.setFactoring, on by default in CdclMain, --factor 0 turns
   it off): a grid of clauses f_p v R_i over factor literals f_1..f_k and
   quotients R_1..R_n becomes x v f_p and -x v R_i on a fresh variable x,
   k*n clauses into k+n, so at-most-one cliques and similar products
   shrink from quadratic to linear size (N. Manthey, M. J. H. Heule and
   A. Biere, Automated reencoding of Boolean formulas, HVC 2012, in the
   form of Kissat's factor; ties as in A. Haberlandt, H. Green and
   M. J. H. Heule, Effective auxiliary variables via structured
   reencoding, SAT 2023). The DRAT proof has the new clauses as RAT
   steps. On 90 SAT Competition 2024/25 instances at 240 s CdclMain
   solves 34 instead of 30 (reg-n UNSAT in 3 s, or_randxor, clique
   formulas, puzzle, planning, oddball weighing and ensemble computation
   are new; three satisfiable instances are lost). It is off in
   CdclSolver otherwise, so lazy clause generation is unchanged.

403. Constraints rewrite themselves to simpler ones during search, as
   Gecode's GECODE_REWRITE does (C. Schulte, G. Tack, M. Z. Lagerkvist,
   Modeling and Programming with Gecode, propagator rewriting): an
   ElementVariableFast whose index is fixed becomes XeqY, so changes of
   the other list variables no longer wake it, and a Reified, Implies or
   ImpliesNot whose Boolean variable is fixed becomes its nested
   constraint (or its negation), without the entailment checks at every
   call. The simpler constraint lives until the store backtracks below
   the level of the rewriting; lazy clause generation explains its
   prunings and failures with the original constraint. On the 83 wide
   set instances where they take most propagation time, total time is
   unchanged at 600 s (-f 1.016 over two rounds, inside the 4 % noise
   between base rounds) with the same instances solved, and -f finds
   better objectives on celar graph11 (3080 vs 4450-14210),
   multi-agent-graph-coverage n75 and orthorio challenge_12.
   -Dorg.jacop.rewrite=false turns it off
   (org.jacop.rewrite.element and org.jacop.rewrite.reified per kind).

404. The FlatZinc front end posts the sums of element constraints indexed
   by an all_different list: when y[k] = c[x[k]] over one constant array c
   and the x[k] are all different over the union U of their domains, the
   sum of the y[k] is fixed (a permutation of U) or bounded by the sums of
   the n smallest and largest values of c over U. Permutation flow shop
   models (d[i,k] = p[i,perm[k]]) get the machine loads, the bound of
   E. Taillard (Benchmarks for basic scheduling problems, EJOR 64(2),
   1993) and of the one-machine relaxations of B. J. Lageweg, J. K.
   Lenstra and A. H. G. Rinnooy Kan (A general bounding scheme for the
   permutation flow-shop problem, Operations Research 26(1), 1978); the
   sums aggregate the assignment formulation of element and all_different
   (P. Refalo, Linear formulation of constraint programming models and
   hybrid solvers, CP 2000). Taillard ta006 had a root bound of 160 and
   is proved optimal (1195) in 8 s with -f; CP-SAT takes 188 s. On the 24
   Taillard instances at 600 s, -f proves 6 (none before). They fire on
   12 of the 240 wide instances. -Dorg.jacop.fz.permutationElementSums=false
   turns them off.

405. Local search detects a permutation flow shop in the store (sequence
   positions in an all different constraint, tables giving each machine's
   processing time of the job at a position, precedence rows s + d <= t
   along machines and positions, the makespan as the cost) and runs the
   iterated greedy algorithm of R. Ruiz and T. Stuetzle (A simple and
   effective iterated greedy algorithm for the permutation flowshop
   scheduling problem, EJOR 177(3), 2007): NEH start (M. Nawaz, E. E.
   Enscore and I. Ham, Omega 11(1), 1983), destruction and greedy
   reinsertion, insertion local search and simulated annealing
   acceptance, with the O(nm) insertion of E. Taillard (Some efficient
   heuristic methods for the flow shop sequencing problem, EJOR 47(1),
   1990). Under -f it runs like the job shop tabu search, alone for up to
   half of the time limit and until ls-patience passes without a better
   sequence; each better sequence is checked in the store. On the 24
   Taillard instances at 600 s with -f every unproved instance is better
   (ta101 12806 -> 11368, ta106 12892 -> 11306) and
   the total time goes from 13971 s to 11097 s.

406. The LP relaxation moves the clause rows that LCG hands it to the
   sparse simplex when they make the dense tableau too large to search
   with. On set covering (OR-Library scp, one objective row and a few
   hundred covering clauses over thousands of columns) the 311 x 3,312
   tableau of scpa1 gave each solve in search 19 pivots: 32 of 33 solves
   stopped at the limit and the clause rows were dropped; on scpb1 and
   larger the root trial of the clauses ran out of its budget and the
   root bound stayed 0. Now a dense trial of the clause rows that stops at
   its root budget is repeated on the sparse simplex, a dense tableau that
   leaves fewer than 100 pivots a solve moves to the sparse simplex when
   that costs at most a quarter per pivot, and clause rows that alone
   carry the bound of the objective are not dropped after limits. Only
   for relaxations whose own rows bound nothing (the objective row
   alone). OR-Library scp at 600 s (14 instances): proved 3 -> 6 by
   default (scp51 288 -> 6 s; scp61, scpa1, scpb1 in 13-39 s) and
   2 -> 7 with -f (also scp51 and scpc1, 5 and 25 s); total time
   7,111 -> 5,114 s and 7,758 -> 4,832 s with two wide instances. It
   fires on none of the 240 wide instances. -Dorg.jacop.lp.extraSparse=false
   turns it off.

407. The CDCL unit propagation (CdclSolver.propagate) no longer writes a
   watch-list entry back to the same place while every entry before it
   stayed where it was, and a conflict leaves the rest of the list as it
   is instead of copying it onto itself. A visit that only checks the
   blocker literal now only reads the list, so the cache lines of long
   watch lists stay clean. The search is the same. Pure SAT on 90 CNFs
   runs 1.3 % faster (geomean 0.987, a copy of the base jar 1.003);
   FlatZinc on the 240 wide-set instances at 600 s solves the same
   instances by default, with total time on them 0.971 by default and
   0.972 under -f.

408. A satisfaction model without value encodings, such as a pure 0/1
   (pseudo-Boolean) model, now gets a root refutation LP too. Before,
   LinearRelaxation.refutation() needed value encodings, and the main
   relaxation never ran cut rounds without an objective. The new LP takes
   the model rows and the clause rows, with the block sums that lazy
   clause generation split substituted back. It minimises a pseudo
   objective t = sum w x over the inequality whose variables occur in the
   most rows (clause rows are never chosen: pure CNF is left to the clause
   refutation of item 385), with t in [min lhs, rhs]. Cut rounds (Gomory, MIR, zero-half) that push the
   bound of t above rhs prove the model infeasible. Pigeonhole and
   odd-cycle arguments are easy for the LP with cuts and exponential for
   resolution (A. Haken, The intractability of resolution, Theoretical
   Computer Science 39, 1985; M. W. Padberg, On the facial structure of
   set packing polyhedra, Mathematical Programming 5, 1973). Pseudo-Boolean
   competition instances proved UNSAT at 600 s that JaCoP could not prove
   before: php_230_229 (7.6 s), phpexit_v2_10_092 (12.4 s), vc-grids
   grid8_161 (1.6 s), vc-lines grid4_121 (0.8 s),
   subsetcard-geq-random4reg-3500 (3.9 s) and miplib disctom (3.2 s);
   vc-grids grid10_35 48.6 -> 0.9 s. The LP fires on 14 of the 240 wide
   instances and on 65 of 222 PB instances. On those 79 at 600 s, 57 ->
   63 are decided and nothing is lost. The total time goes from 15038 to
   11036 s (wide 1345 -> 1313 s, PB 13692 -> 9723 s).
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.refutationObjective=false turns it off, and
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.refutationObjectiveLength (3) is the shortest row taken
   as the objective. The statistic lpRefutationObjective tells when it
   was built.

409. A satisfaction model of clauses alone (bool_clause and the other
   Boolean constraints the FlatZinc front end turns into clauses), asked
   for one solution under free search (-f, or a model without search
   annotation), goes to JaSAT as the CNF it is, as CdclMain solves a
   DIMACS file: with variable elimination, chronological backtracking,
   vivification and local search, which lazy clause generation keeps off
   while the store's propagators are connected to the SAT solver. A model
   whose detectors posted propagators (symmetry breaking, XOR systems,
   cardinality) stays with lazy clause generation. On the 90 CNFs of the
   SAT Competition 2024/25 screen at 240 s the default mode decides 30
   instead of 17 and -f 32 instead of 15 (CdclMain 30); none of the 240
   wide instances takes the new path. -Dorg.jacop.fz.pureClauses=false
   turns it off.

410. Short tables saying that two lists differ are posted as AllDifferentLists.
   ListsDifferDetector finds jacop_short_table_int over x ++ y whose rows
   each fix position k of x and of y to two different values and hold
   every such pair of the domains, which is x != y as XCSP3 pycsp3 writes
   it (BinaryPuzzle of the XCSP 2023 competition: 6,320 tables of 160 rows
   over 160 variables at size 80). A group of lists that all differ
   pairwise becomes one AllDifferentLists, any other pair one of two
   lists. On the 12 BinaryPuzzle instances at 600 s, solved 7 -> 12 and
   total time 3,708 -> 1,011 s (three base runs ran out of memory at
   4 GB). -Dorg.jacop.fz.listsDiffer=false turns it off.

411. Free search (-f) on routing models: on the giant-tour CVRP model of
   the MiniZinc Challenge 2015 with CVRPLIB X instances, -f at 600 s finds
   23,954 instead of 44,120 on X-n110-k13 and 26,191 instead of 56,345 on
   X-n120-k6 (CP-SAT 9.15 with one worker 32,871 and 36,139).
   - Route neighbourhoods of LNS start from the solution that LCG hands
     over to free search without learning; before, LNS waited for a
     better solution of free search's own and on X-n110-k13 first ran
     after about 490 s.
   - A slow LCG search is kept for a bound raise only when it closes
     lcg-slow-bound-gain (default 0.0005) of the gap to the best cost; a
     raise from 4 to 5 against 59,211 no longer keeps it. From
     lcg-hopeless-at (2 % of the time limit) LCG gives way below
     lcg-hopeless-rate (1 conflict/s).
   - Element constraints are no longer queued for a change of a position
     their index cannot select (Constraint.wakeNeeded()): +50 % failures/s
     on X-n110-k13, where each variable is in about 330 element lists.
   - LNS takes the share lns-share-routes (3) of free search's calls on
     models with routes, instead of lns-share (0.8).
   On the 88 wide-set instances with an element or circuit constraint the
   time over the set goes from 15,991 to 15,202 s at 600 s;
   benchmarks/cvrp-giant-tour in osolpro has the runs.

412. JaSAT (CdclMain and pure clause models) gives a share of its time to
   a feasibility-jump local search over the root-simplified formula, run at
   each rephasing point (W. Luteberget, G. Sartor, Feasibility Jump: an
   LP-free Lagrangian MIP heuristic, Mathematical Programming Computation
   15, 2023; for clauses a jump is a flip, so it is clause weighting of the
   breakout kind, P. Morris, The breakout method for escaping from local
   minima, AAAI 1993). Its runs restart from a random assignment with fresh
   weights after a Luby number of flips (M. Luby, A. Sinclair, D.
   Zuckerman, Optimal speedup of Las Vegas algorithms, Information
   Processing Letters 47, 1993); without the restarts it solves neither
   target instance. A model it finds becomes the phase the decisions
   follow until the next conflict, and eliminated variables are
   reconstructed as before. -Djacop.jasat.jump=<share> (CdclMain --jump)
   sets the share, default 0.1, 0 turns it off; it never runs under LCG or
   with assumptions. On the 90 CNF instances of the SAT gap screen at
   240 s: 30 -> 35 solved (sgen 32.7 s and tree-decomposition 147 s, which
   Kissat and JaCoP did not solve in 600 s, fixed-shape-random, puzzle,
   pythagorean-triples), heule-nol 49 -> 13 s, no instance lost, total
   time 0.947; UNSAT instances take the share, 1.107 on the 13 proved.
   At 600 s 41 -> 45 of the 90 are solved, total time 0.942 on the 60
   not solved by both at 240 s.

413. The FlatZinc front end keeps large tables in memory once and builds
   less for them, so the XCSP 2026 models with large tables no longer run
   out of memory at 6 GB. Short tables with equal rows share one
   HybridTable index (CrosswordDesign posts 96 of them, 4 distinct);
   parameter arrays (of integers or sets) of 1024 or more entries that
   MiniZinc writes several times are kept once (the 31 copies of the MDD
   label sets of WordDesign2-words-mdd-025, XCSP 2023, took 5.7 GB); the detectors that scan constraint arguments no
   longer turn parameter arrays into constant variables (84,000 on
   CrosswordDesign-09, all LCG variables); the symmetry detection releases
   its caches after it runs; table variable elimination reads the rows of
   a table only when it joins it, and joins at most a million rows
   (org.jacop.fz.tableElimination.joinInput); table posting copies only
   the rows that fit the domains; and the parser no longer keeps every
   token of an array literal alive. The new mznlib predicates
   fzn_xcsp_table_rows, fzn_xcsp_short_table_rows and
   fzn_xcsp_negative_table_rows take a table as a one-dimensional array of
   rows, which MiniZinc writes once. Live heap: CrosswordDesign-12 7.0 GB
   to 2.5 GB, Steiner3-11 5.4 GB to 1.2 GB, Inglenook-100 out of memory
   at 10 GB to solved in 30 s at 2.9 GB. Over the 30 held-back XCSP 2026
   instances at 600 s and 6 GB, 24 now find solutions and 5 more run to
   the limit (before: none of them); the 2026 CSP replay score goes from
   116 to 130 (fifth to third). The default search is unchanged.

414. JaSAT (CdclMain, plain SAT without an external propagator) vivifies
   on a sorted schedule as Kissat 4 and CaDiCaL 2 do: after each clause
   database reduction, the core, middle and local learnt tiers and the
   irredundant clauses share a budget of 10 % of the propagations since
   the last round (weights 3/3/1/3). Literals are sorted by occurrence and
   candidates lexicographically so that consecutive probes reuse their
   decisions; a conflict or an implied literal is analysed back to the
   decisions, and the clause is shrunk, removed when subsumed or implied
   by irredundant clauses, or strengthened by instantiation. Before, only
   core and middle learnt clauses were vivified, once each. References:
   Li, Xiao, Luo, Manya, Lu, Li, Clause vivification by unit propagation
   in CDCL SAT solvers, Artificial Intelligence 279 (2020); Luo, Li, Xiao,
   Manya, Lu, An effective learnt clause minimization approach for CDCL
   SAT solvers, IJCAI 2017; Biere, Faller, Fazekas, Fleury, Froleyks,
   Pollitt, CaDiCaL, Gimsatul, IsaSAT and Kissat entering the SAT
   Competition 2024. On 90 SAT Competition 2024/25 instances at 240 s
   JaSAT solves 32 instead of 30 (seed 0) and 32 instead of 28 (seed 1),
   with 3 more UNSAT proofs on seed 1 (planning and tseitin-formulas on
   both seeds); at 600 s over 3 seeds circuit-equivalence-checking takes
   0.90x and grs-fp-comm 0.85x the time. LCG and the FlatZinc front end
   keep the previous vivification; -Djacop.jasat.vivifySorted=false
   restores it for CdclMain too.

415. The LP relaxation links the columns of a table with a key column (the
   element constraints over one index that the front end merges into one
   table, such as the options of a class in car sequencing) to the value
   encoding of the key: y = sum_v t(v) b_v for every other column y, the
   linear formulation of element (P. Refalo, Linear formulation of
   constraint programming models and hybrid solvers, CP 2000, LNCS 1894).
   The extra rows of a satisfaction model (value encodings, linearized
   constraints), kept before only when the root LP was infeasible, stay on
   probation for 64 solves and are kept if at least 10 % of the solves
   since then failed the node; a relaxation that fails that often is no
   longer starved by its work budget, and its extra rows are not dropped
   for the pivot limits they hit. Car sequencing (CSPLib 001) p6-76 is
   proved UNSAT with -f in 24 s instead of 318 s; the 108 CSPLib car
   sequencing instances at 600 s with -f: 83 -> 87 solved (p10-93, p36-92
   UNSAT, pb_200_01, pb_200_07), total time -7 %. With -s it prints lpProbationKept
   and lpFailingSolves. -Dorg.jacop.lp.keyedTables=false,
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.satisfactionProbation=0 and -Dorg.jacop.lp.failRate=0
   turn them off.

416. Free search (-f), and model search without a search annotation, of a
   weighted partial MaxSAT model (clauses, 0/1 linear rows and a cost
   that is a weighted sum of 0/1 variables, as the MaxSAT Evaluation
   instances flatten) runs MaxSAT local search,
   org.jacop.search.ls.MaxSatLocalSearch, in the style of SATLike (Z. Lei
   and S. Cai, Solving (weighted) partial MaxSAT by dynamic local search
   for SAT, IJCAI 2018; S. Cai and Z. Lei, Old techniques in new ways:
   clause weighting, unit propagation and hybridization for maximum
   satisfiability, Artificial Intelligence 287, 2020): dynamic clause
   weights with a bound on soft weights, smoothing, and best-from-multiple-
   selection (S. Cai, Balance between complexity and quality: local search
   for minimum vertex cover in massive graphs, IJCAI 2015). The FlatZinc
   front end reads the model back (org.jacop.fz.MaxSatModel): equalities
   and negations of 0/1 variables are merged, relaxation variables that
   occur in one clause become soft clauses, and symmetry breaking
   constraints are left out and solutions mapped onto ones they accept.
   A short burst runs first; its best assignment is checked in the store,
   printed and given to lazy clause generation as its first solution,
   whose bound comes after the core-guided phase; then the local search
   continues in slices from LNS's best solution as LNS proposals. On the
   85 MaxSAT Evaluation 2024 instances where it fires at 240 s, -f is
   better on 16 and worse on none (57 proved both ways), default search
   better on 17 and worse on 1 (60 proved, was 58): ramsey k3_n17
   13395 -> 10372 (CP-SAT 600 s: 11579), HaplotypeAssembly 1874 -> 1560,
   mpe random-net-100-2 95863 -> 91062, drmx-cryptogen 872 -> 819. On
   the 23 gap instances of the screen at 600 s, -f is better on 9 and
   worse on 2 (10 proved, was 9; total time 0.945), default search
   better on 8 and worse on 3 (10 proved, was 9; 0.962). It fires on 2
   of the 240 wide instances, with the same results. --free-config maxsat=0 turns it
   off; maxsat-share (0.05 of the time limit), maxsat-max (60000 ms),
   maxsat-patience (1000 ms), maxsat-lns (200000 flips per proposal),
   maxsat-bound, maxsat-hard and maxsat-smooth tune it.
   The store check of a hint before lazy clause generation (also of the
   project schedule) now runs the root's pending propagation first.

417. With lazy clause generation, a linear constraint of more than 20 terms
   (instead of 40) becomes sums of blocks of about sqrt(n) terms, so its
   explanations are shorter (as the lazy decomposition of Abio and Stuckey,
   "Conflict directed lazy decomposition", CP 2012, does on demand). A
   census of reasons on the 62 wide-quick instances showed sums of 21 to 40
   terms giving reasons of 261 atoms on average, 71 % of all reason atoms on
   lot-sizing pigment20a. Such sums occur in 27 of the 240 wide instances.
   On them at 600 s with -f, two rounds: total time 3,315 -> 2,342 s
   (0.707) and solved 50 -> 52 of 54 (pigment20a 600 -> 64-258 s,
   neighbours-6 69-92 -> 26-46 s, liner-sf tp7_4 192 -> 123 s in round 1; slower
   fast-food ff58 19 -> 26-28 s); default mode, one round: 3,403 ->
   3,368 s, solved 23 -> 24 (neighbours-6 proved in 341 s; slower fast-food
   ff58 227 -> 371 s and mario medium_4 287 -> 359 s). wide-quick at 240 s
   with -f: 6,531 -> 6,422 s, solved 36 -> 37. --free-config lcg-split=40
   restores the old limit.
   
418. JaSAT (CdclSolver, CdclMain) tries Kissat's lucky assignments before
   the first search (Biere and Fleury, Proc. SAT Competition 2022): all
   variables true or false when every clause has a literal of that sign,
   then forward and backward passes in input order deciding false and then
   true, with propagation and one flip per conflict. They run before
   variable elimination and again after it, only in the first solve()
   without assumptions or an external propagator (so not under LCG). An
   attempt that finds no model leaves the search exactly as without it
   (watches, clause literal order and phases are restored, no units are
   kept). On the 90 SAT Competition 2024/25 CNFs of the SAT gap screen
   they find a model for 2 (cryptography-simon, unsolved at 600 s before,
   now 0.14 s as Kissat; subgraph-isomorphism) and cost at most 0.24 s.
   CdclSolver.setLucky(false) and CdclMain --lucky 0 turn them off.

419. Lifted cover cuts of knapsack rows in the LP cut rounds
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.coverCuts=true, off by default). Each row is relaxed
   to a 0/1 knapsack over its 0/1 columns (fixed columns to the side,
   other integer columns at their bound, negative weights complemented),
   a minimal cover is chosen greedily from the LP solution (Crowder,
   Johnson and Padberg, Operations Research 31(5), 1983) and the items
   outside it are lifted at once with the superadditive lifting function
   of Gu, Nemhauser and Savelsbergh, scaled to integers (Sequence independent
   lifting in mixed integer programming, Journal of Combinatorial
   Optimization 4(1), 2000; Lifted cover inequalities for 0-1 integer
   programs: computation, INFORMS Journal on Computing 10(4), 1998).
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.coverSearch=true also separates them in search.
   Statistic lpCoverCuts. On the 18 OR-Library GAP instances of types b-d
   at 600 s, proved 7 -> 8 (default) and 7 -> 9 (-f), total time 0.93 and
   0.92 (gapc_10x100 and gapc_20x100 are proved, root bound of gapc_10x100
   1396.0 -> 1399.0, optimum 1402).

420. Free search (-f) runs a memetic tabu search before lazy clause
   generation when every constraint of the model defines one variable from
   others (products and other functional constraints, linear equalities)
   and the cost is a function of at most 64-value decision variables, as on
   low autocorrelation binary sequences (Gallardo, Cotta and Fernandez,
   Finding low autocorrelation binary sequences with memetic algorithms,
   Applied Soft Computing 9(4), 1252-1262, 2009). org.jacop.fz.FunctionalModel detects such models and
   org.jacop.search.ls.FunctionalTabuSearch evaluates the definitions
   incrementally (linear sums by deltas, partial sums inlined) and runs a
   population with crossover, mutation and tabu walks. Its best assignment
   is checked in the store and printed; LCG then starts from it with its
   cost as bound and without the core-guided phase, and LNS continues the
   tabu search from the incumbent as a proposer. --free-config tabu=0
   turns it off; tabu-share (0.9 of the time left), tabu-max, tabu-stall,
   tabu-generations and tabu-lns tune it. On the XCSP 2025
   LowAutocorrelation instances at 240 s it finds 108 (optimum) for n=40,
   about 856 for n=100 and 3820-4020 for n=200 (before 180, 1226-1354 and
   4976-5172; Choco 120, 1038 and 4292 at 1800 s).

421. Next to the redundant GCC that the FlatZinc front end posts on counts
   written by hand, it posts the two sums every complete GCC implies when
   the counted variables count themselves (self-describing sequences,
   magic series): sum(c) = n and sum(v * c[v]) = sum(x) (B. M. Smith,
   Modelling, Handbook of Constraint Programming, 2006). GCC's bound
   consistency alone lowered one bound by one per round of its fixpoint;
   with holes in the domains, as the facts of root probing under -f leave
   them, it ran 125 rounds per call. MiniZinc Challenge nmseq 202 -f takes
   11 s instead of 32 s, nmseq 395 25 s instead of 37 s (default search
   11 s and 25 s instead of 15 s and 35 s). -Dorg.jacop.fz.gccSums=false
   turns them off.

422. A Hamiltonian path written as a numbering gets a redundant circuit.
   NumberedPathDetector finds an all_different over N variables with values
   in 1..N where every cell c holds N or a neighbour holds x[c] + 1
   (among over the model's reified x[d] - x[c] = 1, or a short table of
   (v, *, .., v + 1, .., *) rows), as in the CalvinPuzzle and Hidato
   models of the XCSP competitions. It posts successor variables, s[c] = d <-> x[d] =
   x[c] + 1 with the model's own literals, a start node whose successor is
   the cell of value 1, and Circuit over them, so subtours are pruned (G.
   Pesant, M. Gendreau, J.-Y. Potvin and J.-M. Rousseau, "An exact
   constraint logic programming algorithm for the traveling salesman
   problem with time windows", Transportation Science 32(1), 1998). The
   numbering alone finds the dead ends of a path only when it is nearly
   complete. Over the 1,761 XCSP FlatZinc files of 2022-2025 it fires on
   the 14 CalvinPuzzle (2023, 05-12 and their table variants) and the 9
   Hidato-table (2022) instances only. With -f at 600 s, CalvinPuzzle goes
   from 11 to 14 solved and the total time from 3007 to 49 s
   (CalvinPuzzle-10/12 and table-12 unknown -> 2-15 s, 09 424 -> 2 s), and
   Hidato-table from 5 to 9 solved and 2779 to 1162 s (11-11 257 -> 19 s,
   12..15 unknown -> 30-591 s).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.numberedPath=false turns it off; it is off with --proof.

423. Memory of large layered graphs (mdd, regular) and of large clause sets.
   Constraints ExtensionalSupportMdd given the same arrays (the FlatZinc
   front end keeps equal long arrays once) share the graph built from them
   when their variables have equal domains, instead of a graph each; the
   arrays are no longer copied. The automatic choice of the propagation
   engine of a layered graph keeps the counters while their state fits in
   a quarter of the heap and takes support search past it, which keeps no
   counter per node. Under lazy clause generation, layered graphs become
   clauses while those fit in 1/8 of the heap
   (-Djacop.lcg.graphClauses.heapShare); larger ones are propagated and
   explained by cuts. The linear relaxation counts the arcs of a flow
   model before it builds it. In JaSAT, elimination and the local search
   walk are skipped when their copies of the clauses would not fit in a
   quarter of the heap, and the learnt clauses are reduced early when they
   take more than 1/16 of it. The FlatZinc front end keeps the equal sets
   of a set array of 1024 or more sets once (MagicSquare-mdd-16 read 31
   million label sets, 4.2 GB). WordDesign2-words-mdd-025 (XCSP 2023, 300
   MDDs on one diagram of 525,720 nodes and 1.8 million edges) ran out of
   memory at 6 GB while posting and is now solved in 147 s;
   MagicSquare-mdd-10 (-f) ran out of memory at 6 GB (and 12 GB) during
   the search and MagicSquare-mdd-16 while reading; both now run to the
   time limit.

424. Routing with time windows: the LP relaxation of a Routes constraint
   whose customers have times gets big-M arc-time rows
   t_j >= t_i + d_ij - M (1 - x_ij), M = latest_i + d_ij - earliest_j over
   the reduced windows (M. Desrochers, G. Laporte, Improvements and
   extensions to the Miller-Tucker-Zemlin subtour elimination constraints,
   Operations Research Letters 10(1), 1991, 27-36), kept in the search LP,
   so its reduced-cost fixing prunes arc literals at every node; and
   2-path cuts x(delta-(S)) >= 2 for customer sets that one route cannot
   visit in one stretch within their windows (B. Kohl, J. Desrosiers,
   O. B. G. Madsen, M. M. Solomon, F. Soumis, 2-path cuts for the vehicle
   routing problem with time windows, Transportation Science 33(1), 1999,
   101-116), checked exactly by a bounded search over the windows. At most
   1,000 arc-time rows are kept (org.jacop.lp.arcTimeMax), those whose
   travel time is the largest share of M.
   GiantTourDetector now also reads the XCSP TSPTW form (subcircuit over
   x + 1, time rows enforced by int_lin_le_imp and exempt on the arc back
   to the depot). On the 44 instances that post Routes (18 Solomon VRPTW,
   16 XCSP 2023 TSPTW, 5 MZNC 2025 tsptw and the 5 wide-set routing
   instances) at 240 s the default mode proves 30 -> 34 (total time 0.80)
   and -f 31 -> 34 (0.90); at 600 s on the 14 still open it proves
   0 -> 6 and 1 -> 5 (TSPTW2 n040w060, n040w080, n060w040 and n060w060,
   RC101_50, tsptw n100w120.003).
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.arcTimeRows=false and -Dorg.jacop.lp.twoPathCuts=false
   turn them off.

425. The split of long linear constraints for lazy clause generation
   (lcg-split) keeps every sum at 20 terms or fewer: a constraint of more
   than 400 terms, whose blocks of sqrt(n) terms and sum of blocks were
   both longer than 20 terms, becomes blocks of 20 terms whose sum is split
   again, a tree of sums (as the lazy decomposition of Abio and Stuckey,
   "Conflict directed lazy decomposition", CP 2012, introduces partial
   sums). It fires on 21 of the 240 wide instances, mostly on objectives.
   At 600 s: same 12 proved with -f, total time 5,588 -> 5,542 s, better
   objectives on gbac UD3 (269 -> 173), ihtc i01, unit-commitment, worse
   on warehouse olil4 (47,617 -> 61,327); default search, same 8 proved,
   better objectives on roster chicroster large_20 (no solution -> 98),
   surface-based-tsp ch130 (9,040 -> 8,110), celar graph11 and
   unit-commitment, worse on none. At 240 s with -f: total 2,448 ->
   2,323 s (roster chicroster 79 -> 37 s, network_50_cstr 88 -> 16 s).
   --free-config lcg-split-tree=0 restores blocks of sqrt(n) terms; with -s
   it prints lcgSplitSums.

426. With --lcg, -f and an objective that sums at least four squares (x * x
   or x ^ 2 among the terms reached through up to three linear constraints
   from the cost variable), free search without learning runs first for
   10 % of the time limit and LCG then goes on from its best solution. A
   sum of squared deviations is a balancing objective (balanced academic
   curricula, nurse workloads, low autocorrelation): LCG's bound literals
   say little about which value of a decision raises it, while free search
   picks each value by its bound on the objective (value-impact). XCSP 2023
   GBACP at 600 s: geometric mean of the best objective 0.63 of before,
   UD07 7149 -> 378, UD03 2911 -> 177, UD05 340 -> 217 (CoSoCo in the 2023
   competition: 2338 and 273); MiniZinc Challenge gbac 0.98,
   LowAutocorrelation 0.98. Of the 240 wide instances only two gbac instances are reached.
   --free-config free-first=<share> sets the share, 0 turns it off.

427. The core-guided phase of LCG no longer loops forever when it probes
   the objective's bound (the probes of commit 7c2bc093d) after the
   objective's own minimum has reached the best solution: every probe
   target was already implied, so the step doubled until it overflowed
   and the loop never ended. MiniZinc Challenge 2023 roster-shifts-bool
   s100_e20_c8 under -f with -t 240 s or more never ended (bound and best
   both 4955 at 7 s); it now proves the optimum in 7 s. Of the 240 wide
   instances it is the only one that met the loop at 240 s.

428. Subtour cuts for successor models (opt-in,
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.successorForest=true). The FlatZinc front end finds an
   output array x and an element list pos whose arcs x[i] = j are proved
   acyclic by probing (posting pos[j] <= pos[i] fails after x[i] = j), the
   Miller-Tucker-Zemlin formulation of a path or tour (Miller, Tucker and
   Zemlin, JACM 1960), and posts a redundant graph.Forest over the arc and
   node literals. The linear relaxation separates on it the generalized
   subtour elimination cuts of TreeCuts (Lucena and Resende, Discrete
   Applied Mathematics 2004; Fischetti, Salazar-Gonzalez and Toth, INFORMS
   Journal on Computing 1998). The tree cut rounds now also move to the
   sparse simplex when the dense tableau runs out of pivots before the
   bound has moved. On prize-collecting 32-4-8-2 (MiniZinc Challenge 2016)
   the root bound goes from 172 (the assignment relaxation) to 102.5, for
   the optimum 92, and on cvrp simple2 from 24 to 34 (optimal). It stays
   off by default: of 187 optimisation instances of the wide set it fires
   on 9, cuts on 2, and the proof times are no better (32-4-8-2 at 240 s:
   28 s -> 34 s by default, 27-38 s -> 20 s with -f).

429. The linear relaxation (--lp, and LCG free search -f) keeps the rows
   that linear overlap factoring (272) reduces to the shared sum alone.
   A row k * S rel c whose whole sum S became the new variable z is
   k * z rel c, which the FlatZinc front end posts as a bound of z or as
   XmulCeqZ, so the relaxation, which substitutes S for z, never saw it.
   These rows are now recorded at posting and given to the relaxation,
   which expands them back into the original row. On MIPLIB p0548 (pure
   integer, generic MPS model) the root cut bound goes from 464 to 7,325
   (optimum 8,691) and -f proves it in 26 s instead of 297 s. At 600 s,
   on the 18 MIPLIB and wide instances where the extra rows exist and
   240 s results differed, the 7 proved instances take 7,271 s against
   7,624 s in total (all 7 faster), and 8 of the 11 others end with a
   better objective (mitre and p2756 at the optimum), 3 worse (air04,
   warehouse olil4, cap6000). The rows are counted in the statistic
   lpUnitRows.

430. JaSAT substitutes equivalent literals before variable elimination. The
   strongly connected components of the binary implication graph (B.
   Aspvall, M. F. Plass, R. E. Tarjan, A linear-time algorithm for testing
   the truth of certain quantified Boolean formulas, Information Processing
   Letters 8(3), 1979; A. Van Gelder, Toward leaner binary-clause reasoning
   in a satisfiability solver, Annals of Mathematics and Artificial
   Intelligence 43, 2005) and clausal congruence closure over AND, XOR and
   ITE gates found in the clauses (A. Biere, K. Fazekas, M. Fleury, N.
   Froleyks, Clausal Congruence Closure, SAT 2024) give classes of equal
   literals; each class is replaced by one representative, observed and
   assumption variables never being replaced. The new GateExtractor finds
   the gates and is shared with variable elimination. DRAT proofs cover the
   new steps (XOR and ITE equivalences by case splits on the gate inputs).
   On the 90 SAT Competition instances of the JaSAT comparison at 240 s,
   against develop with bounded variable addition, two rounds: solved 32 ->
   33 and 34 -> 33, total time 15,656 -> 15,552 s and 15,520 -> 15,396 s;
   hardware-model-checking (2.2 s, unsolved at 600 s before) and
   grs-fp-comm (210-237 s) are proved only with it, the other flips
   (puzzle, planning, scheduling) go both ways between rounds. Under --lcg it fires on 19 of the 240 wide instances, with
   the same results. --substitute 0 and --congruence 0 in CdclMain,
   -Djacop.jasat.substitute=false and -Djacop.jasat.congruence=false turn
   it off.

431. The core-guided phase of LCG (-f) stops at its time and work limits
   between two cores. The SAT solver checks those limits only after a
   conflict it learns from, and a core that the assumptions meet at their
   first conflict ends the call before that check, so a phase whose cores
   all came that way ran on: on MiniZinc Challenge 2012 train instance.2,
   the core probe, given about 2 s of a 60 s run, took 33 s, and branch and
   bound found 71820 only after it. The probe now ends at 0.9 s, and 71820
   comes at 8 s instead of 233 s of a 240 s run. The statistic
   lcgCoreLimitStops counts the phases so ended. It fires on 51 of the 240
   wide instances at 60 s; on those, -f at 240 s takes 3,669 -> 3,593 s
   (39 = 39 proved), at 600 s 7,382 -> 7,675 s with one proof lost
   (crosswords grid-05.04, proved by base once in 166 s and by neither in
   four 400 s reruns), and default search at 240 s 5,684 -> 5,719 s.

432. Free search without learning (-f, when LCG free search gives way)
   no longer keeps the LCG solver it took over from: its SAT clauses,
   trail and three literal maps per variable stayed live next to the
   clauses posted again as constraints. Five references held it: the
   solver kept for its statistics, the finished speed-check thread, the
   trail pointer of every Boolean variable (AtomicTrail.detach now clears
   it), a local of the method that runs free search, and the store's
   cached copy of its level listeners after the trail was removed
   (ListenerSet now drops the copy on removal). On rcpsp-wet-diverse
   j90_48_4 (MiniZinc Challenge 2019, 2.35 million variables) with a
   6 GB heap the live heap in free search is 4.3-4.8 GB instead of the
   whole 6 GB.

433. The root cut rounds of the linear relaxation go past up to two rounds
   that leave the LP bound unchanged, instead of stopping at the first.
   The first rounds of a dual degenerate LP often change only the basis;
   the next round then moves the bound (MIPLIB p2756: root bound
   2689.7 -> 3102 for the optimum 3124, proved in about 100 s under
   default search and -f at 600 s, unproved before). Cuts of stalled
   rounds that never move the bound are dropped again. A stalled round
   happens on 39 of the 240 wide instances and the bound moves after it
   on 6; at 600 s these 39 solve 29 = 29 (default) and 31 -> 32 (-f,
   total time 0.92), a 29-instance MIPLIB set 17 -> 16 and 17 -> 18 (the
   default-mode losses have no stalled round). The extra rounds cost a
   median of 0.00 s, at most 0.25 s. -Dorg.jacop.lp.cutStalls=0 restores
   the old rule; statistics lpCutStalls and lpCutStallsRecovered.

434. JaSAT can decide by a VMTF queue (variable move-to-front) in focused
   mode, as CaDiCaL and Kissat do, and by its activity heap in stable
   mode: CdclSolver.setVmtf(true), CdclMain --vmtf 1; off by default,
   and ignored under an external propagator (LCG). With one heap for
   both modes, the stable phases leave scores that steer the focused
   phases. Unlike CaDiCaL and Kissat, focused mode still bumps the heap,
   and the queue starts with the first variable at its end. L. Ryan,
   Efficient algorithms for clause-learning SAT solvers, MSc thesis,
   Simon Fraser University, 2004; A. Biere, A. Froehlich, Evaluating CDCL
   variable scoring schemes, SAT 2015. Of the 90 SAT Competition 2024/25
   instances of the SAT gap screen at 240 s it solves 41 instead of 40,
   total time 0.955 (with the queue alone 38); on the 25 instances that
   changed, at 600 s, 24 instead of 21 and 0.740 (hardware-verification,
   scheduling, stedman-triples, unknown-cases newly solved, random-csp
   513 -> 37 s, edge-matching lost), but most UNSAT instances are slower
   (tseitin-formulas 184 -> 564 s, planning 200 -> 496 s), hence off by
   default.

435. The FlatZinc front end posts the job view of an open shop written in
   the order of its operations (PermutedTasksDetector): when the
   operations of a chain s[j] + d[j] <= s[j+1] run on all different
   resources m[j] that use every resource of their union, each start is
   channelled by an element s[j] = t[m[j]] over one array t and each
   duration is the same function of the resource (a table or a constant
   element), it posts a unary resource over t with those durations and
   the window rows s[first] <= t[k], t[k] + p[k] <= s[last] + d[last].
   These are the redundant constraints of a dual model (Cheng, Lee and Wu,
   Constraints 4(2), 1999), the job disjunctive of Malapert, Cambazard,
   Gueret, Jussien, Langevin and Rousseau (INFORMS J. Computing 24(2),
   2012). On the 14 XCSP 2025 SchedulingOS instances (Gueret-Prins) at
   240 s: proved 12 -> 14 in both modes, total time 758 -> 191 s (default)
   and 723 -> 140 s (-f); gp-10-01 1194 -> optimum 1093 in 41 s, gp-10-05
   proved 1071 (no 2025 solver proved it). It fires on no other XCSP
   2022-2025 instance and on none of the wide set.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.permutedTasks=false turns it off.

436. The FlatZinc front end posts a short table over 0/1 variables whose
   every row fixes one variable (the others are stars) as the clause of
   those literals, instead of a HybridTable. XCSP writes CNF formulas so:
   Crypto1 of the XCSP 2022 competition is 31,334 such tables over 1,906
   Booleans, which now form a model of clauses alone that JaSAT solves:
   all 10 Crypto1 instances in 20 to 152 s with free search, none before
   in 600 s. -Dorg.jacop.fz.shortTableClauses=false turns this off.

432. Under --lcg a table whose unfixed columns all have two values (0/1
   tables, differential cryptanalysis S-boxes) is posted as the clauses
   of all prime implicates of its forbidden points, computed with the
   Quine-McCluskey method (W. V. Quine, The problem of simplifying truth
   functions, American Mathematical Monthly 59(8), 1952; E. J. McCluskey,
   Minimization of Boolean functions, Bell System Technical Journal 35(6),
   1956). Unit propagation on all prime implicates of a relation is domain
   consistent, so the clauses prune what the table pruned, without a
   literal per tuple and without calling the table propagator. Tables up
   to 12 unfixed columns and 512 prime implicates are encoded
   (jacop.lcg.tableClauses.boolean.arity, .limit); the statistic
   lcgBooleanTablesAsClauses counts them and
   -Djacop.lcg.tableClauses.boolean=false turns it off. On XCSP 2022
   DC-rijndael-xor5-r08 LCG makes about 6 times as many conflicts per
   second. At 600 s with -f, on the 45 XCSP 2022-2025 instances where it
   fires (DC, Crypto1, Spot5, Nonogram, Fortress, Tal, Ortholatin,
   Fillomino, SolitaireBattleship), solved goes from 31 to 38 (6 Crypto1,
   DC-midori r14) and the total time from 8,936 to 4,805 s
   (DC-rijndael r06 316 -> 42 s; r07 29 -> 24 and r08 46 -> 36 at the
   limit); Nonogram-069 takes 16 s instead of 9 s. It fires on 6 of the
   240 wide instances (spot5, connect, table-layout, project-planning),
   with the same results in default mode and with -f.

437. The FlatZinc front end can rewrite a distance on a cycle,
   m = min(|e|, c - |e|) with -c < e < c, as m = |f| with
   e + c * k = f, k in -1..1 and -c/2 <= f <= c/2 (opt-in,
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.cyclicDistance=true). The minimum prunes nothing on e
   until |e| is decided, so a bound on the cyclic bandwidth does not
   narrow the positions; once k is fixed, the linear equation does. When
   the intermediate variables are used only there, the minimum is
   replaced and a difference e = x - y is substituted. At 600 s with -f,
   on XCSP 2022 CyclicBandwidth caterpillar35 goes from 105 to 29 (the
   best of the 2022 field), caterpillar29 from 64 to 24 and mesh2D25x26
   from 112 to 34, but the lower bounds of lazy clause generation grow
   more slowly, so caterpillar13, 16 and 19, proved in 21, 213 and 336 s
   without it, are not proved in 600 s; it therefore stays off. It fires
   on none of the 240 wide instances.

438. Reachable (and Tree, Path, Steiner, which post it) prunes with a root
   variable that is not fixed yet: in an undirected graph, nodes that no
   root value reaches are removed and what every path from a selected node
   to the root values goes through is selected; in a directed graph the
   nodes and edges that every path from the valid roots to a selected node
   goes through are selected, as they were for a fixed root. Reachable,
   connected and undirected tree now reach domain consistency in the
   consistency checker, which gets cases for the graph globals. Forest
   fails a complete assignment whose selected edges close a cycle.

439. Deviation and Spread round the bounds of their cost to its residue:
   every term a * x[i] - b is a multiple of g = gcd(a, b), so a deviation
   is congruent to a * sum - n * b modulo 2g and a spread to
   g (a * sum - n * b) modulo 2g^2. Span gives a present master at least the
   duration of each present interval, makes absent an interval longer than
   the master can be, and equates the master with the only interval that
   can give both its start and its end. Alternative and Span now wake on
   holes, which their propagation reads. The consistency checker gets cases
   for tables, sequence, stretch, same, exact cover, among, alternative,
   span, diffn, deviation, spread, the GCC variants and reservoir.

440. JaSAT (CdclMain, plain SAT) sweeps and probes before variable
   elimination. Sweeping (Kissat's sweep) takes, for each variable, the
   clauses within two or three steps of it (at most 256 variables and
   1024 clauses, widened after each completed round), solves them with a
   small separate CDCL solver, and refines backbone and equivalence
   candidates by its models and by flipping literals; confirmed backbone
   literals become units and confirmed equivalences are substituted in
   place, with an extension stack for the models. Failed-literal probing
   propagates the roots of the binary implication graph, learns the 1UIP
   unit of a failed literal and lifts units and equivalences implied by
   both signs. Both write DRAT steps. Sweeping gets 100 million steps of
   work in its first round and 10 % of the search's propagations after
   that, and is delayed after unproductive rounds. CdclMain --sweep 0|1
   and --probe 0|1 (default 1); CdclSolver.setSweeping and setProbing are
   off by default, so LCG and FlatZinc are unchanged. On the SAT
   Competition 2024 bitvector instance from the 90-instance SAT gap set
   JaSAT finds 5,757 equivalences and refutes it in 2.5 s (unsolved at
   600 s before, Kissat 4.0.4 18 s); at 600 s on the 18 instances where
   the 240 s round differed, 15 -> 17 solved and total time 3,188 ->
   2,770 s. References: Biere, Fleury, Froleyks, Heule, The Kissat SAT
   solver entering the SAT Competition 2023 (sweeping with the kitten
   sub-solver); Biere, Fazekas, Fleury, Froleyks, Clausal congruence
   closure, SAT 2024; Kuehlmann, Dynamic transition relation
   simplification for bounded property checking, ICCAD 2004; Mishchenko,
   Chatterjee, Jiang, Brayton, FRAIGs: a unifying representation for
   logic synthesis and verification, ERL Technical Report 2005; Lynce,
   Marques-Silva, Probing-based preprocessing techniques for
   propositional satisfiability, ICTAI 2003; Heule, Jarvisalo, Biere,
   Efficient CNF simplification based on binary implication graphs,
   SAT 2011.

441. After free search without learning (-f, an objective that sums
   squares), lazy clause generation printed a solution only when it was
   better than its incumbent, which can be the hint of the search before
   the linear relaxation and worse than the best solution printed: it
   printed a solution as good as the best one again. It now prints only
   solutions better than every solution printed.

442. LCG free search (-f) pauses its neighbourhood search when it has not
   improved the best solution for longer than twice the time it took to
   find it, and at least 30 s (--free-config lns-stall=2,
   lns-stall-min=30000; lns-stall=0 turns the pause off): branch and bound
   and the lower-bound searches then get the time to prove the optimum.
   Neighbourhood search after an optimum found early took 130-155 s of
   240 s on instances that never proved it. At 600 s, on the 23 wide
   instances that stay in LCG and
   were not proved, proved goes from 5 to 8 (fox-geese-corn 17,
   java-routing trip_8_3, valve-network horizon20, trains18) at the same
   total time; on a second seed of the 7 that change, from 1 to 4
   (pigment20a 233 -> 98 s), total 3,833 -> 3,206 s. On wide-quick at
   240 s, 53 = 53 solved, total 5,147 -> 5,177 s; on the other 31 wide
   optimisation instances not proved in 45 s, 12 = 12 solved at 600 s,
   total 12,422 -> 12,209 s (timeouts counted at the limit).

443. Not-all-equal clauses go to JaSAT with one variable fixed. The
   FlatZinc front end posts nvalue over 0/1 variables with a fixed count
   of 2 (not all equal) as the clause and its complement, and with a
   count of 1 as equivalences, under lazy clause generation, instead of
   the Values propagator (-Dorg.jacop.fz.booleanNValue=false turns it
   off). A model of clauses alone in which the complement of every clause
   is a clause too has the complement symmetry: the complement of a
   solution is a solution, so the pure clause hand-off to JaSAT
   (PureClauseModel) fixes the variable with the most occurrences to 1,
   as Heule, Kullmann and Marek do for the Boolean Pythagorean triples
   problem (Solving and Verifying the Boolean Pythagorean Triples
   Problem via Cube-and-Conquer, SAT 2016), and lex-leader symmetry
   breaking is left out for such a model, since its constraints would
   keep the model with lazy clause generation
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.complementSymmetry=false turns both off). New class
   org.jacop.jasat.cdcl.ComplementSymmetry. XCSP 2023
   PythagoreanTriples-7000 and 7500, unsolved in 600 s before, take
   36 s and 87 s (the 2023 field at 2400 s: Picat 22 s and 366 s, Kissat
   163 s and 314 s); 7824 and the unsatisfiable 7825 stay open. Fires on
   none of the 240 wide instances (no model there is clauses alone) and,
   of the 90 SAT Competition formulas of the pure clause screen, on
   heule-folkman only (unsolved in 600 s with and without it).

444. Hamiltonian paths written as a numbering (NumberedPathDetector) are
   searched along the path. The detector also posts the cell of each
   value (Assignment, the inverse of the numbering), and a satisfaction
   model without a search annotation (not under -f) then follows
   int_search(cells, input_order, indomain_min): the cells of the values
   1, 2, ... in turn, so each decision extends the path from its last
   cell, and the Circuit of the successors fails a partial path that cut
   off cells (-Dorg.jacop.fz.numberedPath.search=false keeps the default
   search). It also removes, at the root, the values that a cell cannot
   hold at its distance from the cells of known values: a cell at
   distance d from the cell of value v holds no value within d - 1 of v
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.numberedPathDistances=false turns it off). The XCSP
   2022 Hidato-table instances that give no cell are Hamiltonian paths
   of the king's graph; at 600 s in default mode the 12x12 to 15x15
   grids take 3.1-4.5 s instead of 72-280 s (Glasgow 6-31 s in the 2022
   field) and the 9 Hidato instances 26 s instead of 777 s in total; the
   14 CalvinPuzzle instances of 2023 take 66 s instead of 92 s in total
   (08 and table-08 3.3 -> 6.6 s and 4.6 -> 10.4 s). Fires on none of
   the 240 wide instances.

445. JaSAT (CdclSolver) visits watches faster, with the same search: a
   watch is one long (clause and blocking literal), so a visit whose
   blocking literal is true is one load, and the watch is written back
   only once an earlier watch has left the list; every watch list keeps
   its length in its first slot (no separate size arrays, one cache line
   fewer per added watch); arena compaction copies the clauses in the
   order the watch lists of the most active variables visit them (as
   CaDiCaL's arena moving); chronological backtracking stops looking for
   the assignment level at the current level. On the 83 SAT Competition
   2024/25 CNFs of the SAT screen that run at least 3 s, reaching the same
   conflict count takes 0.91x the time (geomean) before congruence
   closure, scheduled vivification and jump search came in, and 0.95x
   with them (they spend time this change does not touch); on fpga-routing JaSAT
   makes as many propagations as Kissat but visits about 80 watches per
   propagation, 86 % of them stopped by the blocking literal. CdclMain
   takes --conflicts N (stop after N conflicts) and prints the learnt
   clause count and arena size.

446. With -s -v (or --search-statistics), the portfolio (-p) prints the
   statistics of each thread after its answer, in a block of
   portfolioThread.<name>.<key> lines: how the thread ended (complete,
   stopped, done, or running when it had not stopped 10 s after the
   answer), its solve time, nodes, failures, restarts, propagations,
   solutions and objective, and when it ran them, its LCG conflicts, SAT
   propagations, average learnt clause length, conflicts before LCG gave
   way and LNS runs. A portfolio slower than one thread can then be
   traced to its threads (issue 797).

447. Under lazy clause generation, the reason why two variables have no
   common value (an element over variables dropping an index, x = y,
   x + c = z, x + y = c, x != y) cites only the bounds that separate their
   ranges, each weakened as far as the other allows, and, when the ranges
   overlap, only the bounds that set the common range; before, it cited
   both bounds of both variables. With both variables fixed the old reason
   named both values, so a learnt clause pinned the value of every
   variable it went through. MiniZinc Challenge 2016 java-auto-gen
   binpack_11 under its search annotation: 293k -> 7.5k failures,
   27 -> 2.1 s (Chuffed 0.14 32k failures). Paired runs at 600 s on the
   85 wide instances it reaches: binpack_11 38 -> 6 s, lot-sizing
   pigment20a 184 -> 88 s, evm-super-compilation 140 -> 75 s, oocsp_racks
   030_mii8 95 -> 47 s, seat-moving sm-10-13-00 loses its proof; wide-quick
   at 240 s takes 0.944 of the time. On for fixed search, off under -f
   (costas-array and blocks-world slower over three rounds);
   -Djacop.lcg.shiftSplitBounds=true|false overrides.

448. Memory of many MDDs on one diagram, in the MiniZinc library and in
   support search. fzn_mdd, fzn_mdd_nondet and fzn_cost_mdd pass the
   arrays of the diagram as they are: array1d made a new array at each
   call, which MiniZinc wrote again every few calls (WordDesign2-words-
   mdd-025, XCSP 2023: 31 copies of 1.8 million edges, a FlatZinc of
   1.1 GB that took 7 GB and 170 s to flatten; now 36 MB in 6 s). The
   support search of a layered graph keeps the nodes without support as
   a list with a bitset instead of three arrays over the nodes, and the
   constraints of a store on one built graph share the stamps of the
   search, which are only read during one call (66 KB instead of 6 MB
   per constraint on that diagram). The symmetry pass reads an array
   longer than 64 once however many constraints share it, and the flow
   encodings of the linear relaxation take the edges on a path and
   their arc columns once per built graph over equal domains: both read
   the 1.8 million edges again for each MDD (setup of -055, 1,485 MDDs,
   164 s -> 9 s). The explanations of a layered graph keep the edges of
   a level by value and skip the values gone from the domain (20 % more
   LCG conflicts per second on -035, same explanations). All sizes,
   -035 to -100 (4,951 MDDs), now set up in 6 to 23 s within 6 GB;
   -035 and -045 are solved (-045 ran out of memory at 6 GB before),
   -055 to -100 stay unsolved at 600 s: LCG makes about one conflict a
   second on these diagrams, and search without learning does not find
   a solution either.

449. The FlatZinc front end posts a short table (jacop_short_table_int) of
   at least 100,000 rows as a multi-valued decision diagram
   (ExtensionalSupportMdd) when its reduced diagram has at most a tenth
   of the table's cells as edges. The diagram is built from the rows with
   stars matching the domain of their column; scopes with the same table,
   repeated-variable pattern and domains share one diagram and the table
   is filtered once for them. A table listing a rule compiles to a few
   hundred edges: the connectivity table of Benzenoide (XCSP 2023), 3
   million rows of arity 8 on 331 scopes, becomes 203 edges. The
   symmetry detection now keys an array of constants by its contents
   when it compares the model's lex-leader constraints, instead of
   spelling out every element for every constraint and symmetry, which
   ran out of memory on these tables. Benzenoide-12 and -13, out of
   memory or without a solution in 600 s before, reach 87 and 72 (the
   best solutions of the ranked solvers of 2023 were 3,033 and 3,125).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.tableMdd.rows (0 turns it off) and
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.tableMdd.ratio set the gates. References: Bryant,
   Graph-based algorithms for Boolean function manipulation, IEEE
   Transactions on Computers 35(8), 1986; Cheng and Yap, An MDD-based
   generalized arc consistency algorithm for positive and negative table
   constraints and some global constraints, Constraints 15(2), 2010.

450. Disjoint and Diff2 limit a rectangle's length from the least start of
   each segment of its start domain, not from the interval of the domain
   that meets a forbidden region, and begin a new segment after a region
   whose end is a hole of the domain. Disjoint lets a rectangle of zero
   width take its place, as documented now: it fails a segment or point
   strictly inside another rectangle, which satisfied() and the fixed
   check accepted, and lets it lie on the common border of two touching
   rectangles, which the merged barrier profile pruned. Disjoint and
   DisjointNoProfile now count the same solutions as a brute force on the
   rectangle fingerprint. ArcTimes runs its passes until the nodes queued
   by its own pruning of a variable that is both a successor and a time
   are done. The consistency checker gets cases for Disjoint, Diff2 with
   zero lengths, SubcircuitCost, WeightedSubcircuits, Routes, ArcTimes,
   GlobalCardinality, Alldifferent, Decreasing, CardinalityMatrix,
   ConjunctionSum and UnaryEarlinessTardiness. Sum narrows
   the variables after a hole moved a bound of the sum, EqBool over one
   variable holds, DisjointConditional releases (condition 0) a pair whose
   compulsory parts overlap, and MaxCliqueBound.satisfied() no longer
   throws on an open vertex. The checker also gets cases for the Boolean
   reifications, IfThenBool, In, Sum, SumWeight, MaxCliqueBound,
   DisjointConditional, TableCover and SoftAlldifferent. ConditionalSelect
   now wakes on holes, which can rule a branch out. The checker also
   gets cases for CostRegular, MultiCostRegular, SoftGCC,
   IfThenElseBool, SequenceMdd and CostMdd. SetConsistencyChecker does
   the same for the set constraints: AdiffBeqC puts in b an element of a
   that c cannot hold, CardAeqX passes holes of its cardinality variable
   to the set, and Match keeps its list increasing and gives an element
   of the glb to the only list variable that can take it. XmulYeqC is
   domain consistent: with c != 0 it keeps the divisors of c whose
   quotient is in the other factor (dividing by an interval around 0 kept
   every value up to |c|), and with c = 0 a factor without 0 fixes the
   other one to 0. The checker gets cases for NetworkFlow, Steiner,
   WeightedSpanningTree, BoundedPath, UnaryTransition, MultiCumulative,
   VectorBinpacking, XplusYgtC and XplusClteqZ.

451. JaLP shifts costs instead of moving columns to artificial bounds, and
   its presolve holds inequality rows at the bound that a column singleton
   pushes them to. A nonbasic column whose reduced cost ends up on the
   wrong side of the tolerance by at most 1e-5 (as the slacks of
   inequality rows do, by a few 1e-9, after the dual updates) has its cost
   shifted to make the reduced cost 0, where it was moved to its 1e6
   artificial bound before and took the basic values with it (cost
   shifting, A. Koberstein, "The dual simplex method, techniques for a fast
   and stable implementation", PhD thesis, Paderborn 2005; as in HiGHS).
   The shifts are taken back at the optimum; when a column would then move
   to an artificial bound, the primal simplex takes it into the basis
   (-Dorg.jacop.jalp.shiftMax=0 turns shifting off). The presolve makes an
   inequality row with two columns an equation when one is a column
   singleton whose cost pushes it to a bound that the row implies (the
   dual bound of a column singleton, Andersen and Andersen 1995, section
   3.5), so that the doubleton equation substitutes it
   (-Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveTight=false). LP bench, 687 LPs at 120 s:
   648 -> 653 solved, 0 wrong, 0.948 of the time on LPs over 0.5 s;
   comp07-2idx 159,258 pivots -> 2,992 (TIME -> 3.3 s), thor50dday
   45,687 -> 845 pivots (108 -> 4 s), dano3mip 75 -> 27 s, the n370
   family 5,600 -> 230 pivots, ns1116954, supportcase10/42 and cont4 now
   solved; pilot87 and neos-1354092 take 2x the pivots. The pivots of
   JaCoP's own root LPs (jacop-root, 215) do not change; on the 21 wide
   runs where it fires, 600 s, the same solved and total time.

452. Regular, ExtensionalSupportMdd and Among (set and int forms) no
   longer listen to the removal of every store level. They record a level
   with Store.recordChange when they first change their state there, and
   the store calls their removeLevel only for those levels. Before, every
   backtrack called all of them; on XCSP 2018 NurseRostering-10 (2,080
   regular and 1,328 among constraints) those calls, mostly cache misses
   on untouched state, took 46 % of the time. The search is the same
   (fixed search, and free search under -Djacop.virtualClock=true). With
   -f on 34 models with regular or among (11 wide, 23 XCSP 2022-2025), it
   explores 13 % more nodes in 240 s (geomean) and 9.5 % more in 600 s;
   at 600 s NurseRostering-10 goes from 5,584 to 5,147 (field best 5,054)
   and NurseRostering-07 from 1,377 to 1,297, with 3 better, 1 worse
   (Warehouse-60-120-01, 6,496 vs 6,504) and 20 equal results.
452. The FlatZinc front end reads three more forms of XCSP 2017-2019
   models: a binary table whose missing pairs are exactly x - y = d is an
   edge x - y != d of the all-different detection (the table stays
   posted; pigeonsPlus-11-04 35 -> 0.7 s); when the columns of a table
   (all, or all but one) have the same sum in every row, that sum of 3
   or more terms is posted as a linear equality (Bacp: 16 -> 24 of 24
   proved at 240 s, total 2,084 -> 50 s; -Dorg.jacop.fz.tableSums=false
   turns it off); and a model of clauses and 0/1 negative tables only
   posts the tables as clauses, so it is a pure clause model, whose root
   clause refutation now runs before JaSAT takes it (hole-10: unknown at
   240 s -> 0.7 s; -Dorg.jacop.fz.negativeTableClauses=false turns it
   off). None of them fires on the wide set or XCSP 2022-2025 except the
   disequality edges, which fire without a clique on spot5.

453. JaSAT (CdclSolver) eliminates variables with a rising bound and with
   gates, as Kissat: the bound (resolvents allowed over the clauses they
   replace) is 0 before the search and rises through 1, 2, 4, 8 to 16
   with each complete elimination run during search, resolvents may have
   up to 100 literals, and when a variable is the output of an AND, XOR,
   ITE gate or an equivalence (GateExtractor) only the resolvents of its
   defining clauses with the other clauses are added (Een, Biere,
   "Effective Preprocessing in SAT through Variable and Clause
   Elimination", SAT 2005; Biere, Jarvisalo, Kiesl, "Preprocessing in SAT
   Solving", Handbook of Satisfiability, 2nd ed., 2021). Later rounds try
   only the variables whose clauses changed, and a run during search is
   skipped when the last one found nothing and no new root fact or clause
   came. With an external propagator (lazy clause generation) elimination
   stays as before. On the 90 SAT Competition 2024/25 CNFs at 240 s JaSAT
   solves 44 instead of 39 (seed 1: 45 instead of 41) in 0.94x (0.977x)
   the time; on the structured UNSAT ones it made 2-3x the propagations
   per conflict of Kissat because it eliminated far fewer variables
   (algorithm-equivalence-checking 344 vs 124), now 136. At 600 s on 36
   affected instances 31 instead of 26 are solved (planning,
   grs-fp-comm, quasigroup-completion faster or new), total 0.71x.
   CdclMain takes --elim-bound, --elim-clause-limit and --elim-gates.
   GateExtractor looks for an XOR only on clause variable sets that have
   enough clauses of one parity, each set once (sorted keys of both
   occurrence lists instead of a scan of both lists per base clause): the
   same gates in less time, Hamming-20-10-05-09 (XCSP 2024 as CNF) reaches
   100k conflicts in 14.9 s instead of 20.1 s.

454. Sampling dives for float objectives (FloatDive): a float search that
   has not proved its optimum after 10 % of the time-out alternates runs
   of branch and bound (node budget 10,000, doubling) with batches of
   dives under the bound best - gap. A dive narrows the float search
   variables in order to a box of the precision around a sampled value
   and propagates after each one; the value is uniform in the domain or,
   half of the time, Gaussian around the point of the last improving
   dive, a (1+1) evolution strategy with the 1/5 success rule of
   Rechenberg (Evolutionsstrategie, Frommann-Holzboog, 1973). Sampling
   points for an upper bound follows the upper bounding of interval
   branch and bound (I. Araya, G. Trombettoni, B. Neveu, G. Chabert,
   J. Global Optim. 60(2), 2014) and diving with propagation the diving
   heuristics of MIP (T. Berthold, Primal heuristics for mixed integer
   programs, diploma thesis, TU Berlin, 2006). A batch spends as many
   propagations as the run before it, halving after each batch that
   finds nothing, and stops below 1/64. On ex6_1_3 (Ibex optim/hard)
   branch and bound crawled along a flat valley at -0.3243 and -f stayed
   at the LP point -0.2562; with dives both reach -0.3524 to -0.3521
   (optimum -0.3525). On the 41 optimisation instances of the float
   quick set at 600 s: default 14 better objectives, none worse, 23
   proved instead of 22, total time 10,996 s instead of 11,577 s; -f 9
   better, 2 worse, 4 instances with a first solution, proofs and total
   time unchanged. -Dorg.jacop.fz.floatDives=false turns them off;
   org.jacop.fz.floatDiveStart (0.1) and floatDiveNodes (10000) tune them.

455. Explanations of large layered graphs (MDD, regular, cost MDD) under
   lazy clause generation are found with less work, with the same
   reasons. The four cuts of an explanation (nearest and far, source or
   target first; Gange, Stuckey and Szymanek, MDD propagators with
   explanation, Constraints 16(4), 2011) are walked together, one bit per
   cut on each node, with the labels of a level looked up per value block
   and the arrays of a walk kept per thread. On graphs of at least 4,096
   nodes a far cut, which walks through the unreachable nodes or dead
   ends, most of the graph deep in the search, is found from the other
   side when that is smaller: the edges of removed values out of the
   reached nodes (or into the nodes reaching the last level), each kept
   when a depth-first search finds a path of unreachable nodes (dead ends)
   to the cut; the nearest cuts of a failure are read from per-node masks
   of the labels of its edges. On WordDesign2-words-mdd-055 (XCSP 2023,
   1,485 MDDs on a diagram of 525,720 nodes and 1.83 million edges) an
   explanation takes about 7 ms instead of 60 ms, and LCG makes about 11
   conflicts a second instead of 1; WordDesign2-words-mdd-035 and 045
   are solved in about 70 s instead of 485 s.

456. JaLP proves infeasibility that its presolve sees without simplex
   pivots where it can. When the presolve finds the bounds infeasible, or
   its reduced LP ends infeasible, BoundProof propagates the row activity
   bounds over the whole LP with a trail and resolves the conflict
   backwards into a Farkas ray over the rows (feasibility-based bound
   tightening as in Achterberg, Bixby, Gu, Rothberg and Weninger,
   "Presolve reductions in mixed integer programming", INFORMS Journal on
   Computing 32 (2020) 473-506; Brearley, Mitra and Williams, Mathematical
   Programming 8 (1975) 54-83), checked like a simplex proof. Otherwise
   the dual simplex looks for the proof from the slack basis with zero
   costs, then goes on with the costs if the LP is feasible after all.
   farkas() returns the ray either way; provedByBounds() tells which.
   On the 29 infeasible Netlib LPs 12 are proved with no pivot and the
   total time goes from 13.7 to 4.1 s (gosh 8.9 -> 2.3 s, greenbea 1.7 ->
   0.04 s); pivots on the 658 feasible LPs of the LP bench are unchanged.
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.boundProof=false turns it off.

457. Under lazy clause generation the trail keeps, for every variable, a
   copy of its domain to tell which atoms a change makes. It is now kept up
   to date from the atoms themselves: a change adds its atoms to the copy
   and a backtrack takes them back out, instead of copying the whole
   domain after every change and every backtrack (the delta idea of
   Gecode advisors and Choco delta monitors). Search is unchanged. The
   copying was 1.9 % of the solve time at the median of the wide set and
   up to 30 % (de Bruijn, tower, routing-flexible, fast-food); 40 s
   profiles: fast-food ff58 6.2 -> 2.3 %, ghoulomb 12.3 -> 3.8 %, tower
   12.3 -> 6.1 %. Wide set at 600 s: instances solved by both 2.8 % (fixed)
   and 3.4 % (-f) faster in geometric mean, total time and solved counts
   unchanged within the timing noise of LCG runs.
   -Djacop.lcg.incrementalShadow=false restores the copying;
   -Djacop.lcg.checkShadow=true checks every copy against its domain.

458. Free search before LCG (entry 426) finds the squares of an objective
   behind partial sums: the walk from the cost variable follows up to
   eight linear constraints instead of three, and x + y = z as well. An
   objective whose declared bound reaches beyond JaCoP's range is posted
   as a tree of partial sums, four levels deep on XCSP 2023 GBACP-UD01,
   UD03, UD07 and UD09 and their table forms, so free search never ran
   first there. At 600 s with -f on those 8 instances: geometric mean of
   the best objective 0.20 of before, better on all 8 (UD07 3727 -> 310,
   table-UD01 13084 -> 793, table-UD03 7354 -> 423); at 240 s on all 20
   GBACP instances 0.50. No wide instance changes: only two gbac
   instances have a squared objective, found at both depths.

459. jacop_nvalue over variables whose domains fall into blocks that share
   no value is posted as one count of distinct values (Values) per block
   and a sum of the counts. Each count then has the bound of its own
   block (the NValue bounds of Bessiere, Hebrard, Hnich, Kiziltan and
   Walsh, Constraints 11(4), 2006, hold per block and add up), and a
   learnt clause or a core over one block leaves the others out. In MiniZinc Challenge gfd-schedule each kind of item takes its
   group from a range of its own: the proof that 2018 n200f5d50m40k6
   needs 17 groups (no 16 without deadline penalty) took LCG more than
   250,000 conflicts without an end under one count, and 1,153 failures
   with one count per kind. On the 20 gfd-schedule instances at 240 s
   with -f: solved 15 -> 20 (2016 and 2022 n180, 2018 n80 and n200, 2022
   n200; CP-SAT proves them in 11 to 24 s), total time 1,398 -> 261 s;
   default search solved 12 -> 13, total 1,974 -> 1,750 s. Easy
   instances take up to 4 s more. It splits no other jacop_nvalue of the
   MiniZinc Challenge or the 53 XCSP instances with one.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.nvalueBlocks=false turns it off.

460. The speed check of LCG free search (entry 291) counts the solutions626
   that check on, so a check made right after LCG improved its cost saw
   no gain over its window and LCG gave way. With -f at 600 s: celar
   graph11 (MiniZinc Challenge 2013) 8,749 -> 3,080, ptv pax_period_20_5a
   70 -> 60; unit-commitment L10 equal over two runs, L12 1,484,650 ->
   1,489,050. On the wide-quick set at 240 s only celar graph11 changes.

461. The linear relaxation defines every block sum of a tree of sums
   (entry 425) by its own row. It took the first variable it could define
   in a row, and the row of an inner block also holds the sums of the
   blocks below it: when that row came first, a lower sum was defined by
   it and the inner sum stayed a free column. On XCSP 2025 BusScheduling-c2
   536 of 7,199 sums were free and the LP gave no guidance. Of the 21 wide
   instances with a tree, 10 had free sums. With -f at 240 s on those 10
   and the 12 BusScheduling instances: total 2,108 -> 1,911 s,
   BusScheduling-r5 proved (240 -> 19 s), t2 19 -> 6 s, c1a 42 -> 73 s,
   rcpsp-wet-diverse j60_19_6 58,529 -> 5,788, celar graph11 16,734 ->
   11,670.

462. New constraint Dag(from, to, ns, es, base): the selected edges of a
   directed graph form a directed acyclic graph and select their nodes
   (MiniZinc dag, until now decomposed into longest-path distances). It
   fails on a cycle of selected edges and removes every edge that would
   close one (an edge u -> v when the selected edges lead from v to u),
   which is domain consistent for acyclicity; the graph variable view is
   that of Dooms, Deville and Dupont, "CP(Graph): Introducing a graph
   computation domain in constraint programming" (CP 2005). Under lazy
   clause generation it explains a removed edge by the selected edges of
   the path, and a failure by the edges of the cycle.

463. New constraint PiecewiseLinear(x, y, xKnot, yKnot) for float
   variables: (x, y) lies on the polygonal line through the knots
   (MiniZinc piecewise_linear, until now decomposed into a 0/1 variable
   and a fraction per segment). It is bounds consistent on the union of
   the segments, with outward rounding (interval projection of Benhamou
   and Older, J. Logic Programming 32, 1997, on each segment; disjunctive
   hull of the segments as in Refalo, "Tight cooperation and its
   application in piecewise linear optimization", CP 1999).

464. A table (HybridTable) with at least 512 live single-value classes on
     a column whose domain has many holes copies the domain to a bit set
     before it filters the column, instead of a binary search in the
     domain per class. On CrosswordDesign (XCSP 2026), with word columns
     of 130,000 values, a node is about 3x faster and 9 of 11 instances
     end with a better crossword at 240 s.

465. Under LCG free search (-f), the core-guided phase is no longer skipped
   after a probe without a solution of its own when the search makes
   fewer than 50 conflicts per second of work (too slow for branch and
   bound to prove the optimum) and the probe's cores raise the lower bound
   fast enough to meet the best solution within the phase. On MiniZinc
   Challenge ma-path-finding (2017, 2022) this proves 8 of 9 instances in
   600 s instead of 5 (g16_p10_a30 in 281 s, g16_p20_a30 in 366 s); the
   rule fires on no instance of wide-quick and on one of the 187
   optimisation instances of the wide set. -Djacop.lcg.coreProbeRate=false
   turns it off, -Djacop.lcg.coreProbeSlowRate sets the rate.

466. The FlatZinc front end posts a multi-valued decision diagram (mdd)
   that states exactly x_1 + ... + x_n = S over the current domains as
   that sum. The check is exact: every edge carries one value, every node
   one partial sum, the diagram is deterministic, and its accepted tuples
   are counted and compared with the tuples of the domains whose sum is
   S. XCSP 2023 MagicSquare-mdd writes every line of the square this way
   (partial-sum diagrams as in Perez and Regin, "Efficient operations on
   MDDs for building constraint programming models", IJCAI 2015); the
   order-10 square needed 5.7 GB and found no square in 600 s, and is
   now solved in 5 s; at 600 s JaCoP solves all 7 instances instead of 2
   (the 2023 field solved 3). Off under --proof;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.mddLinear=false turns it off.

467. Under LCG a closed global cardinality with fixed counts (the counts
   add up to the number of variables), over variables of at most 4
   values, becomes clauses before search: a sequential counter per value
   (Abio and Stuckey, "Conflict directed lazy decomposition", CP 2012).
   The default unary counters stop at 4000 clauses per constraint, and
   the 7 day-of-week GCCs of XCSP 2025 RotatingRostering-052-2-3 need
   about 9,900 each, so they stayed propagators and the instance was
   unknown at 600 s; it is now unsatisfiable in 9 s. On the 19 XCSP
   instances where the rule fires, 18 are decided at 600 s instead of 15
   (EFPA-6-4-4-13 and -14 found) and the total time drops from 2576 s to
   1141 s. Open GCCs (RotatingWorkforce2) are left alone: as clauses they
   searched slower. -Djacop.lcg.gccClauses=false turns it off and
   -Djacop.lcg.gccClauses.dom sets the domain size.

468. The FlatZinc front end compiles a satisfaction model whose integer
   variables have at most three values (and one at least with a value other
   than 0 and 1) into clauses and solves them with JaSAT, under --lcg and
   free search, for one solution and without proof logging. Values get
   order and direct literals (Tamura, Taga, Kitagawa and Banbara,
   Compiling finite linear CSP into SAT, Constraints 14(2), 2009);
   products become support clauses after narrowing the value sets; longer
   sums become an MDD over the partial sums (Abio, Nieuwenhuis, Oliveras,
   Rodriguez-Carbonell and Mayer-Eichberger, A new look at BDDs for
   pseudo-Boolean constraints, JAIR 45, 2012), or binary adders when the
   diagram is too large; counts, alldifferent, lex chains, value
   precedence, tables and regular get their usual encodings. The store is
   built as before: a model of the clauses is fixed in the store and
   checked, and is otherwise dropped and the model solved as before. On the
   30 XCSP CSP instances where it fires (FastMatrixMultiplication,
   Hadamard, Hamming, BinaryPuzzle), 600 s, 20 decided in 6,413 s become
   24 in 4,645 s; none of the 240 instances of the wide benchmark set
   fires. -Dorg.jacop.fz.clausal=false turns it off;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.clausal.maxDomain, .maxLiterals and .mddNodes set the
   gates.

469. The cyclic distance rewrite of the FlatZinc front end (entry 437) is
   on by default for models with at least 400 cyclic distances
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.cyclicDistance.minDistances, default 400). There
   proofs are out of reach and the upper bound counts: at 600 s with -f,
   XCSP 2022 CyclicBandwidth caterpillar29 goes from 71 to 24,
   caterpillar35 from 103 to 29 and mesh2D25x26 from 106 to 25. The nine
   smaller instances (67 to 299 distances) keep the minimum, and with it
   the proofs of caterpillar13 and 16. -Dorg.jacop.fz.cyclicDistance=true
   still rewrites every model, and =false none. No other instance of the
   wide set or of the XCSP 2022-2025 sets has the pattern. Since the
   detector now reads every model, it keeps the definitions of the terms
   of a linear equation by position instead of copying the other terms
   for each one: an equation of n terms took n^2 time and memory (the
   1,176,224-term objective of rcpsp-wet-diverse j90_48_4 ran out of
   memory at -Xmx4G in this pass).

470. The FlatZinc front end rewrites products of two functions of one small
   index (MiniZinc's (good[p] = g) * num[p], both looked up by the chosen
   preference p) as sum(v) f(v) * (p = v), linear over the value literals
   of p, and unfolds clauses of implied comparisons between such functions
   into one clause per combination of the index values. The 2020 Challenge
   stable-goods s-d16 is proved in 57 s with -f and in 283 s by default
   instead of not at all in 600 s; over 9 stable-goods and saeling models
   the total time goes from 3,673 to 3,119 s with -f and from 4,251 to
   3,922 s by default. Only stable-goods triggers it among the 240 wide
   benchmark models. -Dorg.jacop.fz.indexFunctions=false turns it off,
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.indexFunctions.clauses=false only the unfolding.

471. Tables of more than TableCliques.MAX_ARITY (6) columns over the same
   tuples share one tuple array, their reformulation and the cells and
   bit-sets of their HybridTable, as smaller tables already did.
   pigeonsPlus-08-08 (XCSP 2017) posts one table of 5.8 million rows on
   eight lists of eight variables: every table built its own copy and
   the model ran out of memory at -Xmx4G while posting; it is now proved
   unsatisfiable.

472. The FlatZinc parser keeps an array literal whose elements are all int
   literals as one int[] instead of one node per element (48 bytes each);
   a node is made for an element only when it is read. Reading
   pigeonsPlus-08-08 (six arrays of 46 million entries) took 165 s and
   up to 2 GB of nodes per array; with both changes the instance is
   proved unsatisfiable in 60 s at -Xmx4G (out of memory before), the
   highest live heap after a collection going from 4.1 GB to 2.5 GB.

473. A search that follows the root solution of the linear relaxation
   (LpGuidedSelect) no longer scans every column at each decision when
   the relaxation has no value column: it decides only from value
   columns, so it fell back to its other heuristic every time after the
   scan. On MiniZinc Challenge 2021 physician-scheduling instance10 the
   scan took 18 % of the run (12,440 columns at each of 1.3 million
   decisions); at 600 s the objective goes from 10295 to 8260. The search
   is unchanged.

474. Large neighbourhood search under -f (AdaptiveLns) finds the decision
   variables near a node of the constraint graph with a walk that expands
   only the nodes on a shortest path to a decision variable, using the
   distances of all nodes computed once; it finds the same variables in
   the same order. On MiniZinc Challenge 2019 rcpsp-wet-diverse j60_19_6
   each walk visited about a million constraint memberships and the
   walks took 61 % of a 300 s run; now 1.4 %, with 20 times the nodes
   (objective 45397 -> 66765 at 300 s, the best known 69664 at 600 s).

475. Free search (-f) on models with vehicle routes: LNS uses only route
   removal, related (Shaw) removal and random neighbourhoods (the graph,
   propagation, objective, constraint and line neighbourhoods are off
   unless lns-kinds names them), and takes lns-share-routes 10 instead of
   3 of free search's calls. On CVRPLIB X-n110-k13 (giant tour of the
   MiniZinc Challenge 2015 model) the generic neighbourhoods took 44 % of
   the runs for 6 of 101 improvements. Paired runs, -f: the 10 X instances
   at 600 s 9 better and 1 worse (X-n110-k13 19,389 -> 18,722, X-n115-k10
   17,240 -> 14,877, X-n120-k6 20,089 -> 16,397); 38 routing instances at
   240 s 18 better, 11 worse, time 7,167 -> 7,080 s; the 88 wide-set
   instances with element or circuit at 240 s 6,369 -> 6,387 s, with only
   3 of them running route neighbourhoods (the rest run unchanged code).

476. The linear relaxation separates cuts of the clique partitioning
   polytope at the root when the objective sums pair indicators
   b <-> x[i] = x[j] (community detection: modularity objectives): the
   triangle inequalities of Groetschel and Wakabayashi (Mathematical
   Programming 45, 1989), the clique inequalities of a partition into at
   most k classes of Chopra and Rao (Mathematical Programming 59, 1993) and
   the 2-partition inequalities with one node on one side. A value Boolean
   b <-> x = v counts as the pair of x with an anchor node of value v, so
   a node fixed by value precedence keeps its pairs. Only partitions whose
   pairs the objective sums within three rows get cuts, and the cuts are
   kept only if they raise the root bound by 2 % or more. Community
   detection Zakhary.s12.k3 of the Challenge 2024 is proved at the root
   (-f 96 s -> 3 s), Zakhary.s12.k4 157 s -> 4 s, mexican.s26.k4 215 s ->
   2 s. On the 34 Challenge instances where a partition is found, at
   600 s: -f proved 21 -> 22, total time 0.814; default search proved
   9 -> 10, total time 0.930.
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.partitionCuts=false turns them off.

477. The permutation element sums of the FlatZinc front end (entry 404)
   also take binary tables whose one column is a total function of the
   other, and, when the indices are a permutation of their values, post
   the count of each value next to the sum: y[k] = c[x[k]] with the x[k]
   a permutation of U takes each value v exactly |{u in U : c[u] = v}|
   times (CountValues with fixed counters, for at most 64 distinct
   values and at most half as many as indices). This is the cardinality
   form of the aggregated assignment formulation of element and
   all_different (P. Refalo, Linear formulation of constraint
   programming models and hybrid solvers, CP 2000). In the mystery
   shopper problem (CSPLib prob004, XCSP 2024 MisteryShopper) the
   visitor group of each visit is a table of the visitor, the visitors
   of a week are a permutation, and the groups that visit a visitee over
   the W weeks are all different, so each group must make as many visits
   as there are visitees, W times its size; the counts and the
   all_different constraints make the linear relaxation infeasible when
   the groups differ in size. All 10 UNSAT instances (including
   6-12-1, which no 2024 solver decided) are proved in 1.6-2.9 s at
   600 s; before, JaCoP proved 6 (up to 180 s) and timed out on 5-08-1,
   5-10-1, 6-12-1 and 6-12-2.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.permutationElementCounts=false turns the counts off.

478. The linear relaxation expands a row whose sums lcg-split or linear
   overlap factoring defined up to 65,536 terms (was 4,096; property
   org.jacop.lp.maxExpandedTerms). Merging repeated variables at every
   level (jacop #815) keeps a row at most as long as the distinct
   variables it reaches, so the old cap only kept rows that are long in
   the model as sums of their blocks: the objective of XCSP 2025
   BusScheduling-c2, a sum of 14,771 Booleans. -f proved it in none of 5
   runs of 120 s; with this change and lcg-split-tree=0 in 3 of 3 (17-31
   s), and with the tree-split fix of jacop #863 in 2 of 2 (39, 45 s). On
   the 24 XCSP 2024/2025 and wide instances with
   a row of 4,097 to 65,536 terms, at 240 s: -f 10 = 10 proved, total
   3,749 -> 3,720 s; default search 10 = 10 proved, 3,751 -> 3,662 s
   (BusScheduling c1a 89 -> 30 s, r5a 69 -> 32 s).

479. The FlatZinc front end writes a sum of conjunctions over all pairs of
   two sets of Boolean variables, sum(i in A, j in B) w *
   bool2int(a[i] /\ b[j]), as w * sum(a) * sum(b) when the conjunctions
   and their bool2int have no other use (ProductOfSumsDetector, system
   property org.jacop.fz.productOfSums, groups of at least
   org.jacop.fz.productOfSums.minTerms = 64 conjunctions). The
   hypervolume objective of diverse solution models (2019 Challenge
   rcpsp-wet-diverse, after Ingmar, Garcia de la Banda, Stuckey and
   Tack, Modelling diversity of solutions, AAAI 2020) flattens to this
   form: j90_48_4 posts 1,176,224 conjunctions, which become two
   SumBool and one XmulYeqZ. With -f at -Xmx4G it ran out of memory
   before the search; it now finds objective 694,296 within 600 s with a
   live heap of 1.5 GB.

480. The Hall cuts of an all-different in the linear relaxation
   (AlldifferentCuts.separate) list the variables only of the cuts they
   return. A long constraint has a violated prefix of almost every
   length, and listing them all took quadratic memory: a FlatZinc model
   with an 80,000-variable all-different ran out of a 4 GB heap at the
   root, and now reaches its answer in 9 s. The cuts are the same.

481. The JaLP presolve treats a long row of column singletons in linear
   time. The implied-free test of each singleton read the whole row; it
   now first tests the row activity, kept while no bound, column or
   entry changes, and reads the row only when that test does not fail
   by more than its rounding error. The search for parallel columns
   tried every earlier column of the same pattern; it now tries only
   those of about the same cost over their first entry, latest first.
   The reductions are the same (20,000 random LPs, with the activity
   test on every row). A row of 100,000 singletons that differ in cost
   is solved in 0.2 s instead of about a minute (50,000: 16 s), and a 40,000
   term FlatZinc sum of bool2int over a conjunction from 32 s to 25 s.

482. The tabu search of functional models (FunctionalModel, CHANGELOG
   419) leaves to the solver a model with a function other than a sum
   or a product over more than 1,024 variables, which the search
   evaluates whole after every trial move. With the cost a count over
   3,000 variables the walk took 54 s of a 60 s run for 128 steps and
   gave no answer at a 20 s limit; the solver answers in 2 s. Low
   autocorrelation sequences still take the tabu search.

483. FlatZinc objective presolve contracts disjunctions: when the
   literals of r <-> or(b) exclude each other, now also because they
   share an at-most-one sum(b) <= 1 or an exactly-one set, and all have
   the same cost in the objective, their terms become one term on r
   (sum(c * b) = c * r). CP-SAT's presolve makes the same step through
   its exactly-one sets (Perron, Didier and Gay, The CP-SAT-LP Solver,
   CP 2023). In MiniZinc Challenge 2026 kitchen the cost of each placed
   fixture becomes a cost per used segment, 272 objective terms -> 156,
   and learnt clauses and cores no longer name single fixtures. With -f
   at 600 s larger.instance (optimum 470) is proved in 3 of 5 runs
   (355 to 405 s), never before in 5 runs; kitchen_b1 35 -> 16 s; the
   other three instances are unchanged. On the 240 wide instances it
   fires only on kitchen. -Dorg.jacop.presolve.objectiveContraction=false
   turns it off.

484. Free search (-f) on satisfaction problems no longer hands over from
   lazy clause generation to search without learning for good when the
   satisfaction probe finds learning weak or slow: the two alternate in
   slices whose budgets double, LCG keeps its clauses, trail and
   activities between its slices (LcgSolver.lend), and search without
   learning resumes the same FreeSearch, keeping its weights and its
   restart nogoods (Lecoutre, Sais, Tabary and Vidal, "Nogood recording
   from restarts", IJCAI 2007) from slice to slice. After weak learning
   search without learning gets three times LCG's time
   (lcg-sat-cp-share), after slow learning the same time
   (lcg-sat-slow-share). Paired runs at 600 s, -f: the 41 instances the
   hand-over affects 25 -> 29 and 23 -> 28 solved over two seeds, time
   0.85 and 0.87 (BlockedQueens-50 proved in 295 and 323 s, Antichain1-126
   and -252 solved, BlockedQueens-48 still open); 93 XCSP CSP instances
   solved slowly before 71 -> 80, time 0.89 (MagicSquare-mdd-09 and
   Crossword-m18-ogd2008-vg-16-17 lost); the 53 satisfaction instances of
   the wide set 47 -> 47, time 0.91.

485. Free search (-f) branches with conflict history search, CHS (Habet
   and Terrioux, "Conflict history based heuristic for constraint
   satisfaction problem solving", J. Heuristics 27, 2021), where it used
   dom/wdeg, in the plain CP search of --lcg off and of the hand-over from
   lazy clause generation, for satisfaction problems; optimisation keeps
   dom/wdeg, since CHS found worse solutions there. On 94 hard XCSP
   2022-2025 CSP instances at 240 s it decides 63 instead of 59 under -f
   (total time -8 %) and 49 instead of 41 under -f --lcg off (-13 %); at
   600 s on the 23 of them that reach the plain CP search 10 instead of
   8 (-9 %, none lost). --free-config
   heuristic=domwdeg restores dom/wdeg. Three more heuristics are available
   for experiments: heuristic=pick, pick/dom (Audemard, Lecoutre and
   Prud'homme, "Guiding Backtrack Search by Tracking Variables During
   Constraint Propagation", CP 2023), heuristic=frba, frba/dom (Li, Yin and
   Li, "Failure Based Variable Ordering Heuristics for Solving CSPs", CP
   2021), and heuristic=rr, a round robin of heuristics over the runs
   between restarts in which all of them learn from every run, as in ACE's
   portfolio of the XCSP 2025 competition (rr-set=cacd;pick;frba,
   rr-runs=1).

486. New constraint TspKOpt(x, offset, d, length): k-opt dominance for a
   symmetric travelling salesman tour x with arc lengths d (Isoart and
   Regin, A k-Opt Based Constraint for the TSP, CP 2021; Jonker and
   Volgenant, Nonoptimal edges for the symmetric traveling salesman
   problem, Operations Research 1984; 2-opt and 3-opt moves of Lin, Bell
   System Technical Journal 1965). Once a tour no longer
   than length.max has been seen, it removes every arc that a 2-opt move
   with a fixed arc, or a 3-opt move on paths of fixed arcs, would make
   strictly shorter, and fails a path that such a move shortens. Strict
   improvement keeps every optimal tour. Removals and failures are
   explained for LCG. The FlatZinc front end posts it on pure symmetric
   TSP models (the ones TspEdgeElimination detects) with
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.tspKOpt=true; it is off by default, since tours of
   instances that are not solved end worse. TSPLIB, 42 instances at
   600 s: default 16 -> 17 proved, time 16,065 -> 15,413 s (gr48 245 ->
   18 s, brazil58 proved in 174 s), 25 unsolved tours worse; -f 27 -> 28
   proved, time 11,642 -> 11,044 s, 11 tours worse.

484. Root re-cuts of the linear relaxation, off by default
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.rootRecut=true, which turns on
   org.jacop.lp.restartSolves and org.jacop.lp.restartBounds and keeps the
   extra rows). The reduced costs of a solve at the root level fix columns
   there and the propagation that follows tightens other bounds, but the
   cut rounds ran only over the bounds that solve started from. The
   relaxation now solves again with new cut rounds whenever the bounds of
   the root level changed, and when an incumbent closed a tenth of the gap
   left at the last such solve, as the LP worker of CP-SAT cuts again at
   level zero after each fixing. Free search without learning, which
   restarts to its own root a level above the first solve, gives that level
   to the relaxation as its root (LinearRelaxation.searchRoot). The solves
   take at most org.jacop.lp.restartShare (0.05) of the time since the
   relaxation was made. MiniZinc Challenge 2023 unit-commitment, -f 600 s:
   bound L10 1,626,260 -> 1,629,955 (optimum 1,703,450, 1,661,820 in one
   240 s run), L12 1,348,290 -> 1,360,605; with the L10 optimum as upper
   bound the root bound goes from 1.644M to 1.681M in 2 s and to 1.7024M at
   180 s. L15 -f found worse solutions (40.6M-45.7M against 35.1M-38.4M).
   Wide-quick set at 240 s, total time -f 2,906 -> 2,914 s and default
   4,593 -> 4,587 s; wide set at 60 s, -f 0.995 and default 1.001; the same
   instances solved.

487. JaLP's presolve aggregates: in an equation of 3 to 16 columns, a column
   of 2 to 8 entries whose bounds the equation implies is substituted out
   of its other rows, and the equation and the column go (free column
   substitution, Andersen and Andersen, Presolving in linear programming,
   Mathematical Programming 71 (1995) 221-245, section 3.3; the
   aggregator of HiGHS). The pivot is at least 1 % of the other entries of
   its row and column, and the fill is bounded per substitution and in
   all. Found by comparing traces of a patched HiGHS 1.15.1 with JaLP's
   new trace (-Dorg.jacop.jalp.trace=file: one line per presolve
   reduction, pivot and factorization): MIPLIB 2017 app1-2 13.4 s -> 1.8 s,
   13,897 -> 610 pivots (HiGHS 681), with the same presolved size as
   HiGHS. On the 687 LPs of the osolpro LP bench at 120 s: no wrong
   answer, the same 647 solved plus pds-80, total time 7,013 -> 6,806 s,
   fome12 111 -> 60 s, sgpf5y6 62 -> 30 s.
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveAggregate=false turns it off.

488. The FlatZinc front end skips a model rewriting pass when the model has
   none of the constraints it rewrites (cyclic distance, min of sums,
   product of sums, weighted completion, giant tour, table variable
   elimination, index function, index disjunction, numbered path, partial
   copy, set union): one scan of the constraint names instead of a pass
   over the model each. The pair cliques and the distance of the linear
   relaxation use primitive hash sets and arrays instead of boxed maps,
   and affine folding builds its occurrence map only when it has a
   definition. On 29 MaxSAT Evaluation 2021 models with the slowest
   set-up, the set-up at 600 s takes 1,083 s instead of 2,239 s in all
   (protein p1 315 -> 44 s, ar-2 108 -> 65 s), and two rna-alignment
   models now find a solution. Wide-quick set at 240 s: the same 46
   instances solved, total time 4,007 -> 3,987 s.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.detectorGates=false runs every pass as before.

489. Float optimisation. Free search (-f) splits the float variables of the
   model before the ones MiniZinc introduced for intermediate results
   (var_is_introduced, is_defined_var), largest domain first in each group
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.floatDecisionFirst=false splits by largest domain over
   all): on hart6 it split the exponentials, declared var float, and found
   no solution in 300 s. A model whose float constraints are all linear
   rows of the float relaxation (LinearFloatRelaxation.isLinearModel)
   bisects the objective from the point of the relaxation from the start,
   also under its annotated search: transp1 (61 equations, 100
   inequalities) proved in 77 nodes, before stuck at 199,697.9 against the
   optimum 199,693.06. The statistics of the float relaxation and of the
   float dives (floatLp*, floatDive*) are JaCoP's own statistics, printed
   with -s -v or --search-statistics, no longer with -s alone. Float set
   (osolpro benchmarks/float-set) at 240 s: float-quick -f 28 -> 31 solved,
   total time 0.88; the 22 linear float optimisation models 16 -> 18
   proved, total time 0.66 default, 0.61 -f.

490. A reified MiniZinc count with a variable value (count_eq_reif, and
   b -> count(x, y) = c, which MiniZinc posts through it) is decomposed in
   JaCoP's MiniZinc library into the literals x[i] = y and a reified sum,
   instead of Reified(CountVar). MiniZinc shares the literal of two
   variables between the counts that compare them, such as the neighbour
   colours of a grid, and lazy clause generation learns on these literals.
   Amaze (MiniZinc Challenge 2012, 2014 and 2019) and harmony (2024), the
   only Challenge models that reach this constraint, 22 instances at 240 s:
   default (model search) total 695 -> 490 s (2012 amaze 2012-06-29
   183 -> 10 s, 2014 amaze mod2012-06-22 35 -> 8 s), -f 344 -> 286 s
   (2012 amaze 2012-03-08 now proved in 181 s), --lcg off 1,405 -> 1,261 s;
   at 600 s default 1,050 -> 906 s and -f 261 -> 220 s.

491. Free search (-f) on optimisation problems can alternate LCG and free
   search without learning in slices instead of handing over for good
   (--free-config lcg-cop-slices=1, off by default), as jacop #888 does for
   satisfaction problems. When LCG gives way as slow with a solution, free
   search without learning runs in a store level the LCG solver lends
   (LcgSolver.lend) for as long as LCG ran; while a slice improves the best
   solution another slice follows, and after a slice without a better one
   LCG goes on under the best cost with its learnt clauses, for twice its
   earlier time. A turn of LCG that neither raises the bound nor improves
   the best solution hands the rest of the time to free search. The
   neighbourhoods of free search start from LCG's best solution and from
   each better one an LCG turn finds (lcg-cop-hint, on). 26 instances of
   the wide set and XCSP 2025 where LCG gives way with a solution, 600 s
   -f: total time 4,609 -> 4,450 s, proved 9 -> 10 (XCSP 2025 Coprime-20,
   122 s); better cargo challenge16 (129,647 -> 124,041), opd, saeling,
   stable-goods s-d19; worse ihtc-2024 i09 (4,115 -> 4,355) and i01
   (4,333 -> 4,637), unit-commitment L12, warehouse olil4. Wide-quick set
   at 240 s: the slices start on 2 of 62 instances.

492. Fixed search on a satisfaction model tastes the annotation's search
   without learning once. At 5 % of the time limit (free-config
   lcg-fixed-at) LCG stops and depth-first search without learning runs
   for a quarter of LCG's time (lcg-fixed-share); when it got further in
   the annotation's tree than LCG did (the lexicographic position of the
   annotation's values in their root order), it goes on to the end,
   otherwise LCG goes on. Only for one int_search or bool_search with
   input_order and indomain_min or indomain_max, whose tree both searches
   walk in the same order. On costas-array (MiniZinc Challenge 2010, 2011,
   2015) LCG cuts about 10 % of the failures at a 15th of the nodes per
   second. 37 instances (15 costas-array and the 22 such satisfaction
   instances of the wide set), 600 s: solved 30 -> 33 (costas-array 17 x2,
   2010 19), total time 5,383 -> 3,875 s; costas-array 16 and 18 about
   150-220 s -> 40 s; LCG kept on black-hole, solbat, oocsp and nmseq, which
   pay the taste (5-13 s).

493. Cheaper propagation of GlobalCardinality, ChannelReif and ChannelImply
   per call. GlobalCardinality keeps the value list of each variable as a
   bit set when the values fit in 512 bits: the value graph of the
   strongly connected component pass is the union of the bit sets of the
   matched variables, and a value outside a variable's component is found
   by word tests (Regin, Generalized arc consistency for global
   cardinality constraint, AAAI 1996, same pruning as before).
   ChannelReif and ChannelImply record which 0/1 variables changed since
   the last call and visit only their items, with one pass over all items
   only when x changed. MiniZinc Challenge warehouses and
   rotating-workforce (the 20 wide-set instances with GlobalCardinality),
   600 s: -f total 0.981 (4 of 5 warehouses faster), default 1.000 with the
   same nodes; 2019 nside to 300k nodes 37 -> 26 s with the same search.
   The 38 wide-set instances with a channel, 600 s: default total
   5,390 -> 5,382 s with the same instances proved, -f 4,046 -> 3,924 s
   with one more proved (valve-network horizon20 in 498 s).

494. Under LCG, the runs of a model's search annotation take the assignment
   as a solution in more cases once the annotation has fixed its
   variables: the linear relaxation no longer counts as an open
   constraint (its rows are combinations of the model's constraints), and
   a free variable of a constraint that is not entailed is decided at the
   best solution's value, after which the test goes on (at most 32 per
   branch, and only while it completes models). Before, either one kept
   the solver deciding every free variable left, one decision level each.
   MiniZinc Challenge 2014 ship-schedule, fixed search: 6Ships 272,286 ->
   8,321 nodes (3.9 -> 3.6 s), 7Ships 430,734 -> 52,345, 3Ships 69,145 ->
   1,812. Wide-quick set at 240 s, model search: same 46 solved, total
   4,074 -> 4,081 s; the 31 wide instances where it acts, 600 s: same 16
   solved, total 10,552 -> 10,545 s, 4 better / 1 worse objectives.
   Statistic lcgCompletionDecisions.

495. Lazy clause generation enumerates the solutions of satisfaction
   problems with -a and -n N (N > 1): every solution adds a blocking clause
   and the search ends, unsatisfiable, once all solutions are printed
   (Chu and Stuckey, Lazy clause generation reengineered, CP 2009; the
   all-solutions mode of Chuffed). When every decision is on a printed
   variable the clause is the negated decisions, otherwise one literal per
   printed variable (the members of a printed set by their mirrors).
   Before, -a and -n N dropped LCG and searched without learning. It is
   on by default with LCG, except under -p, --proof and --presolve;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.lcgAll=off restores the old behaviour. Outputs that are
   not solver variables fall back to search without learning, as
   "%% --lcg ignored" reports. The FlatZinc satisfaction tests (1,261) print the
   same solution sets. Wide set, 53 satisfaction instances with -a at
   240 s: decided 23 -> 26, total time 7,555 -> 7,089 s, the 23 decided by
   both 355 -> 171 s (search_stress2 06_05 94 -> 0.5 s, amaze 46 -> 2 s,
   fillomino 7x7_0 and cryptanalysis obj16 all solutions found).
   With -a -f at 240 s: decided 21 -> 29, total 7,995 -> 6,377 s. The 30
   instances open at 240 s, at 600 s: decided 0 -> 4, total 18,000 ->
   16,482 s. Models with millions of solutions print fewer of them: the
   blocking clauses take memory (slow_convergence 1000 runs out of 4 GB
   after 244,554 solutions, search without learning prints 4.4 million in
   600 s) and LCG is slower per solution.
   AllDisjoint and PartitionSet explain their pruning for LCG element by
   element (an element leaving a set is explained by another set holding
   it, an element of the universe joining a set by every other set being
   without it), and Reified and Not of AinB and AeqB explain entailment
   and disentailment, instead of the generic reason of all set atoms.

496. Under lazy clause generation a regular or MDD constraint whose
   clauses do not fit in the budget at 400 bytes per described edge and
   node has its literals and clauses counted, and becomes clauses when
   those fit; the budget is charged with the counted bytes. The edges
   between two nodes are one arc, so most graphs take far less: the 17
   regular constraints of pentominoes 04 (MiniZinc Challenge 2020) were
   priced at 1.46 GB and take 169 MB, so with a 10 GB heap two of them
   were explained by cuts of the graph and the fixed search took 348-487 s
   (Chuffed 24 s); it is solved in 8-11 s now, and two pentominoes-zayenz
   instances unsolved at 600 s in 15 s and 104 s. Long clauses of a graph
   are cleared of duplicates by sorting. 23 regular and MDD instances
   (MiniZinc Challenge, XCSP 2022-2025, the regular instances of the wide
   set), 600 s, 4 GB: total time 0.59 (fixed search) and 0.79 (-f).

497. The clausal compilation of satisfaction models (entry 468) takes
   domains of up to 64 values (was 3). A variable that appears only in
   tables, alldifferent, the lists of counts and sums of three or more
   terms gets direct literals with at-least-one and at-most-one clauses
   and no order literals (direct encoding: de Kleer, A comparison of ATMS
   and CSP techniques, IJCAI 1989; Walsh, SAT v CSP, CP 2000); binary
   tables get the support encoding, on which unit propagation keeps arc
   consistency (Kasif, On the parallel complexity of discrete relaxation
   in constraint satisfaction networks, AIJ 45, 1990; Gent, Arc
   consistency in SAT, ECAI 2002); strictly increasing is compiled.
   Models with a regular constraint over more than 3 values
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.clausal.regularDomain) and CNFs of more than 2M
   clauses (-Dorg.jacop.fz.clausal.maxClauses) stay with lazy clause
   generation; -Dorg.jacop.fz.clausal.direct=false gives every variable
   order literals. It fires on 211 of 798 XCSP CSP models of 2022-2026
   (30 before) and on 5 of the 240 wide-set instances under -f. 600 s on
   the 76 of them that take more than 30 s: 32 -> 38 decided, total
   29,947 -> 26,975 s; newly decided Eternity-08-07, HyperSudoku-06,
   FRB-45-21-1, FRB-53-24-1, EFPA-4-4-5-11 (UNSAT), EFPA-6-4-4-13,
   Ortholatin-08; lost SocialGolfers-08-04-11 and FRB-45-21-3.

498. New XCSP3 front end org.jacop.fz.xcsp.Xcsp2jacop and launcher
   xcsp-jacop: it reads an XCSP3-core instance (.xml, .xml.lzma or
   .xml.xz) with the JDK's streaming XML parser, posts it as FlatZinc over
   JaCoP's built-ins, solves it with the FlatZinc front end and all its
   options (-t, -f, -p, LCG) and prints the XCSP3 competition output
   (s, o, v and c lines). Tables are filtered by the domains of their
   variables and stored once per content, so Inglenook of XCSP 2026 (a
   26-million-pair table on one line) is read in 4 s instead of 270 s and
   6 GB through pycsp3 and MiniZinc. Comparisons that occur only in
   clauses, or in sums bounded on one side, are half reified, and a
   constant matrix element is a table, as MiniZinc posts them. A domain
   beyond JaCoP's integer range is cut, and the answer then claims neither
   unsatisfiability nor an optimum. XCSP 2022-2025, quick set (147
   instances, one per family and track) and the 38 instances the pycsp3
   translation cannot handle, 600 s, against pycsp3 + MiniZinc 2.9.4 on
   the same jar: 140 against 127 proved (130 against 127 on the quick
   set), total time 16,562 s against 17,924 s on the quick set with 600 s
   for an unproved instance; better objective on 9 open instances,
   worse on 3. All 2,250 instances of 2022-2026 at 10 s: every solution
   passes the XCSP3 SolutionChecker and no answer contradicts the
   competition results.

499. The core-guided phase of free LCG search takes the terms of a split
   objective sum also when they are narrow (at most 8 levels each, not
   only 0-1) and span up to twice the levels of the block sums. Before,
   such terms lost to the blocks as soon as these spanned fewer levels,
   and each core then raised the bound by one on a single block. On
   neighbours (MiniZinc Challenge 2018, 2021, 2024; cells of 1..5) at
   600 s with -f: neighbours7 436 -> 263 s, neighbours1 18 -> 7 s,
   neightbours-new-20 proved in 102 s (not in 600 s before), neighbours9
   26 -> 40 s; total over the 15 instances 5,366 -> 4,684 s. It fires on
   2 other instances of the 240 of the wide set (tc-graph-color k5_05
   15 -> 7 s, league model30-4-6 2.3 -> 3.6 s).
   -Djacop.lcg.coreNarrowSpan=1 restores the 0-1 rule only.

500. JaLP's aggregator also substitutes a column whose bounds are implied
   by any of its rows, not only by the equation it is substituted from
   (implied free columns as in HiGHS). On the 687 LPs of the osolpro LP
   bench the presolved LPs are 1,112,853 -> 244,977 rows and columns larger
   than HiGHS's in all (pds-20: 27,026 -> 8,778 rows, HiGHS 8,732); at
   120 s no wrong answer, 2 more solved, total time 6,792 -> 6,631 s,
   pds-40 to pds-100 35-50 % faster. -Dorg.jacop.jalp.aggregateAnyRow=false
   turns it off.

501. Rows that the linear relaxation of an optimisation model keeps for the
   root bound they raise are on probation in search: after 256 solves
   they are dropped when fewer than 10 % of the solves failed the node and
   the solves took at least 60 % of the time of the search since the
   root. 2012 amaze 2012-03-19 with the reified-count decomposition: 650
   rows raise the root bound 97 -> 104 but save 8 of 8,179 nodes, and
   the search goes 5.5 -> 2.0 s (fd) and 3.8 -> 1.8 s (-f).
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.optimisationProbation=0 keeps the rows,
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.probationShare sets the share of time.

502. In a model without a search annotation, the FlatZinc front end counts
   the loads of a bin packing (jacop_bin_packing) as defined by it, as if
   a defines_var annotation named them: free search branches on the bins
   of the items first (item 369) and LNS relaxes bins only (lns-defined).
   XCSP translations carry no defines_var annotations; on the balanced
   academic curriculum of XCSP 2026 (BACPj, objective the sum of squared
   period loads) free search branched on the loads, filled the periods
   with small loads and found no solution of 01-20 and 01-40 in 600 s
   (-f); now the first solution comes in 18 and 282 s, and 01-04, 01-05
   and 01-06 are proved in 21, 18 and 15 s instead of 25-108, 176-181 and
   99-258 s. The MiniZinc Challenge models with a bin packing all carry a
   search annotation and are unchanged: with the loads defined there,
   gbac under -f got worse at 600 s (6 of 19 objectives worse, 1 better).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingLoadsDefined=false turns it off.

503. Two opt-in strengthenings of the root linear relaxation.
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.clauseRows=true: the clause rows of lazy clause
   generation over columns the relaxation already has no longer drop out
   when the share of further rows is full. They come after every other
   extra row, and the extra-row trial keeps them only when they make the
   relaxation infeasible or raise its bound; otherwise it keeps the rows
   before them. On MiniZinc Challenge 2023 unit-commitment L10 the share
   left out 2,668 such rows, among them every x + not x = 1 of the
   commitments: the root relaxation solved as a MIP had the optimum
   1,633,000 instead of 1,703,450.
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.vbMir=true: the MIR cuts of the rows also substitute
   variable bounds x <= a u and x >= a u, u a 0/1 column, for the bounds of
   x when they are nearer to the LP value (the complemented MIR with
   variable bounds of H. Marchand and L. A. Wolsey, Aggregation and mixed
   integer rounding to solve MIPs, Operations Research 49(3), 2001), as the
   c-MIR separators of SCIP and HiGHS do. The statistic lpVbCuts counts
   them.
   With both, the L10 root cut bound goes from 1,622,000 to 1,698,000 (the
   HiGHS root reaches 1,702,500). 600 s bounds: L10 1,621,135 ->
   1,698,060 (-f) and 1,621,210 -> 1,696,570 (default), L12 1,348,290 ->
   1,419,720 (-f), L15 15,857,935 -> 17,442,805 (-f). Both flags on the 41
   instances whose relaxation they change (39 of the 240 of the wide set
   plus L10 and L15), 600 s: the same 26 solved in each mode, total time
   default 0.994 and -f 1.007.

504. The FlatZinc front end rewrites an absolute difference that only
   excludes values: t = x - y and a = |t|, with t and a used nowhere
   else, become bounds on x - y and x - y != d for the at most 8 values d
   (-Dorg.jacop.fz.absoluteDifference.maxHoles) that the domains of t and
   a exclude inside the range of x - y. The disequalities are read by the
   all-different detection as stated ones, and a rewrite is made only
   when all of them end up in all-different constraints (or there are
   none), so MiniZinc's flattening of abs(q[i] - q[j]) != j - i in
   n-queens models loses its 2 auxiliary variables and 2 constraints per
   pair and the diagonals become all-different constraints with offsets.
   Lone disequalities are not kept: on Rlfap-graph-01 (XCSP 2022, about
   5 per pair, no clique) they explain worse under lazy clause generation
   than |t| (optimum proved in 13 s without, not in 240 s with them).
   CPAI08 at 240 s, solutions checked: queens-100 41 -> 2.5 s, queens-180
   52 -> 11 s, queens-400 unknown -> 27 s, queens-500 out of memory ->
   50 s, queensKnights 81-94 -> 6 s. XCSP 2022 BlockedQueens and Rlfap
   (33 instances, 240 s): total time 3,472 -> 3,253 s, Rlfap-scen-10
   proved in 44 s; no instance of the wide set fires. Off with
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.absoluteDifference=false and under --proof.

505. The FlatZinc front end posts a network flow with fixed weights
   (network_flow, network_flow_cost) as its flow conservation rows and
   cost row under free search (-f), and as those rows next to NetworkFlow
   otherwise. NetworkFlow's reduced-cost arc analysis, a shortest path
   per arc, cost about 80 % of the time on the small networks of the
   MiniZinc Challenge mapping problem (16 networks of 11 nodes and 36
   arcs) and pruned too little to pay for it. Mapping and nfc 2015-2022
   (30 instances, 120 s, two seeds): -f 841 -> 598 s (2018 ring_mp3
   28-111 -> 7 s), default 945 -> 687 s (2016 mesh3x3_mp3 proved in
   12-18 s, not in 120 s before). -Dorg.jacop.fz.networkFlow=global
   restores the old posting; rows and both choose one.

505. The FlatZinc front end bounds the cost of a bin packing whose bin loads
   feed a cost function y = g(load) in a linear row of the objective (the
   balanced academic curriculum: the squared distance of each period load
   of a curriculum to its ideal band). The least sum of g over all
   packings of the items comes from a branch and bound over the items,
   with bin symmetry classes, and goes into the model as
   sum(y) = z, z >= minimum, with z in place of the y terms of the row,
   so that the objective's root bound adds the minima up (the deviation
   constraint of Schaus, Deville, Dupont and Regin, CPAIOR 2007, and the
   dispersion constraints of Pesant, INFORMS Journal on Computing 27(4),
   2015, bound the same sum of a balance cost). The square x * x of a
   factor of 65 to 512 values now gets the squares as its domain; before,
   the count of factor pairs went over its limit and left an interval.
   Wider factors keep the interval: the squares of 1,001 values each made
   euler_39 search 7 s -> 94 s.
   MiniZinc Challenge gbac, the 9 reduced instances on which it fires,
   600 s: -f proved 4 -> 7, total 3,482 -> 1,939 s (2020 UD5 600 -> 80 s,
   2016 UD10 263 -> 3 s); default search proved 0 -> 4, total 5,401 ->
   3,705 s, every objective equal or better. The full-size gbac instances
   are too big for the enumeration (2,000,000 nodes per bin packing) and
   it does not fire there. -Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingCost=false turns it
   off.

506. Lazy clause generation under a fixed search keeps the activity orders
   of its decisions lazily (VarHeap.setLazy): the bumps, inserts and
   removals keep only which variables are in the heap, and the heap is
   rebuilt in linear time when its top is read, which a fixed search does
   only once the annotation has fixed its variables. A heap whose
   rebuilds cost more than twice the sifting they saved goes back to the
   eager order. The search is the same. Wall time for the same work
   (virtual clock): MiniZinc Challenge sudoku_opt p29 -4.4 %, gfd-schedule
   n100f7d50m20k10 -2.6 %, wide-quick set default search -0.8 to -1.3 % in
   three rounds over the 59 of 61 instances with the same search; two
   search differently, after the annotation, where the order breaks ties
   of activity otherwise (roster chicroster_dataset_large_20 99 -> 98 in a
   third less time, hoist PU_2_8_1 +3 %); -f unchanged. -Djacop.jasat.lazyOrder=false and
   -Djacop.lcg.lazyOrder=false keep the orders eager. -s prints the
   literals of the learnt clauses as lcgLearntLiterals. LcgSolver also
   reads the bounds of the root domains, checked for the literal of every
   atom, from int arrays: the same search, sudoku_opt p29 and
   gfd-schedule n100 -2.9 % each, wide-quick -f -0.9 %, default -0.2 %.

507. The FlatZinc front end posts lists that differ, written as clauses of
   reified disequalities (x[1] != y[1]) \/ ... \/ (x[k] != y[k]), as one
   AllDifferentLists over the largest group of lists that all differ
   pairwise (DisjunctiveListsDifferDetector; allDifferent on lists,
   F. Boussemart, C. Lecoutre, G. Audemard and C. Piette, XCSP3-core: A
   format for representing constraint satisfaction/optimization problems,
   arXiv:2009.00514, 2020, section 4.1.1). A clause qualifies when each of
   its Booleans is defined by one int_ne_imp, int_lin_ne_imp or
   reification and used nowhere else; a plain disequality of two lists
   counts as an edge too, and a greedy clique is taken per connected group.
   ExaminationTimetabling1 of XCSP 2026 states "no two events in the same
   room and period" this way: 32k clauses and 64k half reifications over
   261 events. 600 s, -f, 10 instances: 6 better (D1-2-16 568 -> 538,
   D1-2-17 677 -> 658, D1-1-16 395 -> 384), 2 equal, 2 worse (D7-1-17
   404 -> 434, D7-2-17 803 -> 848), and the first solution of the D1
   instances comes in 13-59 s instead of 43-121 s; with the native XCSP
   reader D1-3-17 finds its first solution in 15 s and none in 60 s
   without it. SocialGolfers of XCSP 2024 (18 CSP instances, 240 s): the
   same 9 decided in the same time. It fires on 1 of the 240 instances of
   the wide set (gfd-schedule n180f7d50m30k18, neutral).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.listsDifferClauses=false turns it off.

508. The JaLP presolve stuffs singleton columns (G. Gamrath, T. Koch,
   A. Martin, M. Miltenberger and D. Weninger, "Progress in presolving
   for mixed integer programming", Mathematical Programming Computation
   7, 2015): in a row with one side, the column singletons whose cost
   asks to use up the row are taken from the largest gain per unit of
   the row; one that fits even with the other columns at their worst
   moves to its bound, one the better ones already overflow the row with
   stays at the other. trucking_15 presolves to 87 x 98 like HiGHS (was
   87 x 600). -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveStuffing=false turns it off.

509. The JaLP presolve removes rows that the bounds other rows imply on
   their columns keep (E. D. Andersen and K. D. Andersen, "Presolving in
   linear programming", Mathematical Programming 71, 1995, section 3.3,
   with implied column bounds as HiGHS keeps them). Each row bounds its
   columns, one step, from the bounds of the others; the bounds of a
   removed row are not used after it goes, so every removed row is
   implied by rows that are kept. Column bounds do not change. Over the
   687 LPs of the osolpro LP bench the presolved sizes above HiGHS's drop
   from 244,977 to 210,986 rows and columns (with item 507); agg, cont4
   and rocII-5-11 presolve to HiGHS's size. -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveImpliedRows=false
   turns it off.

510. Free search (-f) with an annotation drops the annotation's runs when
   50,000 conflicts found no solution: from then on LCG takes turns of
   the SAT solver's own decisions on literals and of activity branching
   only, as without an annotation (--free-config lcg-annotation-drop=<n>,
   0 for never; LcgSolver.setAnnotationDrop; statistic
   lcgAnnotationDropped). The runs of an annotation that does not lead to
   a solution also hold back the other turns: on amaze2 2012-07-04
   (first_fail on the path successors, indomain_min) the literal runs of
   the rotation spent 417,000 conflicts in 300 s without a solution,
   while the rotation without the annotation finds one after about
   200,000 conflicts in all. Two seeds at 240 s: 28 instances where no
   solution comes early (amaze2, cutstock, 2DBinPacking, bus_scheduling,
   costas-array, orthorio and the wide set's others), solved 25 -> 29,
   total time 8,486 -> 7,416 s, cutstock type16 finds 1,146 where it
   found nothing; the 53 satisfaction instances of the wide set, solved
   93 -> 94, total time 3,996 -> 3,648 s. The rule fires on 3 of the 240
   instances of the wide set within 60 s.

511. FlatZinc front end as a GraalVM native image (fzn-jacop-native): it
   starts in milliseconds and runs compiled code at once, where the JVM,
   even with its ahead-of-time cache, needs 0.1 s to start and runs JaCoP
   in its interpreter for the first 0.2-0.5 s. fzn-jacop-native-build (or
   mvn -Pnative) makes it with native-image; with Oracle GraalVM it is
   compiled with profile-guided optimization from the aot-training models,
   which makes long runs as fast as on the JVM. fzn-jacop uses the image
   when it is newer than the jar (JACOP_JVM=1 runs the JVM), and the
   MiniZinc Challenge image runs it. org.jacop.fz.NativeMain runs the
   solver in a thread with a 100 MB stack; org.jacop.fz.NativeMetadata
   lists the reflection the image needs. MiniZinc Challenge filters
   fir_1_4: 0.39 s -> 0.05 s; 22 easy Challenge instances of 2012-2016:
   faster in all 44 runs, 26 under 0.3 s (none before). Wide-quick set at
   240 s, the same instances solved, total time -f 2,250 -> 2,123 s and
   default 4,047 -> 3,967 s, 0.26x / 0.28x the time (geometric mean) on
   the instances both solve; at 600 s two -f proofs past 400 s are lost
   (celar graph11, whirlpool d29).

512. Root probing and the Diffn area check are cheaper, with the same search.
   DualFeasibleFunctions keeps a memo of its packing tests, keyed by the
   container and the set of item sizes: root probing of MiniZinc Challenge
   2014 rectangle-packing rpp23 made 34,513 tests on 346 distinct sets.
   LcgSolver writes out equals and hashCode of its probing atoms (with the
   values of the generated ones, so sets keep their order), and probing
   stops collecting the consequences of a variable's values once none is
   common to all. -f wall time on rectangle-packing rpp18-rpp26 -15 to
   -19 %; at 240 s, Diffn instances proved by both 918 -> 836 s (-f) and
   188 -> 175 s (default search); wide-quick -f at 600 s total 0.995.

513. Table explanations (HybridTable under LCG) are cheaper on wide tables,
   with the same reasons. Once a row is killed by one atom of a column
   fixed before the explained position, only a column dead at the root
   can beat it, so the other columns are checked against their root
   domains instead of being scored one by one; the columns with covered
   values and with entries in the reason are kept in lists. MiniZinc
   Challenge 2020 minimal-decision-sets, whose element lookups become
   tables of 127-196 columns: fixed search australian_train5 46 -> 39 s,
   colic_train4 74 -> 57 s, cleve_train5 27 -> 23 s at 600 s with the same
   failures; table explanations 33 % -> 14 % of the run. On the 67
   Challenge instances with such tables and on wide-quick the total time
   is unchanged (0.99-1.01).

508. The FlatZinc front end bounds the parts of a linear objective that
   belong to loosely coupled blocks of the model (ObjectiveBlockBound,
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.blockBound=false turns it off). Constraints and clauses
   join the variables they share into fragments in the order of their
   scope size, as in the block detection of Gamrath and Luebbecke (SEA
   2010); one that would put more than half of the objective's terms
   together is a linking constraint and is left out. The least cost of
   each block's terms, found by LCG in a copy of the block, is the
   Lagrangian relaxation with zero multipliers (Geoffrion 1974), for the
   travelling tournament problem its independent lower bound (Easton,
   Nemhauser and Trick, CP 2001). It is posted as cost_b >= least_b with
   the objective as the sum of the cost_b, only when it raises the
   objective's lower bound by 5 % of the distance to the upper bound; it
   takes at most 2 % of the time limit and 5 s. MiniZinc Challenge
   traveling-tppv circ8 (2014, 2017): the root bound rises from 0 to
   70-74 and all five are proved at 600 s, fd in 33-111 s and -f in
   10-60 s, against no proof before (Gecode proves circ8bbal in 536 s).
   Of 48 instances of the topic and wide sets where blocks are found the
   bound is posted on 14; on the 8 of them not in traveling-tppv the time
   is unchanged.

509. The FlatZinc front end posts redundant unary resources with optional
   tasks for flexible job shops (-Dorg.jacop.fz.optionalUnary=false turns
   it off): from disjunctions s1 + d1 <= s2 \/ s2 + d2 <= s1 \/ m1 != m2
   it takes, for every machine v, the tasks that are pairwise
   conditionally disjoint and posts CumulativeUnary with task t present
   when m_t = v (unary resources with optional activities, Vilim, Bartak
   and Cepek, Constraints 2005). MiniZinc Challenge 2014 stochastic-fjsp
   fjsp-a1-s3 under fd: 452 after 600 s -> optimum 207 proved in 15 s.

510. LCG explanations drop the atoms that another atom of the same
   explanation implies: of the bounds of one variable only the strongest
   lower and upper bound stay, and a removed value stays only when no
   cited bound excludes it. A reason or failure that excludes a range of
   values one value at a time cites, for each value, the weakest bound
   that removed it (lifting, Feydy and Stuckey, CP 2009), so an element
   over a range of positions cited one index bound per position where the
   strongest alone implies the others: on MZNC 2013 javarouting trip_6_3
   ElementInteger reasons had 6.3 distinct atoms where 1 was needed. The
   clause is a subset of the old one, so it stays implied and is
   stronger, and conflict analysis no longer resolves the weaker bounds.
   -Djacop.lcg.dropDominated=false keeps every atom (and --proof keeps
   them, so the proof steps stay as before). Wide-quick set at 240 s:
   -f total time 2,580 -> 2,367 s, solved 52 -> 53 (lot-sizing pigment20a
   proved in 63 s), celar graph11 35,355 -> 6,364, mapping mesh_star
   45 -> 14 s; default mode neutral (4,275 -> 4,304 s, same solved).
   Wide set at 240 s, -f: total time 13,154 -> 13,005 s, 196 instances
   solved by both 2,681 -> 2,445 s (blocks-world 16-4-83 173 -> 92 s,
   liner-sf-repositioning tp7_4 177 -> 106 s), whirlpool d29 lost at the
   limit (154 s before); default mode 19,811 -> 19,825 s, 169 = 169
   solved.

511. LCG reasons of products (MulExplanation) and of x + y = z with a
   fixed term cite only the bounds they need. A product reason guarded
   its range exclusion with the variable's own earlier bound, which the
   trail gives as the root bound, so a third of its atoms were the
   variable's own; it now first explains the exclusion from the other
   factors' bounds alone, each lifted by bisection to the weakest value
   that still excludes the range and cited by the earliest trail atom
   that reaches it (lifting, Feydy and Stuckey, CP 2009), and a failure
   is explained from all bounds lifted the same way. x + y = z explains a
   bound and a failure from the lifted linear bounds before the shift,
   which cited both bounds of the fixed term. -Djacop.lcg.mulLift=false
   turns the product lifting off and -Djacop.lcg.shiftLast=false the
   linear sum reasons. MZNC 2018
   train instance.17: objective <= 35000 proved in 10 s (unfinished
   before), optimum found at 27 s instead of 98 s. Wide set at 240 s,
   -f: total time 13,288 -> 13,038 s, the 194 instances solved by both
   2,434 -> 1,998 s (lot-sizing pigment20a 182 -> 51 s, javarouting
   trip_7_2 212 -> 127 s, ship-schedule 8Ships 59 -> 11 s); wide-quick
   default mode 4,504 -> 4,536 s, same solved.

512. Under lazy clause generation the FlatZinc front end also posts the
   load of each bin of a bin packing as a linear sum of 0/1 variables, one
   per item that may go in the bin, channelled to the item's bin (the
   model's own reified equalities where it has them). Binpacking explains
   a load bound or a removed bin by the bins of many items at once (97
   atoms per reason on MZNC 2017 steelmillslab bench_19_5); the sums give
   the learnt clauses the 0/1 variables and lifted linear reasons (the
   load decomposition of P. Shaw, "A constraint for bin packing", CP
   2004). -Dorg.jacop.fz.binPackingSums=lcg (default) | true | false; at
   most 65,536 item-bin pairs. bench_19_5 under --lcg with a fixed search:
   28 -> 13 in 120 s. MZNC gbac (19 instances), -f at 240 s, two rounds:
   total 2,840 -> 2,647 s and 2,886 -> 2,608 s, 2017 UD4 proved in
   103-111 s (unsolved before), 2016 UD3 objective 230 -> 165 and 250 ->
   238. -f does not run steelmillslab under LCG, so it is unchanged there.

513. CostNetworkBound (entry 320) is on by default. The FlatZinc front end
   builds the cost function network of an objective that sums unary and
   binary cost tables (weighted CSP: protein design, frequency assignment)
   and posts the bound when its root bound is above the bound the objective
   has after the linear relaxation and the network has at most 500 cost
   terms (-Dorg.jacop.cfn.maxTerms): a reason of the bound cites the whole
   network, and on the CELAR networks of 1034 to 3417 terms explanations
   grew from 8 to 144 atoms for a bound below 1 % of the optimum, so
   solutions got worse. MiniZinc Challenge 2018 proteindesign12
   1HZ5.12p.9aa.usingEref_self_x is proved in 12 s instead of more than
   600 s (8 s instead of 477 s under -f). On the 46 celar, spot5 and
   proteindesign12 instances where a network is built, at 600 s: default
   search 9 -> 18 proved (proteindesign12 4 -> 13 of 15, 0.07 of the time
   where both prove), -f 31 -> 36 (proteindesign12 9 -> 13, 0.18 of the
   time); spot5 unchanged, celar within its seed spread. On the wide set 48 of
   187 optimisation instances build a network (4.5 s of detection in all,
   at most 0.5 s on one); the bound is posted only on proteindesign12.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.costNetwork=false turns it off.

514. The FlatZinc front end removes int_ne and int_lin_ne constraints that
   the declared domains already satisfy (disjoint domains, a fixed side
   outside the other domain, a constant outside the range of the sum)
   before the detectors run, and IntensionTableDetector looks output
   variables up in a set. gfd-schedule n180f7d50m30k18 drops 15,040 of
   its 15,041 disequalities and its set-up takes 3.7 s instead of 4.4 s;
   on the 20 gfd-schedule Challenge instances and 2 cryptanalysis ones
   free search totals -19 % at 600 s, fixed search the same. -Dorg.jacop.fz.entailedDisequalities=false
   keeps them.

515. LCG free search (-f) of an optimisation model that is fast enough for
   the speed check (lcg-slow-rate) now also gives way to free search
   without learning at that check when learning cuts its tree little: its
   conflicts jump back fewer than 1.3 levels with an LBD of at least 0.35
   times their depth (the test of the satisfaction probe; --free-config
   lcg-opt-jump, lcg-opt-lbd) and it stopped improving (lcg-slow-stall,
   lcg-slow-gain). lcg-opt-probe=report only prints the test as
   lcgOptProbe, off turns it off; -s prints lcgWeak. On the MiniZinc
   Challenge 2017 community-detection instances, whose objective is a sum
   of pair weights of about 10,000 kept in a window of 10 to 103 values,
   LCG jumps back 1.05 to 1.3 levels and free search without learning
   makes about 100 times its nodes per second: at 600 s, two runs each,
   Sampson.s10.k3 is proved in 65 and 68 s (base: 111 s and not proved),
   mexican.s26.k4 ends at 48 instead of 42, dolphin.s62.k3 at 67 and 71
   instead of 45 and 65, Strike.s2.k8 and s6.k5 are unchanged. The test
   holds on 3 of the 187 optimisation instances of the wide set, and the
   wide-quick set at 240 s gives way on none.
515. The FlatZinc front end multiplies a linear row by a variable that
   the model multiplies with every variable of the row, and posts the
   product row as a redundant linear constraint (FactoredRowDetector,
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.factoredRows=false turns it off): from
   sum(i) a[i] * x[i] <= b and z[i] = x[i] * m (int_times) with m >= 0
   it posts sum(i) a[i] * z[i] <= b * m (an equality for an equality
   row, reversed for m <= 0). This is the first level of the
   reformulation-linearization technique (Sherali and Adams, SIAM J.
   Discrete Math. 1990; Sherali and Tuncbilek, J. Global Optim. 1992)
   over the products the model already has. In cutting stock models
   with a pattern per stock piece it gives the linear relaxation the
   material bound s * sum(m) >= sum(p) size[p] * demand[p]. MiniZinc
   Challenge 2019 stack-cuttingstock under -f at 240 s: d5 and d14
   proved in 3.4-3.5 s (none proved in 240 s before; 900-975 s in the
   2019 replay), d7 and d8, open in the 2019 Challenge, proved optimal
   (31, 58) in 8-10 s, d3 5.0 -> 1.1 s; hoist-benchmark PU_3_8_5 under
   fd 125 -> 6.5 s.

516. Opt-in variable-bound MIR of the Gomory rows of the linear relaxation
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.vbGomory=true). The exact slack Gomory separator, which
   aggregates the rows by an integer scaling of a row of the basis inverse
   with the row slacks as integer columns, also builds the complemented MIR
   of that aggregation with the variable bounds x <= a u and x >= a u
   substituted (H. Marchand and L. A. Wolsey, Aggregation and mixed integer
   rounding to solve MIPs, Operations Research 49(3), 2001; R. E. Gomory,
   An algorithm for integer solutions to linear programs, 1963), and
   substitutes the slacks back by their rows. It counts in lpVbCuts. The
   relative progress a cut round must make to go on is now a property,
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.cutProgress (default 0.01). On MiniZinc Challenge 2023
   unit-commitment L10 the root cut bound goes from 1,698,000 to 1,699,000
   (1,700,500 with cutProgress 0.0005, cutRoundsMax 100 and
   cutExtraSeconds 20; the HiGHS root reaches 1,702,500). 600 s bounds with
   clauseRows and vbMir on in both: L10 1,696,315 -> 1,697,495 (-f) and
   1,695,410 -> 1,696,915 (default), L12 1,419,720 -> 1,433,245 (-f) and
   1,418,835 -> 1,434,205 (default). On the 41 instances whose relaxation
   the flags of 503 change, 600 s: -f 26 -> 27 solved (physician-scheduling
   instance10-duplicated_0.23 proved in 332 s), total time 0.960; default
   the same 26 solved, total 0.998.

517. Opt-in -Dorg.jacop.lp.columnRows=true: when the linear relaxation has
   more rows than its share, every further row over columns already in the
   relaxation goes to the tail of rows the sparse simplex tries last, not
   only the clause rows of 503. MiniZinc Challenge 2017 routing-flexible
   drops the reified leg-length and direction rows of the path: GCM_0005
   root bound 10,509 -> 46,529 (cut bound 50,926; optimum 76,514), GCM_0006
   9,628 -> 66,473 proved in 91 s at 600 s (default; base no proof), 59 ->
   20 s under -f. 2024 cable-tree-wiring A041 default bound 375,450 ->
   630,395. 2023 unit-commitment L12 bound 1,328,295 -> 1,303,645 (default)
   and 1,348,290 -> 1,321,165 (-f). On the 39 instances whose relaxation it
   changes, 600 s: default one more solved (GCM_0006), total time 0.949;
   -f the same solved, total time 0.968.

518. Three gaps of the 2019 MiniZinc Challenge replay are closed.
   (a) A permutation with an assignment cost whose cycles are at most K
   long (labels cycle[i] = cycle[succ[i]] and bin_packing(K, cycle,
   unit weights), kidney-exchange) gets the cycle formulation of
   Abraham, Blum and Sandholm (EC 2007) and Roth, Sonmez and Unver
   (American Economic Review 2007) for K <= 4 and at most 20,000 cycles:
   a 0/1 variable per cycle, one cycle per vertex, succ[i] and the cost
   as sums of them, posted as redundant linear rows. The bound of
   3_20_0.25_2 goes from 1275 (assignment) to the optimum 1008; the five
   2019 instances are proved in 1.6-2.3 s with -f (8-14 s before,
   Chuffed 0.7-2.4 s) and 1.4-3.3 s in the default search (2.4-10 s).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.boundedCycles=false turns it off.
   (b) Elements over constant arrays whose index is defined by
   int_lin_eq from two to four variables (a lookup in a multi-dimensional
   array, liner-sf-repositioning) become one table over the defining
   variables and the looked-up values when the index occurs nowhere
   else, as the multi-dimensional element of Gecode and the element
   expansion of the CP-SAT presolve (Perron, Didier and Gay, CP 2023).
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.linearIndexElement=false turns it off.
   (c) Lazy clause generation explains x = y + k (and its mirror) by the
   values in both root domains only: a disentailment over 0..100000
   against three values was given the generic reason of 998 atoms,
   now 5. liner-sf fm3_3 -f 40 s -> 8-39 s (Chuffed 9.4 s), tp7_4 600 s
   default unproved -> proved in 480-505 s.
   Of the 240 wide-set instances (a) and (b) fire on 4; at 600 s none is
   worse. Wide-quick at 240 s is unchanged (-f total 0.978 without the
   seed-noisy work-task-variation, default 1.004).

519. The FlatZinc front end bounds an objective that sums step costs of
   counts of values in one array (MiniZinc Challenge ptv: passengers
   per service and station, cost 0 below 265, 10 below 529, ...). For
   every such array the least cost of its counts over the assignments
   of the array's variables is found by a branch and bound over the
   step of each count with a maximum flow check, and posted as
   objective = sum of the arrays' costs + the other terms, each cost at
   least its least value: the bound of a separable concave cost over
   the transportation polytope of the counts (Guisewite and Pardalos,
   Annals of Operations Research 1990). On ptv pax_period_20_5a the
   bound is the optimum 50, which is now proved with -f in 18-81 s
   (never in 1200 s before; CP-SAT 2.3 s, Chuffed stalls at 70); the
   other four ptv instances are unchanged. It fires on none of the 240
   wide-set instances. -Dorg.jacop.fz.countStepCost=false turns it off.

520. The FlatZinc front end posts a strict diffn whose rectangles are fixed
   along one axis (origins and lengths, as the time axis of accap) as unary
   resources (CumulativeUnary) over the other axis, one for each maximal set
   of rectangles that overlap along the fixed axis: the maximal cliques of
   an interval graph, found by a sweep (Gilmore and Hoffman, "A
   characterization of comparability graphs and of interval graphs",
   Canadian Journal of Mathematics 16, 1964). Rectangles of length 0 along
   either axis are kept apart pairwise. Under lazy clause generation a
   failure of Diffn is explained by the bounds of all its rectangles, about
   140 atoms on accap, while each unary resource explains its own tasks.
   MiniZinc Challenge accap (15 instances of 2019, 2022 and 2024), 300 s:
   default search better objectives on 10, worse on 3 (2019 instance6
   110 -> 85, optimum 82; Chuffed reaches 91 in 600 s); -f proved 9 -> 11,
   total 2,443 -> 1,449 s, every proof equal or faster, but instance8 and
   instance9 end at 3x the objective, where a slow LCG keeps its turn while
   it improves by 1 at a time. code-generator (15 instances, 240 s):
   default search one proof gained and one lost, -f neutral.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.diffnFixedAxis=off keeps Diffn; pairs posts the pairwise
   disjunctions of MiniZinc's decomposition instead, both posts both.

521. Lazy clause generation: the reasons of the value precedence
   constraint (SeqPrecedeChain, which index-value symmetry breaking posts
   for interchangeable values) cite "x != v" for a value v removed from x
   in place of the bound atom that removed it ([x <= 11] for 12). The
   bound atom is true only in the branch that tightened the bound, so the
   learnt clauses did not carry over. The literal [x = v] is cited when it
   exists and was false before the propagated literal; a missing one is
   made at the next learnt clause, never during conflict analysis. The
   rest stays as it was. MiniZinc Challenge 2015 open_stacks with
   default search: wbp_20_20_1 52 -> 4 s, wbo_10_20_1 16 -> 6 s,
   wbop_15_30_1 23 -> 17 s; the 38 open_stacks (2009/2011/2015) and wide
   set instances that post a value precedence, 600 s: default search
   proved 32 -> 33 (2011 problem_10_20_1 in 382 s), total time -5.9 %;
   -f the same (+0.1 %). -s prints the cited literals as
   lcgValueLiterals; -Djacop.lcg.valueLiterals=false cites the bounds
   again.

522. Free search on models that order their own rows and columns with
   lex-leader constraints (MiniZinc Challenge opd): each lex_lesseq(X, Y)
   of the model whose swap of X and Y, with the variables the model
   defines from them (the products of two rows), maps every other
   constraint, the domains and the objective onto themselves is left to
   the canonicalizer of local search, as the symmetry breaking JaCoP
   posts is: local search moves freely and a solution is moved onto the
   symmetric one the lex-leader constraints accept. The row and column
   lex-leader constraints of a 0/1 matrix together admit a doubly
   lexical order of any matrix (A. Lubiw, Doubly lexical orderings of
   matrices, SIAM J. Comput. 16(5), 1987), which sorting rows and
   columns in turn reaches. The check runs only when local search
   starts, takes at most 10 s and a twentieth of the time left, and
   either keeps all of the model's lex-leader constraints or none.
   With -f and no ls, such a model gets local search for half of the
   time left, until it stalls (--free-config ls-lex=0 turns that off;
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.symmetryModelLexLs=false turns the check off). Under
   --lcg, local search replaces the block sums of a long sum split for
   lazy clause generation by their definitions (Builder.substituted), so
   a move changes the whole sum at once; before, the model of
   flener_et_al_15_350_100 had 2,400 rows instead of 120 and every moved
   solution broke a block sum. Variables the canonicalizer does not
   move take the values propagation gives them. -s prints lsModelLex.
   -f, 240 s, the 17 instances whose model has lex-leader constraints
   (12 of the wide set, 5 of opd 2015): proved 10 -> 13, total 1,899 ->
   1,075 s, no objective worse; opd 2015 flener_et_al_10_350_100 28 ->
   22, its lower bound, in 15 s (Chuffed 25 at 240 s), small_bibd_11_22_10
   and 13_26_06 proved in 8 s, opd 2017 flener_et_al_15_350_100 32 -> 25
   (best known 24). The 14 other wide set instances whose local search
   changes (split sums under -f): proved 9 -> 10, total 1,376 -> 1,277 s,
   no objective worse.

523. Lazy clause generation: a count over more than lcg-split (20)
   variables is split like a long sum, into counts over blocks of about
   sqrt(n) variables and a sum of the block counts, the totalizer of
   O. Bailleux and Y. Boufkhad, Efficient CNF encoding of Boolean
   cardinality constraints, CP 2003, LNCS 2833, cut to one level as for
   linear constraints in I. Abio and P. J. Stuckey, Encoding linear
   constraints into SAT, CP 2014, LNCS 8656. A reason of the whole count
   cites every variable that takes or misses the value; a block count
   cites its block and the sum its block counts, so learnt clauses stay
   short (peaceable queens 8: 53 -> 3.8 atoms per Count reason). The
   block counts are defined variables, which free LCG search branches
   on last; the linear relaxation keeps them as columns with the value
   rows of their blocks (substituting them as block sums lost the count
   row: kletzander test03 root bound 9,385 -> 350). -s prints
   lcgSplitCounts; --free-config lcg-split-count=0 keeps counts whole.
   MZNC 2021 peaceable_queens 8 -f: time-out at 240 s -> proved in 88 s.
   240 s, the 16 wide set instances with such counts: -f proved 14 ->
   15, total 589 -> 546 s; default total 1,041 -> 950 s. The 62 MZNC
   instances (2011-2026) with such counts, -f: total 4,271 -> 4,279 s
   without the 8 league instances, whose times swing both ways between
   rounds; gametes, arithmetic-target and pq_n7 faster in both rounds.

524. Lazy clause generation: CountValues over more than lcg-split (20)
   variables is split the same way, into CountValues over the blocks
   with a counter per block and value, and for each value a sum of its
   block counters, unless the block counters would outnumber the
   variables (warehouse assignments with 66 facilities over 1,936
   tokens, 6,402 counters, got worse objectives). -s counts the block
   counters in lcgSplitCounts; --free-config lcg-split-count-values=0
   keeps CountValues whole (lcg-split-count=0 keeps both whole). MZNC
   2022 generalized-peacable-queens n9_q5: CountValues reasons 69 -> 8.9
   atoms, -f proved in 90-180 s (time-out at 240 s); n8_q3 175-213 s ->
   74-76 s. 240 s, the 10 MZNC 2022-2023 instances it splits
   (generalized-peacable-queens, chessboard): -f total 2,069 -> 1,921 s
   and 2,136 -> 1,917 s in two rounds, proved 3 -> 4 and 2 -> 3,
   chessboard n39_2 452 -> 622 and 466 -> 634 (maximised); default
   2,043 -> 1,892 s, proved 2 -> 3.

525. FlatZinc set-up on big Pseudo-Boolean models is faster, with the same
   search. The output-variable check used by alias merging is a set
   instead of a scan of every output array, side components take only
   their own clauses, LinearOverlapDetector walks the shorter row, the
   detector gates of #906 read a cached census of constraint kinds and
   cover ten more detectors, and the XOR pairing and common-subexpression
   keys avoid boxed maps. PB 2016 set-up: cache-ibm-q-unbounded 40.5 ->
   24.6 s, ped2.B.recomb1 32.6 -> 17.4 s, atlanta-ip 17.0 -> 11.6 s,
   simp-nonunif 29.9 -> 22.8 s; wide-quick at 240 s total 1.002.

526. Pseudo-Boolean models: two root deductions that CP-SAT makes and
   JaCoP missed on small PB16 instances (Pseudo-Boolean Competition 2016).
   The XorSystem detector reads an equation modulo 2 whose odd
   coefficients leave one or two variables as what it states: x = parity,
   or x = y / x != y (before, such equations were too short for a parity
   row and dropped); -s prints xorImpliedParities. factor-mod-size=8
   (OPT-NLC): optimum proved in 2 s, before not proved in 600 s. The root
   LP refutations merge clauses over every subset of m literals of a set
   of n literals (the binomial encoding of a cardinality constraint) into
   the row sum >= n - m + 1 (SubsetCardinalities; cardinality detection in
   CNF after A. Biere, D. Le Berre, E. Lonca and N. Manthey, "Detecting
   cardinality constraints in CNF", SAT 2014; resolution needs
   exponential proofs of such counting arguments, A. Haken, "The
   intractability of resolution", TCS 39, 1985); -s prints
   lpRefutationCardinalities. fixed-bandwidth-19 (PB 2015, cardinalities
   in clauses): unsatisfiable in 0.7 s, before unknown at 600 s;
   ECgrid5x30split 94 -> 1.5 s. On the 26 instances of the wide set, PB25
   and PB16 where either fires, 600 s: total 2,509 -> 1,708 s (default
   search) and 2,326 -> 1,410 s (-f), one more proved, none lost.
   -Dorg.jacop.lp.subsetCardinalities=false turns the merge off,
   org.jacop.lp.subsetCardinalityLength (default 5) bounds the clauses.

527. Lazy clause generation under a fixed search on an optimisation model
   first runs the annotation's search without learning for 0.5 % of the
   time limit, at most a second, and LCG then gets three times as long,
   at least a second. When LCG has not reached that search's best cost by
   then and is at least three times slower per node, it gives way to the
   annotation's search without learning under the best cost. When that
   search then goes twice as long as it had run (at least 10 s) without a
   better solution, LCG goes on under the best cost. Small sparse diffn
   (at most 30 rectangles, whose minimal areas fill at most 5 % of the
   box) is posted as MiniZinc's pairwise disjunctions under LCG fixed
   search, so that learning gets the disjunct literals. MiniZinc Challenge
   2017 city-position, the same improving solutions 16 times faster per
   node without learning, at 600 s: city-5-05 proved in 44 s (223 s),
   city-5-06 in 133 s (320 s), city-5-08 proved 4,500 in 502 s (927,199
   unproved); city-8-06 at 1200 s 2,468,829 -> 2,224,509. cargo
   (2013-2018), 10 sparse-diffn instances at 600 s: 0 -> 7 proved. Wide
   set fd, 202 instances at 600 s, before the hand-back: solved 138 -> 144,
   total time 0.936; the 18 on which LCG gave way or lost, with it: total
   8,255 -> 7,071 s. --free-config fixed-first=0 turns the
   first search off (fixed-first-max, fixed-first-lcg, fixed-first-rate,
   fixed-first-stall and fixed-first-stall-min tune it), and
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.diffnPairs=0 the pairs (diffnPairsDensity sets the
   density). -s prints fixedFirst=decided, lcg, cp or cp-lcg.

528. Lazy clause generation records the atoms of a domain change from what
   the domain reports of it instead of comparing a shadow copy of the
   domain's runs with the new domain (option B of the LCG cost per
   conflict study, osolpro #685). IntervalDomain, SmallDenseDomain and
   LazyBitSetDomain tell the DomainChangeRecorder whether a narrowing moved
   only the bounds (changingBounds), removed one value (removingValue) or
   an interval (removingInterval); AtomicTrail then writes the bound atoms
   from the old bounds and the != atoms of the values inside the new
   bounds that it has not yet recorded, and keeps no runs, so a backtrack
   restores only the bounds and the size. The atoms are the same as
   before, so the search is the same. In a JFR profile under --lcg the
   shadow share falls from 7.0 to 3.2 % (tower), 4.5 to 2.2 %
   (ghoulomb) and 5.1 to 4.1 % (valve-network). Wide-quick (62
   instances) at 240 s with -f: total 2,858 -> 2,779 s and 2,834 ->
   2,658 s in two rounds, 52 -> 53 solved; default search 4,540 ->
   4,534 s. -Djacop.lcg.directChanges=false restores the shadow diff;
   -Djacop.lcg.checkDirect=true keeps the shadows and throws when the
   two recordings disagree on any change (testing only).

529. The clausal compilation of satisfaction models (entry 489) leaves
   models with a difference x - y = d (a linear equality of three terms
   with coefficients of both signs) over more than 3 values to lazy clause
   generation (-Dorg.jacop.fz.clausal.sumDomain, default 3). It fires on
   Costas arrays, NonTransitiveDice and Primes (already over the clause
   limit) of the 211 XCSP models the compilation took. At 600 s with -f,
   four runs each, total over costas-array 17 (MZNC 2011) and
   CostasArray-15..18 (XCSP 2022): 2,417 -> 1,262 s; costas-array 17
   314-389 -> 61-84 s, CostasArray-16 and -17 lose 11-48 s.

530. CountValues (global_cardinality with variable counts) bounds the sum
   of the counters of every set of at least two classes (counted values
   and the rest, up to six classes) by the variables that may take a class
   of the set and those that can take only classes of it, the Hall-set
   reasoning of the flow model of global cardinality (J.-C. Regin,
   "Generalized arc consistency for global cardinality constraint", AAAI
   1996). Each counter of the set gets the bounds the others leave, and a
   set at a bound takes or loses the variables it may take. Before, a
   counter could keep a bound that needs more variables than the values
   it shares them with leave: x0 in {0, 1, 3}, x1 in 0..3, x2 in 2..3,
   x3 in 0..1, at least one 0 among them left three 1s possible. The
   consistency checker now finds CountValues bounds(Z) consistent at every
   node (111 of 35,753 nodes missed it). The 25 FlatZinc test models with
   count_values: same search or same results, total time 202 -> 201 s.

531. The JaLP presolve finds dominated columns over propagated dual bounds
   (E. D. Andersen and K. D. Andersen, "Presolving in linear
   programming", Mathematical Programming 71 (1995) 221-245, section 3.5;
   the implied dual row bounds of HiGHS). Before, only a column singleton
   without an upper or lower bound bounded the dual of its row. Now every
   column without one gives a linear inequality over the duals whose
   activity bounds tighten them, in rounds. A column bound that its rows
   imply also counts as absent (the bound of a column singleton within
   the row's limit, of other columns beyond a margin), when it is implied
   only from bounds that stay. The dominated columns are found over the
   true costs, and weakly dominated ones (reduced cost possibly 0) are
   fixed too when no dual bound came from them. The pass limit of the
   presolve counts only the passes in which the rows and columns change,
   so that rules found one at a time no longer keep the later ones out
   (scrs8: 258 aggregations had never run). Over the 687 LPs of the LP
   bench, 73 presolve smaller and 1 larger: woodw 5,344 -> 4,066 columns
   (HiGHS 4,083), irp 19,370 -> 19,013 (HiGHS 19,035). At 120 s, 0 wrong
   answers, 649 instead of 648 solved and a total of 6,866 -> 6,747 s
   (satellites2-40 from the time limit to 23 s).
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveDualBounds=false,
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveImpliedFree=false and
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveWeakDominated=false turn the parts off.

532. The JaLP presolve fixes a parallel column of another cost when its
   twin can take up any move of it without a bound (HiGHS
   detectParallelRowsAndCols): with column k lambda times column j,
   x_j + lambda t and x_k - t keep every row, so the cheaper direction
   drives one of them to its bound. Before, parallel columns were merged
   only when their costs had the ratio of their entries. sgpf5y6:
   58,528 -> 40,841 columns (HiGHS 39,029). With entry 524, the excess of
   JaLP's presolved sizes over HiGHS's on the LP bench falls from 211,938
   to 162,586 rows and columns, and the presolve changes the root LP of
   15 of the 88 wide-set runs that reach JaLP; at 240 s, two rounds
   against develop: fixed search 0.997 and 0.998 of the total time, -f
   1.005 and 0.982, the same instances solved.
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveParallelDominated=false turns it off.

533. The JaLP presolve can remove dependent equations (Andersen and
   Andersen 1995, section 3.6; HiGHS removeDependentEquations): the
   equations with their right-hand sides are factorized as the columns of
   a matrix, and one left without a pivot is a combination of the others
   with the same right-hand side. eq_eq20 20 -> 7 rows, qap10 1,820 ->
   1,547 (HiGHS 1,548), nug08-3rd 19,728 -> 18,261 (HiGHS 18,268). It is
   off by default: on the 147 LPs of the LP bench where it removes rows,
   the simplex took 7.6 % longer at 120 s (2,085 -> 2,244 s; dano3 44k ->
   72k pivots after one row went, pds-100 lost).
   -Dorg.jacop.jalp.presolveDependent=true turns it on.

534. The linear relaxation of an objective tries all linearized rows of the
   model's constraints (big-M rows of reifications and implications, the
   sides of min and max, ...) up to 8,000 of them
   (-Dorg.jacop.lp.trialRows), not only a share of twice the linear rows
   plus 100, and keeps the extra rows in search, where three pivot limits
   used to drop them, when they raise the root bound over the domain of
   the objective at least as much as the rows before them and at least
   double it (-Dorg.jacop.lp.raiseKeeps=false turns that off). As in
   CP-SAT's linearization level 2, which adds the linear relaxation of
   every linearizable constraint (L. Perron, F. Didier and S. Gay, "The
   CP-SAT-LP solver", CP 2023, LIPIcs 280). MiniZinc Challenge 2017
   routing-flexible GCM_0005: root bound 10,509 -> 46,529 (optimum
   76,514; CP-SAT 11 s, 214 s with linearization_level:0); at 600 s with
   default search GCM_0005 and GCM_0006 are proved in 215 and 292 s
   (before not proved), GCM_0007 314 -> 263 s. wide-quick at 240 s: total
   time 0.997 (default search) and 1.008 (-f) before the doubling rule,
   the same instances solved.

535. FlatZinc parsing and set-up of big models are faster, with the same
   parse tree. The hot syntactic lookaheads of the FlatZinc grammar
   (array literal, set literal, array access, packed integer element) are
   token-kind checks instead of lookahead scans, and three detector passes
   (DisjunctiveListsDiffer, LinearIndexElement, CountStepCostBound) return
   at once when the model has none of the constraint kinds they read.
   Same parse tree on all 4,856 FlatZinc test and PB models. PB 2016
   simp-nonunif-100_100.10 set-up 29.1 -> 26.0 s (median of 3); wide-quick
   at 240 s total 1.006 (noise), PB5 at 600 s total 0.986.

536. Under -f, lazy clause generation that is slow at the speed check (10 % of
   the time limit) but goes deep between its conflicts is kept instead of
   giving way to search without learning: with a solution, at least 100
   decisions per conflict, more conflicts than solutions, under 50
   conflicts/s and learnt clauses of at most 200 literals on average.
   cable_tree_wiring A073 (MiniZinc Challenge 2020): optimum proved in 3 of
   3 seeds at 600 s (before 2 of 3, the third ended at 296M);
   rcpsp-wet-diverse j60 proved in one seed (before never). On the wide set
   (240 instances, -f, 600 s) the rule fires only on warehouse olil4 without
   the clause length limit and on none with it. free-config lcg-slow-depth
   (0 = off), lcg-slow-depth-rate and lcg-slow-depth-length; -s prints
   lcgSlowDeep.

537. Fixed search on an optimisation model that has no solution yet
   tastes the annotation's search without learning, as fixed
   satisfaction does (entry 492), also with -a: lazy clause generation
   stops at lcg-fixed-at of the time limit, the search without learning
   runs for lcg-fixed-share of that time, and the one further in the
   annotation's tree goes on (a solution found in the taste keeps the
   search without learning). Before, LCG without a solution gave way only
   below 30 conflicts/s. Sampson.s10.k3 (MiniZinc Challenge 2017
   community-detection, input_order on the communities): LCG makes about
   3,000 conflicts/s and found 8 in 600 s; now the optimum 10 is proved in
   88 s. On the 13 wide-set optimisation instances without a solution
   after 55 s of fixed search, 600 s: total 7,276 -> 6,763 s, none worse.

538. Covering models: column dominance at presolve (CoveringDominance). A 0/1
   variable with a cost of at least 0 in a minimized objective that occurs
   only in the objective and positively in clauses is fixed to 0 when
   another such variable of no higher cost is in all its clauses (the column
   reduction of set covering: R. S. Garfinkel and G. L. Nemhauser, "Integer
   Programming", Wiley 1972, chapter 8; C. Toregas and C. ReVelle, "Binary
   logic solutions to a class of location problem", Geographical Analysis
   5, 1973; CP-SAT finds such pairs in its var_domination presolve). It
   reads the objective through the block sums of lazy clause generation and
   runs under it, without proof logging, when at least 100 variables are
   dominated (org.jacop.fz.coveringDominance.min); -s prints coveringFixed. The
   linear relaxation takes the columns fixed at the root as constants when
   at least 1000 are fixed, and leaves out rows of constants; -s prints
   lpFoldedColumns. pdc.r (Pseudo-Boolean Competition 2016, 6,904 covering
   clauses over 19,021 variables): 16,053 columns fixed, root bound 15 ->
   88.5, optimum 94 proved in 17-21 s, before 97 found in 120 s.
   600 s, the 20 instances where either part fires: total -f 3,887 ->
   3,307 s (pdc.r not proved -> 26 s), default 3,377 -> 3,814 s, which is
   one instance, fir09_area_opers, now outside the rule and proved or not
   by the base as well.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.coveringDominance=false and -Dorg.jacop.lp.foldFixed=0
   turn them off.

539. Binpacking explanations under lazy clause generation are shorter.
   An item kept out of several bins at once because one bin cannot reach
   its smallest load without it is explained by that bin's reason once,
   not once per removed bin; and an explanation through the loads a bin
   can take leaves out the excluded items too heavy to fit under the
   load's maximum, citing that maximum instead. steelmillslab 2019
   bench_19_6 with LCG (fixed search, no CP slice): 801 -> 46 atoms per
   Binpacking reason, 11,400 -> 8,700 conflicts, 16.4 -> 11.4 s. The 7
   wide-set instances with Binpacking, 240 s, two rounds: totals 1.006
   (default), 1.002 (-f), 1.008 (LCG forced); gbac UD3 -f best 312/334 ->
   198/211. wide-quick 240 s -f: 2,835 -> 2,801 s, 53 = 53 proved.

540. JaLP no longer calls an LP infeasible from a dual ray that only
   rounding makes look like a proof. The bound proof of a dual row now
   sums the ray over every column, the basic columns of the other rows
   too; before, it took them as zero, which holds only for an exact ray.
   On mittelmann cont4 the presolved LP got a ray with entries near 1e32
   whose basic columns summed to 1e32 instead of 0: the reduced solve was
   called INFEASIBLE after 15,045 pivots and the whole LP was re-solved
   until the time limit (HiGHS interior point finds the reduced LP
   feasible). Over the 687 LPs of the LP bench at 120 s, 0 wrong answers,
   the same pivots on every LP solved by both, 649 instead of 648 solved
   (supportcase10 114 s, at the limit) and a total of 6,705 -> 6,695 s.

541. Lazy clause generation no longer shrinks learnt clauses to one
   literal per lower decision level (all-UIP shrinking, M. Fleury and
   A. Biere, "Efficient all-UIP learned clause minimization", SAT 2021;
   it stays on in the pure SAT solver). Under LCG the literals of a lower
   level are bounds and values reached by propagation, which other orders
   of the decisions reach too; shrinking replaced them by the decision of
   that level and pinned the clause to one prefix of the search, so every
   later first decision refuted the same subtrees again. MZNC 2015
   open_stacks problem_20_20_1 (fixed search): 90,000 -> 39,000 nodes,
   30 -> 18 s. 600 s, open stacks and precedence set (fixed search):
   total 4,318 -> 3,468 s, one more decided (wbo_30_30_1 600 -> 30 s,
   problem_10_20_1 476 -> 220 s); wide-quick total 1.004 (default search;
   lot-sizing pigment20a 85 -> 140 s, 21 % more nodes) and 0.883 (-f, one
   more decided: celar graph11 600 -> 82 s).
   -Djacop.lcg.shrink=true restores shrinking.

539. Pseudo-Boolean models of clauses go to JaSAT whole. A linear constraint
   over 0/1 variables that is at most two clauses per unfixed variable over
   its own variables (as lazy clause generation already takes it,
   jacop.lcg.clauseShaped) and a lex-leader constraint of symmetry breaking
   over 0/1 variables now count as clauses, so a model of them alone is a
   CNF that JaSAT solves with its preprocessing. A lex-leader constraint
   x <=lex y becomes the clauses of F. A. Aloul, I. L. Markov and K. A.
   Sakallah, "Efficient symmetry breaking for Boolean satisfiability",
   IJCAI 2003, with one new variable per position for the equal prefix.
   -s prints pureClauseShaped and pureClauseLexPairs. 3col-left3reg-l090
   (Pseudo-Boolean Competition 2016: x + y <= 1 on each edge and colour,
   one colour per vertex): JaSAT eliminates 6,988 of 7,089 variables and
   refutes it in 8 s, before 35 s under lazy clause generation. 600 s, the
   9 instances where it fires: total default 2,124 -> 1,553 s, -f 1,404 ->
   741 s (battleship 271 -> 4 s, heule-nol unknown -> 28-326 s).

540. The MaxSAT local search of free search takes the linear constraints
   that are clauses (see 539) as hard clauses; before, it left out every
   linear row over cost variables alone as implied by the objective, and
   on hanoi5 (Pseudo-Boolean Competition 2016, 1,931 rows x + y <= 1
   over the 3,862 cost variables) it searched without them, reached cost
   1,810, below the optimum 1,931, and the store rejected its
   assignments. When the search reaches no assignment of the hard clauses
   by its patience, JaSAT looks for one in the rest of its budget, and the
   local search goes on from it for at most half the time it searched
   before (the restart from a SAT model of SATLike-c, Z. Lei, Z. Lyu,
   S. Cai, X. Zhang, J. Yin, Z. Wu and Y. Zhang, MaxSAT Evaluation 2021).
   hanoi5: an assignment of the optimal cost in 0.7 s. -s prints
   maxsatSatTime; --free-config maxsat-sat=0 turns the JaSAT step off.
   600 s, the 47 instances of the MaxSAT Evaluation 2024 and the PB
   Competition 2016 where it changes the local search: total -f 7,436 ->
   7,390 s, default 8,445 -> 8,281 s, 37 and 35 proved in both; the
   first solution comes earlier on 16 instances and later on 5 with -f.

541. -a and -n k on a satisfaction model print each assignment of the
   output variables once in every engine, and -n k prints k different
   ones (all of them, with ==========, when there are fewer). Before,
   depth-first search (--lcg off, the default engine under --presolve on
   or -p) printed an assignment again for each value of a variable that
   the search annotation names but the output does not: 9 lines for 3
   assignments on int_search([x, h]) with x and y printed, and -n 3 could
   print one solution three times, where lazy clause generation and free
   search printed each once. A model whose annotation names such a
   variable keeps an exact key per printed solution (the printed values
   packed in the bits of their root domains, 8 bytes per 64 bits plus 8
   of hash table) and drops a solution whose key was printed. The search
   follows its decisions (DepthFirstSearch.setDecisionListener, a new
   DecisionListener): after a solution below a decision on a hidden
   variable taken once the outputs are fixed, its other branches are not
   searched, and keys are kept only below the highest decision on a
   hidden variable taken with outputs still open. A model whose
   annotation names only printed variables keeps nothing and searches as
   before. -s solutions= counts the printed solutions; --search-statistics
   prints repeatedOutputBranches, repeatedOutputs and peakOutputKeys.

542. FlatZinc: an if-then-else over reified equalities of one variable,
   the flattening of if c then x = a else x = b endif
   (jacop_if_then_else_bool([c, true], [p, q], true) with
   int_eq_reif(x, a, p) and int_eq_reif(x, b, q)), now also posts the
   ConditionalSelect([c, true], [a, b], x) that jacop_if_then_else_int
   posts, so x stays within the branches still possible. Before, a
   variable declared var int kept the whole integer range: in
   work-task-variation (MiniZinc Challenge 2025) the objective sums 480 of
   them, the linear relaxation bounded it by -536870910 and each lazy
   clause generation reason of the sum cited about 260 bounds; now most of
   them are fixed at the root, the reasons have 17 atoms, the root bound is
   0, and -f proves the optimum in 27 s instead of 51-55 s (600 s). It
   fires on 5 of the 240 instances of the wide set (also lot-sizing
   pigment20a and three sdn-chain). wide-quick, 240 s, -f, two rounds:
   total 2,919 / 2,924 -> 2,840 / 2,785 s, 52 -> 53 proved (pigment20a 240
   -> 70 s). In default search work-task-variation ends worse (1,365 ->
   2,155 at 600 s): the search without learning that runs before LCG now
   improves its solution in small steps for minutes, so it never counts
   as stalled and LCG does not take over.
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.conditionalEquality=false turns it off.

543. fzn-jacop killed by SIGTERM or SIGINT without -s (as MiniZinc and the
   competition scripts end a solver at their time limit) prints what it
   prints at its own time-out: the solution it kept and
   "%% =====TIME-OUT=====", or "=====UNKNOWN=====" when it printed none.
   Before, without -a a run printed its solutions only when it ended, so
   a killed run printed nothing and lost the solutions it had found. The
   exit code stays 143, -s prints as before, and runs started in process
   (Fz2jacop.callMain) register no hook.

Version 4.11
=============

1. Changes in XexpYeqZ to be compatible with minizinc definition for
   negative exponents. Domain consistency implemented.

2. Falzinc definitions for asinh, acosh and atanh.

3. New constraints AlldifferentExceptZero and AlldifferentExcept
   available also from minizinc.

4. New constraints ElementBool available also from minizinc.

5. Definition of impact variable selection method for minizinc.

