Version JaCoP 1.3 
=================

285. The FlatZinc front end eliminates variables of degree two between
   tables and elements (bucket elimination, R. Dechter, "Bucket
   elimination: a unifying framework for reasoning", Artificial
   Intelligence 113, 1999; J. Larrosa and R. Dechter, "Boosting search
   with variable elimination in constraint optimization and constraint
   satisfaction problems", Constraints 8, 2003; as bounded variable
   elimination in SAT, N. Een and A. Biere, SAT 2005). A variable that
   occurs in exactly two relations (table, short table, or an element over
   a constant array) and nowhere else is projected out of their join when
   the join has no larger arity than the larger of the two and at most
   max(10,000, r1 + r2) rows (-Dorg.jacop.fz.tableElimination.rows). The
   join of a Berge-acyclic chain propagates as strongly as the chain, so
   the search keeps its pruning on fewer variables and constraints.
   Eliminated variables are printed with values rebuilt from the joined
   relations in reverse elimination order. It is off for satisfaction
   models with -a or -n above 1, with --proof, and with
   -Dorg.jacop.fz.tableElimination=false. Nested lookups
   tab[x, inv[y]] of the XCSP 2025 SEDF models flatten to chains of five
   constraints through four auxiliary variables; on the 25 instances where
   it fires (10 SEDF, 15 code-generator), at 600 s, SEDF goes from 6 to 10
   proved (SEDF-28-1..4 unknown -> 203-393 s, 25-1 210 -> 24 s); the
   code-generator instances, in default mode and with -f, prove the same
   optima in about the same time, with timed-out objectives within noise.
